const PASSAGES = [
{"id":"f-0001","page":"freq","group":"1〜100","kind":"国際情勢","text":"For decades, the small republic at the tip of the peninsula was regarded as one of the most stable states in the region. That image collapsed last spring, when a group of army officers attempted a coup amid growing anger over food prices. The rebellion lasted only three days. Loyal troops made a tactical retreat from the capital, regrouped in the hills, and then retook the main government buildings with little bloodshed. The president, however, did not treat the victory as a mandate to rule more harshly. Instead, he softened his stance, dropped the hostile rhetoric he had used against his critics, and promised a referendum on a new constitution that would limit his own powers. Several officers who had led the plot fled abroad and applied for asylum, and their request caused a diplomatic clash with a neighboring country, which argued that a sovereign state has the right to try its own soldiers. Analysts say the real test is still to come. The president is seventy-four, and he has not named a successor. Unless the reforms improve the governance of the country before he leaves office, they warn, the next crisis may not end so quietly.","ja":"半島の先端にあるその小さな共和国は、何十年もの間、地域で最も安定した国の一つと見なされてきた。その印象は昨年の春に崩れた。食料価格への怒りが高まるさなか、軍の将校の一団がクーデターを試みたのだ。反乱は3日しか続かなかった。政府に忠実な部隊は首都から戦術的に撤退し、丘陵地帯で態勢を立て直した後、ほとんど流血なしに主要な政府庁舎を奪い返した。しかし大統領は、この勝利をより厳しく統治するための信任とは受け取らなかった。むしろ姿勢を和らげ、批判者に向けていた敵意のある言い回しをやめ、自らの権限を制限する新憲法について国民投票を行うと約束した。計画を主導した将校の何人かは国外へ逃れて亡命を申請し、その申請は隣国との外交上の対立を引き起こした。隣国は、主権国家には自国の兵士を裁く権利があると主張したのである。専門家は、本当の試練はこれからだと言う。大統領は74歳で、後継者を指名していない。彼が退任する前に改革によって国の統治が改善されなければ、次の危機はこれほど静かには終わらないかもしれないと、彼らは警告している。","words":[["peninsula","freq:peninsula"],["coup","freq:coup"],["amid","freq:amid"],["rebellion","freq:rebellion"],["tactical","freq:tactical"],["mandate","freq:mandate"],["stance","freq:stance"],["hostile","freq:hostile"],["rhetoric","freq:rhetoric"],["referendum","freq:referendum"],["asylum","freq:asylum"],["clash","freq:clash"],["sovereign","freq:sovereign"],["successor","freq:successor"],["governance","freq:governance"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the president do after the coup attempt failed?","o":["He ordered the arrest of his critics.","He offered a public vote on limiting his own powers.","He moved the capital to the hills.","He asked a neighboring country for military help."],"a":1,"why":"promised a referendum on a new constitution that would limit his own powers（自らの権限を制限する新憲法について国民投票を約束した）とある。"},{"q":"Why do analysts think another crisis is possible?","o":["The president is old and has chosen no one to follow him.","The army still controls the capital.","Food prices have risen again.","The new constitution has already been rejected."],"a":0,"why":"The president is seventy-four, and he has not named a successor（74歳で後継者を指名していない）ことが理由として挙げられている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0002","page":"freq","group":"1〜100","kind":"事件の報道","text":"A former city official who allegedly accepted payments from a construction firm was released on bail yesterday, three weeks after his arrest. He had been held in detention while prosecutors examined thousands of documents seized from his office. The case began with the disclosure of e-mails by a junior employee, who had also filed a complaint of harassment against the official. According to the e-mails, the official gave the firm explicit instructions on how to win a contract for a new bridge. His legal counsel insists that the messages have been misunderstood and says his client will enter a plea of not guilty. A trial date is still pending, partly because the defense argues that the matter falls outside the jurisdiction of the city court. The case is complicated by the fact that the firm's founder, now deceased, signed most of the contracts himself. There is also speculation that other officials were involved, although no further arrests have been made. Under the provisions of a law passed last year, a conviction could bring a prison sentence of up to ten years. The mayor has made a pledge to publish every contract the city has signed since 2015.","ja":"建設会社から金銭を受け取ったとされる市の元職員が、逮捕から3週間たった昨日、保釈された。検察が彼の事務所から押収した数千点の書類を調べる間、彼は拘留されていた。事件の発端は、ある若手職員によるメールの公開だった。この職員は元職員に対して嫌がらせの苦情も申し立てていた。メールによれば、元職員は新しい橋の契約を取る方法について、会社に明白な指示を与えていた。彼の弁護人は、メールは誤解されていると主張し、依頼人は無罪を申し立てると述べている。公判の日程はまだ決まっていない。その理由の一つは、この件は市の裁判所の管轄外だと弁護側が主張していることだ。会社の創業者はすでに亡くなっており、その創業者が契約の大半に自ら署名していたことが、事件を複雑にしている。ほかの職員も関わっていたという憶測もあるが、新たな逮捕者は出ていない。昨年成立した法律の規定では、有罪判決を受ければ最長10年の禁錮刑になりうる。市長は、2015年以降に市が結んだすべての契約を公表すると誓った。","words":[["allegedly","freq:allegedly"],["bail","freq:bail"],["detention","freq:detention"],["disclosure","freq:disclosure"],["harassment","freq:harassment"],["explicit","freq:explicit"],["counsel","freq:counsel"],["plea","freq:plea"],["pending","freq:pending"],["jurisdiction","freq:jurisdiction"],["deceased","freq:deceased"],["speculation","freq:speculation"],["provisions","freq:provisions"],["conviction","freq:conviction"],["pledge","freq:pledge"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did the case first come to light?","o":["The mayor published the city's contracts.","Prosecutors searched a construction site.","A junior employee made some e-mails public.","The firm's founder confessed."],"a":2,"why":"The case began with the disclosure of e-mails by a junior employee（若手職員によるメールの公開が発端）とある。"},{"q":"What is one reason the trial date has not been set?","o":["The official is still in detention.","The defense says the city court has no authority over the case.","The e-mails have been lost.","The new law has not yet come into effect."],"a":1,"why":"the defense argues that the matter falls outside the jurisdiction of the city court（市の裁判所の管轄外だと弁護側が主張している）ため。"}]},
{"id":"f-0003","page":"freq","group":"1〜100","kind":"エネルギー政策","text":"For most of the last century, the island's economy depended on two things: iron ore from its northern mines and crude oil from wells off its coast. Both brought wealth, but neither was sustainable. By the 1990s the mines were nearly empty, and burning fossil fuels had made the island one of the heaviest polluters per person in the world. The government responded with a plan of unusual magnitude. It set a threshold for emissions from every large factory and required companies above that level to offset the excess by paying for wind and solar projects. At first, participation was voluntary, and few firms joined. The plan gained momentum only when the rules became law, thereby forcing even the largest producers to act. Today, a robust network of wind farms supplies most of the island's electricity, and engineers are testing synthetic fuels for ships and aircraft. Some researchers on the island are even working on nuclear fusion, although they admit it will not be a viable source of power for decades. Critics argue that the optimal policy would have been to act twenty years earlier. Still, few countries have changed direction so completely in a single generation.","ja":"前世紀の大半を通じて、その島の経済は2つのものに頼っていた。北部の鉱山の鉄鉱石と、沖合の油井から出る原油である。どちらも富をもたらしたが、どちらも持続可能ではなかった。1990年代までに鉱山はほぼ掘り尽くされ、化石燃料を燃やしてきた結果、島は1人あたりの汚染が世界で最も多い地域の一つになっていた。政府は異例の規模の計画で対応した。すべての大工場の排出量に基準値を設け、それを超える企業には、風力や太陽光の事業に資金を出して超過分を埋め合わせるよう求めたのだ。当初、参加は任意で、加わる企業はほとんどなかった。計画に勢いがついたのは、規則が法律になり、それによって最大手の生産者までもが行動せざるをえなくなってからである。今日では、力強い風力発電網が島の電力の大半を供給し、技術者たちは船や航空機向けの合成燃料を試験している。島の研究者の中には核融合に取り組む人さえいるが、それが実用的な電源になるのは何十年も先だと彼らは認めている。批判する人々は、最適な政策は20年早く行動することだったと主張する。それでも、一世代のうちにこれほど完全に方向を変えた国はほとんどない。","words":[["ore","freq:ore"],["crude","freq:crude"],["sustainable","freq:sustainable"],["fossil","freq:fossil"],["magnitude","freq:magnitude"],["threshold","freq:threshold"],["offset","freq:offset"],["voluntary","freq:voluntary"],["momentum","freq:momentum"],["thereby","freq:thereby"],["robust","freq:robust"],["synthetic","freq:synthetic"],["fusion","freq:fusion"],["viable","freq:viable"],["optimal","freq:optimal"]],"qs":[{"q":"When did the government's plan begin to succeed?","o":["When the mines were closed","When taking part became a legal duty","When oil prices fell","When fusion power became available"],"a":1,"why":"The plan gained momentum only when the rules became law（規則が法律になって初めて勢いがついた）とある。"},{"q":"What do critics say about the island's policy?","o":["It should have started much sooner.","It costs too much.","It relies too heavily on wind.","It has harmed the shipping industry."],"a":0,"why":"the optimal policy would have been to act twenty years earlier（20年早く行動するのが最適だった）と主張している。"}]},
{"id":"f-0004","page":"freq","group":"1〜100","kind":"建物の修復","text":"When the old Marlow concert hall closed in 1998, few people expected it to open again. Water had caused serious structural damage to the roof, and only a fraction of the original seats remained. Yet the hall had one quality that could not be replaced: its acoustic design, which musicians had long considered a marvel. A group of local residents therefore began a campaign for its restoration. The work took eleven years. Builders discovered that the vertical beams supporting the balcony were still intact, but the elaborate plaster ceiling had to be remade by hand. The architects copied the shape of the original, which resembled the hull of a wooden ship turned upside down, with great precision, because even a small change might have altered the sound. They also had to obtain clearance from the city for every alteration, since the building was protected by law. The result has won praise for its aesthetic appeal as well as its sound. The most notable sign of success came last month, when a famous orchestra chose the hall for its only gig in the region. The full house that night was a testament to what a determined community can achieve.","ja":"古いマーロウ・コンサートホールが1998年に閉館したとき、再び開くと考えた人はほとんどいなかった。水のせいで屋根には深刻な構造上の損傷があり、元の座席はごく一部しか残っていなかった。それでも、このホールには取り替えのきかない特質が一つあった。音楽家たちが長く驚異と見なしてきた音響設計である。そこで地元住民の一団が修復を求める運動を始めた。工事には11年かかった。作業員は、バルコニーを支える垂直の梁が無傷のまま残っていることを発見したが、手の込んだしっくいの天井は手作業で作り直さなければならなかった。建築家たちは、木造船の船体を逆さにしたような元の形を非常に正確に写し取った。わずかな変更でも音が変わるおそれがあったからだ。また、この建物は法律で保護されていたため、変更のたびに市の許可を得なければならなかった。完成したホールは、音だけでなく美的な魅力でも称賛されている。成功を示す最も注目すべき出来事は先月にあった。有名なオーケストラが、この地域でのただ一度の演奏会の場にこのホールを選んだのである。その夜の満員の客席は、決意を持った地域社会に何ができるかを示す証しだった。","words":[["structural","freq:structural"],["fraction","freq:fraction"],["acoustic","freq:acoustic"],["marvel","freq:marvel"],["restoration","freq:restoration"],["vertical","freq:vertical"],["intact","freq:intact"],["elaborate","freq:elaborate"],["hull","freq:hull"],["precision","freq:precision"],["clearance","freq:clearance"],["aesthetic","freq:aesthetic"],["notable","freq:notable"],["gig","freq:gig"],["testament","freq:testament"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the architects copy the ceiling's original shape so carefully?","o":["The city required cheaper materials.","A different shape might have changed how the hall sounds.","The original plans had been lost.","Musicians wanted a more modern look."],"a":1,"why":"even a small change might have altered the sound（わずかな変更でも音が変わるおそれがあった）ためである。"},{"q":"What recent event showed that the project had succeeded?","o":["The hall won a national building prize.","The city removed its legal protection.","A well-known orchestra chose to perform there.","The original seats were found and returned."],"a":2,"why":"a famous orchestra chose the hall for its only gig in the region（有名なオーケストラが地域で唯一の演奏会の場に選んだ）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0005","page":"freq","group":"1〜100","kind":"心理学の研究","text":"Psychologists have long tried to gauge how people recover from trauma, and the results are often surprising. In one recent study, researchers followed two hundred adults who had lived through a serious accident. The criteria for inclusion were strict: every participant had to have been injured, and none could have a history of mental illness. Over three years, the volunteers took cognitive tests and described their feelings in interviews, while cameras recorded their facial expressions. The findings were revealing. Recovery was rarely a linear process. Many participants improved quickly, fell back after a few months, and then improved again. The researchers also found a stark difference between two groups. Those who described the accident as a void in their lives, something that had simply taken things away, recovered slowly. Those who saw it as a turning point, even a painful one, did far better. The study's authors say this has a profound meaning for treatment: helping patients rebuild their sense of self, or ego, may matter as much as medicine. They add that there is an ethical side to the work. In the worst-case scenario, forcing people to talk too early can deepen the damage, however kind the sentiment behind it.","ja":"心理学者は長い間、人が心の傷からどう回復するかを測ろうとしてきたが、その結果はしばしば意外なものだ。最近のある研究では、重大な事故を経験した成人200人を追跡した。対象に含める基準は厳しく、参加者は全員が負傷しており、精神疾患の病歴がないことが条件だった。3年にわたり、参加者は認知テストを受け、面接で気持ちを語り、その間カメラが顔の表情を記録した。結果は実態をよく明らかにするものだった。回復が一直線に進むことはめったになかった。多くの参加者は急速に良くなり、数か月後に後戻りし、それからまた良くなった。研究者たちは2つの集団の間に際立った違いも見つけた。事故を人生の空白、つまり単に何かを奪っていったものと表現した人々は、回復が遅かった。たとえつらくても、それを転機と見た人々は、はるかに良い経過をたどった。研究の著者たちは、これは治療にとって重大な意味を持つと言う。患者が自己の感覚、すなわち自我を立て直すのを助けることは、薬と同じくらい重要かもしれないのだ。彼らは、この仕事には倫理的な側面もあると付け加える。最悪の場合、あまりに早く無理に話をさせると、背後にある気持ちがどれほど親切でも、傷を深めてしまうことがある。","words":[["gauge","freq:gauge"],["trauma","freq:trauma"],["criteria","freq:criteria"],["inclusion","freq:inclusion"],["cognitive","freq:cognitive"],["facial","freq:facial"],["revealing","freq:revealing"],["linear","freq:linear"],["stark","freq:stark"],["void","freq:void"],["profound","freq:profound"],["ego","freq:ego"],["ethical","freq:ethical"],["scenario","freq:scenario"],["sentiment","freq:sentiment"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the study find about the process of recovery?","o":["It usually took exactly three years.","It often moved forward and backward rather than steadily.","It depended mainly on the type of injury.","It was faster for people with a history of illness."],"a":1,"why":"Recovery was rarely a linear process（回復が一直線に進むことはめったになかった）。良くなって後戻りし、また良くなったとある。"},{"q":"Which participants recovered better?","o":["Those who regarded the accident as a turning point","Those who refused to be interviewed","Those who took more medicine","Those who described the accident as an emptiness"],"a":0,"why":"Those who saw it as a turning point ... did far better（転機と見た人々のほうがはるかに良かった）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0006","page":"freq","group":"1〜100","kind":"文学の解説","text":"The epic poem known as the Song of Aldren, written about nine hundred years ago, tells the story of a mountain clan and the prophet who led it. In the first part, the prophet preaches a strict doctrine: his people will find salvation only if they give up war. Their neighbors, who see the peaceful clan as easy prey, attack again and again. The second part, a sequel written by a later poet, is darker. A wicked nephew of the prophet seizes power, and a succession of weak leaders follows. The clan's descent into violence, coupled with a long famine, almost destroys it. Scholars once read the poem as a purely religious work. More recent studies take a secular view and treat it as a record of real political struggles. They point out that the poem seems to harbor doubts about its own hero, whose rules bring suffering as well as peace. Whatever the truth, its influence has been enormous. Its opening lines, which herald the coming of a just ruler, echo through the literature of the region, and its themes have passed from the realm of legend into everyday speech.","ja":"約900年前に書かれた「アルドレンの歌」として知られる叙事詩は、山岳の一族と、それを率いた預言者の物語である。第一部で預言者は厳しい教義を説く。戦いを捨てたときにのみ民は救いを得る、というものだ。この平和な一族を格好の獲物と見る近隣の人々は、何度も攻撃を仕掛ける。後世の詩人が書いた続編である第二部は、もっと暗い。預言者の邪悪なおいが権力を奪い、弱い指導者が相次ぐ。一族が暴力へと転落していき、そこに長い飢饉が重なって、一族は滅びかける。学者たちはかつて、この詩を純粋に宗教的な作品として読んでいた。より最近の研究は世俗的な見方をとり、現実の政治闘争の記録として扱っている。それらの研究は、この詩がその主人公自身に対して疑いを抱いているように見えると指摘する。彼の定めた規則は、平和だけでなく苦しみももたらすからだ。真実がどうであれ、その影響は非常に大きい。公正な支配者の到来を告げる冒頭の数行は、この地域の文学の至るところに響いており、その主題は伝説の領域から日常の言葉の中へと入り込んでいる。","words":[["clan","freq:clan"],["prophet","freq:prophet"],["doctrine","freq:doctrine"],["salvation","freq:salvation"],["prey","freq:prey"],["sequel","freq:sequel"],["wicked","freq:wicked"],["succession","freq:succession"],["descent","freq:descent"],["coupled","freq:coupled"],["secular","freq:secular"],["harbor","freq:harbor"],["herald","freq:herald"],["echo","freq:echo"],["realm","freq:realm"]],"qs":[{"q":"What happens in the second part of the poem?","o":["The clan defeats its neighbors.","The prophet gives up his beliefs.","The clan nearly collapses under bad leaders and famine.","A just ruler finally arrives."],"a":2,"why":"A wicked nephew ... seizes power, and a succession of weak leaders follows、さらに飢饉が重なり almost destroys it（滅びかける）とある。"},{"q":"How do recent studies differ from earlier readings of the poem?","o":["They treat it as a record of political conflict.","They believe it was written by a single poet.","They consider it a purely religious text.","They doubt that it influenced later literature."],"a":0,"why":"More recent studies take a secular view and treat it as a record of real political struggles（世俗的な見方をとり、現実の政治闘争の記録として扱う）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0007","page":"freq","group":"1〜100","kind":"研究施設の紹介","text":"Visitors to the research station on the northern plateau often arrive expecting something grand. What they find is a group of low huts spread over about one acre of frozen ground, where the chill is severe even in summer. The station is run by a provincial university and is an affiliate of a larger national institute. Its task is to measure small changes in the earth's magnetic field, and its instruments are so sensitive that staff must leave their phones in a locked box. Life there is not easy. The rough terrain makes deliveries difficult, and last winter a bout of flu left only two scientists well enough to work. The station also faced an unexpected threat when someone tried to hack into its computers, apparently hoping to steal data that might be sold. Since then, the team has agreed to swap its records with a partner station abroad every week, so that a copy always exists elsewhere. Next month, the scientists will give a public demo of their equipment in the nearest town. They hope it will persuade local people that the strange huts on the plateau are worth the money spent on them.","ja":"北の高原にある観測所を訪れる人は、しばしば何か壮大なものを期待してやって来る。実際に目にするのは、凍った地面の約1エーカーに散らばる低い小屋の集まりで、そこは夏でさえ冷え込みが厳しい。観測所は州立大学が運営しており、より大きな国立研究所の系列機関である。その任務は地球の磁場のわずかな変化を測ることで、機器が非常に敏感なため、職員は電話を鍵のかかった箱に入れておかなければならない。そこでの生活は楽ではない。険しい地形のせいで物資の配送は難しく、昨年の冬にはインフルエンザがひとしきり流行して、働ける科学者が2人しか残らなかった。さらに、売れるかもしれないデータを盗もうとしたらしく、何者かがコンピューターに不正侵入を試みるという予想外の脅威にも直面した。それ以来、チームは毎週、海外の提携観測所と記録を交換することにしており、写しが常に別の場所に存在するようにしている。来月、科学者たちは最寄りの町で機器の公開実演を行う。高原の奇妙な小屋には費やした金に見合う価値があると、地元の人々に納得してもらえることを彼らは期待している。","words":[["acre","freq:acre"],["chill","freq:chill"],["provincial","freq:provincial"],["affiliate","freq:affiliate"],["magnetic","freq:magnetic"],["terrain","freq:terrain"],["bout","freq:bout"],["hack","freq:hack"],["swap","freq:swap"],["demo","freq:demo"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why must staff keep their phones in a locked box?","o":["To stop data from being stolen","To protect the phones from the cold","Because the instruments are extremely sensitive","Because there is no signal on the plateau"],"a":2,"why":"its instruments are so sensitive that staff must leave their phones in a locked box（機器が非常に敏感なため）とある。"},{"q":"What did the team start doing after the computer attack?","o":["Exchanging records with a station overseas each week","Moving the huts to a nearby town","Hiring more scientists","Selling its data to a national institute"],"a":0,"why":"the team has agreed to swap its records with a partner station abroad every week（毎週、海外の提携観測所と記録を交換する）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0008","page":"freq","group":"101〜200","kind":"紛争と和平","text":"The siege of the river town of Vareth lasted almost a year. A local militia surrounded the town, cut off its food supply, and at one point held forty aid workers hostage. When the fighting finally came to a halt, the world learned how many civilians had died, and some governments began to describe the killings as genocide. In the aftermath, the United Nations approved the deployment of peacekeepers and sent a delegation to arrange talks between the two sides. The negotiations were difficult. The central government insisted that any agreement must respect its sovereignty, while leaders in the north demanded autonomy for their region. Neighboring countries, which had sent humanitarian aid during the siege, expressed solidarity with the survivors but disagreed about who was responsible for the aggression. Two years later, the peace remains fragile. An international tribunal has begun to hear evidence against several commanders, and many families are still waiting to learn what happened to missing relatives. Aid agencies warn that attention is already moving elsewhere. They argue that rebuilding schools and hospitals is as important as punishing the guilty, because young people with no prospects are easily drawn back into armed groups.","ja":"川沿いの町バレスの包囲は、ほぼ1年続いた。地元の民兵組織が町を取り囲んで食料の供給を断ち、一時は40人の援助関係者を人質に取った。戦闘がようやく止まったとき、世界はどれほど多くの民間人が死んだかを知り、いくつかの政府はこの殺害を集団虐殺と呼び始めた。その直後、国連は平和維持部隊の配備を承認し、双方の話し合いを取り持つために代表団を送った。交渉は難航した。中央政府は、どんな合意も自国の主権を尊重するものでなければならないと主張し、北部の指導者たちは自分たちの地域の自治を要求した。包囲の間に人道支援を送っていた近隣諸国は、生存者への連帯を表明したが、侵略の責任が誰にあるかについては意見が分かれた。2年たった今も、平和はもろいままだ。国際法廷が複数の司令官に対する証拠の審理を始めており、多くの家族は行方不明の親族に何が起きたのかを知る日をまだ待っている。援助団体は、関心がすでにほかへ移りつつあると警告する。将来の見込みのない若者は簡単に武装集団へ引き戻されるのだから、学校や病院の再建は罪を犯した者を罰するのと同じくらい重要だと、彼らは主張している。","words":[["siege","freq:siege"],["militia","freq:militia"],["hostage","freq:hostage"],["halt","freq:halt"],["genocide","freq:genocide"],["aftermath","freq:aftermath"],["deployment","freq:deployment"],["delegation","freq:delegation"],["sovereignty","freq:sovereignty"],["autonomy","freq:autonomy"],["humanitarian","freq:humanitarian"],["solidarity","freq:solidarity"],["aggression","freq:aggression"],["fragile","freq:fragile"],["tribunal","freq:tribunal"]],"qs":[{"q":"What made the negotiations difficult?","o":["The peacekeepers refused to enter the town.","The two sides wanted different things regarding control of the north.","Neighboring countries stopped sending aid.","The tribunal had not yet been created."],"a":1,"why":"中央政府は sovereignty（主権）の尊重を、北部の指導者は autonomy（自治）を求めたとある。"},{"q":"What do aid agencies argue?","o":["The commanders should be released.","The siege was not as serious as reported.","Rebuilding public services matters as much as punishment.","The missing relatives have all been found."],"a":2,"why":"rebuilding schools and hospitals is as important as punishing the guilty（学校や病院の再建は処罰と同じくらい重要）と主張している。"}]},
{"id":"f-0009","page":"freq","group":"101〜200","kind":"企業の不祥事","text":"When Hallam Trading admitted last year that a rogue employee at its overseas subsidiary had hidden huge losses, the firm's credibility collapsed almost overnight. The employee had bought wheat and other commodity contracts far beyond his limits, and concealed the results through the manipulation of internal records. Hallam's managers at first refused to disclose the size of the loss. When they finally did, shareholders began litigation, arguing that the directors were personally liable because they had ignored repeated warnings. The case has started a wider debate about accountability in large companies. Hallam held a near monopoly on grain exports from the region, and critics say this gave it enough leverage over regulators to escape serious oversight for years. A parliamentary committee has now proposed new rules that would require greater transparency, including monthly reports on trading positions. It also wants senior executives to be held accountable for failures in the departments they supervise. Business groups reply that the rules would be expensive and would punish honest firms. Even so, Hallam itself may survive. Thanks to a large cash surplus built up in better years, it has been able to pay its debts, though its reputation will take far longer to repair.","ja":"ハラム商事が昨年、海外子会社の一人の不正な社員が巨額の損失を隠していたと認めたとき、同社の信用はほとんど一夜にして崩れた。その社員は自分の限度をはるかに超えて小麦などの商品の契約を買い、社内記録を操作してその結果を隠していた。ハラムの経営陣は当初、損失の規模を公表することを拒んだ。ようやく公表すると、株主は訴訟を起こし、取締役たちは度重なる警告を無視したのだから個人として法的責任があると主張した。この事件は、大企業の説明責任をめぐるより広い議論を引き起こした。ハラムはその地域からの穀物輸出をほぼ独占しており、批判する人々は、そのおかげで規制当局に対して十分な影響力を持ち、何年も本格的な監督を逃れてきたと言う。議会の委員会は今回、取引状況の月次報告を含め、より高い透明性を求める新しい規則を提案した。委員会はまた、上級役員が自分の監督する部門の失敗について責任を負うことも求めている。経済団体は、その規則は費用がかかり、誠実な企業を罰することになると反論する。それでも、ハラム自体は生き残るかもしれない。業績の良かった年に積み上げた多額の現金の余剰のおかげで債務を返済できているからだ。ただし、評判の回復にははるかに長い時間がかかるだろう。","words":[["rogue","freq:rogue"],["subsidiary","freq:subsidiary"],["credibility","freq:credibility"],["commodity","freq:commodity"],["manipulation","freq:manipulation"],["disclose","freq:disclose"],["litigation","freq:litigation"],["liable","freq:liable"],["accountability","freq:accountability"],["monopoly","freq:monopoly"],["leverage","freq:leverage"],["oversight","freq:oversight"],["transparency","freq:transparency"],["accountable","freq:accountable"],["surplus","freq:surplus"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did shareholders take the directors to court?","o":["The directors had sold their own shares.","The directors had paid no attention to warnings.","The directors had bought wheat secretly.","The directors had refused to pay the firm's debts."],"a":1,"why":"they had ignored repeated warnings（度重なる警告を無視した）ので個人として責任があると主張した。"},{"q":"According to critics, how did Hallam avoid close supervision for years?","o":["By moving its headquarters abroad","By publishing monthly reports","By hiring former regulators","By using the influence that its market position gave it"],"a":3,"why":"a near monopoly ... gave it enough leverage over regulators to escape serious oversight（ほぼ独占していたことで規制当局への影響力を持ち、監督を逃れた）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0010","page":"freq","group":"101〜200","kind":"法律と政治","text":"Few laws in the country's history have caused as much fury as the Public Order Act. The statute was passed in a single night, three days after the assassination of the interior minister, and its supporters said there was every justification for firm action. It allowed the police to hold suspects for a month without charge, and it treated some forms of protest as treason. Opponents called these powers arbitrary. They pointed out that the first people arrested were not terrorists but juvenile offenders and members of a small fringe party. Reports of abusive treatment in police cells soon followed. The judiciary proved unwilling to interfere, and for two years the law remained in force. What changed the situation was money. To pay for the extra police, the government decided to levy a new tax on fuel, and this turned a legion of ordinary drivers against it. Opposition leaders, sensing an opportunity to woo these voters, promised to repeal the act if they won the election. They did win, though not outright, and the coalition they formed has so far managed only to soften the law, not to abolish it.","ja":"この国の歴史の中で、治安維持法ほど激しい怒りを招いた法律はほとんどない。この法令は内務大臣の暗殺から3日後、一晩で可決され、支持者たちは断固とした措置をとる正当な理由は十分にあると述べた。この法律は警察が容疑者を起訴なしで1か月拘束することを認め、ある種の抗議活動を反逆罪として扱った。反対派はこうした権限を恣意的だと呼んだ。最初に逮捕されたのはテロリストではなく、少年犯罪者や小さな非主流政党の党員だったと彼らは指摘した。まもなく、警察の留置場での虐待的な扱いが報告された。司法は介入しようとせず、法律は2年間効力を保った。状況を変えたのは金だった。増員した警察の費用をまかなうため、政府は燃料に新しい税を課すことを決め、これが大勢の一般の運転者を政府の敵に回した。野党の指導者たちは、こうした有権者の支持を得る好機と見て、選挙に勝てばこの法律を廃止すると約束した。野党は実際に勝ったが、完全な勝利ではなく、彼らが組んだ連立政権は今のところ法律を緩めただけで、廃止には至っていない。","words":[["fury","freq:fury"],["statute","freq:statute"],["assassination","freq:assassination"],["justification","freq:justification"],["treason","freq:treason"],["arbitrary","freq:arbitrary"],["juvenile","freq:juvenile"],["fringe","freq:fringe"],["abusive","freq:abusive"],["judiciary","freq:judiciary"],["levy","freq:levy"],["legion","freq:legion"],["woo","freq:woo"],["repeal","freq:repeal"],["outright","freq:outright"]],"qs":[{"q":"What finally turned many ordinary people against the government?","o":["A new tax on fuel","The arrest of a party leader","A report on police cells","A decision by the courts"],"a":0,"why":"levy a new tax on fuel（燃料への新税）が a legion of ordinary drivers（大勢の一般の運転者）を敵に回したとある。"},{"q":"What has happened to the act since the election?","o":["It has been abolished completely.","It has been made stricter.","It has been declared illegal by judges.","It has been made milder but still exists."],"a":3,"why":"managed only to soften the law, not to abolish it（緩めただけで廃止はしていない）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0011","page":"freq","group":"101〜200","kind":"医学研究の歩み","text":"Dr. Elena Marsh was a pioneer in the study of how daily habits affect health in old age. In the 1970s, the norm among doctors was to advise elderly patients to rest. Marsh proposed a different hypothesis: that gentle activity, such as walking and stretching, would lower mortality. To test it, she followed four thousand residents of a suburban district for twenty years. Her results showed a clear correlation between regular movement and longer life. Active people had a lower likelihood of heart disease, and they appeared to have stronger immunity against infections that are often lethal for the old. She also noticed a therapeutic effect on the mind: active participants were less likely to need psychiatric care. Critics pointed out that her sample was not typical. The district was wealthy, and its demographic profile differed from that of the country as a whole. Marsh accepted the criticism and spent her last years producing a synthesis of similar studies from eleven countries, which largely confirmed her findings. That final paper has become one of the most quoted in its field, and it received its ten-thousandth citation last year, three decades after her death.","ja":"エレナ・マーシュ博士は、日々の習慣が老年期の健康にどう影響するかという研究の先駆者だった。1970年代、医師の間では高齢の患者に安静を勧めるのが標準だった。マーシュは別の仮説を立てた。散歩やストレッチのような軽い活動が死亡率を下げる、というものだ。これを検証するため、彼女はある郊外地区の住民4,000人を20年間追跡した。結果は、定期的な運動と長寿の間に明確な相関関係があることを示した。活動的な人は心臓病になる可能性が低く、高齢者にとってしばしば致命的となる感染症に対して、より強い免疫を持っているようだった。彼女は心への治療的な効果にも気づいた。活動的な参加者は精神科の治療を必要とすることが少なかったのだ。批判する人々は、彼女の標本が典型的ではないと指摘した。その地区は裕福で、人口構成が国全体とは異なっていた。マーシュは批判を受け入れ、晩年を費やして11か国の同様の研究を統合した論文をまとめ、それは彼女の発見をおおむね裏づけた。この最後の論文はその分野で最もよく引用されるものの一つとなり、彼女の死から30年たった昨年、1万回目の引用を受けた。","words":[["pioneer","freq:pioneer"],["norm","freq:norm"],["hypothesis","freq:hypothesis"],["stretching","freq:stretching"],["mortality","freq:mortality"],["suburban","freq:suburban"],["correlation","freq:correlation"],["likelihood","freq:likelihood"],["immunity","freq:immunity"],["lethal","freq:lethal"],["therapeutic","freq:therapeutic"],["psychiatric","freq:psychiatric"],["demographic","freq:demographic"],["synthesis","freq:synthesis"],["citation","freq:citation"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was Marsh's hypothesis?","o":["Elderly patients should rest as much as possible.","Wealthy people live longer than poor people.","Light exercise would reduce deaths among the old.","Infections are the main cause of heart disease."],"a":2,"why":"gentle activity, such as walking and stretching, would lower mortality（軽い活動が死亡率を下げる）という仮説である。"},{"q":"How did Marsh respond to criticism of her sample?","o":["She combined results from studies in many countries.","She repeated the study in the same district.","She withdrew her original paper.","She argued that the district was typical."],"a":0,"why":"producing a synthesis of similar studies from eleven countries（11か国の同様の研究を統合した）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0012","page":"freq","group":"101〜200","kind":"自然保護","text":"High in the northern hills, at an elevation of 1,400 meters, lies a reservoir that few tourists ever see. Built in the 1930s to supply a distant city, it has since become a sanctuary for wildlife. The rare mountain goose nests on its banks each spring, and in early summer the slopes turn violet with wild flowers. Birdwatchers describe the place as a hidden gem. Getting there is not easy. Visitors must haul their equipment up a steep path, and in places they have to clutch a rope fixed to the rock. The area is also prone to sudden storms, when winds reach a dangerous velocity. A small research hut stands near the water. Its exterior is plain, but inside, scientists monitor atmospheric conditions and track the birds with an autonomous drone that flies a fixed route each morning. Their work has revealed a problem. Foxes, attracted by food that careless campers leave behind like bait, have begun to raid the nests, and last year only one nest in five survived. The scientists have asked the authorities to limit camping, but the reservoir belongs to an obscure water company that has shown little interest. For now, volunteers guard the nests at night.","ja":"北の丘陵の高所、標高1,400メートルに、観光客がほとんど目にすることのない貯水池がある。1930年代に遠くの都市へ水を供給するために造られ、それ以来、野生生物の保護区となってきた。毎年春には希少な山地のガンが岸辺に巣を作り、初夏には斜面が野の花ですみれ色に染まる。野鳥観察者たちはこの場所を隠れた名所と呼ぶ。そこへ行くのは簡単ではない。訪問者は険しい道を機材を引きずって登らなければならず、所々で岩に固定された綱をしっかり握る必要がある。この一帯は突然の嵐も起こりやすく、そのとき風は危険な速度に達する。水辺の近くに小さな研究小屋が建っている。外観は質素だが、中では科学者たちが大気の状態を観測し、毎朝決まった経路を飛ぶ自律型のドローンで鳥を追跡している。彼らの調査で、ある問題が明らかになった。不注意なキャンプ客が餌のように残していく食べ物に引き寄せられたキツネが巣を襲うようになり、昨年は5つに1つの巣しか残らなかったのだ。科学者たちは当局にキャンプの制限を求めたが、貯水池は無名の水道会社の所有で、会社はほとんど関心を示していない。今のところ、ボランティアが夜に巣を見張っている。","words":[["elevation","freq:elevation"],["reservoir","freq:reservoir"],["sanctuary","freq:sanctuary"],["goose","freq:goose"],["violet","freq:violet"],["gem","freq:gem"],["haul","freq:haul"],["clutch","freq:clutch"],["prone","freq:prone"],["velocity","freq:velocity"],["exterior","freq:exterior"],["atmospheric","freq:atmospheric"],["autonomous","freq:autonomous"],["bait","freq:bait"],["obscure","freq:obscure"]],"qs":[{"q":"What problem have the scientists discovered?","o":["The reservoir is losing water.","The drone frightens the birds.","Storms have destroyed the research hut.","Foxes drawn by campers' food are attacking nests."],"a":3,"why":"Foxes, attracted by food that careless campers leave behind ... have begun to raid the nests（キャンプ客の残した食べ物に引き寄せられたキツネが巣を襲う）とある。"},{"q":"Why has camping not been limited?","o":["The owner of the reservoir has shown little concern.","The volunteers opposed the idea.","Tourists rarely visit the area.","The authorities have banned the scientists."],"a":0,"why":"the reservoir belongs to an obscure water company that has shown little interest（所有する水道会社がほとんど関心を示していない）ためである。"}]},
{"id":"f-0013","page":"freq","group":"101〜200","kind":"人物の追悼記事","text":"Margaret Oduya, who died last week at ninety-one, was the most admired judge of her generation. During her long tenure on the Supreme Court, she was held in high esteem even by lawyers who lost their cases before her. Her written opinions were famous for their clarity, and her manner in court was blunt. She once told a government lawyer that his argument was \"a critique of common sense.\" Oduya was not an orthodox figure. The heir to a large farming estate, she gave most of her land to her workers and devoted herself to the advocacy of legal aid for the poor. She believed that judges should use their discretion to protect the weak, a view that made her the sworn enemy of several ministers. Colleagues remember her as shy and somewhat withdrawn in private, which they found a curious irony in someone so fierce in public. In an age when political discourse has grown louder and less honest, many found her plain speaking refreshing. Her last years were occupied by a long saga over the publication of her diaries, which the government tried to block. They will finally appear next spring.","ja":"先週91歳で亡くなったマーガレット・オドゥヤは、その世代で最も敬愛された裁判官だった。最高裁判所での長い在職期間中、彼女の前で敗訴した弁護士からさえ深く尊敬されていた。彼女の書く意見書は明快さで知られ、法廷での態度は率直だった。あるとき彼女は政府側の弁護士に、あなたの主張は「常識への批評だ」と言った。オドゥヤは型どおりの人物ではなかった。大農園の相続人でありながら、土地の大半を働き手に譲り、貧しい人々への法律扶助の擁護に身をささげた。彼女は、裁判官は弱者を守るために裁量を使うべきだと信じており、その考えのために複数の大臣の宿敵となった。同僚たちは、私生活での彼女は内気でやや引っ込み思案だったと振り返り、公の場であれほど激しい人にしては奇妙な皮肉だと感じていた。政治の議論がますます騒がしく、不誠実になった時代に、多くの人は彼女の飾らない物言いを新鮮に感じた。晩年は、日記の出版をめぐる長い騒動に費やされた。政府が出版を阻止しようとしたのだ。日記は来春ようやく刊行される。","words":[["tenure","freq:tenure"],["esteem","freq:esteem"],["clarity","freq:clarity"],["blunt","freq:blunt"],["critique","freq:critique"],["orthodox","freq:orthodox"],["heir","freq:heir"],["advocacy","freq:advocacy"],["discretion","freq:discretion"],["sworn","freq:sworn"],["withdrawn","freq:withdrawn"],["irony","freq:irony"],["discourse","freq:discourse"],["refreshing","freq:refreshing"],["saga","freq:saga"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did several ministers regard Oduya as an enemy?","o":["She refused to retire from the court.","She published her diaries without permission.","She thought judges should use their powers to protect weak people.","She gave her land to her workers."],"a":2,"why":"judges should use their discretion to protect the weak（裁判官は弱者を守るために裁量を使うべき）という考えが大臣たちの敵意を招いた。"},{"q":"What did colleagues find surprising about her?","o":["She was quiet in private but fierce in public.","She disliked writing opinions.","She came from a poor family.","She often changed her views."],"a":0,"why":"shy and somewhat withdrawn in private（私生活では内気）なのに so fierce in public（公の場では激しい）ことを a curious irony と感じていた。"}]},
{"id":"f-0014","page":"freq","group":"101〜200","kind":"伝統産業","text":"Not long ago, selling handmade paper seemed a hopeless business proposition. Cheap factory products had achieved almost total dominance, and the few remaining workshops survived only by serving a small niche: artists and restorers of old books. One such workshop, in a mountain village, has now found unexpected success, albeit in a way its founders never imagined. Three years ago, a museum asked the workshop to help repair a twelfth-century scroll. The work was filmed, and the video spread widely online. Orders arrived from around the world, and the village was suddenly thrust into the spotlight. The owners welcome the attention but are wary of it. They point to the risks inherent in rapid growth. Other craft villages, they say, have fallen into a vicious circle in which demand leads to the exploitation of young workers, quality falls, and customers disappear. To avoid this, the workshop has refused to expand. It still employs only nine people, trains each of them for five years, and limits the number of sheets it sells. The owners admit that this approach is not wholly rational in business terms. But they argue that some things are valuable precisely because they cannot be produced quickly.","ja":"少し前まで、手すきの紙を売ることは見込みのない事業に思われていた。安い工場製品がほぼ完全な優位を築き、わずかに残った工房は、芸術家や古書の修復家という小さなすき間市場を相手にすることでかろうじて生き延びていた。そうした工房の一つである山村の工房が今、予想外の成功を収めている。もっとも、創業者たちが想像もしなかった形ではあるが。3年前、ある博物館が12世紀の巻物の修復を手伝うようこの工房に依頼した。その作業が撮影され、動画がネットで広く拡散した。世界中から注文が届き、村は突然注目の的に押し出された。経営者たちは注目を歓迎しつつも警戒している。彼らは急成長に本来伴う危険を指摘する。ほかの工芸の村は、需要が若い働き手の搾取につながり、品質が落ち、客が離れるという悪循環に陥ったと言うのだ。これを避けるため、工房は拡大を拒んできた。今も従業員は9人だけで、一人ひとりを5年かけて育て、販売する紙の枚数を制限している。経営者たちは、このやり方が事業としては完全に合理的とは言えないと認める。しかし、すぐには作れないからこそ価値があるものもあると、彼らは主張している。","words":[["proposition","freq:proposition"],["dominance","freq:dominance"],["niche","freq:niche"],["albeit","freq:albeit"],["scroll","freq:scroll"],["thrust","freq:thrust"],["inherent","freq:inherent"],["vicious","freq:vicious"],["exploitation","freq:exploitation"],["wholly","freq:wholly"]],"qs":[{"q":"What brought the workshop sudden attention?","o":["A film of its repair work spread on the internet.","A factory began copying its paper.","A museum bought the workshop.","Its founders wrote a popular book."],"a":0,"why":"The work was filmed, and the video spread widely online（作業が撮影され、動画がネットで広まった）とある。"},{"q":"Why has the workshop chosen not to grow?","o":["It cannot find enough artists.","The village has banned new buildings.","Its owners fear the harm that fast growth has done elsewhere.","Orders from abroad have stopped."],"a":2,"why":"ほかの村が a vicious circle（悪循環）に陥ったことを挙げ、To avoid this, the workshop has refused to expand とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0015","page":"freq","group":"201〜300","kind":"選挙の情勢","text":"The election in Sarmenia next month will be the most closely contested in the country's history. The incumbent president, Daro Velin, came to power six years ago after a popular uprising against his predecessor, who had ruled largely by decree. Velin promised to end the injustice of a system in which a small privileged group controlled both business and politics. His critics say he has simply replaced one hierarchy with another. His main rival, a former teacher named Ila Moren, was unknown a year ago. Her campaign began to gain traction when she won the endorsement of the country's largest trade union, and she has since signed a pact with two smaller parties. The alliance is an uneasy one. Its members have deep ideological differences, and one faction has threatened to leave unless Moren promises to cancel a trade accord with a neighboring state. Velin's supporters, meanwhile, have hinted that they may boycott the vote if international observers are allowed to monitor it. Opinion polls put the two candidates almost level. Whoever wins will inherit a divided country and an economy that has barely grown in three years.","ja":"来月のサルメニアの選挙は、同国史上最も激しく争われるものになるだろう。現職の大統領ダロ・ベリンは6年前、主に布告によって統治していた前任者に対する民衆の蜂起の後に権力の座に就いた。ベリンは、少数の特権層が経済と政治の両方を支配する体制の不公正を終わらせると約束した。彼を批判する人々は、彼はある序列を別の序列に置き換えただけだと言う。主な対立候補である元教師のイラ・モレンは、1年前には無名だった。彼女の選挙運動は、国内最大の労働組合の支持を得たときに勢いがつき始め、その後、2つの小政党と協定を結んだ。この連合は不安定だ。構成する党の間には深い思想上の違いがあり、ある派閥は、モレンが隣国との貿易協定の破棄を約束しなければ離脱すると脅している。一方ベリンの支持者たちは、国際監視団による監視が認められるなら投票をボイコットするかもしれないとほのめかしている。世論調査では2人の候補はほぼ並んでいる。誰が勝っても、分断された国と、3年間ほとんど成長していない経済を引き継ぐことになる。","words":[["contested","freq:contested"],["incumbent","freq:incumbent"],["uprising","freq:uprising"],["predecessor","freq:predecessor"],["decree","freq:decree"],["injustice","freq:injustice"],["privileged","freq:privileged"],["hierarchy","freq:hierarchy"],["traction","freq:traction"],["endorsement","freq:endorsement"],["pact","freq:pact"],["ideological","freq:ideological"],["faction","freq:faction"],["accord","freq:accord"],["boycott","freq:boycott"]],"qs":[{"q":"What helped Moren's campaign begin to succeed?","o":["A trade agreement with a neighboring state","The support of a major trade union","A popular uprising","The president's decision to step down"],"a":1,"why":"she won the endorsement of the country's largest trade union（国内最大の労働組合の支持を得た）ときに勢いがつき始めた。"},{"q":"Why is Moren's alliance described as uneasy?","o":["Its members disagree deeply, and one group may leave.","The trade union has withdrawn its support.","International observers have criticized it.","Opinion polls show it far behind."],"a":0,"why":"deep ideological differences があり、one faction has threatened to leave（ある派閥が離脱すると脅している）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0016","page":"freq","group":"201〜300","kind":"冤罪事件","text":"The Dunmore case is one of the most infamous in the history of the state's courts. In 1994 a corpse was found in a field in the vicinity of a small farming town, and police opened a homicide investigation. Within a week they arrested a nineteen-year-old who was on probation for reckless driving. He had no history of violence, but a neighbor claimed to have seen him near the field. He was convicted of a felony and sentenced to life in prison without parole. For years, lawyers argued that the verdict was unsafe. The neighbor's statements were inconsistent, and the seizure of the young man's clothes had been carried out without a warrant, which should have made that evidence invalid. The courts refused to reopen the case until new forensic tests, unavailable in the 1990s, showed that blood on the clothes belonged to someone else. Under closer scrutiny, the original investigation fell apart, and the only credible witness turned out to be one the police had ignored. The man was freed after twenty-six years. The state has since introduced a statutory right to new scientific testing for every prisoner serving a life sentence.","ja":"ダンモア事件は、その州の裁判の歴史の中で最も悪名高いものの一つだ。1994年、小さな農業の町の近辺の畑で死体が見つかり、警察は殺人事件として捜査を始めた。1週間のうちに、警察は無謀運転で保護観察中だった19歳の若者を逮捕した。彼に暴力の前歴はなかったが、隣人が畑の近くで彼を見たと主張した。彼は重罪で有罪となり、仮釈放なしの終身刑を言い渡された。何年もの間、弁護士たちはこの評決は信頼できないと主張した。隣人の供述は矛盾しており、若者の衣服の押収は令状なしに行われていたため、本来その証拠は無効とされるべきだった。裁判所は再審を拒み続けたが、1990年代には使えなかった新しい科学捜査の検査によって、衣服の血が別人のものだとわかった。より厳しく精査されると当初の捜査は崩れ去り、唯一の信用できる証人は警察が無視していた人物だったことが判明した。男性は26年後に釈放された。州はその後、終身刑で服役中のすべての受刑者に、新しい科学的検査を受ける法定の権利を導入した。","words":[["infamous","freq:infamous"],["corpse","freq:corpse"],["vicinity","freq:vicinity"],["homicide","freq:homicide"],["probation","freq:probation"],["reckless","freq:reckless"],["felony","freq:felony"],["parole","freq:parole"],["inconsistent","freq:inconsistent"],["seizure","freq:seizure"],["invalid","freq:invalid"],["forensic","freq:forensic"],["scrutiny","freq:scrutiny"],["credible","freq:credible"],["statutory","freq:statutory"]],"qs":[{"q":"What led the courts to reopen the case?","o":["The neighbor changed his statement.","The real killer confessed.","The police admitted they had no warrant.","New scientific tests showed the blood was another person's."],"a":3,"why":"new forensic tests ... showed that blood on the clothes belonged to someone else（新しい科学捜査の検査で血が別人のものとわかった）とある。"},{"q":"What change did the state make afterward?","o":["It ended life sentences for young offenders.","It gave prisoners serving life a legal right to new testing.","It banned evidence from neighbors.","It closed the town's police station."],"a":1,"why":"a statutory right to new scientific testing for every prisoner serving a life sentence（終身刑の受刑者に新しい科学的検査を受ける法定の権利）を導入した。"}]},
{"id":"f-0017","page":"freq","group":"201〜300","kind":"医療研究","text":"Some doctors now speak of an epidemic of dementia, and the search for its causes has become one of the largest research efforts in medicine. A collaborative project involving nine universities is taking an unusual approach. Instead of studying the elderly, it follows people from birth. Researchers record maternal health during pregnancy, traumatic events in childhood, and changes in metabolism in middle age, hoping to find early signs of trouble. The project's methodology depends on new technology. Participants wear a small apparatus on the wrist that measures sleep and movement and can detect even the slight twitch of a muscle. Because the data are sent automatically, people can take part remotely, without visiting a clinic. Once a year they also complete tests of memory retention and spatial skills on a home computer. The amount of information being gathered is unprecedented. The first results suggest that people whose sleep is compromised in their forties show faster mental decline later. The researchers stress, however, that nothing is yet definitive. It will take another twenty years, they say, before the youngest participants are old enough to show whether the early patterns really predict the disease.","ja":"今では認知症の流行について語る医師もおり、その原因の探究は医学で最大級の研究活動の一つになっている。9つの大学が参加するある共同プロジェクトは、珍しい方法をとっている。高齢者を調べるのではなく、人を出生時から追跡するのだ。研究者たちは、妊娠中の母親の健康、子ども時代の心に傷を残す出来事、中年期の代謝の変化を記録し、問題の早期の兆候を見つけようとしている。このプロジェクトの手法は新しい技術に支えられている。参加者は手首に小さな装置を着け、それが睡眠と動きを測り、筋肉のわずかなけいれんさえ検出できる。データは自動的に送られるので、参加者は診療所へ行かずに遠隔で参加できる。年に一度、自宅のコンピューターで記憶の保持力と空間能力のテストも受ける。集められている情報の量は前例がない。最初の結果は、40代で睡眠が損なわれている人は、その後の知的機能の低下が速いことを示唆している。しかし研究者たちは、まだ何も決定的ではないと強調する。初期の傾向が本当に病気を予測するのかがわかるほど最年少の参加者が年を取るまで、さらに20年かかるという。","words":[["epidemic","freq:epidemic"],["dementia","freq:dementia"],["collaborative","freq:collaborative"],["maternal","freq:maternal"],["traumatic","freq:traumatic"],["metabolism","freq:metabolism"],["methodology","freq:methodology"],["apparatus","freq:apparatus"],["twitch","freq:twitch"],["remotely","freq:remotely"],["retention","freq:retention"],["spatial","freq:spatial"],["unprecedented","freq:unprecedented"],["compromised","freq:compromised"],["definitive","freq:definitive"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is unusual about the project's approach?","o":["It studies people from the beginning of their lives.","It uses only patients in hospitals.","It ignores sleep and movement.","It is run by a single university."],"a":0,"why":"Instead of studying the elderly, it follows people from birth（高齢者ではなく、出生時から追跡する）とある。"},{"q":"What do the first results suggest?","o":["Childhood events have no effect on later health.","Memory tests at home are unreliable.","Poor sleep in one's forties is linked to quicker decline later.","The disease can already be predicted with certainty."],"a":2,"why":"people whose sleep is compromised in their forties show faster mental decline later（40代で睡眠が損なわれた人はその後の低下が速い）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0018","page":"freq","group":"201〜300","kind":"水資源","text":"The desert town of Kessim sits above an ancient lake of underground water. A geological survey in the 1960s showed that the supply was large but finite: rain refills it far more slowly than people remove it. For decades nobody worried. The extraction of water allowed farms to spread, and thick vegetation appeared where there had been only sand. Today the water level is falling by a meter a year, and the town's reliance on a single source looks dangerous. The council has introduced strict measures to curb waste. Each dwelling now receives a fixed allocation, and a large share of public expenditure goes toward repairing old pipes. A belt of trees has been planted as a buffer against the advancing sand, protected by wire mesh from stray goats that would eat the young plants. Some residents complain that the rules are too harsh. Older people, however, remember the drought of 1979, when the sky stayed dark with dust for days, as if during an eclipse. They say that the question is not one of comfort. It is whether their offspring will be able to live here at all. On a cosmic scale, they point out, sixty years of plenty is nothing.","ja":"砂漠の町ケシムは、太古の地下水の湖の上にある。1960年代の地質調査は、水の量は多いが限りがあることを示した。雨が水を補う速さは、人が汲み出す速さよりはるかに遅いのだ。何十年もの間、誰も心配しなかった。水の汲み上げによって農場が広がり、砂しかなかった場所に茂った草木が現れた。今日、水位は年に1メートルずつ下がっており、町がただ一つの水源に依存していることは危険に見える。町議会は無駄を抑えるための厳しい措置を導入した。各住居には今、決まった量が割り当てられ、公共支出の大きな割合が古い水道管の修理に充てられている。迫ってくる砂に対する緩衝帯として木が帯状に植えられ、若木を食べてしまう野良ヤギから金網で守られている。規則が厳しすぎると不満を言う住民もいる。しかし年配の人々は1979年の干ばつを覚えている。そのとき空は、まるで日食の間のように、何日も砂ぼこりで暗いままだった。彼らは、これは快適さの問題ではないと言う。自分たちの子孫がそもそもここで暮らせるかどうかの問題なのだ。宇宙的な尺度で見れば、60年の豊かさなど無に等しいと、彼らは指摘する。","words":[["geological","freq:geological"],["finite","freq:finite"],["extraction","freq:extraction"],["vegetation","freq:vegetation"],["reliance","freq:reliance"],["curb","freq:curb"],["dwelling","freq:dwelling"],["allocation","freq:allocation"],["expenditure","freq:expenditure"],["buffer","freq:buffer"],["mesh","freq:mesh"],["stray","freq:stray"],["eclipse","freq:eclipse"],["offspring","freq:offspring"],["cosmic","freq:cosmic"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the survey in the 1960s reveal?","o":["The water was unsafe to drink.","The lake was refilled quickly by rain.","The town was built on unstable ground.","The water supply was large but would not last forever."],"a":3,"why":"the supply was large but finite（量は多いが限りがある）とある。"},{"q":"Why do older residents support the strict rules?","o":["They want farms to keep spreading.","They remember a severe drought and fear for future generations.","They own most of the old pipes.","They dislike the newly planted trees."],"a":1,"why":"1979年の干ばつを覚えており、whether their offspring will be able to live here at all（子孫がここで暮らせるかどうか）の問題だと言っている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0019","page":"freq","group":"201〜300","kind":"書評","text":"In his memoir, published last month, the novelist Paul Kerrin describes a childhood spent in a strict religious community in the far north. The members believed they had made a covenant with God to live apart from the modern world. There were no clergy; each Sunday, an ordinary member of the congregation led the prayers. Rules were rigid. Music, novels, and bright clothing were forbidden, since the elders saw them as signs of greed and pride. Kerrin left at eighteen, and his early novels were a bitter parody of the life he had escaped. The memoir is gentler. He admits that he was once too ready to mock people he now sees as righteous, if sometimes naive. He writes with affection about the simplicity of their days, and about summer evenings when the whole village gathered to behold the sun setting over the sea, a moment he calls pure bliss. He also recognizes how deeply the community helped to mold his character. The book is not a story of redemption, and Kerrin has not returned to the faith. But he suggests that in a noisy and selfish age, such places may serve as a beacon for people who are searching for another way to live.","ja":"先月出版された回顧録の中で、小説家ポール・ケリンは、極北の厳格な宗教共同体で過ごした子ども時代を描いている。そこの人々は、現代世界から離れて暮らすという誓約を神と結んだと信じていた。聖職者はおらず、毎週日曜には会衆の中の普通の一員が祈りを導いた。規則は厳格だった。音楽、小説、派手な服は禁じられていた。長老たちがそれらを強欲と高慢のしるしと見なしたからだ。ケリンは18歳でそこを去り、初期の小説は、逃れてきた生活を辛辣にもじったものだった。回顧録はもっと穏やかだ。今では、時に世間知らずではあっても正しい人々だったと思える相手を、かつての自分はあまりに安易にあざけっていたと彼は認める。彼は、人々の日々の簡素さや、村中が集まって海に沈む夕日を眺めた夏の夕べを、愛情を込めて書いており、その時間をまさに至福だったと呼ぶ。また、共同体が自分の人格を形作るうえでどれほど深く関わったかも認めている。この本は救済の物語ではなく、ケリンは信仰に戻ってはいない。しかし彼は、騒がしく利己的な時代にあって、そうした場所は別の生き方を探す人々にとっての指針になりうると示唆している。","words":[["memoir","freq:memoir"],["covenant","freq:covenant"],["clergy","freq:clergy"],["congregation","freq:congregation"],["rigid","freq:rigid"],["greed","freq:greed"],["parody","freq:parody"],["righteous","freq:righteous"],["naive","freq:naive"],["simplicity","freq:simplicity"],["behold","freq:behold"],["bliss","freq:bliss"],["mold","freq:mold"],["redemption","freq:redemption"],["beacon","freq:beacon"]],"qs":[{"q":"How does the memoir differ from Kerrin's early novels?","o":["It is more critical of the elders.","It treats the community more kindly.","It describes his return to the faith.","It was written before he left the village."],"a":1,"why":"初期の小説は a bitter parody（辛辣なもじり）だったが、The memoir is gentler（回顧録はもっと穏やか）とある。"},{"q":"What does Kerrin suggest about such communities today?","o":["They should be opened to tourists.","They are certain to disappear soon.","They are dangerous for children.","They may guide people looking for a different way of life."],"a":3,"why":"such places may serve as a beacon for people who are searching for another way to live（別の生き方を探す人々の指針になりうる）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0020","page":"freq","group":"201〜300","kind":"都市の経済","text":"The port city of Alverra has enjoyed phenomenal growth over the past decade, and its leaders want the world to know why. The city's flagship policy is a scheme whereby new businesses are exempt from local taxes for five years. It rests on a simple premise: that people who start companies create more wealth than the taxes they would have paid. Many of the new firms were founded by migrant workers, who were able to utilize skills that had been ignored in their home countries. One of them, a software company, is now among the foremost in its field, and its stellar results have attracted investors from abroad. Not everyone is pleased. Older businesses complain that newcomers are invading their turf while paying nothing. A more serious point of contention is inequality. Critics argue that the city's problems are systemic. Property prices have tripled, so that young people without an inheritance cannot afford a home, and the bounty of the boom has gone mainly to those who were already rich. The mayor accepts some of this criticism. She says the city must now forge a new agreement with its citizens, one that shares the gains more widely.","ja":"港湾都市アルベラはこの10年、驚異的な成長を遂げ、市の指導者たちはその理由を世界に知らせたがっている。市の看板政策は、新しい企業が5年間、地方税を免除されるという制度だ。それは単純な前提に基づいている。会社を起こす人々は、払うはずだった税金よりも多くの富を生み出す、というものだ。新しい企業の多くは移民の働き手によって設立された。彼らは母国では顧みられなかった技能を生かすことができたのである。その一つであるソフトウェア会社は今やその分野で一流の企業に数えられ、その見事な業績は海外の投資家を引き寄せている。誰もが喜んでいるわけではない。古くからの企業は、新参者が何も払わずに自分たちの縄張りを荒らしていると不満を言う。より深刻な争点は格差だ。批判する人々は、市の問題は構造全体に及ぶものだと主張する。不動産価格は3倍になり、相続財産のない若者は家を買えず、好況の恵みは主にもともと裕福だった人々に渡った。市長はこの批判の一部を受け入れている。市は今、利益をもっと広く分かち合う新しい合意を市民との間に築かなければならないと、彼女は言う。","words":[["phenomenal","freq:phenomenal"],["flagship","freq:flagship"],["whereby","freq:whereby"],["exempt","freq:exempt"],["premise","freq:premise"],["migrant","freq:migrant"],["utilize","freq:utilize"],["foremost","freq:foremost"],["stellar","freq:stellar"],["turf","freq:turf"],["contention","freq:contention"],["systemic","freq:systemic"],["inheritance","freq:inheritance"],["bounty","freq:bounty"],["forge","freq:forge"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is the idea behind the city's main policy?","o":["New companies produce more wealth than the taxes they are excused from paying.","Older businesses should pay higher taxes.","Foreign investors should run the port.","Migrant workers should return home after five years."],"a":0,"why":"people who start companies create more wealth than the taxes they would have paid（起業する人々は、払うはずだった税金より多くの富を生む）という前提である。"},{"q":"What do critics say about the boom?","o":["It has lowered property prices.","It was caused by a single software company.","Its benefits have gone mostly to people who were already wealthy.","It has ended because of the mayor's new agreement."],"a":2,"why":"the bounty of the boom has gone mainly to those who were already rich（好況の恵みは主にもともと裕福だった人々に渡った）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0021","page":"freq","group":"201〜300","kind":"翻訳をめぐる評論","text":"Judging the quality of a translation is a subjective matter, but some cases are clear. When the first English version of the novel The Salt Road appeared in 1952, reviewers praised it. Only later did readers notice how far it had been allowed to drift from the original. The translator had chosen to bypass difficult passages altogether, and had cut a whole chapter in which a soldier fastens the strap of his dead brother's watch around his own wrist, a scene that many consider the heart of the book. A new translation, published this year, tries to render every sentence faithfully. It is ironic that some critics find it inferior to the old one. They say that its long sentences are tiring and that it lacks the energy of the earlier version. Its defenders reply that the first translator used invented scenes to prop up a story he did not trust. They also praise the care taken over continuity: names and places, which changed from page to page in the 1952 edition, are now consistent. Perhaps the fairest judgment is that the two versions serve different readers. The old one is a lively adventure; the new one is in closer alignment with what the author actually wrote.","ja":"翻訳の質を判断するのは主観的な事柄だが、明白な場合もある。小説「塩の道」の最初の英語版が1952年に出たとき、書評家たちはそれを称賛した。それが原作からどれほどそれていたかに読者が気づいたのは、後になってからだった。翻訳者は難しい箇所をすっかり飛ばすことを選び、兵士が死んだ兄の腕時計のひもを自分の手首に留める章を丸ごと削っていた。多くの人がこの本の核心と考える場面である。今年出版された新訳は、すべての文を忠実に表現しようとしている。一部の批評家がそれを旧訳より劣ると見ているのは皮肉なことだ。彼らは、新訳の長い文は読んでいて疲れ、旧訳の勢いがないと言う。新訳を擁護する人々は、最初の翻訳者は自分が信頼していない物語を支えるために創作した場面を使ったのだと反論する。彼らはまた、一貫性への配慮も称賛する。1952年版ではページごとに変わっていた人名や地名が、今は統一されているのだ。おそらく最も公平な判断は、2つの版は異なる読者の役に立つ、というものだろう。旧訳は生き生きとした冒険物語であり、新訳は作者が実際に書いたものにより近く沿っている。","words":[["subjective","freq:subjective"],["drift","freq:drift"],["bypass","freq:bypass"],["strap","freq:strap"],["render","freq:render"],["ironic","freq:ironic"],["inferior","freq:inferior"],["prop","freq:prop"],["continuity","freq:continuity"],["alignment","freq:alignment"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was wrong with the 1952 translation?","o":["It was never reviewed.","It was written in sentences that were too long.","It left out difficult parts and an important chapter.","It kept the names of people and places consistent."],"a":2,"why":"bypass difficult passages altogether（難しい箇所を飛ばし）、cut a whole chapter（1章を丸ごと削った）とある。"},{"q":"Why do some critics prefer the old translation?","o":["It is closer to what the author wrote.","It was translated by the author's brother.","It contains no invented scenes.","It is livelier and less tiring to read."],"a":3,"why":"新訳は long sentences are tiring、lacks the energy of the earlier version（長い文は疲れ、旧訳の勢いがない）と言っている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0022","page":"freq","group":"301〜400","kind":"二国間の和解","text":"For forty years after the partition of the old kingdom, the two states of Norvia and Sarel had no official contact. The division had been violent. It caused an exodus of two million people, and each side accused the other of the persecution of minorities. Trade stopped, and a convoy of trucks could wait for days at the single crossing point. The first sign of change came when a new monarch took the throne in Norvia. Unlike his father, he had no interest in the suppression of opposing views, and he quietly sent a trusted aide to meet his counterpart in Sarel. Months of secret diplomacy followed, with a third country, known for its neutrality, acting as host. The bilateral agreement signed last week is modest. It reopens three roads and allows divided families to visit one another. Yet many observers call it a milestone. Hard-line groups on both sides are angry. In Sarel, some army officers have questioned whether their allegiance lies with a government that deals with the enemy, and there are fears of a revolt. Supporters reply that reconciliation was never going to be painless, and that the alternative is another forty years of silence.","ja":"旧王国の分割から40年間、ノルビアとサレルの2国には公式の接触がなかった。分割は暴力を伴った。200万人の大量脱出を引き起こし、双方が相手を少数派の迫害で非難した。貿易は止まり、トラックの車列がただ一つの検問所で何日も待つこともあった。変化の最初の兆しは、ノルビアで新しい君主が即位したときに現れた。父親と違って、彼は反対意見の抑圧に関心がなく、信頼する側近をひそかに送ってサレルの担当者と会わせた。中立で知られる第三国が仲介役となり、数か月の秘密外交が続いた。先週署名された二国間協定は控えめなものだ。3本の道路を再開し、離れ離れになった家族が互いを訪ねることを認める。それでも多くの観察者はこれを画期的な出来事と呼ぶ。双方の強硬派は怒っている。サレルでは一部の軍将校が、敵と取引する政府に自分たちの忠誠があるのかと疑問を呈しており、反乱の懸念もある。支持者たちは、和解が痛みを伴わないはずはなく、それに代わる道はさらに40年の沈黙だと反論している。","words":[["partition","freq:partition"],["exodus","freq:exodus"],["persecution","freq:persecution"],["convoy","freq:convoy"],["monarch","freq:monarch"],["suppression","freq:suppression"],["aide","freq:aide"],["counterpart","freq:counterpart"],["diplomacy","freq:diplomacy"],["neutrality","freq:neutrality"],["bilateral","freq:bilateral"],["milestone","freq:milestone"],["allegiance","freq:allegiance"],["revolt","freq:revolt"],["reconciliation","freq:reconciliation"]],"qs":[{"q":"What led to the first contact between the two states?","o":["A new ruler in Norvia sent a representative in secret.","Traders demanded that the roads be reopened.","Army officers in Sarel proposed talks.","A third country threatened to stop its aid."],"a":0,"why":"a new monarch ... quietly sent a trusted aide to meet his counterpart in Sarel（新しい君主が側近をひそかに送った）とある。"},{"q":"Why do some people fear a revolt in Sarel?","o":["Families have been refused permission to travel.","Some officers doubt whether they should stay loyal to the government.","The agreement closes the only crossing point.","The monarch has broken the agreement."],"a":1,"why":"some army officers have questioned whether their allegiance lies with a government that deals with the enemy（敵と取引する政府への忠誠を疑問視している）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0023","page":"freq","group":"301〜400","kind":"労働争議","text":"The case of Lena Voss has caused unusual friction between a large hospital and its staff. Voss, a nurse with twenty years of experience, was dismissed after she refused to follow a directive that required nurses to work alone on night wards. She argued that the rule created an occupational risk, and she would cite three recent accidents as proof. The hospital described her refusal as a confrontation with management. Her union called the dismissal unlawful and took the matter to arbitration. When that failed, the case went before a magistrate. The hearing revealed more than anyone expected. Documents showed that a trustee of the hospital had voted on the night-shift policy by proxy, without attending a single meeting on the subject. The magistrate's ruling was a sharp indictment of the hospital's leadership. Given the severity of the risks, she wrote, Voss had been right to object. The hospital's board met the next day and, in a unanimous decision, withdrew the directive and offered Voss her job back. She has accepted, though she admits that she still feels the wrath of some senior managers whenever she walks through the door.","ja":"レナ・フォスの事件は、ある大病院と職員の間に異例の摩擦を引き起こした。20年の経験を持つ看護師のフォスは、看護師が夜間病棟で一人で勤務することを求める指令に従うのを拒み、解雇された。彼女はその規則が職業上の危険を生むと主張し、証拠として最近の3件の事故を挙げた。病院は彼女の拒否を経営陣との対決と表現した。彼女の労働組合は解雇を違法だとして、問題を仲裁に持ち込んだ。それが不調に終わると、事件は治安判事の前に持ち出された。審理は誰もが予想した以上のことを明らかにした。病院の理事の一人が、この件の会議に一度も出席せずに、夜勤の方針について代理で投票していたことが書類からわかったのだ。判事の判断は、病院の指導部に対する厳しい非難だった。危険の深刻さを考えれば、フォスが異議を唱えたのは正しかったと判事は書いた。病院の理事会は翌日に集まり、全会一致の決定で指令を撤回し、フォスに復職を申し出た。彼女は受け入れたが、今でも玄関を入るたびに一部の上級管理職の激しい怒りを感じると認めている。","words":[["friction","freq:friction"],["directive","freq:directive"],["occupational","freq:occupational"],["cite","freq:cite"],["confrontation","freq:confrontation"],["dismissal","freq:dismissal"],["unlawful","freq:unlawful"],["arbitration","freq:arbitration"],["magistrate","freq:magistrate"],["trustee","freq:trustee"],["proxy","freq:proxy"],["indictment","freq:indictment"],["severity","freq:severity"],["unanimous","freq:unanimous"],["wrath","freq:wrath"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did Voss refuse to follow the rule?","o":["She wanted higher pay for night work.","She thought working alone at night was dangerous.","She disliked the hospital's managers.","She had been asked to work in another ward."],"a":1,"why":"the rule created an occupational risk（職業上の危険を生む）と主張し、3件の事故を挙げた。"},{"q":"What did the hospital's board do after the ruling?","o":["It appealed to a higher court.","It replaced the magistrate.","It cancelled the rule and invited Voss to return.","It closed the night wards."],"a":2,"why":"withdrew the directive and offered Voss her job back（指令を撤回し、復職を申し出た）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0024","page":"freq","group":"301〜400","kind":"外来種への対策","text":"Twenty years ago, a single specimen of the silver carp was found in the Darrow River. Today the fish is everywhere. An invasive species with no natural predator in the region, it has been able to thrive at the expense of native fish, and it now threatens the irrigation channels on which local farms depend. Scientists at the state university believe they may have a solution. After mapping the genome of the carp, they identified a gene that controls reproduction, and they have developed a substance that switches it off. Early trials on a small tract of river were encouraging. The efficacy of the treatment was high, and there was no sign of contamination further downstream. Not everyone is convinced. Some biologists are skeptical about the validity of trials carried out over so short a period, and they argue that it would be premature to release the substance widely. The researchers accept that more rigorous testing is needed. They also admit that the method may not be feasible on a large scale unless a company agrees to produce the substance commercially, which none has yet offered to do.","ja":"20年前、ダロー川で銀色のコイの標本がただ1匹見つかった。今日、その魚は至るところにいる。この地域に天敵のいない外来種であるため、在来の魚を犠牲にして繁栄し、今では地元の農場が頼るかんがい水路を脅かしている。州立大学の科学者たちは、解決策があるかもしれないと考えている。コイのゲノムを解読した後、彼らは繁殖を制御する遺伝子を特定し、その働きを止める物質を開発した。川の小さな区域での初期の試験は有望だった。処置の有効性は高く、下流に汚染の兆候はなかった。誰もが納得しているわけではない。一部の生物学者は、これほど短い期間で行われた試験の妥当性に懐疑的で、その物質を広く放出するのは時期尚早だと主張する。研究者たちは、より厳密な試験が必要だと認めている。また、企業がその物質を商業的に生産することに同意しない限り、この方法は大規模には実行できないかもしれないとも認めている。今のところ、それを申し出た企業はない。","words":[["specimen","freq:specimen"],["invasive","freq:invasive"],["predator","freq:predator"],["thrive","freq:thrive"],["irrigation","freq:irrigation"],["genome","freq:genome"],["tract","freq:tract"],["efficacy","freq:efficacy"],["contamination","freq:contamination"],["skeptical","freq:skeptical"],["validity","freq:validity"],["premature","freq:premature"],["rigorous","freq:rigorous"],["feasible","freq:feasible"],["commercially","freq:commercially"]],"qs":[{"q":"How does the scientists' substance work?","o":["It poisons the carp's food.","It attracts fish that eat the carp.","It turns off a gene needed for the carp to reproduce.","It cleans polluted water downstream."],"a":2,"why":"a gene that controls reproduction（繁殖を制御する遺伝子）を特定し、それを switches it off（止める）物質を開発したとある。"},{"q":"What practical obstacle do the researchers mention?","o":["No company has offered to manufacture the substance.","Farmers refuse to allow testing.","The carp have already disappeared.","The state university has stopped its funding."],"a":0,"why":"unless a company agrees to produce the substance commercially, which none has yet offered to do（商業生産を申し出た企業がまだない）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0025","page":"freq","group":"301〜400","kind":"精神医療の書評","text":"In her new book, the psychiatrist Dr. Hana Reyes describes patients who appear confident but are deeply insecure. One of them, a successful lawyer, came to her after the onset of severe anxiety. In court he was calm and witty. That public persona, Reyes writes, was a kind of armor. In private he led a solitary life and had grown almost paranoid, convinced that his colleagues were planning a betrayal. His fears would manifest themselves at night, when he checked the locks again and again. Reyes argues that such cases are more common than we think, and that the stigma attached to mental illness stops many people from seeking help. Her approach is simple. She believes that advice is meaningless unless the doctor first tries to comprehend how the patient sees the world. Her own word for this is apt: she calls it \"borrowed eyes.\" It is something akin to empathy, she explains, but more active. The most moving pages describe the moment when the lawyer, after months of silence, gave a spontaneous laugh at one of his own fears. Recovery, Reyes suggests, often begins with moments like that.","ja":"精神科医のハナ・レイエス博士は新著の中で、自信がありそうに見えて実は深く不安を抱えている患者たちを描いている。その一人である成功した弁護士は、ひどい不安が始まった後に彼女のもとを訪れた。法廷での彼は冷静で機知に富んでいた。その公の顔は一種のよろいだったとレイエスは書く。私生活では孤独な暮らしを送り、同僚たちが裏切りを企てていると思い込んで、ほとんど被害妄想になっていた。彼の恐怖は夜になると表れ、彼は何度も鍵を確かめた。レイエスは、こうした例は私たちが思うより多く、精神疾患につきまとう不名誉な印象が多くの人に助けを求めることをためらわせていると主張する。彼女のやり方は単純だ。医師がまず患者の目に世界がどう映っているかを理解しようとしなければ、助言は無意味だと彼女は考えている。それを表す彼女自身の言葉は適切で、「借りた目」と呼んでいる。共感に似たものだが、もっと能動的だと彼女は説明する。最も心を打つページは、その弁護士が何か月もの沈黙の後、自分の恐怖の一つを思わず笑った瞬間を描いている。回復はしばしばそういう瞬間から始まると、レイエスは示唆している。","words":[["psychiatrist","freq:psychiatrist"],["insecure","freq:insecure"],["onset","freq:onset"],["persona","freq:persona"],["solitary","freq:solitary"],["paranoid","freq:paranoid"],["betrayal","freq:betrayal"],["manifest","freq:manifest"],["stigma","freq:stigma"],["meaningless","freq:meaningless"],["comprehend","freq:comprehend"],["apt","freq:apt"],["akin","freq:akin"],["empathy","freq:empathy"],["spontaneous","freq:spontaneous"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does Reyes say stops many people from getting help?","o":["The high cost of treatment","The shame connected with mental illness","A shortage of doctors","Fear of losing their jobs"],"a":1,"why":"the stigma attached to mental illness stops many people from seeking help（精神疾患への不名誉な印象が助けを求めるのを妨げる）とある。"},{"q":"What does Reyes mean by \"borrowed eyes\"?","o":["Checking patients' homes at night","Watching patients in court","Reading patients' private letters","Trying to understand how the patient sees the world"],"a":3,"why":"the doctor first tries to comprehend how the patient sees the world（患者の目に世界がどう映るかを理解しようとする）ことをそう呼んでいる。"}]},
{"id":"f-0026","page":"freq","group":"301〜400","kind":"小説の紹介","text":"The novel The Salt Shrine, first published in 1931, has just been reissued, and it deserves new readers. Its protagonist is Tomas, a young preacher sent to a fishing village where an old creed is still followed. The villagers share a communal life built around a shrine on the cliffs, and they believe in a prophecy that the sea will one day take back the land. Tomas arrives determined to preach against these beliefs. He speaks of purity and warns the villagers to beware of false gods. Slowly, however, he realizes that his real motive is a lust for control, a desire for domination over people he does not understand. When a storm destroys half the village, some of the fishermen blame him and seek vengeance. What makes the book remarkable is its realism. The author, who grew up in such a village, describes the work of the boats and the rhythm of the seasons in exact detail. A small plaque in her home town is the only memorial to her. The new edition prints the novel in its entirety for the first time, restoring two chapters that the original publisher cut.","ja":"1931年に初めて出版された小説「塩の聖堂」が再刊されたばかりで、新しい読者を得るに値する作品だ。主人公は、古い信条が今も守られている漁村に送られた若い説教師トマスである。村人たちは崖の上の聖堂を中心とした共同の生活を営み、海がいつか陸地を取り戻すという予言を信じている。トマスはこうした信仰に反対する説教をしようと決意してやって来る。彼は清らかさを語り、偽りの神々に用心するよう村人に警告する。しかし彼は次第に、自分の本当の動機が支配への強い欲望、つまり理解していない人々を支配したいという願いであることに気づく。嵐が村の半分を破壊すると、漁師の何人かは彼のせいだと考え、復讐しようとする。この本を際立たせているのは写実性だ。そうした村で育った作者は、船の仕事や季節の移ろいを正確に細かく描いている。彼女の故郷の小さな銘板が、彼女をしのぶ唯一の記念物である。新版は、元の出版社が削った2章を復元し、初めてこの小説の全部を収めている。","words":[["protagonist","freq:protagonist"],["preacher","freq:preacher"],["creed","freq:creed"],["communal","freq:communal"],["shrine","freq:shrine"],["prophecy","freq:prophecy"],["preach","freq:preach"],["purity","freq:purity"],["beware","freq:beware"],["lust","freq:lust"],["domination","freq:domination"],["vengeance","freq:vengeance"],["realism","freq:realism"],["plaque","freq:plaque"],["entirety","freq:entirety"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does Tomas come to realize about himself?","o":["He believes in the village's prophecy.","He really wants power over the villagers.","He is afraid of the sea.","He is unable to preach."],"a":1,"why":"his real motive is a lust for control, a desire for domination（本当の動機は支配への欲望）と気づくとある。"},{"q":"What is new about the reissued edition?","o":["It has been translated for the first time.","It includes photographs of the village.","It contains two chapters that were removed from the original.","It was rewritten by the author's family."],"a":2,"why":"restoring two chapters that the original publisher cut（元の出版社が削った2章を復元）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0027","page":"freq","group":"301〜400","kind":"地方政治","text":"When the town of Hollin announced plans for a new sports arena, few people outside the region noticed. The project was to be built by a consortium of three local firms, and the procurement process seemed routine. Then a national newspaper reported that the contract had been awarded without competition. The mayor defended the decision. Business had been slack for years, he said, and the arena would lure visitors and create jobs. He pointed out that only accredited builders had been considered and that a sponsorship deal with a drinks company would cover a third of the cost. His critics were not satisfied. They noted that the consolidation of the three firms into a single group had taken place only weeks before the contract was signed, and that the group planned to use a proprietary booking system that would tie the town to it for decades. The affair dominated the local election. The mayor, once at the apex of his popularity, kept his own constituency by a marginal vote on a record turnout. In his inaugural speech he promised an inquiry. A contingent of protesters outside the hall suggested that the two sides were far from a mutually acceptable outcome.","ja":"ホリンの町が新しい競技場の計画を発表したとき、地域の外で気づいた人はほとんどいなかった。この事業は地元3社の企業連合が建設することになっており、調達の手続きは通常どおりに見えた。その後、ある全国紙が、契約が競争なしに与えられたと報じた。町長はその決定を擁護した。景気は何年も低迷しており、競技場は訪問客を呼び込んで雇用を生むと彼は述べた。公認の建設業者だけが検討の対象になったこと、飲料会社との後援契約が費用の3分の1をまかなうことも指摘した。批判する人々は納得しなかった。3社が一つのグループに統合されたのは契約署名のわずか数週間前であり、そのグループは町を何十年も縛ることになる独自の予約システムを使う計画だと彼らは指摘した。この問題は地方選挙の最大の争点となった。かつて人気の頂点にあった町長は、記録的な投票率の中、わずかな票差で自分の選挙区を守った。就任演説で彼は調査を約束した。会場の外に集まった抗議者の一団は、双方が互いに受け入れられる結末からは程遠いことを示していた。","words":[["consortium","freq:consortium"],["procurement","freq:procurement"],["slack","freq:slack"],["lure","freq:lure"],["accredited","freq:accredited"],["sponsorship","freq:sponsorship"],["consolidation","freq:consolidation"],["proprietary","freq:proprietary"],["apex","freq:apex"],["constituency","freq:constituency"],["marginal","freq:marginal"],["turnout","freq:turnout"],["inaugural","freq:inaugural"],["contingent","freq:contingent"],["mutually","freq:mutually"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the newspaper reveal about the arena contract?","o":["It was given to the firms without any competing offers.","It was paid for entirely by a drinks company.","It was signed before the plan was announced.","It was awarded to builders from outside the region."],"a":0,"why":"the contract had been awarded without competition（競争なしに与えられた）と報じたとある。"},{"q":"How did the mayor do in the election?","o":["He lost to a protest leader.","He won easily.","He withdrew before the vote.","He kept his seat by a very small margin."],"a":3,"why":"kept his own constituency by a marginal vote（わずかな票差で選挙区を守った）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0028","page":"freq","group":"301〜400","kind":"事件の回顧","text":"The kidnapping of the banker Rolf Adler in 1978 remains one of the strangest crimes in the city's history. Adler, a classy dresser known for his tailored suits, was seized outside his office by two muscular men and driven away in a van. Within hours, a note demanded a ransom of two million marks. The police treated the safety of the victim as paramount and told reporters nothing, fearing that publicity would stoke public anger and push the kidnappers into violence. Officers set up a perimeter around a farmhouse where the gang was thought to be hiding, and commanders believed that a rescue was imminent. But the building was empty. Adler was found two days later, wandering on a country road with a fracture of the left arm. He said he had escaped through a window. The case took an odd turn at the trial, when one of the accused stood up to salute the judge and claimed that Adler himself had planned the whole affair to pay his debts. The court dismissed the story as a malicious invention. Adler never spoke about the matter again.","ja":"1978年の銀行家ロルフ・アドラーの誘拐は、今もこの都市の歴史で最も奇妙な犯罪の一つである。仕立てのよい背広で知られる上品な着こなしのアドラーは、事務所の外で2人のたくましい男に捕まり、バンで連れ去られた。数時間のうちに、200万マルクの身代金を要求する手紙が届いた。警察は被害者の安全を最も重要なものと位置づけ、記者には何も話さなかった。報道が世間の怒りをあおり、誘拐犯を暴力に走らせることを恐れたのだ。警官たちは一味が隠れていると見られた農家の周囲を固め、指揮官たちは救出が間近だと考えた。しかし建物は空だった。アドラーは2日後、左腕を骨折した状態で田舎道をさまよっているところを発見された。窓から逃げたと彼は語った。事件は裁判で奇妙な展開を見せた。被告の一人が立ち上がって裁判官に敬礼し、借金を返すためにアドラー自身がすべてを計画したのだと主張したのである。裁判所はその話を悪意のある作り話として退けた。アドラーはこの件について二度と語らなかった。","words":[["classy","freq:classy"],["muscular","freq:muscular"],["ransom","freq:ransom"],["paramount","freq:paramount"],["stoke","freq:stoke"],["perimeter","freq:perimeter"],["imminent","freq:imminent"],["fracture","freq:fracture"],["salute","freq:salute"],["malicious","freq:malicious"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the police tell reporters nothing?","o":["They did not know who had been kidnapped.","They feared publicity would make the kidnappers violent.","They had already paid the money.","They suspected a reporter of helping the gang."],"a":1,"why":"publicity would stoke public anger and push the kidnappers into violence（報道が怒りをあおり、誘拐犯を暴力に走らせる）ことを恐れた。"},{"q":"What claim was made at the trial?","o":["Adler had arranged the kidnapping himself.","The police had hidden the ransom.","The farmhouse belonged to the judge.","Adler had never been injured."],"a":0,"why":"Adler himself had planned the whole affair to pay his debts（アドラー自身が借金返済のために計画した）と被告の一人が主張した。"}]},
{"id":"f-0029","page":"freq","group":"401〜500","kind":"民主化の歩み","text":"When the military dictatorship in Corvenia collapsed in 1991, few observers expected democracy to last. The generals had ruled for thirty years, and the country had no tradition of free elections. The first vote was chaotic. Forty parties competed, each with its own manifesto, and a militant group in the south threatened to disrupt polling. Yet the electorate turned out in huge numbers, which gave the new parliament real legitimacy. The early years were difficult. A deep rift opened between the president, who wanted an amnesty for officers of the old regime, and lawmakers who demanded trials. The president used his veto three times to block their bills. Some feared that the army would intervene again, but the generals no longer held sway over public opinion. A new constitution finally settled the matter. It ended the supremacy of the president over the courts, banned any system resembling apartheid in the treatment of minorities, and forbade judges from holding any party affiliation. It also allowed the dissolution of parliament only after a public vote. Thirty years on, the country that was once the dominion of a few officers has changed its government peacefully six times.","ja":"1991年にコルベニアの軍事独裁政権が崩壊したとき、民主主義が長続きすると予想した観察者はほとんどいなかった。将軍たちは30年間統治しており、その国には自由選挙の伝統がなかった。最初の投票は混乱した。40の政党がそれぞれの政策綱領を掲げて争い、南部の過激派組織は投票を妨害すると脅した。それでも有権者は大勢投票に行き、そのことが新しい議会に本物の正当性を与えた。初期の数年は困難だった。旧政権の将校への恩赦を望む大統領と、裁判を求める議員たちの間に深い亀裂が生じた。大統領は議員たちの法案を阻むために3度拒否権を使った。軍が再び介入するのではと恐れる人もいたが、将軍たちはもはや世論への影響力を持っていなかった。新しい憲法がついにこの問題を決着させた。それは裁判所に対する大統領の優位を終わらせ、少数派の扱いにおいて人種隔離政策に似たいかなる制度も禁じ、裁判官が政党に所属することを禁じた。また、議会の解散は国民投票の後にのみ認められることになった。30年たった今、かつて少数の将校の支配下にあったその国は、6回、平和的に政権を交代させている。","words":[["dictatorship","freq:dictatorship"],["manifesto","freq:manifesto"],["militant","freq:militant"],["electorate","freq:electorate"],["legitimacy","freq:legitimacy"],["rift","freq:rift"],["amnesty","freq:amnesty"],["veto","freq:veto"],["intervene","freq:intervene"],["sway","freq:sway"],["supremacy","freq:supremacy"],["apartheid","freq:apartheid"],["affiliation","freq:affiliation"],["dissolution","freq:dissolution"],["dominion","freq:dominion"]],"qs":[{"q":"What gave the first parliament its authority?","o":["The support of the generals","The large number of people who voted","The small number of parties","A treaty with a neighboring state"],"a":1,"why":"the electorate turned out in huge numbers, which gave the new parliament real legitimacy（有権者が大勢投票し、それが正当性を与えた）とある。"},{"q":"What caused the dispute between the president and lawmakers?","o":["Whether former officers should be pardoned or tried","Whether to hold another election","Whether judges could join parties","Whether the south should become independent"],"a":0,"why":"大統領は an amnesty for officers of the old regime を望み、議員は trials を求めたとある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0030","page":"freq","group":"401〜500","kind":"企業の破綻","text":"The demise of Harlow Bank was sudden, but the causes had been building for years. Under its ruthless chief executive, the bank had lent huge sums to property developers, accepting half-built towers as collateral. When prices fell, those towers were worth far less than the loans. By the spring, the bank was on the verge of collapse. An official inquiry later described a culture of deception at the top. Managers had hidden the losses through fraudulent accounting, and the board, which the report called incompetent, had asked no questions. The inquiry also accused the regulators of negligence, since they had ignored two written warnings. Several directors were found guilty of professional misconduct. What angered the public most was the hypocrisy of the man at the helm, who had lectured small businesses about careful borrowing while taking enormous risks himself. The government stepped in to salvage what it could, but the affair did much to undermine trust in the whole banking system. The inquiry recommended drastic reforms, including personal fines for directors. Three years later, customers are still waiting for tangible results.","ja":"ハーロウ銀行の破綻は突然だったが、その原因は何年も前から積み重なっていた。冷酷な最高経営責任者のもとで、同行は不動産開発業者に巨額を貸し付け、建設途中の高層ビルを担保として受け入れていた。価格が下がると、それらのビルの価値は融資額をはるかに下回った。春までに、銀行は崩壊の瀬戸際に立っていた。後に公的な調査は、経営陣に欺きの体質があったと述べた。経営幹部は不正な会計処理によって損失を隠し、報告書が無能と呼んだ取締役会は何も問いただしていなかった。調査はまた、規制当局が2度の書面による警告を無視したとして、その怠慢も指摘した。複数の取締役が職務上の不正行為で有罪とされた。世間を最も怒らせたのは、かじ取り役だった人物の偽善だった。彼は小企業に慎重な借り入れを説きながら、自らは巨大な危険を冒していたのだ。政府は救えるものを救おうと介入したが、この事件は銀行制度全体への信頼を大きく損なった。調査は、取締役個人への罰金を含む思い切った改革を勧告した。3年たった今も、顧客は具体的な成果を待っている。","words":[["demise","freq:demise"],["ruthless","freq:ruthless"],["collateral","freq:collateral"],["verge","freq:verge"],["deception","freq:deception"],["fraudulent","freq:fraudulent"],["incompetent","freq:incompetent"],["negligence","freq:negligence"],["misconduct","freq:misconduct"],["hypocrisy","freq:hypocrisy"],["helm","freq:helm"],["salvage","freq:salvage"],["undermine","freq:undermine"],["drastic","freq:drastic"],["tangible","freq:tangible"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the bank get into trouble?","o":["Its customers withdrew their savings.","The buildings it held as security lost their value.","The government raised taxes on banks.","Its chief executive resigned without warning."],"a":1,"why":"When prices fell, those towers were worth far less than the loans（担保のビルの価値が融資額を大きく下回った）とある。"},{"q":"What made the public especially angry?","o":["The inquiry took three years.","Small businesses were fined.","The regulators were not named.","The bank's leader had preached caution while taking great risks."],"a":3,"why":"lectured small businesses about careful borrowing while taking enormous risks himself（慎重さを説きながら自らは大きな危険を冒していた）という偽善である。"}]},
{"id":"f-0031","page":"freq","group":"401〜500","kind":"農業と気候","text":"For centuries, the valley of the Sorna was one of the most fertile regions on the continent. Each peasant family grew enough wheat to feed itself and to sell a little at market. Records from the last great famine, in 1847, show how fragile that life was. Two years of low precipitation were followed by a winter of violent gales, and soil erosion carried away much of the best land. Today the valley faces a different threat. Warmer winters have allowed a new insect pest to spread, and the old varieties of wheat are highly susceptible to it. The incidence of crop failure has tripled in a decade. Scientists at a local institute are working on an answer. By studying a natural mutation found in wild grasses, they have bred a more adaptive variety that can withstand both the insect and long dry periods. Trials suggest that its grain is fully edible and tastes much like the old kind. The researchers warn, however, that the prevalence of the pest is rising faster than they expected. Without wider planting of the new variety, they say, the situation could become dire within five years.","ja":"何世紀もの間、ソルナ川の谷は大陸で最も肥沃な地域の一つだった。農民の家族はそれぞれ、自分たちが食べ、少しを市場で売るのに足りるだけの小麦を育てた。1847年の最後の大飢饉の記録は、その暮らしがいかにもろかったかを示している。降水量の少ない年が2年続いた後、激しい強風の冬が来て、土壌の侵食が最良の土地の多くを運び去った。今日、この谷は別の脅威に直面している。冬が暖かくなったことで新しい害虫が広がり、古い品種の小麦はそれに非常に弱い。不作の発生率は10年で3倍になった。地元の研究所の科学者たちは答えを探っている。野生の草に見つかった自然の突然変異を研究することで、その害虫にも長い乾燥期にも耐えられる、より適応力の高い品種を作り出した。試験によれば、その穀粒は問題なく食べられ、味も古い品種とよく似ている。しかし研究者たちは、害虫の広がりが予想より速く進んでいると警告する。新品種の作付けがもっと広がらなければ、5年以内に状況は悲惨なものになりかねないという。","words":[["fertile","freq:fertile"],["peasant","freq:peasant"],["famine","freq:famine"],["precipitation","freq:precipitation"],["gales","freq:gale"],["erosion","freq:erosion"],["pest","freq:pest"],["susceptible","freq:susceptible"],["incidence","freq:incidence"],["mutation","freq:mutation"],["adaptive","freq:adaptive"],["withstand","freq:withstand"],["edible","freq:edible"],["prevalence","freq:prevalence"],["dire","freq:dire"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is the new threat to the valley?","o":["A shortage of farm workers","An insect that has spread because winters are warmer","A fall in the price of wheat","Flooding from the river"],"a":1,"why":"Warmer winters have allowed a new insect pest to spread（暖かい冬のせいで新しい害虫が広がった）とある。"},{"q":"What have the scientists produced?","o":["A chemical that kills the insect","A machine that prevents erosion","A wheat that resists the insect and dry weather","A new way of storing grain"],"a":2,"why":"a more adaptive variety that can withstand both the insect and long dry periods（害虫にも乾燥にも耐える品種）を作ったとある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0032","page":"freq","group":"401〜500","kind":"学問の方法","text":"When Professor Ruth Adeyo began her career in anthropology, the field was dominated by a single paradigm. Scholars would spend a year or two in a village, collect its mythology, and then make a general assertion about the society as a whole. Adeyo found this approach unsatisfactory. From her standpoint, such studies relied too much on the impressions of one visitor and too little on empirical evidence. She proposed a different method. Her team built a corpus of ten thousand recorded conversations from forty communities, and used it to test ideas that earlier writers had simply presumed to be true. One of her best-known findings concerns time. It had long been claimed that certain languages do not differentiate between past and future. Her data showed that speakers of those languages made clear temporal distinctions; they simply used gesture rather than grammar. Critics complain that her work is narrow and that a society cannot be said to comprise only what can be counted. Adeyo replies that she has no objection to bold conceptual thinking, or even to a hypothetical case, provided it is tested. Few would deny that she is a practitioner of the highest caliber.","ja":"ルース・アデヨ教授が人類学の研究を始めたとき、この分野は一つの枠組みに支配されていた。学者たちは村で1、2年を過ごしてその神話を集め、それから社会全体について一般的な主張をした。アデヨはこのやり方に満足できなかった。彼女の観点からすれば、そうした研究は一人の訪問者の印象に頼りすぎ、実証的な証拠に頼ることが少なすぎた。彼女は別の方法を提案した。彼女のチームは40の共同体で録音した1万件の会話から成る言語資料を作り、以前の著者たちが単に正しいと思い込んでいた考えを検証するのに使った。彼女の最もよく知られた発見の一つは時間に関するものだ。ある種の言語は過去と未来を区別しないと長く主張されてきた。彼女のデータは、それらの言語の話者が明確な時間的区別をしていることを示した。彼らは文法ではなく身ぶりを使っていただけなのだ。批判する人々は、彼女の研究は視野が狭く、社会は数えられるものだけから成るとは言えないと不満を述べる。アデヨは、検証されるのであれば、大胆な概念的思考にも、仮定の事例にさえ異論はないと答える。彼女が最高の力量を持つ実践者であることを否定する人はほとんどいないだろう。","words":[["anthropology","freq:anthropology"],["paradigm","freq:paradigm"],["mythology","freq:mythology"],["assertion","freq:assertion"],["standpoint","freq:standpoint"],["empirical","freq:empirical"],["corpus","freq:corpus"],["presumed","freq:presume"],["differentiate","freq:differentiate"],["temporal","freq:temporal"],["comprise","freq:comprise"],["conceptual","freq:conceptual"],["hypothetical","freq:hypothetical"],["practitioner","freq:practitioner"],["caliber","freq:caliber"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did Adeyo dislike about the traditional approach?","o":["It depended too heavily on one person's impressions.","It required too many researchers.","It ignored the myths of the villages.","It used too many recordings."],"a":0,"why":"relied too much on the impressions of one visitor and too little on empirical evidence（一人の訪問者の印象に頼りすぎた）とある。"},{"q":"What did her data show about certain languages?","o":["They have no words for the future.","Their grammar is unusually complex.","They are disappearing rapidly.","Their speakers mark time with gestures instead of grammar."],"a":3,"why":"made clear temporal distinctions; they simply used gesture rather than grammar（身ぶりで時間の区別をしていた）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0033","page":"freq","group":"401〜500","kind":"劇評","text":"The new play at the Crown Theatre, The Minister's Garden, is a satire on political life, and it is the most entertaining production the city has seen this year. It sets out to portray a government minister who retreats to his country house while a scandal grows in the capital. The first act is light, full of sarcasm at the expense of his advisers. In the second, the tone darkens. A long feud with his brother comes to the surface, and the hostility between them turns the comedy into something close to tragedy. The lead actor is remarkably versatile. He moves from clowning to grief without any loss of restraint, and in the final scene he drops the veil of charm to show the vile ambition beneath. The production pays homage to the political dramas of the 1960s, with period costumes and a vibrant jazz score, but it never sinks into mere nostalgia. Its themes have an obvious resonance today. The writer clearly feels an affinity with the great satirists of the past, and on this evidence she is ready to take on their mantle.","ja":"クラウン劇場の新作「大臣の庭」は政治の世界への風刺であり、今年この都市で見られた中で最も楽しい舞台だ。この劇は、首都で不祥事が大きくなる中、田舎の邸宅に引きこもる政府の大臣を描こうとする。第一幕は軽く、彼の顧問たちをからかう皮肉に満ちている。第二幕では調子が暗くなる。兄との長い確執が表面に出てきて、2人の間の敵意が喜劇を悲劇に近いものに変える。主演俳優は驚くほど多才だ。彼は節度を少しも失わずに道化から悲嘆へと移り、最後の場面では愛想のよさというベールを外して、その下にある卑劣な野心を見せる。この舞台は当時の衣装と活気のあるジャズの音楽によって1960年代の政治劇に敬意を表しているが、決して単なる懐古趣味には陥らない。その主題は今日にも明らかに響くものがある。作者は明らかに過去の偉大な風刺作家たちに親近感を抱いており、この作品を見る限り、彼らの役目を引き継ぐ準備ができている。","words":[["satire","freq:satire"],["portray","freq:portray"],["sarcasm","freq:sarcasm"],["feud","freq:feud"],["hostility","freq:hostility"],["versatile","freq:versatile"],["restraint","freq:restraint"],["veil","freq:veil"],["vile","freq:vile"],["homage","freq:homage"],["vibrant","freq:vibrant"],["nostalgia","freq:nostalgia"],["resonance","freq:resonance"],["affinity","freq:affinity"],["mantle","freq:mantle"]],"qs":[{"q":"How does the play change in the second act?","o":["It becomes a musical.","It moves to the capital.","It becomes darker and closer to tragedy.","It introduces a new minister."],"a":2,"why":"In the second, the tone darkens とあり、喜劇が something close to tragedy に変わるとある。"},{"q":"What does the reviewer say about the lead actor?","o":["He plays very different moods with great control.","He is better at comedy than at serious scenes.","He wrote the jazz score himself.","He appeared in dramas of the 1960s."],"a":0,"why":"remarkably versatile とあり、道化から悲嘆へ without any loss of restraint（節度を失わずに）移ると評している。"}]},
{"id":"f-0034","page":"freq","group":"401〜500","kind":"都市の再開発","text":"The demolition of the old Baxter textile mill began last week, ending an argument that had divided the town for a decade. The mill had been one pillar of the local economy for a century, but its machines became obsolete in the 1980s and the building had stood empty ever since. Engineers warned that the upper floors were unsafe. Last year a falling beam struck a stationary bus, and only luck prevented a casualty. Supporters of the mill wanted to turn it into a vocational college. The council argued that the housing market was not saturated and that homes were needed more. In the final tally, councillors voted nine to six for demolition. The plans try to align the two views. A small enclosure around the old chimney will be kept as a memorial, with a viewing platform atop it, and sensors will monitor ambient noise during the work. Even so, the decision has driven a wedge between older residents, who are accustomed to seeing the mill on the skyline, and newcomers. Some fear the displacement of the small workshops that still rent space beside it.","ja":"古いバクスター織物工場の取り壊しが先週始まり、10年にわたって町を二分してきた論争に終止符が打たれた。工場は1世紀の間、地元経済の柱の一つだったが、その機械は1980年代に時代遅れになり、建物はそれ以来、空いたままだった。技師たちは上の階が危険だと警告していた。昨年、落ちてきた梁が停車中のバスに当たり、死傷者が出なかったのは幸運にすぎなかった。工場を残したい人々は、それを職業訓練の学校にしたいと考えていた。町議会は、住宅市場は飽和しておらず、住宅のほうがもっと必要だと主張した。最終的な集計では、議員たちは9対6で取り壊しに賛成した。計画は2つの意見を調和させようとしている。古い煙突の周りの小さな囲いが記念として残され、その上に展望台が設けられる。作業中は感知器が周囲の騒音を監視する。それでも、この決定は、空を背にした工場の姿を見慣れている古くからの住民と、新しく来た人々との間にくさびを打ち込んだ。工場の隣で今も場所を借りている小さな作業場が立ち退かされることを心配する人もいる。","words":[["demolition","freq:demolition"],["pillar","freq:pillar"],["obsolete","freq:obsolete"],["stationary","freq:stationary"],["casualty","freq:casualty"],["vocational","freq:vocational"],["saturated","freq:saturated"],["tally","freq:tally"],["align","freq:align"],["enclosure","freq:enclosure"],["atop","freq:atop"],["ambient","freq:ambient"],["wedge","freq:wedge"],["accustomed","freq:accustomed"],["displacement","freq:displacement"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did supporters of the mill want to do with it?","o":["Sell its machines abroad","Use it as a bus station","Keep it empty as a memorial","Make it a place for job training"],"a":3,"why":"wanted to turn it into a vocational college（職業訓練の学校にしたかった）とある。"},{"q":"What part of the mill will remain?","o":["The upper floors","The area around the chimney","The small workshops inside","The original machines"],"a":1,"why":"A small enclosure around the old chimney will be kept as a memorial（煙突の周りの小さな囲いが記念として残される）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0035","page":"freq","group":"401〜500","kind":"捜査の記録","text":"The fire at the Dalton paint factory was at first thought to be an accident. A faulty fuse, the manager said, had started it. Inspector Mara Quinn was not convinced. Walking along the north flank of the building, she noticed that one wall had begun to tilt outward, as if pushed from inside. In a cavity beneath the floor, her team found a cache of empty fuel cans. That changed everything: this was sabotage. Quinn questioned the staff one by one. Most were helpful, but the night watchman remained mute, staring at the table with a stiff posture. She did not press him. Instead, she asked him to switch on the lights in the corridor, and watched him flick the wrong switch twice. He had not, she realized, worked there for long. The records confirmed it. He had been hired only a week earlier, with false papers. The investigation that followed moved at the pace of a snail, since the man who had paid him lived abroad. It was two years before anyone stood trial, and the factory was never rebuilt.","ja":"ダルトン塗料工場の火事は、最初は事故だと考えられていた。欠陥のあるヒューズが原因だと工場長は言った。マーラ・クイン警部は納得しなかった。建物の北側面に沿って歩いていて、彼女は一つの壁が内側から押されたかのように外へ傾き始めていることに気づいた。床下の空洞で、彼女の班は隠された空の燃料缶を見つけた。それですべてが変わった。これは破壊工作だったのだ。クインは従業員を一人ずつ取り調べた。ほとんどは協力的だったが、夜警は無言のまま、こわばった姿勢で机を見つめていた。彼女は無理に問い詰めなかった。その代わりに廊下の明かりをつけるよう頼み、彼が2度、間違ったスイッチをはじくのを見ていた。この男はここで長く働いていないのだと彼女は気づいた。記録がそれを裏づけた。彼はわずか1週間前に、偽の書類で雇われていた。その後の捜査はカタツムリのような速さでしか進まなかった。彼に金を払った男が国外に住んでいたからだ。誰かが裁判にかけられるまでに2年かかり、工場は二度と再建されなかった。","words":[["fuse","freq:fuse"],["flank","freq:flank"],["tilt","freq:tilt"],["cavity","freq:cavity"],["cache","freq:cache"],["sabotage","freq:sabotage"],["mute","freq:mute"],["posture","freq:posture"],["flick","freq:flick"],["snail","freq:snail"]],"qs":[{"q":"What convinced the inspector that the fire was deliberate?","o":["The manager's confession","Fuel cans hidden under the floor","A letter sent to the police","A broken fuse in the corridor"],"a":1,"why":"In a cavity beneath the floor, her team found a cache of empty fuel cans（床下の空洞で隠された燃料缶を見つけた）ことで sabotage とわかった。"},{"q":"How did she realize the watchman was new?","o":["He did not know the manager's name.","He was wearing the wrong uniform.","He chose the wrong light switch twice.","He arrived late for questioning."],"a":2,"why":"watched him flick the wrong switch twice（間違ったスイッチを2度はじいた）のを見て気づいた。"}]},
{"id":"f-0036","page":"freq","group":"501〜600","kind":"政権の変質","text":"At his inauguration four years ago, President Marek Dolan promised a crusade against corruption. Many voters believed him. Today, his critics describe his government as increasingly authoritarian. The change began with a purge of senior judges, whom he accused of protecting the old elite. It continued with an ordinance that allowed the police to close any precinct of the capital to demonstrators. When truck drivers set up a blockade of the main highway in protest, the leaders were arrested and several foreign workers among them faced deportation. Dolan's supporters argue that strong measures were needed in a country long divided by caste and by the segregation of rich and poor districts. They point to real societal gains: new schools, cheaper medicine, and a court ruling that upheld the right of every child to free meals. But opposition is growing even inside his own party. At a recent caucus, a third of the members refused to back him, and one constituent after another has written to complain about the arrests. Some lawmakers now speak openly of impeachment. Whether they have the votes is far from clear.","ja":"4年前の就任式で、マレク・ドラン大統領は汚職に対する改革運動を約束した。多くの有権者は彼を信じた。今日、彼を批判する人々は、彼の政権がますます権威主義的になっていると言う。変化は上級裁判官の粛清から始まった。彼は裁判官たちが旧来の特権層を守っていると非難したのだ。続いて、警察が首都のどの管区でもデモ参加者に対して閉鎖できるようにする条例が出された。トラック運転手たちが抗議して幹線道路を封鎖すると、指導者たちは逮捕され、その中にいた数人の外国人労働者は強制送還に直面した。ドランの支持者たちは、カーストによって、また富裕地区と貧困地区の分離によって長く分断されてきた国には強い措置が必要だったと主張する。彼らは実際の社会的な成果を挙げる。新しい学校、より安い薬、そしてすべての子どもが無料の食事を受ける権利を支持した裁判所の判断である。しかし、反対は彼自身の党の内部でも強まっている。最近の党員集会では、議員の3分の1が彼を支持することを拒み、有権者が次々と逮捕について苦情の手紙を書いている。今では弾劾を公然と口にする議員もいる。彼らに必要な票があるかどうかは、まったく明らかではない。","words":[["inauguration","freq:inauguration"],["crusade","freq:crusade"],["authoritarian","freq:authoritarian"],["purge","freq:purge"],["ordinance","freq:ordinance"],["precinct","freq:precinct"],["blockade","freq:blockade"],["deportation","freq:deportation"],["caste","freq:caste"],["segregation","freq:segregation"],["societal","freq:societal"],["upheld","freq:upheld"],["caucus","freq:caucus"],["constituent","freq:constituent"],["impeachment","freq:impeachment"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was the first sign that the government was changing?","o":["Senior judges were removed.","Truck drivers were arrested.","Free school meals were ended.","The capital was closed to visitors."],"a":0,"why":"The change began with a purge of senior judges（上級裁判官の粛清から始まった）とある。"},{"q":"What shows that opposition exists within the president's own party?","o":["Party members organized the highway protest.","A third of the members would not support him at a meeting.","The party has already voted to remove him.","The party's leaders have left the country."],"a":1,"why":"At a recent caucus, a third of the members refused to back him（党員集会で3分の1が支持を拒んだ）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0037","page":"freq","group":"501〜600","kind":"密輸組織の摘発","text":"For three years, a drug cartel had operated in the port of Lisandro under a cloak of respectability. Its leaders owned restaurants and gave money to local charities. The police had tried again and again to gather evidence, but to no avail. The breakthrough came from an unexpected direction. A young chemist at the city hospital, carrying out an autopsy on a fisherman, found traces of snake venom mixed with a chemical of unusual toxicity. The pathology report puzzled her, and she passed it to a detective who acted as liaison with the customs service. He recognized the mixture at once. The cartel used it to punish informers. Working in total secrecy, a small team placed a transmitter in a fishing boat and managed to intercept messages between the gang and its suppliers. One message contained a threat that the detectives took to be a bluff; another gave the date of a delivery. On that night, the police launched a blitz on six addresses. There was something sinister about how quietly the leaders gave themselves up, as if they expected to be free again without much hassle. So far, they have been wrong.","ja":"3年の間、ある麻薬カルテルがリサンドロの港で、まっとうな外見を隠れみのにして活動していた。その幹部たちは料理店を所有し、地元の慈善団体に寄付をしていた。警察は何度も証拠を集めようとしたが、無駄だった。突破口は思いがけない方向から来た。市立病院の若い化学者が、ある漁師の検死を行っていて、異常に毒性の高い化学物質と混ざったヘビの毒の痕跡を見つけたのだ。その病理報告は彼女を戸惑わせ、彼女はそれを税関との連絡役を務める刑事に渡した。刑事はその混合物をすぐに見分けた。カルテルが密告者を罰するのに使うものだった。完全な秘密のうちに、小さな班が漁船に送信機を仕掛け、一味と供給元の間の通信を傍受することに成功した。ある通信には刑事たちがはったりと受け取った脅しが含まれ、別の通信には受け渡しの日付があった。その夜、警察は6か所に一斉に踏み込んだ。幹部たちがあまりに静かに投降したことには、どこか不吉なものがあった。大した面倒もなくまた自由の身になれると思っているかのようだった。今のところ、彼らの思惑は外れている。","words":[["cartel","freq:cartel"],["cloak","freq:cloak"],["avail","freq:avail"],["autopsy","freq:autopsy"],["venom","freq:venom"],["toxicity","freq:toxicity"],["pathology","freq:pathology"],["liaison","freq:liaison"],["secrecy","freq:secrecy"],["transmitter","freq:transmitter"],["intercept","freq:intercept"],["bluff","freq:bluff"],["blitz","freq:blitz"],["sinister","freq:sinister"],["hassle","freq:hassle"]],"qs":[{"q":"What gave the police their breakthrough?","o":["A confession by one of the leaders","A report from a restaurant owner","An unusual poison found during an examination of a body","A message sent by a charity"],"a":2,"why":"autopsy で snake venom mixed with a chemical of unusual toxicity（異常に毒性の高い化学物質と混ざったヘビの毒）が見つかったことが突破口になった。"},{"q":"How did the detectives learn the date of a delivery?","o":["They listened in on the gang's messages.","They questioned the fisherman's family.","They followed the leaders to the port.","They searched the hospital's records."],"a":0,"why":"intercept messages between the gang and its suppliers（一味と供給元の通信を傍受）し、その一つが日付を伝えていた。"}]},
{"id":"f-0038","page":"freq","group":"501〜600","kind":"都市の再生","text":"Twenty years ago, the eastern docks of Varna were a symbol of urban degradation. Warehouses stood empty, the plumbing in nearby flats dated from the 1920s, and residents lived with a perpetual smell of oil. Today the district is the most fashionable part of the metropolis. Its regeneration was not the result of a single grand plan. The city sold the old buildings for a nominal sum, on a conditional basis: buyers had to commence repairs within a year and to install modern heating and ventilation. Grants were proportional to the number of jobs created. At first progress was slow. Then an influx of young designers and restaurant owners changed the mood, and the majestic brick warehouses became a popular backdrop for films. Success has brought problems of its own. Rents have tripled, and the families who stayed through the bad years are being pushed out. The streets fill each weekend with visitors who leave behind an accumulation of disposable cups and bottles. The council now faces an awkward question. Having saved the docks from decay, it must decide whom they have been saved for.","ja":"20年前、バルナの東埠頭は都市の荒廃の象徴だった。倉庫は空のまま立ち、近くの集合住宅の配管は1920年代のもので、住民は絶え間ない油の臭いとともに暮らしていた。今日、この地区は大都市で最もおしゃれな一角だ。その再生は一つの壮大な計画の結果ではなかった。市は古い建物を名目だけの金額で、条件付きで売却した。買い手は1年以内に修繕を始め、近代的な暖房と換気の設備を取り付けなければならなかった。助成金は生み出した雇用の数に比例した。最初、進展は遅かった。やがて若いデザイナーや料理店主の流入が雰囲気を変え、雄大なれんが造りの倉庫は映画の人気の背景になった。成功はそれ自体の問題をもたらした。家賃は3倍になり、苦しい年月を通じてとどまった家族が追い出されつつある。週末ごとに通りは訪問客であふれ、彼らは使い捨てのカップや瓶の山を残していく。市議会は今、扱いにくい問いに直面している。埠頭を衰退から救った今、誰のために救ったのかを決めなければならないのだ。","words":[["degradation","freq:degradation"],["plumbing","freq:plumbing"],["perpetual","freq:perpetual"],["metropolis","freq:metropolis"],["regeneration","freq:regeneration"],["nominal","freq:nominal"],["conditional","freq:conditional"],["commence","freq:commence"],["ventilation","freq:ventilation"],["proportional","freq:proportional"],["influx","freq:influx"],["majestic","freq:majestic"],["backdrop","freq:backdrop"],["accumulation","freq:accumulation"],["disposable","freq:disposable"]],"qs":[{"q":"On what condition did the city sell the old buildings?","o":["Buyers had to live in them for ten years.","Buyers had to begin repairs soon and fit modern systems.","Buyers had to open restaurants.","Buyers had to allow films to be made there."],"a":1,"why":"commence repairs within a year and to install modern heating and ventilation（1年以内に修繕を始め、近代的な設備を付ける）ことが条件だった。"},{"q":"What problem has success caused?","o":["The warehouses are empty again.","Film companies have left the district.","The grants have run out.","Long-term residents can no longer afford to stay."],"a":3,"why":"Rents have tripled, and the families who stayed through the bad years are being pushed out（家賃が3倍になり、とどまっていた家族が追い出されている）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0039","page":"freq","group":"501〜600","kind":"高原の自然","text":"The limestone plateau of Karsa lies at a latitude where winters are long and rain is scarce. Few people dwell there now. A century ago, however, the area supported a busy quarry and several villages, each with its own orchard of plum trees. When the quarry closed, the people left, and nature began to return. Scientists who study the plateau describe it as a system slowly moving back toward equilibrium. Pine trees, whose sap hardens into a valuable resin, have spread across the abandoned fields. The wood pigeon, once hunted almost to extinction, now nests in the old quarry walls. To understand these changes, researchers isolate small plots of land and rotate their measurements between them, recording every parameter from soil temperature to wind speed. Their main concern is fire. Dry pine needles are perfect fuel, and combustion can begin with a single spark. The climate itself is in flux, with hotter summers each decade. The team believes that controlled burning in spring may be the only way to prevent a disaster, though the idea of deliberately setting fire to a recovering forest is hard for many visitors to accept.","ja":"カルサの石灰岩の高原は、冬が長く雨の乏しい緯度にある。今ではそこに住む人はほとんどいない。しかし1世紀前、この一帯にはにぎやかな採石場といくつかの村があり、村ごとにスモモの果樹園があった。採石場が閉鎖されると人々は去り、自然が戻り始めた。この高原を研究する科学者たちは、ここをゆっくりと均衡に向かって戻りつつある系として説明する。樹液が固まって貴重な樹脂になるマツの木が、放棄された畑に広がった。かつてほぼ絶滅するまで狩られたモリバトは、今では古い採石場の壁に巣を作っている。こうした変化を理解するために、研究者たちは小さな区画を切り離し、その間で順番に測定を行って、土の温度から風速まであらゆる条件を記録している。彼らの最大の懸念は火事だ。乾いたマツの葉は格好の燃料であり、燃焼はたった一つの火花から始まりうる。気候そのものも絶えず変化しており、10年ごとに夏が暑くなっている。研究チームは、春に管理して焼くことが災害を防ぐ唯一の方法かもしれないと考えている。もっとも、回復しつつある森にわざと火をつけるという考えは、多くの訪問者には受け入れがたい。","words":[["plateau","freq:plateau"],["latitude","freq:latitude"],["scarce","freq:scarce"],["dwell","freq:dwell"],["quarry","freq:quarry"],["orchard","freq:orchard"],["equilibrium","freq:equilibrium"],["sap","freq:sap"],["resin","freq:resin"],["pigeon","freq:pigeon"],["isolate","freq:isolate"],["rotate","freq:rotate"],["parameter","freq:parameter"],["combustion","freq:combustion"],["flux","freq:flux"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did nature begin to return to the plateau?","o":["The people left after the quarry shut down.","The government planted pine trees.","Hunting was banned by law.","The climate became wetter."],"a":0,"why":"When the quarry closed, the people left, and nature began to return（採石場が閉鎖されて人々が去り、自然が戻り始めた）とある。"},{"q":"What do the researchers think may prevent a disaster?","o":["Cutting down the pine trees","Closing the plateau to visitors","Burning parts of the forest on purpose in spring","Reopening the old villages"],"a":2,"why":"controlled burning in spring may be the only way to prevent a disaster（春の管理された野焼きが唯一の方法かもしれない）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0040","page":"freq","group":"501〜600","kind":"作家の評伝","text":"Few writers have been as prolific as Helen Marsh, who published forty novels and more than a thousand essays. A new biography, the first full scholarly study of her life, shows how much effort lay behind that output. Marsh was the youngest sibling in a family of seven, and she grew up fighting to be heard. After a brief stint as a schoolteacher, she moved to London and began to write. Her early reviews were sarcastic and sometimes cruel, and they earned her the lasting resentment of several rivals. Readers who know only that fiery public voice may be surprised by the woman who appears in her letters. In private she was gracious, and she wrote about her own failures with real humility. Her marital life was unhappy, and she remained restless, moving house eleven times. The biographer does not hide her faults. Marsh could be eccentric, and in old age she grew cynical about politics. But he argues that her best work, though often described as cold and cerebral, has an emotional authenticity that her critics missed. The evidence he presents is hard to dismiss.","ja":"ヘレン・マーシュほど多作な作家はほとんどいない。彼女は40冊の小説と1,000編を超える随筆を出版した。彼女の生涯についての初めての本格的な学術研究である新しい伝記は、その仕事量の背後にどれほどの努力があったかを示している。マーシュは7人きょうだいの末っ子で、話を聞いてもらうために闘いながら育った。短い期間、教師を務めた後、彼女はロンドンに移って書き始めた。初期の書評は皮肉で、時に残酷でもあり、そのせいで何人かの競争相手から長く恨まれることになった。その激しい公の声しか知らない読者は、彼女の手紙に現れる女性に驚くかもしれない。私生活での彼女は親切で、自分自身の失敗について本物の謙虚さをもって書いた。結婚生活は不幸で、彼女は落ち着かないまま、11回引っ越した。伝記作家は彼女の欠点を隠さない。マーシュは風変わりなところがあり、老年には政治について冷笑的になった。しかし彼は、冷たく知的だとよく評される彼女の最良の作品には、批評家たちが見落とした感情の真実味があると論じる。彼が示す証拠は、退けるのが難しい。","words":[["prolific","freq:prolific"],["scholarly","freq:scholarly"],["sibling","freq:sibling"],["stint","freq:stint"],["sarcastic","freq:sarcastic"],["resentment","freq:resentment"],["fiery","freq:fiery"],["gracious","freq:gracious"],["humility","freq:humility"],["marital","freq:marital"],["restless","freq:restless"],["eccentric","freq:eccentric"],["cynical","freq:cynical"],["cerebral","freq:cerebral"],["authenticity","freq:authenticity"]],"qs":[{"q":"What might surprise readers of Marsh's letters?","o":["She disliked writing essays.","She was kind and modest in private.","She had never worked as a teacher.","She admired all her rivals."],"a":1,"why":"In private she was gracious, and she wrote about her own failures with real humility（私生活では親切で、謙虚に自分の失敗を書いた）とある。"},{"q":"What does the biographer argue about her best work?","o":["It was written by someone else.","It is too cold to be enjoyed.","It should not be compared with her essays.","It has a genuine emotional quality that critics overlooked."],"a":3,"why":"has an emotional authenticity that her critics missed（批評家が見落とした感情の真実味がある）と論じている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0041","page":"freq","group":"501〜600","kind":"祭りの紹介","text":"Every August, the hill town of Montira holds a festival that dates from circa 1300. It begins quietly, with a sermon in the old church, and builds over three days to a noisy climax in the main square. On the second evening comes the procession for which the town is famous. Hundreds of men and women, clad in white robes, climb the hill by candlelight while a low chant passes along the line. Each group carries a wooden emblem that represents its street. Visitors often remark on the resemblance to ceremonies in other countries, and the scene is indeed reminiscent of older traditions around the Mediterranean. Historians believe there is a kernel of truth in the legend that the festival began as thanks for the end of a plague. A small exposition in the town hall explains its history, though the displays are not very intuitive and few signs are in English. The festival is immensely popular. Last year a multitude of forty thousand people filled a town built for five thousand, which is indicative of a growing problem. The mayor is now considering a limit on numbers.","ja":"毎年8月、丘の町モンティラは1300年頃にさかのぼる祭りを開く。祭りは古い教会での説教で静かに始まり、3日かけて中央広場でのにぎやかな最高潮へと盛り上がる。2日目の晩には、この町を有名にしている行列がある。白い衣をまとった数百人の男女がろうそくの明かりで丘を登り、低い詠唱が列を伝わっていく。各集団は自分たちの通りを表す木の紋章を掲げている。訪問者はよくほかの国の儀式に似ていると言い、実際その光景は地中海周辺の古い伝統を思わせる。歴史家たちは、この祭りが疫病の終息への感謝として始まったという伝説には一片の真実があると考えている。町役場の小さな展示がその歴史を説明しているが、展示はあまり直感的にわかるものではなく、英語の表示もほとんどない。この祭りは非常に人気がある。昨年は4万人もの群衆が5,000人向けに造られた町を埋めた。これは問題が大きくなっていることを示している。町長は今、人数の制限を検討している。","words":[["circa","freq:circa"],["sermon","freq:sermon"],["climax","freq:climax"],["procession","freq:procession"],["clad","freq:clad"],["chant","freq:chant"],["emblem","freq:emblem"],["resemblance","freq:resemblance"],["reminiscent","freq:reminiscent"],["kernel","freq:kernel"],["exposition","freq:exposition"],["intuitive","freq:intuitive"],["immensely","freq:immensely"],["multitude","freq:multitude"],["indicative","freq:indicative"]],"qs":[{"q":"What do historians think about the legend of the festival's origin?","o":["It was invented for tourists.","It is completely false.","It probably contains some truth.","It came from another country."],"a":2,"why":"there is a kernel of truth in the legend（その伝説には一片の真実がある）と考えている。"},{"q":"Why is the mayor considering a limit on numbers?","o":["Far more people come than the town was built to hold.","The church can no longer be used.","Few visitors understand the displays.","The procession has become too long."],"a":0,"why":"forty thousand people filled a town built for five thousand（5,000人向けの町を4万人が埋めた）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0042","page":"freq","group":"501〜600","kind":"若い選手の育成","text":"Coaches who work with a talented adolescent face a difficult choice. Push too hard, and the young athlete may be injured or lose interest; push too little, and a possible contender for national honors may never develop. Dr. Paolo Renzi, who has studied junior swimmers for fifteen years, believes the answer lies in treating training as elastic rather than fixed. In his program, every plan is adjustable week by week. Coaches record sleep, mood, and calorie intake, and reduce the workload at the first sign of trouble. Renzi pays the utmost attention to mental health. Stress, he says, is contagious in a team: one anxious swimmer can affect the rest. He therefore measures resilience as carefully as speed, using short interviews after each defeat. His methods were doubted at first, since his swimmers trained fewer hours than their rivals. The results have changed many minds. Over ten years, the trajectory of his athletes has been steadier, with fewer injuries and longer careers. The evidence is still slender, Renzi admits, because his groups are small. But several national teams have begun to copy his approach.","ja":"才能のある思春期の若者を指導するコーチは、難しい選択に直面する。強く追い込みすぎれば、若い選手はけがをしたり興味を失ったりするかもしれない。追い込みが足りなければ、全国的な栄誉をねらえる有力候補が育たないかもしれない。15年にわたって年少の水泳選手を研究してきたパオロ・レンツィ博士は、答えは練習を固定したものではなく伸び縮みするものとして扱うことにあると考えている。彼の計画では、あらゆる予定が週ごとに調節できる。コーチは睡眠、気分、カロリーの摂取量を記録し、問題の最初の兆候が出たら練習量を減らす。レンツィは心の健康に最大限の注意を払う。ストレスはチームの中で伝染すると彼は言う。不安を抱えた一人の選手がほかの選手に影響しうるのだ。そこで彼は、敗戦のたびに短い面談を行い、立ち直る力を速さと同じくらい注意深く測っている。彼の選手は競争相手より練習時間が少なかったため、彼の方法は当初疑われた。結果は多くの人の考えを変えた。10年にわたり、彼の選手たちの歩みはより安定しており、けがが少なく、選手生命も長い。自分の集団は小さいので証拠はまだわずかだと、レンツィは認める。しかし、いくつかの代表チームが彼のやり方をまねし始めている。","words":[["adolescent","freq:adolescent"],["contender","freq:contender"],["elastic","freq:elastic"],["adjustable","freq:adjustable"],["calorie","freq:calorie"],["utmost","freq:utmost"],["contagious","freq:contagious"],["resilience","freq:resilience"],["trajectory","freq:trajectory"],["slender","freq:slender"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is the main idea of Renzi's program?","o":["Training plans should change according to the athlete's condition.","Young swimmers should train more hours than their rivals.","Coaches should ignore mood and sleep.","Only the fastest swimmers should be selected."],"a":0,"why":"treating training as elastic rather than fixed とあり、every plan is adjustable week by week（予定は週ごとに調節できる）と説明している。"},{"q":"Why does Renzi say the evidence is still limited?","o":["His swimmers have had many injuries.","His groups of athletes are small.","National teams have rejected his ideas.","He has studied swimmers for only one year."],"a":1,"why":"The evidence is still slender ... because his groups are small（集団が小さいので証拠はまだわずか）と認めている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0043","page":"freq","group":"601〜700","kind":"戦史の再検討","text":"The battle of Kessel Ridge has long been remembered as the day a small rebel force defeated a much larger army. A new history, based on letters found in a village attic, tells a less heroic story. According to the traditional account, the rebels won by stealth. They crept through the forest at night, set an ambush on the mountain road, and emerged victorious by dawn. The letters, written by a soldier to his sister, suggest that the famous maneuver was partly an accident. The vanguard lost its way amidst heavy fog, and the main group arrived two hours late. What saved the rebels was a storm that began to batter the enemy camp just as the fighting started. The writer also describes the days that followed. A comrade who had been slain was buried without ceremony, and prisoners were shot in retaliation for an earlier massacre. He himself became a fugitive, hiding his whereabouts even from his family. Most striking is his bitterness. He had come to believe that ordinary soldiers were only a pawn in a covert struggle between rival commanders, and that the real menace to the cause came from within.","ja":"ケッセル尾根の戦いは長い間、小さな反乱軍がはるかに大きな軍を破った日として記憶されてきた。ある村の屋根裏で見つかった手紙に基づく新しい歴史書は、それほど英雄的ではない物語を語る。従来の説明によれば、反乱軍は隠密行動によって勝った。夜に森の中を忍び足で進み、山道に待ち伏せを仕掛け、夜明けまでに勝利を収めたという。ある兵士が姉に宛てて書いた手紙は、その有名な作戦行動が部分的には偶然だったことを示唆している。先頭部隊は濃い霧のさなかに道に迷い、本隊は2時間遅れて到着した。反乱軍を救ったのは、戦闘が始まったちょうどそのとき敵の野営地を打ちのめし始めた嵐だった。書き手はその後の日々も描いている。戦死した戦友は儀式もなく埋葬され、捕虜は以前の虐殺への報復として射殺された。彼自身は逃亡者となり、家族にさえ居場所を隠した。最も印象的なのは彼の苦々しさだ。彼は、普通の兵士は対立する司令官たちの間のひそかな争いの手先にすぎず、大義にとっての本当の脅威は内部から来ると信じるようになっていた。","words":[["stealth","freq:stealth"],["ambush","freq:ambush"],["victorious","freq:victorious"],["maneuver","freq:maneuver"],["vanguard","freq:vanguard"],["amidst","freq:amidst"],["batter","freq:batter"],["comrade","freq:comrade"],["slain","freq:slain"],["retaliation","freq:retaliation"],["fugitive","freq:fugitive"],["whereabouts","freq:whereabouts"],["pawn","freq:pawn"],["covert","freq:covert"],["menace","freq:menace"]],"qs":[{"q":"According to the letters, what really helped the rebels win?","o":["Their careful planning","The arrival of extra troops","A storm that hit the enemy camp","A secret agreement between commanders"],"a":2,"why":"What saved the rebels was a storm that began to batter the enemy camp（敵の野営地を襲った嵐が反乱軍を救った）とある。"},{"q":"What had the letter writer come to believe?","o":["The enemy would return within a year.","Ordinary soldiers were being used by rival commanders.","His family had betrayed him.","The battle had never taken place."],"a":1,"why":"ordinary soldiers were only a pawn in a covert struggle between rival commanders（兵士は司令官同士のひそかな争いの手先にすぎない）と信じるようになった。"}]},
{"id":"f-0044","page":"freq","group":"601〜700","kind":"刑務所をめぐる訴訟","text":"A court in the capital will rule next month on a case that could change the way prisons are run. The plaintiff is a former inmate of Greyfield Prison, who claims that guards failed to protect him during a brawl in which he was badly injured. His lawyers argue that every prisoner has an entitlement to basic safety, and that the prison's restrictive rules on reporting violence amount to an obstruction of justice. The prison denies any misuse of its powers. It says that the man had signed a waiver on arrival, and that staff acted pursuant to official guidelines. It also points out that he was later found to have a stash of forbidden phones in his cell. The case has already produced surprises. A letter said to prove that guards had been warned turned out to be a hoax. Then the judge decided to summon the prison governor for interrogation about missing video recordings. He has asked both sides for written clarification of the rules by Friday. Whatever the outcome, lawyers expect it to affect every convict in the country, since the same guidelines apply in all state prisons.","ja":"首都の裁判所は来月、刑務所の運営方法を変えうる訴訟について判断を下す。原告はグレイフィールド刑務所の元受刑者で、自分が重傷を負った乱闘の際に看守が自分を守らなかったと主張している。彼の弁護士たちは、すべての受刑者には基本的な安全を求める権利があり、暴力の報告についての刑務所の制限的な規則は司法妨害に当たると論じる。刑務所側は権限の乱用を一切否定している。男性は入所時に権利放棄書に署名しており、職員は公式の指針に従って行動したと言う。また、彼の房から後に禁止された電話の隠し場所が見つかったことも指摘している。この訴訟はすでに意外な展開を見せている。看守が事前に警告を受けていたことを証明するとされた手紙は、でっち上げだと判明した。次に裁判官は、消えた映像記録について尋問するために刑務所長を呼び出すことを決めた。彼は双方に対し、金曜までに規則についての書面による説明を求めている。結果がどうであれ、同じ指針がすべての州立刑務所に適用されているため、国内のあらゆる受刑者に影響すると弁護士たちは見ている。","words":[["plaintiff","freq:plaintiff"],["inmate","freq:inmate"],["brawl","freq:brawl"],["entitlement","freq:entitlement"],["restrictive","freq:restrictive"],["obstruction","freq:obstruction"],["misuse","freq:misuse"],["waiver","freq:waiver"],["pursuant","freq:pursuant"],["stash","freq:stash"],["hoax","freq:hoax"],["summon","freq:summon"],["interrogation","freq:interrogation"],["clarification","freq:clarification"],["convict","freq:convict"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does the former prisoner claim?","o":["He was wrongly convicted.","Guards did not protect him during a fight.","His phones were stolen by staff.","He was forced to sign a false statement."],"a":1,"why":"guards failed to protect him during a brawl in which he was badly injured（乱闘の際に看守が守らなかった）と主張している。"},{"q":"Why might the ruling affect prisoners across the country?","o":["The same rules are used in every state prison.","The governor runs several prisons.","The plaintiff plans to sue other prisons.","A new law will be passed next month."],"a":0,"why":"the same guidelines apply in all state prisons（同じ指針がすべての州立刑務所に適用されている）ためである。"}]},
{"id":"f-0045","page":"freq","group":"601〜700","kind":"長寿の研究","text":"Why do some people stay healthy into their nineties while others decline in their sixties? A team at the Halden Institute has spent twenty years looking for an answer. The researchers followed three thousand adults, measuring every physiological change they could, from blood pressure to the strength of the hand. Their intuition was that longevity depends on a few major factors. The data told a more complicated story. Health in old age turned out to rest on many discrete habits that work in tandem. Moderate exercise and regular sleep were complementary: each doubled the benefit of the other. Too much exercise, surprisingly, was detrimental, and a small subset of very active participants suffered early impairment of the joints. The team also found chemical residue from certain medicines in the blood of people who reported frequent nausea, which suggests that some common drugs are being taken at doses far above the optimum. Other laboratories have so far been able to replicate most of these findings. The institute's director describes the results as modest rather than seismic. There is no secret, she says, written in the celestial bodies or in our genes; there are only ordinary choices, repeated daily.","ja":"なぜ90代まで健康でいる人もいれば、60代で衰える人もいるのか。ハルデン研究所のチームは20年をかけて答えを探してきた。研究者たちは成人3,000人を追跡し、血圧から握力まで、測れる限りの生理的な変化を測った。彼らの直感は、長寿は少数の大きな要因で決まるというものだった。データはもっと複雑な物語を語った。老年期の健康は、連動して働く多くの個別の習慣に支えられていることがわかったのだ。適度な運動と規則正しい睡眠は互いに補い合い、それぞれが他方の効果を倍にした。意外なことに、運動のしすぎは有害で、非常に活動的な参加者のごく一部は早い時期に関節の機能が低下した。チームはまた、頻繁な吐き気を訴えた人の血液から特定の薬の化学的な残留物を見つけた。これは、一部のありふれた薬が最適量をはるかに超える量で服用されていることを示唆している。これまでのところ、ほかの研究室もこれらの発見の大半を再現できている。研究所の所長は、この結果を激震というより控えめなものだと表現する。天体にも私たちの遺伝子にも秘密は書かれておらず、あるのは日々繰り返される普通の選択だけだと彼女は言う。","words":[["physiological","freq:physiological"],["intuition","freq:intuition"],["longevity","freq:longevity"],["discrete","freq:discrete"],["tandem","freq:tandem"],["complementary","freq:complementary"],["detrimental","freq:detrimental"],["subset","freq:subset"],["impairment","freq:impairment"],["residue","freq:residue"],["nausea","freq:nausea"],["optimum","freq:optimum"],["replicate","freq:replicate"],["seismic","freq:seismic"],["celestial","freq:celestial"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the data show about health in old age?","o":["It depends on one or two major factors.","It is decided mainly by genes.","It rests on many separate habits working together.","It cannot be measured."],"a":2,"why":"rest on many discrete habits that work in tandem（連動して働く多くの個別の習慣に支えられる）とある。"},{"q":"What surprised the researchers about exercise?","o":["Too much of it caused harm.","It had no effect on sleep.","Older people exercised more than the young.","It reduced the effect of medicines."],"a":0,"why":"Too much exercise, surprisingly, was detrimental（意外にも運動のしすぎは有害だった）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0046","page":"freq","group":"601〜700","kind":"映画評","text":"The Lighthouse Keeper's Daughter is the kind of film that critics usually cringe at: a sentimental story about a lonely girl, a lost dog, and a storm. That it works so well is a tribute to its screenplay, which has real flair. The heroine, Ida, is no sweet child. She is stubborn, quirky, and at times unkind, and the young actress who plays her gives a portrayal of great honesty. The characterization of the father is equally careful. In the first half he seems a superficial figure, a man who makes vulgar jokes to hide his grief. By the end, a single act of courage is enough to redeem him. The director builds suspense slowly, using the sound of the sea rather than music. Some viewers will find the ending too upbeat, and it is true that the last ten minutes tie up every problem a little too neatly. Even so, the film deserves the praise it has begun to garner at festivals. The curator of this year's national film week has chosen it to open the program, and it is easy to see why.","ja":"「灯台守の娘」は、批評家がたいてい身をすくめる類の映画だ。孤独な少女と迷い犬と嵐の、感傷的な物語である。それがこれほどうまくいっているのは、本物のセンスを持つ脚本のおかげだ。主人公のイーダは、かわいらしい子どもではない。頑固で、一風変わっていて、時に不親切でもあり、彼女を演じる若い女優は非常に正直な演技を見せる。父親の人物描写も同じように丁寧だ。前半の彼は表面的な人物に見える。悲しみを隠すために下品な冗談を言う男だ。終わりまでに、たった一度の勇気ある行動が彼の名誉を回復させるのに十分となる。監督は音楽ではなく海の音を使って、ゆっくりと緊張感を高めていく。結末が明るすぎると感じる観客もいるだろう。確かに最後の10分は、あらゆる問題を少しきれいに片づけすぎている。それでも、この映画は映画祭で集め始めた称賛に値する。今年の全国映画週間の企画責任者は、この作品を開幕作品に選んだ。その理由は容易にわかる。","words":[["cringe","freq:cringe"],["sentimental","freq:sentimental"],["screenplay","freq:screenplay"],["flair","freq:flair"],["heroine","freq:heroine"],["quirky","freq:quirky"],["portrayal","freq:portrayal"],["characterization","freq:characterization"],["superficial","freq:superficial"],["vulgar","freq:vulgar"],["redeem","freq:redeem"],["suspense","freq:suspense"],["upbeat","freq:upbeat"],["garner","freq:garner"],["curator","freq:curator"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does the reviewer say makes the film succeed?","o":["Its famous cast","Its expensive special effects","Its well-written script","Its use of music"],"a":2,"why":"That it works so well is a tribute to its screenplay（うまくいっているのは脚本のおかげ）とある。"},{"q":"What criticism does the reviewer make?","o":["The heroine is too sweet.","The father never changes.","The sea is too noisy.","The ending solves everything too neatly."],"a":3,"why":"the last ten minutes tie up every problem a little too neatly（最後の10分は問題をきれいに片づけすぎ）と述べている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0047","page":"freq","group":"601〜700","kind":"民俗の記録","text":"In the 1890s, the eminent scholar Edmund Hale spent six summers traveling with a gypsy community in the mountains of the north. His aim was to record its lore before it disappeared. The task was not easy. The people spoke a dialect that no outsider had written down, and many of their beliefs were kept from strangers. Hale learned that they honored a pagan deity of the forest alongside the Christian saints, and that a small sect among them still held a ceremony at midsummer. It was customary for the women to decorate the wagons with intricate patterns pressed into wet plaster, each one telling the story of a family. Hale's notebooks describe all this in a careful, sometimes repetitive style. He treated no detail as insignificant. When his book appeared, members of the academic fraternity dismissed it as a romantic endeavor with little scientific value. Its reputation has since grown. Folk traditions did not flourish in the century that followed, and for several of the customs he observed, his notes are now the only record that survives. A new edition, with his original photographs, was published last year.","ja":"1890年代、著名な学者エドマンド・ヘイルは、6度の夏を北部の山中で放浪の民の共同体とともに旅して過ごした。彼の目的は、その伝承が消える前に記録することだった。その仕事は簡単ではなかった。人々は外部の者が書き留めたことのない方言を話し、その信仰の多くはよそ者には隠されていた。ヘイルは、彼らがキリスト教の聖人と並んで森の異教の神をあがめていること、そして彼らの中の小さな宗派が今も夏至に儀式を行っていることを知った。女性たちが、ぬれたしっくいに押しつけた複雑な模様で荷馬車を飾るのが習わしで、模様の一つひとつが家族の物語を語っていた。ヘイルのノートは、これらすべてを注意深い、時に繰り返しの多い文体で記している。彼はどんな細部も取るに足りないものとは扱わなかった。彼の本が出たとき、学者仲間はそれを科学的価値の乏しい、ロマンチックな試みとして退けた。その評価はその後高まった。続く1世紀の間、民俗の伝統は栄えず、彼が観察した習慣のいくつかについては、彼の記録が今では唯一残る資料である。彼の撮った写真を収めた新版が昨年出版された。","words":[["eminent","freq:eminent"],["gypsy","freq:gypsy"],["lore","freq:lore"],["dialect","freq:dialect"],["pagan","freq:pagan"],["deity","freq:deity"],["sect","freq:sect"],["customary","freq:customary"],["intricate","freq:intricate"],["plaster","freq:plaster"],["repetitive","freq:repetitive"],["insignificant","freq:insignificant"],["fraternity","freq:fraternity"],["endeavor","freq:endeavor"],["flourish","freq:flourish"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why was Hale's task difficult?","o":["He could not afford to travel.","The community's language was unrecorded and its beliefs were hidden.","The mountains were closed in summer.","His notebooks were lost."],"a":1,"why":"a dialect that no outsider had written down であり、beliefs were kept from strangers（信仰はよそ者に隠されていた）とある。"},{"q":"Why is Hale's book valued today?","o":["It is the only surviving record of some customs.","It proved the customs were Christian.","It was praised by scholars when it appeared.","It is written in a lively style."],"a":0,"why":"for several of the customs he observed, his notes are now the only record that survives（いくつかの習慣については唯一残る記録）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0048","page":"freq","group":"601〜700","kind":"港町の経済","text":"Ten years ago, the port of Brenmar seemed finished. Fishing, the backbone of its economy, had been hit by a strict quota on catches, and unemployment was rampant. Raw sewage and runoff from the streets flowed into the harbor, and the waterfront was lined with empty shops. The reversal of the town's fortunes began with a decision that many thought foolish. Instead of asking for another subsidy for the fishing fleet, the council spent its money on a marine college. The rationale was simple: the sea would remain, even if the fish did not. The college won accreditation within three years and now offers tertiary courses in boat design and ocean science. Its graduates, savvy about both technology and the sea, have started more than sixty small firms. The town is beginning to reap the rewards. House prices have started to rebound, and the harbor has been cleaned. Not all the changes are welcome. Wealthy visitors arrive each summer on a spending spree that pushes up prices for everyone else. Still, few residents would return to the way things were.","ja":"10年前、ブレンマーの港はもう終わりに見えた。経済の柱だった漁業は漁獲量への厳しい割り当てに打撃を受け、失業がまん延していた。未処理の下水と通りからの流出水が港に流れ込み、海岸通りには空き店舗が並んでいた。町の運命の逆転は、多くの人が愚かだと考えた決定から始まった。町議会は漁船団へのさらなる補助金を求める代わりに、資金を海洋大学に使ったのだ。その理由は単純だった。魚がいなくなっても、海は残るというものだ。大学は3年以内に認定を得て、今では船の設計と海洋科学の高等教育課程を提供している。技術にも海にも精通した卒業生たちは、60を超える小さな会社を起こした。町はその成果を得始めている。住宅価格は回復し始め、港はきれいになった。すべての変化が歓迎されているわけではない。裕福な訪問客が毎年夏にやって来て散財し、ほかの皆にとっての物価を押し上げている。それでも、以前の状態に戻りたいと思う住民はほとんどいないだろう。","words":[["backbone","freq:backbone"],["quota","freq:quota"],["rampant","freq:rampant"],["sewage","freq:sewage"],["runoff","freq:runoff"],["waterfront","freq:waterfront"],["reversal","freq:reversal"],["subsidy","freq:subsidy"],["rationale","freq:rationale"],["accreditation","freq:accreditation"],["tertiary","freq:tertiary"],["savvy","freq:savvy"],["reap","freq:reap"],["rebound","freq:rebound"],["spree","freq:spree"]],"qs":[{"q":"What unusual decision did the council make?","o":["It bought new fishing boats.","It closed the harbor to visitors.","It lowered taxes on shops.","It invested in a college instead of supporting the fleet."],"a":3,"why":"Instead of asking for another subsidy for the fishing fleet, the council spent its money on a marine college（補助金を求める代わりに海洋大学に資金を使った）とある。"},{"q":"What downside of the town's recovery is mentioned?","o":["The college has lost its accreditation.","Summer visitors drive up prices.","The harbor is dirtier than before.","Graduates leave the town."],"a":1,"why":"Wealthy visitors arrive each summer on a spending spree that pushes up prices（裕福な訪問客の散財が物価を押し上げる）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0049","page":"freq","group":"601〜700","kind":"水族館の論争","text":"The city aquarium has announced that it will stop keeping dolphins, ending a debate that had lasted for years. The decision was not unanimous. Some staff argued that animals born in captivity could not survive in the open sea, and they were wary of making promises that might prove impossible to keep. But the director said there was a growing sense of urgency. Visitor numbers had fallen, and a recent incident, in which a dolphin tried to snatch a camera from a child, had been widely reported. In an interview, she admitted that the aquarium had been slow to listen to dissent. \"There was a certain arrogance in our position,\" she said. \"We assumed that we knew best.\" The four dolphins will be moved to a sheltered bay, where they can swim freely but will still be fed. The downside is cost: the transfer will take most of this year's budget. Many regular visitors say they feel numb at the news. One elderly man, who used to stroll past the dolphin pool every morning, said the place would not be the same without them.","ja":"市の水族館は、イルカの飼育をやめると発表し、何年も続いた論争に終止符を打った。決定は全員一致ではなかった。飼育下で生まれた動物は外洋では生きられないと主張する職員もおり、彼らは守れないかもしれない約束をすることを警戒していた。しかし館長は、切迫感が高まっていると述べた。来館者数は減っており、イルカが子どもからカメラをひったくろうとした最近の出来事は広く報じられていた。取材に対し、彼女は水族館が異論に耳を傾けるのが遅かったと認めた。「私たちの立場にはある種の傲慢さがありました」と彼女は言った。「自分たちが一番よくわかっていると思い込んでいたのです」。4頭のイルカは、自由に泳げるが餌は与えられる、囲われた湾に移される。欠点は費用で、移送には今年の予算の大半がかかる。多くの常連客は、その知らせにぼう然としていると言う。毎朝イルカのプールのそばを散歩していたある高齢の男性は、イルカがいなければこの場所は同じではなくなるだろうと語った。","words":[["aquarium","freq:aquarium"],["captivity","freq:captivity"],["wary","freq:wary"],["urgency","freq:urgency"],["snatch","freq:snatch"],["dissent","freq:dissent"],["arrogance","freq:arrogance"],["downside","freq:downside"],["numb","freq:numb"],["stroll","freq:stroll"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why were some staff against the decision?","o":["They thought visitor numbers would rise.","They doubted that dolphins raised by people could live in the open sea.","They wanted to buy more dolphins.","They disliked the director."],"a":1,"why":"animals born in captivity could not survive in the open sea（飼育下で生まれた動物は外洋で生きられない）と主張した。"},{"q":"What will happen to the dolphins?","o":["They will be sold to another aquarium.","They will be released far out at sea.","They will be moved to a protected bay and still be fed.","They will stay in the pool until they die."],"a":2,"why":"moved to a sheltered bay, where they can swim freely but will still be fed（囲われた湾に移され、餌は与えられる）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0050","page":"freq","group":"701〜800","kind":"停戦交渉","text":"After six years of civil war, the two sides in Tarvia have agreed to a truce. The agreement, reached through the mediation of a neighboring state, is being described as a watershed. Only a month ago the country seemed on the brink of total anarchy. Rebel forces had threatened to annex the eastern provinces, and the government's bombing of a market town had drawn condemnation from around the world. Neither side could win, and neither would accept an unconditional surrender. What changed was pressure from below. Village councils, which a new law had helped to empower, began refusing to supply either army with food. Faced with hunger in their own ranks, the commanders chose to talk rather than risk a final showdown. The terms are modest. Both sides promise to uphold the ceasefire for ninety days and to discuss the abolition of the special military courts. Observers warn that a single incident could provoke new fighting and put the whole process in jeopardy. It will take far longer, they say, to reconcile communities that have spent years burying their dead.","ja":"6年の内戦の末、タルビアの両陣営は停戦に合意した。隣国の調停によって達したこの合意は、分岐点と評されている。わずか1か月前、この国は完全な無政府状態の瀬戸際にあるように見えた。反政府勢力は東部の諸州を併合すると脅し、政府による市場町への爆撃は世界中の非難を招いていた。どちらの側も勝つことができず、どちらも無条件降伏を受け入れようとしなかった。変わったのは下からの圧力だった。新しい法律によって力を得た村の評議会が、どちらの軍にも食料を供給することを拒み始めたのだ。自軍の飢えに直面して、司令官たちは最終的な対決の危険を冒すよりも話し合うことを選んだ。条件は控えめだ。双方は90日間停戦を守り、特別軍事法廷の廃止について話し合うと約束している。観察者たちは、たった一つの出来事が新たな戦闘を引き起こし、過程全体を危険にさらしかねないと警告する。何年も死者を葬り続けてきた共同体を和解させるには、はるかに長い時間がかかるだろうと彼らは言う。","words":[["truce","freq:truce"],["mediation","freq:mediation"],["watershed","freq:watershed"],["brink","freq:brink"],["anarchy","freq:anarchy"],["annex","freq:annex"],["condemnation","freq:condemnation"],["unconditional","freq:unconditional"],["empower","freq:empower"],["showdown","freq:showdown"],["uphold","freq:uphold"],["abolition","freq:abolition"],["provoke","freq:provoke"],["jeopardy","freq:jeopardy"],["reconcile","freq:reconcile"]],"qs":[{"q":"What finally pushed the commanders to negotiate?","o":["A threat from a neighboring state","Village councils stopped giving food to both armies.","The rebels captured the capital.","The military courts were abolished."],"a":1,"why":"Village councils ... began refusing to supply either army with food（村の評議会がどちらの軍にも食料を出すのを拒んだ）とある。"},{"q":"What do observers warn about?","o":["The truce may last too long.","The neighboring state may invade.","The councils may lose their powers.","One incident could restart the fighting."],"a":3,"why":"a single incident could provoke new fighting（一つの出来事が新たな戦闘を引き起こしかねない）と警告している。"}]},
{"id":"f-0051","page":"freq","group":"701〜800","kind":"薬と依存","text":"When the painkiller Lorvex was introduced, doctors welcomed it as a way to alleviate suffering after surgery. Early studies described it as benign. Within five years, the picture had changed. Patients who took the drug for more than a few weeks often developed a dependency, and some increased the dosage without telling their doctors. The effects were relentless. People reported a rush of adrenaline followed by deep exhaustion, and a few became delusional, convinced that a parasite was living under their skin. One woman told researchers that she had feared for her sanity. Why some patients were affected and others were not remains unclear. A study now under way suggests that the risk may be partly hereditary, linked to a dysfunction in the way the liver breaks down the drug. Recovery is possible but slow. Even after treatment, many patients experience residual anxiety, and a single lapse can lead to a regression that takes months to reverse. The most resilient patients, the study found, were those with strong family support. The manufacturer has now agreed to print clearer warnings, though critics say the change comes years too late.","ja":"鎮痛剤ロルベックスが発売されたとき、医師たちは手術後の苦痛を和らげる手段として歓迎した。初期の研究はそれを害のないものと説明していた。5年のうちに状況は変わった。この薬を数週間を超えて服用した患者はしばしば依存に陥り、中には医師に告げずに服用量を増やす人もいた。その影響は容赦がなかった。人々はアドレナリンが駆けめぐった後に深い疲労が来ると報告し、数人は妄想にとらわれて、寄生虫が皮膚の下にすんでいると信じ込んだ。ある女性は研究者に、自分の正気が心配だったと語った。なぜ影響を受ける患者と受けない患者がいるのかは、まだはっきりしない。現在進行中の研究は、その危険が部分的には遺伝性で、肝臓がこの薬を分解する仕組みの機能不全に関係しているかもしれないと示唆している。回復は可能だが遅い。治療の後でさえ、多くの患者は不安が残り、たった一度の過ちが、元に戻すのに何か月もかかる後戻りを招くことがある。研究によれば、最も立ち直りが早かったのは、家族の強い支えがある患者だった。製薬会社は今回、より明確な警告を印刷することに同意したが、批判する人々は、その変更は何年も遅すぎたと言う。","words":[["alleviate","freq:alleviate"],["benign","freq:benign"],["dependency","freq:dependency"],["dosage","freq:dosage"],["relentless","freq:relentless"],["adrenaline","freq:adrenaline"],["delusional","freq:delusional"],["parasite","freq:parasite"],["sanity","freq:sanity"],["hereditary","freq:hereditary"],["dysfunction","freq:dysfunction"],["residual","freq:residual"],["lapse","freq:lapse"],["regression","freq:regression"],["resilient","freq:resilient"]],"qs":[{"q":"What problem appeared after the drug had been in use for some years?","o":["It stopped relieving pain.","Patients became dependent on it.","It became too expensive.","Doctors refused to prescribe it."],"a":1,"why":"Patients who took the drug for more than a few weeks often developed a dependency（数週間を超えて服用した患者はしばしば依存に陥った）とある。"},{"q":"Which patients recovered best?","o":["Those who had strong support from their families","Those who took a higher dosage","Those who stopped seeing their doctors","Those with no history of surgery"],"a":0,"why":"The most resilient patients ... were those with strong family support（家族の強い支えがある患者が最も立ち直りが早かった）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0052","page":"freq","group":"701〜800","kind":"山の湖の自然","text":"Lake Orrin, high in the northern range, is reached only by canoe. There is no road, and in winter the risk of an avalanche closes the valley completely. Those who make the journey find a place of unusual beauty. The lake fills an almost circular crater, and from the water its walls rise with striking symmetry. Dark, angular rocks line the western shore, while to the east a single slab of pale stone slopes gently into the water, radiant in the morning sun. Scientists come to study the fauna. A myriad of insects live in the forest canopy, and several species of fish are found nowhere else. The lake also contains a submerged forest, drowned when the water level rose centuries ago; its perennial odor of rotting wood is the first thing visitors notice when they step ashore. Astronomers have their own reason for coming. The air is so clear that an astronomical station was built here in 1998 to measure tiny gravitational effects on starlight. Its staff share the valley with bears, and seem to regard the arrangement as fair.","ja":"北の山脈の高所にあるオリン湖には、カヌーでしか行けない。道路はなく、冬には雪崩の危険で谷は完全に閉ざされる。この旅をする人々は、並外れた美しさを持つ場所に出合う。湖はほぼ円形の火口を満たし、水面から見るとその壁は見事な対称をなしてそびえている。西岸には黒く角ばった岩が並び、東側には一枚の淡い色の石の厚板が水の中へなだらかに傾き、朝日に輝いている。科学者たちは動物相を研究しにやって来る。森の林冠には無数の昆虫がすみ、ほかのどこにもいない魚が数種いる。湖には、何世紀も前に水位が上がったときに沈んだ水中の森もある。その一年中漂う朽ち木の臭いは、訪問者が岸に上がって最初に気づくものだ。天文学者にも来る理由がある。空気が非常に澄んでいるので、星の光へのわずかな重力の影響を測るために、1998年にここに天文観測所が建てられた。その職員たちは谷をクマと分け合っており、その取り決めを公平だと考えているようだ。","words":[["canoe","freq:canoe"],["avalanche","freq:avalanche"],["symmetry","freq:symmetry"],["angular","freq:angular"],["slab","freq:slab"],["radiant","freq:radiant"],["fauna","freq:fauna"],["myriad","freq:myriad"],["canopy","freq:canopy"],["submerged","freq:submerged"],["perennial","freq:perennial"],["odor","freq:odor"],["ashore","freq:ashore"],["astronomical","freq:astronomical"],["gravitational","freq:gravitational"]],"qs":[{"q":"How can visitors reach the lake?","o":["By road in summer","Only by boat","By a mountain railway","By helicopter in winter"],"a":1,"why":"is reached only by canoe（カヌーでしか行けない）とある。"},{"q":"Why was a station built at the lake in 1998?","o":["To protect the bears","To study the drowned forest","To make use of the very clear air for observing starlight","To warn of avalanches"],"a":2,"why":"The air is so clear that an astronomical station was built here（空気が非常に澄んでいるので天文観測所が建てられた）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0053","page":"freq","group":"701〜800","kind":"関税をめぐる汚職","text":"The trial of five customs officials opened yesterday with a deposition from a former clerk, who described how a criminal syndicate had paid them to ignore shipments of stolen goods. According to the prosecution, each official accepted a bribe of up to fifty thousand dollars a month. The loot, mostly electronics, was sold abroad, and the profits were hidden in speculative property deals. The sums involved are staggering. Investigators estimate that the state lost two hundred million dollars in unpaid tariff revenue over four years. The defense has raised a procedural objection, arguing that two concurrent investigations shared evidence illegally. It also calls the clerk a dubious witness, since he has been promised a lighter sentence. Economists say the case shows how corruption feeds on volatility. When prices plunge, as they did in 2019, honest importers lose their profitability and are tempted to cheat. A pragmatic solution, some suggest, would be to raise officials' salaries. One university has even used part of its endowment to fund a study of the idea. The government has not yet said whether it supports the proposal. The verdict is expected in March.","ja":"税関職員5人の裁判が昨日、元事務員の宣誓証言で始まった。彼は、ある犯罪組織が盗品の積み荷を見逃してもらうために職員たちに金を払っていた様子を述べた。検察によれば、各職員は月に最大5万ドルの賄賂を受け取っていた。略奪品は主に電子機器で、国外で売られ、利益は投機的な不動産取引に隠された。関わった金額は途方もない。捜査官は、国が4年間で2億ドルの関税収入を失ったと見積もっている。弁護側は手続き上の異議を唱え、並行して行われた2つの捜査が違法に証拠を共有したと主張している。また、元事務員は刑を軽くする約束を受けているのだから疑わしい証人だとも言う。経済学者たちは、この事件は汚職が変動の激しさにつけ込むことを示していると言う。2019年のように価格が急落すると、正直な輸入業者は収益性を失い、不正の誘惑にかられる。実際的な解決策は職員の給与を上げることだと提案する人もいる。ある大学は、この考えの研究に資金を出すために基金の一部を使いさえした。政府はこの提案を支持するかどうかをまだ明らかにしていない。評決は3月に出る見通しだ。","words":[["deposition","freq:deposition"],["syndicate","freq:syndicate"],["bribe","freq:bribe"],["loot","freq:loot"],["speculative","freq:speculative"],["staggering","freq:staggering"],["tariff","freq:tariff"],["procedural","freq:procedural"],["concurrent","freq:concurrent"],["dubious","freq:dubious"],["volatility","freq:volatility"],["plunge","freq:plunge"],["profitability","freq:profitability"],["pragmatic","freq:pragmatic"],["endowment","freq:endowment"]],"qs":[{"q":"What were the officials allegedly paid to do?","o":["To raise tariffs on electronics","To buy property abroad","To overlook stolen goods passing through customs","To give evidence against a clerk"],"a":2,"why":"paid them to ignore shipments of stolen goods（盗品の積み荷を見逃すよう金を払った）とある。"},{"q":"Why does the defense question the clerk's evidence?","o":["He has been offered a reduced punishment.","He was not working there at the time.","He belongs to the syndicate.","He refused to appear in court."],"a":0,"why":"he has been promised a lighter sentence（刑を軽くする約束を受けている）ので疑わしい証人だとしている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0054","page":"freq","group":"701〜800","kind":"巡礼の伝統","text":"Each spring, thousands of people make a pilgrimage on foot to the mountain shrine of Sant Elar. Many are descendants of the shepherds who first settled the valley, and they speak proudly of their ancestry. The journey takes four days. Pilgrims sleep in barns or at any homestead that will take them in, and they eat simple, wholesome food provided by the villages along the way. Local folklore says that the first pilgrims were a group of brethren fleeing a plague. On the final evening comes a ceremonial dance whose choreography has not changed for three hundred years. The dancers repeat a short mantra, and some appear to fall into a trance. For young people from the valley, taking part for the first time is a kind of initiation into adult life. A recently published anthology of pilgrims' writings shows how much the tradition means to them. Again and again, the writers speak of fidelity to the past and of the spiritual enrichment the walk brings. The organizers face a monumental task each year, yet none of them is paid, and none has ever asked to be.","ja":"毎年春、数千人が徒歩で山の聖地サン・エラルへ巡礼する。その多くは最初にこの谷に住みついた羊飼いたちの子孫で、自分たちの祖先について誇らしげに語る。旅は4日かかる。巡礼者は納屋や、泊めてくれる農家の家屋敷で眠り、道沿いの村が用意する質素で健康的な食事を取る。地元の民間伝承によれば、最初の巡礼者は疫病から逃れてきた信者仲間の一団だった。最後の晩には、振り付けが300年間変わっていない儀式の踊りがある。踊り手たちは短い言葉を繰り返し唱え、中には恍惚状態に陥るように見える人もいる。谷の若者にとって、初めて参加することは大人の生活への一種の入会儀礼だ。最近出版された巡礼者の文章の選集は、この伝統が彼らにとってどれほど大切かを示している。書き手たちは繰り返し、過去への忠実さと、この歩みがもたらす精神的な豊かさについて語る。主催者たちは毎年、途方もない仕事に直面するが、誰も報酬を受け取らず、誰も求めたことがない。","words":[["pilgrimage","freq:pilgrimage"],["ancestry","freq:ancestry"],["homestead","freq:homestead"],["wholesome","freq:wholesome"],["folklore","freq:folklore"],["brethren","freq:brethren"],["ceremonial","freq:ceremonial"],["choreography","freq:choreography"],["mantra","freq:mantra"],["trance","freq:trance"],["initiation","freq:initiation"],["anthology","freq:anthology"],["fidelity","freq:fidelity"],["enrichment","freq:enrichment"],["monumental","freq:monumental"]],"qs":[{"q":"According to local stories, who were the first pilgrims?","o":["Shepherds looking for new land","Believers escaping a plague","Dancers from another valley","Writers collecting tales"],"a":1,"why":"the first pilgrims were a group of brethren fleeing a plague（疫病から逃れてきた信者仲間の一団）とある。"},{"q":"What does taking part mean for young people from the valley?","o":["A way of entering adult life","A chance to earn money","A requirement for marriage","A test of their dancing"],"a":0,"why":"a kind of initiation into adult life（大人の生活への一種の入会儀礼）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0055","page":"freq","group":"701〜800","kind":"研究チームの転機","text":"For most of her career, Dr. Amara Okoye worked alone. An avid reader with a formidable intellect, she saw meetings as a tedious waste of time. A two-year hiatus caused by illness changed her mind. When she returned to the laboratory, she found that younger scientists were at the forefront of her field, and that their methods were unfamiliar to her. She decided to pivot. Instead of defending her old approach, she invited three of them to collaborate with her on a study of ocean currents. The partnership proved pivotal. Her colleagues were acquainted with new techniques for the utilization of satellite data, while she supplied the theory. Their first paper contained several noteworthy findings and was followed by a supplemental report that corrected errors in earlier work, including her own. Okoye is mindful that the experience has changed her. Her name was once synonymous with fierce independence, she says, and she had allowed that reputation to harden into a habit. She now advises students to seek out people who know what they do not. The lesson, she admits, came late, but she is glad that it came at all.","ja":"アマラ・オコエ博士は、経歴の大半を一人で研究してきた。熱心な読書家で、並外れた知性を持つ彼女は、会議を退屈な時間の無駄と見ていた。病気による2年間の休止が彼女の考えを変えた。研究室に戻ると、若い科学者たちが彼女の分野の最前線にいて、彼らの方法は自分になじみのないものだった。彼女は方向転換することにした。古いやり方を守るのではなく、海流の研究で協力するよう、そのうちの3人を誘ったのだ。この協力関係は極めて重要なものとなった。同僚たちは衛星データを活用する新しい技術に通じており、彼女は理論を提供した。彼らの最初の論文にはいくつかの注目に値する発見が含まれ、続いて、彼女自身のものを含む以前の研究の誤りを正す補足の報告が出された。オコエは、この経験が自分を変えたことを自覚している。かつて自分の名前は激しい独立心の代名詞であり、その評判が習慣として固まるのを許してしまっていたと彼女は言う。今では学生たちに、自分の知らないことを知っている人を探すよう勧めている。この教訓を得たのは遅かったが、得られただけでもよかったと彼女は認めている。","words":[["avid","freq:avid"],["intellect","freq:intellect"],["tedious","freq:tedious"],["hiatus","freq:hiatus"],["forefront","freq:forefront"],["pivot","freq:pivot"],["collaborate","freq:collaborate"],["pivotal","freq:pivotal"],["acquainted","freq:acquainted"],["utilization","freq:utilization"],["noteworthy","freq:noteworthy"],["supplemental","freq:supplemental"],["mindful","freq:mindful"],["synonymous","freq:synonymous"],["harden","freq:harden"]],"qs":[{"q":"What made Okoye change the way she worked?","o":["A long absence showed her that others had moved ahead.","Her laboratory was closed.","She was told to attend more meetings.","Her theory was proved wrong by a student."],"a":0,"why":"病気による hiatus の後、younger scientists were at the forefront（若い科学者が最前線にいた）と気づいたことがきっかけである。"},{"q":"What did the supplemental report do?","o":["It attacked the younger scientists.","It described new satellites.","It corrected mistakes in earlier studies, including hers.","It explained why she had been ill."],"a":2,"why":"corrected errors in earlier work, including her own（彼女自身のものを含む以前の研究の誤りを正した）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0056","page":"freq","group":"701〜800","kind":"鉄道博物館の夜","text":"The railway museum at Halby has an aura that modern attractions lack. Its main hall holds a great black locomotive from 1912, and in the adjoining workshop volunteers still repair old carriages by hand. Last month the museum was nearly lost. A fire in a storeroom wrought havoc before it was brought under control, and two men were later arrested. In court, the younger of the two gave a bland account of the night, as though describing a walk in the park. The older man could only shuffle his feet and stare at the floor. He showed real remorse, saying that he had not had the decency to warn the night guard, who escaped unhurt. The judge gave both men a quasi-suspended sentence that requires them to help rebuild the storeroom. The volunteers were not impressed. Many of them cling to the belief that the men were paid by a developer who wants the land. Whatever the truth, the museum will reopen in the spring, and the old engine, blackened but undamaged, will once again be the first thing visitors see.","ja":"ハルビーの鉄道博物館には、現代の娯楽施設にはない雰囲気がある。大広間には1912年製の大きな黒い機関車が置かれ、隣接した作業場ではボランティアが今も古い客車を手作業で修理している。先月、この博物館は危うく失われるところだった。物置での火事は消し止められるまでに大きな被害をもたらし、後に2人の男が逮捕された。法廷で、2人のうち若いほうは、公園の散歩でも説明するかのように、その夜のことを当たり障りなく語った。年上の男は足を引きずるように動かし、床を見つめることしかできなかった。彼は本物の後悔を見せ、夜警に警告するだけの良識もなかったと述べた。夜警は無傷で逃げていた。裁判官は2人に、物置の再建を手伝うことを求める、執行猶予に準じる刑を言い渡した。ボランティアたちは納得しなかった。彼らの多くは、男たちはこの土地を欲しがっている開発業者に金をもらっていたという考えに固執している。真相がどうであれ、博物館は春に再開する。そして黒ずんではいるが無傷の古い機関車が、再び来館者が最初に目にするものになる。","words":[["aura","freq:aura"],["locomotive","freq:locomotive"],["adjoining","freq:adjoining"],["havoc","freq:havoc"],["bland","freq:bland"],["shuffle","freq:shuffle"],["remorse","freq:remorse"],["decency","freq:decency"],["quasi","freq:quasi"],["cling","freq:cling"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did the two men differ in court?","o":["Only the older one seemed truly sorry.","Only the younger one admitted the crime.","Both blamed the night guard.","Both refused to speak."],"a":0,"why":"若いほうは a bland account を述べただけだが、年上の男は showed real remorse（本物の後悔を見せた）とある。"},{"q":"What do many volunteers believe?","o":["The fire was an accident.","The museum will never reopen.","The men were hired by someone who wants the land.","The locomotive was badly damaged."],"a":2,"why":"the men were paid by a developer who wants the land（土地を欲しがる開発業者に金をもらっていた）と信じている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0057","page":"freq","group":"801〜900","kind":"諜報事件","text":"The arrest of a man known by the alias \"Mr. Gray\" has caused serious discord between two governments that were until recently close allies. According to prosecutors, Gray spent ten years engaged in espionage while working as a translator for a trade envoy. He was privy to talks on energy policy and is said to have broken every rule of confidentiality by passing notes to a foreign power. His own country denies the charge and calls it a smear. In an act of open defiance, it has refused to hand over documents and has warned that the trial will not deter it from protecting its citizens abroad. Matters grew worse last week, when an extremist group claimed that it would kidnap diplomats unless Gray was freed, describing him as a martyr. Members of the diaspora community in the capital have appealed for calm, fearing attacks on their businesses. Officials on both sides say privately that the priority now is containment. Notwithstanding the angry speeches, neither government wants a dispute over one man to destroy an alliance that has lasted fifty years, and quiet talks are said to have begun.","ja":"「グレイ氏」という偽名で知られる男の逮捕は、最近まで緊密な同盟国だった2つの政府の間に深刻な不和を引き起こした。検察によれば、グレイは通商使節の通訳として働きながら、10年にわたってスパイ活動に従事していた。彼はエネルギー政策についての協議の内情に通じており、外国にメモを渡すことで守秘の規則をことごとく破ったとされる。彼の母国は容疑を否定し、中傷だと呼んでいる。あからさまな反抗として、同国は書類の引き渡しを拒み、裁判が行われても国外の自国民を守ることを思いとどまりはしないと警告した。先週、事態はさらに悪化した。ある過激派集団が、グレイを殉教者と呼び、彼が釈放されなければ外交官を誘拐すると表明したのだ。首都に住む離散した同胞の共同体の人々は、自分たちの店への襲撃を恐れ、冷静になるよう訴えている。双方の当局者は内々に、今の優先事項は事態の封じ込めだと語る。怒りに満ちた演説にもかかわらず、どちらの政府も、一人の男をめぐる争いで50年続いた同盟を壊したくはなく、静かな話し合いが始まったと言われている。","words":[["alias","freq:alias"],["discord","freq:discord"],["espionage","freq:espionage"],["envoy","freq:envoy"],["privy","freq:privy"],["confidentiality","freq:confidentiality"],["smear","freq:smear"],["defiance","freq:defiance"],["deter","freq:deter"],["extremist","freq:extremist"],["kidnap","freq:kidnap"],["martyr","freq:martyr"],["diaspora","freq:diaspora"],["containment","freq:containment"],["Notwithstanding","freq:notwithstanding"]],"qs":[{"q":"How has Gray's own country responded to the charge?","o":["It has admitted that he was a spy.","It has rejected the charge and refused to provide documents.","It has offered to exchange prisoners.","It has closed its embassy."],"a":1,"why":"denies the charge and calls it a smear とあり、has refused to hand over documents（書類の引き渡しを拒んだ）とある。"},{"q":"What do officials on both sides privately want?","o":["To limit the damage to the alliance","To hold the trial in secret","To punish the diaspora community","To end the alliance quickly"],"a":0,"why":"the priority now is containment とあり、neither government wants ... to destroy an alliance（同盟を壊したくない）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0058","page":"freq","group":"801〜900","kind":"刑罰制度の改革","text":"A national commission has published its long-awaited report on the penal system, and its conclusions are harsh. The commissioners visited forty prisons and found conditions in several that they described as despicable. In one, prisoners were held in solitary confinement for months; in another, guards had forcibly cut the hair of religious minorities, a practice the report calls plainly discriminatory. The plight of young offenders received particular attention. Boys as young as fifteen shared cells with violent adults, including a thug convicted of three assaults. The commission rejects the official claim that reports of abuse were a fabrication. It also criticizes the obscene profits made by private firms that run prison shops. Its main proposal concerns the criterion for early release. At present, release depends on a prisoner's behavior alone. The commission wants judges to consider mitigation, such as illness or the needs of a family. There is an implicit warning in the final chapter: if the government does not act, prisoners' groups may seek a court injunction. The report ends with a disclaimer that its figures are estimates, but nobody has seriously disputed them. Public intolerance of such conditions, it says, is growing.","ja":"国の委員会が、長く待たれていた刑罰制度についての報告書を公表した。その結論は厳しい。委員たちは40の刑務所を訪れ、そのいくつかで卑劣と表現するほどの状況を見つけた。ある刑務所では受刑者が何か月も独房に監禁されており、別の刑務所では看守が宗教的少数派の髪を強制的に切っていた。報告書はこの慣行を明白に差別的だと呼んでいる。若い犯罪者の窮状には特に注意が向けられた。15歳ほどの少年が、3件の暴行で有罪となった暴漢を含む粗暴な成人と房を共にしていた。委員会は、虐待の報告は作り話だという当局の主張を退けている。また、刑務所内の売店を運営する民間企業が上げているけしからぬ利益も批判している。主な提案は早期釈放の基準に関するものだ。現在、釈放は受刑者の行いだけで決まる。委員会は、病気や家族の必要といった酌量すべき事情を裁判官が考慮することを求めている。最終章には暗黙の警告がある。政府が行動しなければ、受刑者の団体が裁判所の差し止め命令を求めるかもしれないというのだ。報告書は数字が推定値であるという免責の断り書きで終わるが、それに本気で異議を唱えた人はいない。こうした状況に対する世間の不寛容は強まっていると、報告書は述べている。","words":[["penal","freq:penal"],["despicable","freq:despicable"],["confinement","freq:confinement"],["forcibly","freq:forcibly"],["discriminatory","freq:discriminatory"],["plight","freq:plight"],["thug","freq:thug"],["fabrication","freq:fabrication"],["obscene","freq:obscene"],["criterion","freq:criterion"],["mitigation","freq:mitigation"],["implicit","freq:implicit"],["injunction","freq:injunction"],["disclaimer","freq:disclaimer"],["intolerance","freq:intolerance"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the commission find about young offenders?","o":["They were kept apart from adults.","They were sharing cells with dangerous adults.","They were given early release.","They ran the prison shops."],"a":1,"why":"Boys as young as fifteen shared cells with violent adults（15歳ほどの少年が粗暴な成人と房を共にしていた）とある。"},{"q":"What change does the commission propose for early release?","o":["It should be abolished.","It should depend only on behavior.","It should be decided by private firms.","Judges should also take personal circumstances into account."],"a":3,"why":"wants judges to consider mitigation, such as illness or the needs of a family（病気や家族の必要といった事情の考慮を求める）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0059","page":"freq","group":"801〜900","kind":"探査機の開発","text":"Engineers designing a probe for the icy moon Calder faced a problem that no terrestrial machine has to solve. The probe must drill through ten kilometers of ice and then swim in a salt ocean, and the two tasks are almost incompatible. A drill needs weight; a swimming machine needs to be light. The team's solution depends on the convergence of several new ideas. For propulsion in the water, the probe does not use a propeller. Instead it creates a small vortex behind itself, as certain fish do, which also helps to stabilize it in strong currents. To resist corrosion, its shell is coated with a new formulation of ceramic, chosen for its durability after two years of tests. The engineers admit that their estimation of conditions on Calder may be wrong. During trials in Antarctica, the drill met an anomaly in the ice, a layer of gravel, and the operators had to throttle back the motor. The excavation took twice as long as planned. One scientist jokes that their confidence is a kind of placebo. Still, the breadth of the testing has impressed the space agency, which approved the next stage last month.","ja":"氷の衛星カルダー向けの探査機を設計する技師たちは、地球上の機械なら解く必要のない問題に直面した。探査機は10キロの氷を掘り進み、それから塩水の海を泳がなければならず、この2つの課題はほとんど両立しない。掘削機には重さが必要で、泳ぐ機械は軽くなければならない。チームの解決策は、いくつかの新しい考えが一つにまとまることにかかっている。水中での推進に、探査機はプロペラを使わない。その代わり、ある種の魚のように自分の後ろに小さな渦を作る。これは強い流れの中で機体を安定させる助けにもなる。腐食に耐えるため、外殻は新しい配合のセラミックで覆われている。2年の試験の末に耐久性で選ばれたものだ。技師たちは、カルダーの状況についての自分たちの見積もりが間違っているかもしれないと認める。南極での試験中、掘削機は氷の中の異常、つまり砂利の層にぶつかり、操作員はモーターの出力を絞らなければならなかった。掘削には予定の2倍の時間がかかった。ある科学者は、自分たちの自信は一種の気休めだと冗談を言う。それでも、試験の幅広さは宇宙機関に好印象を与え、機関は先月、次の段階を承認した。","words":[["terrestrial","freq:terrestrial"],["incompatible","freq:incompatible"],["convergence","freq:convergence"],["propulsion","freq:propulsion"],["vortex","freq:vortex"],["stabilize","freq:stabilize"],["corrosion","freq:corrosion"],["formulation","freq:formulation"],["durability","freq:durability"],["estimation","freq:estimation"],["anomaly","freq:anomaly"],["throttle","freq:throttle"],["excavation","freq:excavation"],["placebo","freq:placebo"],["breadth","freq:breadth"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why are the probe's two tasks hard to combine?","o":["Drilling needs weight, but swimming needs lightness.","Ice and salt water damage the same parts.","The moon is too far for radio signals.","The probe cannot carry enough fuel."],"a":0,"why":"A drill needs weight; a swimming machine needs to be light（掘削機には重さが、泳ぐ機械には軽さが必要）とある。"},{"q":"What happened during the trials in Antarctica?","o":["The ceramic coating cracked.","The probe was lost in the ocean.","The drill hit an unexpected layer and the work took longer.","The space agency cancelled the project."],"a":2,"why":"met an anomaly in the ice, a layer of gravel とあり、The excavation took twice as long as planned（掘削に予定の2倍かかった）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0060","page":"freq","group":"801〜900","kind":"映画監督の回顧展","text":"The national film museum has opened a retrospective of the work of Lena Voskuijlen, and it is a reminder of how much modern cinema owes her. Voskuijlen never made a blockbuster. Her films were small, concise, and often funny, built on the easy banter of people who know each other too well. What made them remarkable was her depiction of memory. She was the first director to use the flashback not as explanation but as interruption, a cunning device that lets the past break into the present without warning. The effect, in her best work, is sublime. The exhibition includes letters, costumes, and a room of trivia for devoted fans. It does not gloss over her failures. A candid note beside one poster admits that the film was \"hideous in every way,\" and quotes her own verdict to the same effect. Visitors can also watch a rare interview in which the charismatic director explains the symbolism of water in her films, then laughs and says she simply liked filming rain. The range of work on display is astounding. Anyone who cares about cinema should see it before it closes in June.","ja":"国立映画博物館がレナ・フォスカイレンの作品の回顧展を開いた。それは現代の映画がどれほど彼女に負うところが大きいかを思い出させる。フォスカイレンは大ヒット作を一本も作らなかった。彼女の映画は小さく、簡潔で、しばしば滑稽であり、互いを知りすぎている人々の気さくな冗談のやりとりの上に築かれていた。それらを際立たせたのは記憶の描写だった。彼女は回想場面を説明としてではなく中断として使った最初の監督だった。過去が予告なしに現在に割り込むことを可能にする巧妙な仕掛けである。最良の作品では、その効果は見事だ。展示には手紙や衣装、熱心なファン向けの雑学の部屋もある。彼女の失敗を取り繕ってはいない。あるポスターの横の率直な説明は、その映画が「あらゆる点でひどかった」と認め、同じ趣旨の彼女自身の評価を引用している。来館者は、カリスマ性のあるこの監督が自作における水の象徴性を説明し、それから笑って、単に雨を撮るのが好きだったのだと言う珍しいインタビューも見ることができる。展示されている作品の幅は驚くべきものだ。映画を大切に思う人は、6月に終わる前に見るべきだ。","words":[["retrospective","freq:retrospective"],["blockbuster","freq:blockbuster"],["concise","freq:concise"],["banter","freq:banter"],["depiction","freq:depiction"],["flashback","freq:flashback"],["cunning","freq:cunning"],["sublime","freq:sublime"],["trivia","freq:trivia"],["gloss","freq:gloss"],["candid","freq:candid"],["hideous","freq:hideous"],["charismatic","freq:charismatic"],["symbolism","freq:symbolism"],["astounding","freq:astounding"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was new about the way the director used flashbacks?","o":["She used them to explain the plot.","She used them as sudden interruptions of the present.","She placed them only at the end of films.","She filmed them in color."],"a":1,"why":"not as explanation but as interruption（説明ではなく中断として）使ったとある。"},{"q":"How does the exhibition treat her failures?","o":["It hides them.","It blames the actors.","It admits them openly.","It claims the critics were wrong."],"a":2,"why":"It does not gloss over her failures とあり、a candid note が「ひどかった」と認めている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0061","page":"freq","group":"801〜900","kind":"思春期の心理","text":"Parents often describe puberty as an ordeal, for themselves as much as for their children. A cheerful child becomes grumpy almost overnight, and small disagreements end in hysterical scenes. A new book by the psychologist Dr. Imani Clarke argues that this behavior has a purpose. Teenagers, she writes, crave independence but still need protection, and the conflict between the two produces a kind of existential anxiety. The questions they ask about who they are and what life is for are not a pose. Clarke's advice is practical. She warns parents against being insensitive to the fears behind the anger, but also against total immersion in a child's problems. Moderation, she says, is the key. Talk openly, including about subjects that were once taboo, and accept that your efforts will be imperfect. She has little patience with programs that promise to motivate teenagers through rewards. Lasting motivation is intrinsic, she argues, and it remains elusive until young people find something they care about. Parents who heed that lesson will have reason to rejoice later. The book's final chapter, based on interviews with former patients now in their thirties, makes the point movingly.","ja":"親はしばしば、思春期を子どもにとってと同じくらい自分たちにとっての試練だと言う。陽気だった子がほとんど一夜にして不機嫌になり、小さな意見の食い違いがヒステリックな場面で終わる。心理学者イマニ・クラーク博士の新著は、こうした行動には目的があると論じる。十代の若者は自立を切望しながら、なお保護を必要としており、この2つの間の葛藤が一種の実存的な不安を生むのだと彼女は書く。自分は何者か、人生は何のためにあるのかという彼らの問いは、見せかけではない。クラークの助言は実際的だ。彼女は親に、怒りの背後にある恐れに無神経であってはならないと警告するが、子どもの問題に完全にのめり込むことも戒める。大切なのは節度だと彼女は言う。かつてはタブーだった話題も含めて率直に話し、自分の努力が不完全なものになることを受け入れよ、というのだ。報酬によって十代のやる気を引き出すと約束するプログラムには、彼女はほとんど我慢がならない。長続きするやる気は本人の内から出るものであり、若者が大切に思える何かを見つけるまでは、つかまえにくいままだと彼女は論じる。その教訓に耳を傾ける親は、後で喜ぶ理由を持つことになるだろう。今は30代になったかつての患者への聞き取りに基づく最終章が、そのことを感動的に示している。","words":[["puberty","freq:puberty"],["ordeal","freq:ordeal"],["grumpy","freq:grumpy"],["hysterical","freq:hysterical"],["crave","freq:crave"],["existential","freq:existential"],["insensitive","freq:insensitive"],["immersion","freq:immersion"],["Moderation","freq:moderation"],["taboo","freq:taboo"],["imperfect","freq:imperfect"],["intrinsic","freq:intrinsic"],["elusive","freq:elusive"],["heed","freq:heed"],["rejoice","freq:rejoice"]],"qs":[{"q":"According to Clarke, what causes teenagers' anxiety?","o":["The conflict between wanting independence and needing protection","Too much schoolwork","Parents who refuse to talk","A lack of rewards"],"a":0,"why":"crave independence but still need protection, and the conflict between the two produces ... anxiety（自立への欲求と保護の必要の葛藤が不安を生む）とある。"},{"q":"What does Clarke think of reward programs?","o":["They are the best way to motivate teenagers.","They should begin before puberty.","They work only for boys.","She doubts them, because real motivation comes from within."],"a":3,"why":"Lasting motivation is intrinsic（長続きするやる気は内から出る）と論じ、そうしたプログラムには little patience とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0062","page":"freq","group":"801〜900","kind":"造船業の再編","text":"When the shipbuilder Norvik collapsed three years ago, the government appointed a caretaker board and gave it one instruction: keep the yards open. The task looked hopeless. The firm had no liquidity, its management was heavily centralized in a distant head office, and orders had begun to diminish long before the crisis. The board's first step was to consolidate five yards into two. It then hired a small consultancy to study what the company actually did well. The answer was surprising. Norvik's ships were ordinary, but its apprenticeship scheme was the best in the industry. In lieu of selling the training center, as planned, the board turned it into a separate business that now trains welders for firms across the region. The change was disruptive, and in the first year there was something close to mayhem on the shop floor. But the new income has been a boon. It has corrected the imbalance between costs and sales, and from this spring onward the yards are expected to make a profit. Analysts no longer speculate about closure. A ceremony next month will mark the commencement of work on the first new ship in six years.","ja":"造船会社ノルビクが3年前に破綻したとき、政府は暫定の取締役会を任命し、一つの指示を与えた。造船所を開けておくことだ。その仕事は絶望的に見えた。会社には手元資金がなく、経営は遠く離れた本社に極度に集中しており、受注は危機のずっと前から減り始めていた。取締役会の最初の一手は、5つの造船所を2つに統合することだった。次に小さなコンサルタント会社を雇い、この会社が実際に何を得意としているのかを調べさせた。答えは意外だった。ノルビクの船は平凡だったが、その見習い制度は業界で最良だったのだ。取締役会は、予定していた訓練施設の売却の代わりに、それを別の事業にした。今ではこの事業が地域中の企業のために溶接工を養成している。この変更は混乱を招き、最初の年は現場に大混乱に近いものがあった。しかし新しい収入は恩恵となった。費用と売上の不均衡を正し、この春以降、造船所は利益を出す見込みだ。専門家たちはもう閉鎖について憶測していない。来月の式典は、6年ぶりの新しい船の建造開始を記念する。","words":[["caretaker","freq:caretaker"],["liquidity","freq:liquidity"],["centralized","freq:centralized"],["diminish","freq:diminish"],["consolidate","freq:consolidate"],["consultancy","freq:consultancy"],["apprenticeship","freq:apprenticeship"],["lieu","freq:lieu"],["disruptive","freq:disruptive"],["mayhem","freq:mayhem"],["boon","freq:boon"],["imbalance","freq:imbalance"],["onward","freq:onward"],["speculate","freq:speculate"],["commencement","freq:commencement"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the consultancy discover about the company?","o":["Its ships were the best in the world.","Its head office was too small.","Its training program was its real strength.","Its yards were in the wrong places."],"a":2,"why":"its apprenticeship scheme was the best in the industry（見習い制度が業界で最良）とわかった。"},{"q":"What did the board do with the training center?","o":["It made it into a business that trains workers for other firms.","It sold it to pay debts.","It moved it to the head office.","It closed it during the first year."],"a":0,"why":"turned it into a separate business that now trains welders for firms across the region（地域の企業向けに溶接工を養成する別事業にした）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0063","page":"freq","group":"801〜900","kind":"古い教会の修復","text":"The small church at Elmsworth is anchored to a rock above the sea, and for eight centuries salt wind has worn away its walls. Last year a team of restorers arrived to cleanse the interior of smoke and damp. Beneath a layer of gray paint they found a series of medieval pictures. One shows an apostle preaching to a crowd; another shows a hunter beginning to stalk a deer, a scene whose meaning is uncertain. The most striking image presents the divinity of Christ in the guise of a shepherd. The discovery has excited historians, who hope to trace the lineage of the family that paid for the work. It has also revealed a blatant act of dishonesty. Records show that in 1870 the building fund was emptied by a churchwarden through what we would now call graft, and the pictures were painted over to hide the fact that promised repairs had never been done. The restoration will take two more years. Until then, services continue in a side room, where the sound of the waves is clearly audible through the old glass.","ja":"エルムズワースの小さな教会は海の上の岩にしっかり固定されており、8世紀にわたって潮風がその壁をすり減らしてきた。昨年、修復家のチームが、内部のすすと湿気を洗い清めるためにやって来た。灰色の塗料の層の下から、彼らは一連の中世の絵を見つけた。一枚は群衆に説教する使徒を描き、別の一枚はシカに忍び寄り始める狩人を描いているが、その場面の意味ははっきりしない。最も印象的な絵は、羊飼いの姿を借りてキリストの神性を表している。この発見は歴史家たちを興奮させており、彼らは制作の費用を出した一族の家系をたどりたいと考えている。それはまた、露骨な不正行為も明らかにした。記録によれば、1870年に教会の世話役が、今なら汚職と呼ばれるやり方で建築基金を空にし、約束された修理が行われなかったことを隠すために絵が塗りつぶされたのだ。修復にはさらに2年かかる。それまでの間、礼拝はわきの部屋で続けられる。そこでは古いガラス越しに波の音がはっきり聞こえる。","words":[["anchored","freq:anchored"],["cleanse","freq:cleanse"],["apostle","freq:apostle"],["stalk","freq:stalk"],["divinity","freq:divinity"],["guise","freq:guise"],["lineage","freq:lineage"],["blatant","freq:blatant"],["graft","freq:graft"],["audible","freq:audible"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the restorers find under the gray paint?","o":["A hidden door","Medieval pictures","A list of names","Damage caused by the sea"],"a":1,"why":"Beneath a layer of gray paint they found a series of medieval pictures（灰色の塗料の下に中世の絵を見つけた）とある。"},{"q":"Why were the pictures painted over in the nineteenth century?","o":["To protect them from the salt wind","Because the church disapproved of them","To hide the fact that repairs had not been carried out","Because a new family owned the church"],"a":2,"why":"painted over to hide the fact that promised repairs had never been done（約束された修理が行われなかったことを隠すため）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0064","page":"freq","group":"901〜1000","kind":"併合への国際的反応","text":"The annexation of the Sarn Valley by its larger neighbor has been condemned around the world as unjust. The occupying government issued a proclamation last week declaring the valley a province, and it claims that a vote among residents supported the change. Foreign observers call that vote a sham. Reporters were kept out, and satellite reconnaissance showed troops standing beside the polling stations. The regional parliament, which the occupiers describe as illegitimate, has been closed. Since then, the new rulers have moved quickly to enact oppressive laws. Local officials have been replaced through a system of patronage, and schools may no longer teach in the valley's own language, a rule that critics see as plain bigotry. Abroad, the response has been slow. A plurality of member states in the regional council favors a trade sanction, but the three largest can override any decision. Diplomats point to the disparity between strong speeches and weak action. One former minister, a figure of considerable stature, has warned that silence now will be remembered. If the world accepts this change, he argued, no small nation will be able to rely on its borders again.","ja":"サーン谷がより大きな隣国に併合されたことは、世界中で不当だと非難されている。占領した政府は先週、この谷を州と宣言する布告を出し、住民の投票がこの変更を支持したと主張している。外国の観察者たちはその投票を見せかけだと呼ぶ。記者は締め出され、衛星による偵察は投票所のそばに兵士が立っているのを映していた。占領者が非合法だと言う地方議会は閉鎖された。それ以来、新しい支配者たちは抑圧的な法律を次々に制定している。地元の役人は縁故による登用で入れ替えられ、学校はもはや谷自身の言語で教えてはならないことになった。批判する人々はこの規則をあからさまな偏見と見ている。国外の反応は鈍い。地域評議会の加盟国の相対多数は貿易制裁に賛成しているが、最大の3か国はどんな決定も覆すことができる。外交官たちは、強い演説と弱い行動の間の落差を指摘する。かなりの名声を持つある元大臣は、今の沈黙は記憶されるだろうと警告した。世界がこの変更を受け入れれば、どの小国も二度と自国の国境を頼りにできなくなると彼は論じた。","words":[["annexation","freq:annexation"],["unjust","freq:unjust"],["proclamation","freq:proclamation"],["sham","freq:sham"],["reconnaissance","freq:reconnaissance"],["illegitimate","freq:illegitimate"],["enact","freq:enact"],["oppressive","freq:oppressive"],["patronage","freq:patronage"],["bigotry","freq:bigotry"],["plurality","freq:plurality"],["sanction","freq:sanction"],["override","freq:override"],["disparity","freq:disparity"],["stature","freq:stature"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why do foreign observers reject the vote?","o":["Too few residents were allowed to vote.","It was held under the eyes of soldiers and without reporters.","The result was never announced.","The regional parliament organized it."],"a":1,"why":"Reporters were kept out とあり、troops standing beside the polling stations（投票所のそばに兵士）が映っていたとある。"},{"q":"Why has the regional council failed to act?","o":["Most members oppose any penalty.","The council has been closed.","The three largest members can block decisions.","The valley has asked it not to."],"a":2,"why":"the three largest can override any decision（最大の3か国がどんな決定も覆せる）ためである。"}]},
{"id":"f-0065","page":"freq","group":"901〜1000","kind":"詐欺事件の裁判","text":"The trial of a company director accused of running a bogus investment scheme ended yesterday with a ten-year sentence. Over six years, the man persuaded two thousand people to hand over their savings, promising returns that he knew were impossible. Much of the money was lost in an illicit wager on currency markets. The rest was spent on houses and cars. Prosecutors showed that he had acted in collusion with an accountant, who helped him to hide the deterioration of the fund from investors. The scheme collapsed when a retired teacher asked for the repayment of her money and was refused. She made discreet inquiries and discovered that the firm had no contractual right to hold her savings at all. The judge was scathing. He dismissed the claim that the losses were caused inadvertently, through simple incompetence, and noted that the defendant had once threatened a witness with abduction. Investors listened in disbelief as the full scale of the fraud was read out. The director will be sent to a correctional facility in the north. The court duly ordered that his property be sold, and regulators have promised more stringent checks on firms of this kind.","ja":"偽の投資事業を運営したとして起訴された会社取締役の裁判は昨日、禁錮10年の判決で終わった。6年にわたり、この男は2,000人を説得して貯蓄を預けさせ、不可能だと知っていた利益を約束した。金の多くは為替市場での不法な賭けで失われた。残りは家と車に使われた。検察は、彼が会計士と共謀して行動していたことを示した。会計士は基金の悪化を投資家から隠す手助けをしていた。この事業は、ある退職した教師が自分の金の返済を求めて拒まれたときに崩壊した。彼女は慎重に問い合わせを重ね、その会社には彼女の貯蓄を預かる契約上の権利がそもそもないことを突き止めた。裁判官は痛烈だった。損失は単なる無能のせいでうっかり生じたものだという主張を退け、被告がかつて証人を誘拐すると脅していたことを指摘した。詐欺の全容が読み上げられる間、投資家たちは信じられない思いで聞いていた。取締役は北部の矯正施設に送られる。裁判所は正式に彼の財産の売却を命じ、規制当局はこの種の会社に対するより厳しい検査を約束した。","words":[["bogus","freq:bogus"],["illicit","freq:illicit"],["wager","freq:wager"],["collusion","freq:collusion"],["deterioration","freq:deterioration"],["repayment","freq:repayment"],["discreet","freq:discreet"],["contractual","freq:contractual"],["inadvertently","freq:inadvertently"],["incompetence","freq:incompetence"],["abduction","freq:abduction"],["disbelief","freq:disbelief"],["correctional","freq:correctional"],["duly","freq:duly"],["stringent","freq:stringent"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did the scheme come to an end?","o":["The accountant confessed to the police.","The currency markets closed.","An investor who was refused her money began asking questions.","The director sold his houses."],"a":2,"why":"a retired teacher asked for the repayment of her money and was refused、その後 discreet inquiries をして不正を突き止めたとある。"},{"q":"What did the judge say about the director's defense?","o":["He accepted that the losses were accidental.","He rejected the idea that the losses were an innocent mistake.","He blamed the investors.","He said the accountant was mainly responsible."],"a":1,"why":"dismissed the claim that the losses were caused inadvertently, through simple incompetence（うっかり・無能のせいだという主張を退けた）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0066","page":"freq","group":"901〜1000","kind":"実験の準備","text":"Before a new drug is tested on patients, months of preparatory work take place in the laboratory. At the Varga Institute, researchers studying a treatment for nerve damage begin by using a computer to simulate how the drug moves through the body. The model treats the bloodstream as a continuum and calculates the kinetic energy of each molecule. Only if these results are promising does the team move on. The drug must be soluble in water, and every container must be sterile. Early trials are tentative. Volunteers receive a tiny dose, and doctors test a simple reflex at the knee to see whether the nerves respond. Alongside the numbers, the team collects qualitative data, asking volunteers to describe what they feel. The first study produced a puzzle. There was wide variance between individuals, and an inverse relationship appeared between age and benefit: older volunteers improved more. One scientist suspects that the drug acts on the posterior part of the spinal cord, which changes with age. A small hitch with the supply of the chemical precursor has delayed the next stage, and a report on the feasibility of a larger trial is due in May.","ja":"新しい薬が患者で試される前に、研究室では何か月もの準備作業が行われる。バルガ研究所では、神経損傷の治療法を研究する研究者たちが、まずコンピューターを使って薬が体内をどう移動するかを模擬的に再現する。そのモデルは血流を連続体として扱い、分子一つひとつの運動エネルギーを計算する。この結果が有望な場合にのみ、チームは先へ進む。薬は水に溶けなければならず、容器はすべて無菌でなければならない。初期の試験は暫定的なものだ。志願者はごく少量を投与され、医師は神経が反応するかを見るためにひざの単純な反射を調べる。数値と並行して、チームは質的なデータも集め、志願者に感じたことを述べてもらう。最初の研究は一つの謎を生んだ。個人の間に大きなばらつきがあり、年齢と効果の間に逆の関係が現れたのだ。年長の志願者のほうが大きく改善した。ある科学者は、この薬が年齢とともに変化する脊髄の後部に作用しているのではないかと考えている。化学的な前駆物質の供給にちょっとした支障があって次の段階は遅れており、より大規模な試験の実現可能性についての報告は5月に出る予定だ。","words":[["preparatory","freq:preparatory"],["simulate","freq:simulate"],["continuum","freq:continuum"],["kinetic","freq:kinetic"],["soluble","freq:soluble"],["sterile","freq:sterile"],["tentative","freq:tentative"],["reflex","freq:reflex"],["qualitative","freq:qualitative"],["variance","freq:variance"],["inverse","freq:inverse"],["posterior","freq:posterior"],["hitch","freq:hitch"],["precursor","freq:precursor"],["feasibility","freq:feasibility"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is the first step in the institute's work on the drug?","o":["Giving volunteers a small dose","Modeling the drug's movement on a computer","Testing reflexes at the knee","Writing a report on a larger trial"],"a":1,"why":"begin by using a computer to simulate how the drug moves through the body（まずコンピューターで薬の動きを再現する）とある。"},{"q":"What puzzling result did the first study produce?","o":["Older volunteers benefited more than younger ones.","The drug did not dissolve in water.","Volunteers could not describe their feelings.","Every volunteer responded in the same way."],"a":0,"why":"an inverse relationship ... between age and benefit: older volunteers improved more（年長の志願者のほうが大きく改善した）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0067","page":"freq","group":"901〜1000","kind":"歌劇の新演出","text":"The opera Mirela, written in 1842, is based on a mythical tale from classical antiquity. A princess returns to her ancestral home and finds it ruled by a stranger who claims to be the incarnation of a god. For most of the last century, productions were lavish and nostalgic, with painted temples and long robes. The new staging at the City Opera could not be more different. The director has moved the story to a modern office block. Behind its glass facade, the \"god\" is a company chairman, and the temple is a boardroom. Some critics have called the idea provocative for its own sake. That is unfair. The setting is instantly recognizable, and it gives the old story a topical edge without changing a word. Musically, the evening is a triumph. The soprano's rendition of the great aria in Act Two is the pinnacle of the performance, lyrical and perfectly controlled. If the production has a weakness, it is the final scene, which treats the heroine's death as a mundane event. Even so, this Mirela proves that an opera house need not be a repository of museum pieces.","ja":"1842年に書かれた歌劇「ミレーラ」は、古典古代の神話上の物語に基づいている。王女が先祖代々の家に戻ると、そこは神の化身だと称するよそ者に支配されている。前世紀の大半を通じて、演出は豪華で懐古的であり、描かれた神殿と長い衣が使われた。市立歌劇場の新しい演出は、これ以上ないほど異なっている。演出家は物語を現代の高層の事務所に移した。そのガラスの正面の奥で、「神」は会社の会長であり、神殿は役員会議室だ。この着想は挑発のための挑発だと呼ぶ批評家もいる。それは公平ではない。舞台設定はすぐにそれとわかり、言葉を一つも変えずに古い物語に時事的な鋭さを与えている。音楽の面では、この晩は大成功だ。第二幕の有名なアリアのソプラノの歌唱はこの公演の頂点であり、叙情的で完璧に制御されている。この演出に弱点があるとすれば、最後の場面だ。主人公の死を平凡な出来事として扱っている。それでも、この「ミレーラ」は、歌劇場が博物館の陳列品の貯蔵所である必要はないことを証明している。","words":[["mythical","freq:mythical"],["antiquity","freq:antiquity"],["ancestral","freq:ancestral"],["incarnation","freq:incarnation"],["lavish","freq:lavish"],["nostalgic","freq:nostalgic"],["facade","freq:facade"],["provocative","freq:provocative"],["recognizable","freq:recognizable"],["topical","freq:topical"],["rendition","freq:rendition"],["pinnacle","freq:pinnacle"],["lyrical","freq:lyrical"],["mundane","freq:mundane"],["repository","freq:repository"]],"qs":[{"q":"How does the new production differ from earlier ones?","o":["It uses painted temples.","It cuts the second act.","It sets the story in a modern office.","It changes the words of the opera."],"a":2,"why":"The director has moved the story to a modern office block（物語を現代の事務所に移した）とある。"},{"q":"What does the reviewer consider the high point?","o":["The final scene","The design of the boardroom","The costumes","The soprano's singing of the aria in Act Two"],"a":3,"why":"The soprano's rendition of the great aria in Act Two is the pinnacle of the performance（第二幕のアリアの歌唱が頂点）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0068","page":"freq","group":"901〜1000","kind":"山越えの記録","text":"We began the ascent before dawn, hoping to cross the pass by dusk. The lower slopes were gentle, and for the first hour we walked through a creamy haze that hid everything beyond twenty meters. Then the mist cleared, and the valley lay below us, its lake a pale aqua in the early light. Our guide, a stout man of sixty with sturdy boots and few words, pointed out birds as we climbed. Most of the species here migrate south in autumn, he said, but a few remain, relying on camouflage to survive the snow. Once, absurdly, we saw a peacock that had escaped from a village garden. By noon the landscape had turned bleak. The upper valley is devoid of trees, and we could hear the rumble of falling rock on the far side. A stream that had been a trickle in the morning was now a torrent, swollen by melting ice, and we lost an hour finding a place to cross. That nobody slipped seems, looking back, almost miraculous. We reached the hut at the top as the last light faded.","ja":"私たちは夕暮れまでに峠を越えるつもりで、夜明け前に登り始めた。下のほうの斜面はなだらかで、最初の1時間は、20メートル先のすべてを隠す乳白色のもやの中を歩いた。やがて霧が晴れると、谷が眼下に広がり、その湖は早朝の光の中で淡い水色をしていた。私たちの案内人は、頑丈な長靴を履いた口数の少ない60歳のがっしりした男で、登りながら鳥を指さして教えてくれた。ここにいる種のほとんどは秋に南へ渡るが、数種は残り、雪の中を生き延びるのに保護色に頼っているのだと彼は言った。一度、ばかげたことに、村の庭から逃げ出したクジャクを見かけた。正午までに風景は荒涼としたものに変わった。谷の上のほうには木がまったくなく、向こう側で岩が落ちる低い響きが聞こえた。朝にはちょろちょろ流れていた小川は、解けた氷で水かさが増して激流になっており、私たちは渡る場所を見つけるのに1時間を失った。誰も滑らなかったのは、振り返ればほとんど奇跡的に思える。最後の光が消える頃、私たちは頂上の小屋に着いた。","words":[["ascent","freq:ascent"],["dusk","freq:dusk"],["creamy","freq:creamy"],["haze","freq:haze"],["aqua","freq:aqua"],["stout","freq:stout"],["sturdy","freq:sturdy"],["migrate","freq:migrate"],["camouflage","freq:camouflage"],["peacock","freq:peacock"],["bleak","freq:bleak"],["devoid","freq:devoid"],["rumble","freq:rumble"],["torrent","freq:torrent"],["miraculous","freq:miraculous"]],"qs":[{"q":"How do the birds that stay through the winter survive?","o":["They move to the villages.","They depend on blending in with their surroundings.","They are fed by the guide.","They nest in the hut."],"a":1,"why":"relying on camouflage to survive the snow（保護色に頼って雪の中を生き延びる）とある。"},{"q":"Why did the group lose an hour?","o":["The guide became ill.","The mist returned at noon.","A stream had grown too strong to cross easily.","They stopped to watch a peacock."],"a":2,"why":"小川が a torrent, swollen by melting ice になり、we lost an hour finding a place to cross（渡る場所を見つけるのに1時間を失った）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0069","page":"freq","group":"901〜1000","kind":"創業者の伝記","text":"The public knew Viktor Hallas as the man who built an airline from nothing. A new biography shows how close the effort came to destroying him. In the early years, Hallas slept three hours a night. His insomnia began as a choice and became an illness, and the sleep deprivation affected his judgment. Colleagues recall periods of mania in which he bought aircraft the company could not afford, followed by weeks of paranoia when he accused old friends of plotting against him. One board member called him a lunatic to his face. Yet the author argues that it would be misguided to see only weakness. The same restless psyche that drove Hallas into a frenzy of work also gave him extraordinary grit. During the horrendous winter of 1979, when two rivals went bankrupt, he kept flying at his own insistence and seemed, for a time, invincible. The collapse came later. Hallas reluctantly stepped down at sixty, a manifestation, his doctors said, of exhaustion that had been building for decades. He spent his last years on a small farm, earning his livelihood, as his father had, from the land.","ja":"世間はビクトル・ハラスを、何もないところから航空会社を築いた男として知っていた。新しい伝記は、その努力がどれほど彼を破滅の寸前まで追い込んだかを示している。初期の頃、ハラスは一晩に3時間しか眠らなかった。彼の不眠は自分で選んだものとして始まり、病気になった。そして睡眠不足は彼の判断力に影響した。同僚たちは、会社には買えるはずのない航空機を彼が買った躁状態の時期と、それに続いて、古い友人たちが自分に陰謀を企てていると非難した数週間の被害妄想の時期を覚えている。ある取締役は面と向かって彼を狂人と呼んだ。それでも著者は、弱さだけを見るのは見当違いだと論じる。ハラスを仕事への熱狂に駆り立てたのと同じ落ち着きのない精神が、彼に並外れた根性も与えたのだ。競争相手2社が倒産した1979年のひどい冬の間、彼は自らの強い主張で運航を続け、一時は無敵に見えた。崩壊は後にやって来た。ハラスは60歳でしぶしぶ退任した。医師たちによれば、それは何十年も積み重なってきた疲労の表れだった。彼は晩年を小さな農場で過ごし、父親がそうだったように、土地から生計を立てた。","words":[["insomnia","freq:insomnia"],["deprivation","freq:deprivation"],["mania","freq:mania"],["paranoia","freq:paranoia"],["lunatic","freq:lunatic"],["misguided","freq:misguided"],["psyche","freq:psyche"],["frenzy","freq:frenzy"],["grit","freq:grit"],["horrendous","freq:horrendous"],["insistence","freq:insistence"],["invincible","freq:invincible"],["reluctantly","freq:reluctantly"],["manifestation","freq:manifestation"],["livelihood","freq:livelihood"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does the biography say about Hallas's lack of sleep?","o":["It made him a better manager.","It was ordered by his doctors.","It started by choice and later harmed his judgment.","It began only after he retired."],"a":2,"why":"began as a choice and became an illness, and the sleep deprivation affected his judgment（選択として始まり病気になり、判断力に影響した）とある。"},{"q":"What point does the author make about Hallas's character?","o":["The same nature that harmed him also gave him great determination.","He was weak from the beginning.","He owed his success to his board.","He never worked hard."],"a":0,"why":"The same restless psyche ... also gave him extraordinary grit（同じ落ち着きのない精神が並外れた根性も与えた）と論じている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0070","page":"freq","group":"901〜1000","kind":"町の古い酒場","text":"The Anchor has been the only saloon in Port Ellery since 1880, and its present steward, Tom Baird, has worked there for forty years. From the outset, he says, the job was about more than serving drinks. Fishermen came in to settle arguments, sometimes by brute force, and it was his task to keep the peace. He remembers the storm of 1987, which wrought terrible damage along the harbor. The bar stayed open all night as a shelter, and he fed sixty people on stale bread and a pot of soup. Since then he has always kept a contingency store of candles, blankets, and miscellaneous tools in the cellar. The trade has changed. Younger customers are more compliant with the rules, he says with a smile, and nobody tries to fiddle the bill any longer. But fewer people come. Baird will retire next spring, and no buyer has yet been found. He says he would rather sell cheaply to a local family than see it close. Regulars fear that the building will be turned into flats, and that the town will lose the one room where everyone, sooner or later, has sat down together.","ja":"アンカーは1880年以来、ポート・エレリーでただ一つの酒場であり、今の世話役トム・ベアードはそこで40年働いてきた。最初から、この仕事は酒を出すだけのものではなかったと彼は言う。漁師たちは口論に決着をつけに来て、時には力ずくでそうした。平和を保つのが彼の役目だった。彼は、港沿いにひどい被害を引き起こした1987年の嵐を覚えている。店は避難所として一晩中開けられ、彼は古くなったパンと鍋一杯のスープで60人に食事を出した。それ以来、彼は地下室に、ろうそく、毛布、種々雑多な道具という万一の備えを常に置いている。商売は変わった。若い客は規則によく従うようになり、もう誰も勘定をごまかそうとはしないと、彼は笑みを浮かべて言う。しかし来る人は減った。ベアードは来春に引退するが、買い手はまだ見つかっていない。閉店するのを見るくらいなら地元の家族に安く売りたいと彼は言う。常連たちは、建物が集合住宅に変えられ、誰もが遅かれ早かれ一緒に腰を下ろしてきたただ一つの部屋を町が失うことを恐れている。","words":[["saloon","freq:saloon"],["steward","freq:steward"],["outset","freq:outset"],["brute","freq:brute"],["wrought","freq:wrought"],["stale","freq:stale"],["contingency","freq:contingency"],["miscellaneous","freq:miscellaneous"],["compliant","freq:compliant"],["fiddle","freq:fiddle"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the bar do during the storm of 1987?","o":["It closed for repairs.","It stayed open to shelter and feed people.","It lost its cellar.","It was sold to a new owner."],"a":1,"why":"The bar stayed open all night as a shelter, and he fed sixty people（避難所として開け、60人に食事を出した）とある。"},{"q":"What do regular customers fear?","o":["Prices will rise.","Baird will refuse to retire.","Younger customers will break the rules.","The building will become flats and the town will lose its meeting place."],"a":3,"why":"the building will be turned into flats, and that the town will lose the one room where everyone ... has sat down together とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0071","page":"freq","group":"1001〜1100","kind":"連合加盟をめぐる選挙","text":"The most populous state in the federation goes to the polls next week, and the result may decide whether the country continues its accession talks with the regional union. Five years of austerity have left voters angry. Wages have fallen, and there is deep skepticism about promises made by any party. The governor, whose candidacy was announced only in March, is defending a region long regarded as a stronghold of his party. His opponent accuses him of bribery, citing the appropriation of road funds for projects in districts that support him. He denies it, and no court has yet examined the charge. The two met in a televised debate on Sunday. The moderator pressed both for substantive answers, with limited success. The governor spoke of an economic turnaround; his rival demanded parity in pensions between public and private workers. Neither addressed the largest hurdle to union membership, which is the civil strife in the northern districts. Talks with the rebels there have been postponed for an indefinite period. Whoever wins will have to resume them, because the union has made clear that it will not admit a country at war with itself.","ja":"連邦で最も人口の多い州が来週、投票日を迎える。その結果は、国が地域連合への加盟交渉を続けるかどうかを決めるかもしれない。5年間の緊縮財政は有権者を怒らせた。賃金は下がり、どの党の公約にも深い懐疑がある。3月にようやく立候補が発表された知事は、長く自党の拠点と見なされてきた地域を守る戦いをしている。対立候補は彼を贈収賄で非難し、道路の資金が彼を支持する地区の事業に流用されたことを挙げる。彼は否定しており、その容疑を審理した裁判所はまだない。2人は日曜にテレビ討論で対決した。司会者は双方に実質的な答えを求めたが、あまり成功しなかった。知事は経済の好転を語り、対立候補は公務員と民間労働者の年金の平等を要求した。どちらも、連合加盟への最大の障害である北部地区の内乱には触れなかった。そこの反政府勢力との協議は無期限に延期されている。誰が勝つにせよ、協議を再開しなければならない。連合は、内戦状態にある国は加盟させないと明言しているからだ。","words":[["populous","freq:populous"],["accession","freq:accession"],["austerity","freq:austerity"],["skepticism","freq:skepticism"],["candidacy","freq:candidacy"],["stronghold","freq:stronghold"],["bribery","freq:bribery"],["appropriation","freq:appropriation"],["moderator","freq:moderator"],["substantive","freq:substantive"],["turnaround","freq:turnaround"],["parity","freq:parity"],["hurdle","freq:hurdle"],["strife","freq:strife"],["indefinite","freq:indefinite"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is the governor accused of?","o":["Hiding the results of an earlier vote","Using road money to reward districts that back him","Refusing to debate his rival","Ending the talks with the union"],"a":1,"why":"the appropriation of road funds for projects in districts that support him（道路資金を自分を支持する地区の事業に流用した）とある。"},{"q":"What is the greatest obstacle to joining the union?","o":["Low wages","The cost of pensions","The governor's late candidacy","Fighting inside the country"],"a":3,"why":"the largest hurdle to union membership, which is the civil strife in the northern districts（北部地区の内乱）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0072","page":"freq","group":"1001〜1100","kind":"不動産をめぐる犯罪","text":"When the old Marlin Hotel burned down last winter, the police at first suspected a careless guest. It soon became clear that the fire was arson, and the search for the culprit led to one of the city's best-known businessmen. Investigators believe that the owner, the head of a property conglomerate, wanted the building empty. Tenants on the upper floors had resisted eviction for two years. Several reported intimidation: strangers at the door, cut telephone lines, and letters offering money on condition of anonymity. One elderly couple received a counterfeit court order announcing the foreclosure of their flat. After the fire, the story grew stranger. The owner was found to have avoided tax for a decade, and he now faces separate charges of evasion. A former partner called him a crook and a hypocrite, pointing out that he had led a public campaign for honest business. Old allegations of adultery were dragged up by the newspapers, though they have nothing to do with the case. His expulsion from the chamber of commerce followed within days. The trial begins in May, amid deep public distrust of the property industry. Nobody died in the fire, but the rampage through the tenants' lives is not easily forgiven.","ja":"昨年の冬に古いマーリン・ホテルが焼け落ちたとき、警察は最初、不注意な宿泊客を疑った。まもなく火事は放火だとわかり、犯人の捜索は市で最もよく知られた実業家の一人に行き着いた。捜査官は、不動産複合企業の長である所有者が建物を空にしたかったのだと考えている。上の階の入居者たちは2年間、立ち退きに抵抗していた。何人かは脅しを受けたと訴えた。戸口に現れる見知らぬ人、切られた電話線、匿名を条件に金を申し出る手紙である。ある高齢の夫婦は、自宅の差し押さえを告げる偽造の裁判所命令を受け取った。火事の後、話はさらに奇妙になった。所有者は10年間税を逃れていたことが判明し、今は別に脱税の罪にも問われている。元共同経営者は彼を悪党で偽善者だと呼び、彼が正直な商売を訴える公の運動を率いていたことを指摘した。事件とは何の関係もないのに、昔の不倫疑惑も新聞に蒸し返された。数日のうちに、彼は商工会議所から除名された。不動産業界への世間の深い不信の中、裁判は5月に始まる。火事で死者は出なかったが、入居者たちの生活を踏みにじった行為は簡単には許されない。","words":[["arson","freq:arson"],["culprit","freq:culprit"],["conglomerate","freq:conglomerate"],["eviction","freq:eviction"],["intimidation","freq:intimidation"],["anonymity","freq:anonymity"],["counterfeit","freq:counterfeit"],["foreclosure","freq:foreclosure"],["evasion","freq:evasion"],["crook","freq:crook"],["hypocrite","freq:hypocrite"],["adultery","freq:adultery"],["expulsion","freq:expulsion"],["distrust","freq:distrust"],["rampage","freq:rampage"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why do investigators think the owner wanted the hotel burned?","o":["He wanted to claim it was haunted.","He wanted the tenants out of the building.","He was angry with a careless guest.","He planned to sell it to the city."],"a":1,"why":"wanted the building empty とあり、入居者は had resisted eviction for two years（2年間立ち退きに抵抗していた）とある。"},{"q":"Why did a former partner call the owner a hypocrite?","o":["He had campaigned publicly for honesty in business.","He had refused to pay his partner.","He had left the chamber of commerce.","He had lived in the hotel himself."],"a":0,"why":"he had led a public campaign for honest business（正直な商売を訴える公の運動を率いていた）ことを指摘した。"}]},
{"id":"f-0073","page":"freq","group":"1001〜1100","kind":"湖底の調査","text":"At the bottom of Lake Ferin lies a record of the last ten thousand years. Each spring, melting snow carries fine sediment into the lake, where it settles in thin layers, one for every year. By drilling into the mud, scientists can read these layers like pages. A recent study produced a startling result. The layers showed that the region's volcano, long thought to be dormant, had erupted at intermittent periods far more recent than anyone believed. Ash was mixed with the remains of foliage from trees that grow only in warm conditions. The team was cautious. A single core might be misleading, so they insisted on replication, drilling at six more sites. They also ruled out the diffusion of chemicals between layers, which could have confused the dating, and found the effect negligible. Each iteration confirmed the first. Combined with meteorological records, the data suggest an eruption about four hundred years ago. One puzzle remains: a stone artifact found in the ash, which a cohort of archaeologists is now studying. Whether to call the volcano \"active\" is partly a semantic question. But monitoring equipment, once rare, will soon be ubiquitous on its slopes, and nothing is likely to hinder that.","ja":"フェリン湖の底には、過去1万年の記録が横たわっている。毎年春、解けた雪が細かい堆積物を湖に運び込み、それが1年に1枚ずつ薄い層になって沈む。泥を掘り進めることで、科学者はこれらの層をページのように読むことができる。最近の研究は驚くべき結果を生んだ。長く活動を休止していると考えられてきたこの地域の火山が、誰もが信じていたよりはるかに最近の時期に、断続的に噴火していたことを層が示したのだ。火山灰は、暖かい条件でしか育たない木の葉の残骸と混ざっていた。チームは慎重だった。1本の掘削試料だけでは誤解を招くかもしれないので、彼らは再現にこだわり、さらに6か所で掘削した。年代測定を混乱させうる層の間の化学物質の拡散も検討して排除し、その影響はごくわずかだと確かめた。繰り返すたびに最初の結果が確認された。気象の記録と合わせると、データは約400年前の噴火を示唆している。一つ謎が残る。灰の中から見つかった石の人工遺物で、考古学者の一団が今それを研究している。この火山を「活火山」と呼ぶかどうかは、一部は言葉の意味の問題だ。しかし、かつてはまれだった監視機器が、まもなくその斜面の至るところに置かれることになり、それを妨げるものは何もなさそうだ。","words":[["sediment","freq:sediment"],["startling","freq:startling"],["dormant","freq:dormant"],["intermittent","freq:intermittent"],["foliage","freq:foliage"],["replication","freq:replication"],["diffusion","freq:diffusion"],["negligible","freq:negligible"],["iteration","freq:iteration"],["meteorological","freq:meteorological"],["artifact","freq:artifact"],["cohort","freq:cohort"],["semantic","freq:semantic"],["ubiquitous","freq:ubiquitous"],["hinder","freq:hinder"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the layers in the lake reveal?","o":["The lake is much older than believed.","The volcano erupted more recently than anyone had thought.","The climate has always been cold.","Trees never grew near the lake."],"a":1,"why":"had erupted at intermittent periods far more recent than anyone believed（誰もが信じていたより最近に噴火していた）とある。"},{"q":"Why did the team drill at six more sites?","o":["To find more stone objects","To measure the depth of the lake","To check that the first result could be repeated","To look for chemical pollution"],"a":2,"why":"A single core might be misleading, so they insisted on replication（1本では誤解を招きうるので再現にこだわった）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0074","page":"freq","group":"1001〜1100","kind":"画家の再評価","text":"For most of her life, the painter Clara Venn was dismissed as a minor figure. Critics found her work gritty and strange, and collectors with bourgeois tastes preferred pictures that would look pleasant above a sofa. A major exhibition in the capital now presents her as a visionary. Venn's approach was unconventional from the start. Where her teachers urged students to emulate the old masters, she chose to depict factory yards, bus stations, and empty kitchens. A recurring motif in her work is a single lit window in a dark street. The mood is one of solitude rather than despair, and there is a quiet melancholy in even her brightest canvases. Her private life was simple. She distrusted anything extravagant and lived by the maxim that an artist needs only light, time, and silence. The exhibition's catalog includes an insightful essay on her letters, which reveal a heartfelt attachment to the city she painted. The final room is the most moving. It contains the solemn self-portraits of her last years, painted when she was nearly blind. Their brilliance lies not in technique but in honesty, and they make the long neglect of her work hard to understand.","ja":"画家クララ・ベンは、生涯の大半を通じて、取るに足りない人物として片づけられてきた。批評家は彼女の作品を生々しく奇妙だと感じ、中産階級的な趣味を持つ収集家は、長いすの上に掛けて感じのよい絵を好んだ。首都で開かれている大規模な展覧会は今、彼女を先見の明のある人として紹介している。ベンのやり方は最初から型にはまらなかった。教師たちが学生に昔の巨匠を見習うよう促したのに対し、彼女は工場の構内やバスの停留所、誰もいない台所を描くことを選んだ。彼女の作品に繰り返し現れる主題は、暗い通りにただ一つ明かりのともった窓だ。その雰囲気は絶望というより孤独であり、最も明るい絵にさえ静かな物悲しさがある。彼女の私生活は簡素だった。ぜいたくなものは何でも信用せず、芸術家に必要なのは光と時間と静けさだけだという格言に従って生きた。展覧会の図録には、彼女の手紙についての洞察に満ちた論考が収められており、手紙は自分が描いた都市への心からの愛着を明かしている。最後の部屋が最も心を打つ。そこには、ほとんど目が見えなくなってから描かれた晩年の厳粛な自画像がある。その輝きは技術にではなく正直さにあり、彼女の作品が長く顧みられなかったことを理解しがたいものにしている。","words":[["gritty","freq:gritty"],["bourgeois","freq:bourgeois"],["visionary","freq:visionary"],["unconventional","freq:unconventional"],["emulate","freq:emulate"],["depict","freq:depict"],["motif","freq:motif"],["solitude","freq:solitude"],["melancholy","freq:melancholy"],["extravagant","freq:extravagant"],["maxim","freq:maxim"],["insightful","freq:insightful"],["heartfelt","freq:heartfelt"],["solemn","freq:solemn"],["brilliance","freq:brilliance"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did Venn's choice of subjects differ from what her teachers advised?","o":["She painted everyday places instead of imitating old masters.","She painted only portraits of collectors.","She refused to paint cities.","She copied famous pictures exactly."],"a":0,"why":"教師は emulate the old masters を勧めたが、彼女は factory yards, bus stations, and empty kitchens を描くことを選んだ。"},{"q":"What does the reviewer say about the self-portraits in the final room?","o":["They are technically perfect.","They were painted in her youth.","They show the city at night.","Their strength comes from their honesty."],"a":3,"why":"Their brilliance lies not in technique but in honesty（輝きは技術ではなく正直さにある）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0075","page":"freq","group":"1001〜1100","kind":"新人への助言","text":"Starting a first job is exciting, but it can also be frightening. A novice enters an office full of unwritten rules, and the fear of breaking them creates a kind of inhibition that makes even simple tasks hard. Career advisers suggest a few principles. First, do not feel constrained by your job description. If you see a problem, say so politely; reluctance to speak is usually read as lack of interest. Second, learn the etiquette of the workplace by watching. Who speaks first in meetings? How are disagreements handled? Third, avoid the two classic mistakes. Some newcomers are cocky, sure that they know better than colleagues with twenty years of experience. Others are so edgy that they apologize for everything. Professionalism lies somewhere between. Practical habits matter too. Treat a degree of thrift as obligatory in your first year, since few salaries stretch as far as expected, and take the precaution of saving a little each month. Finally, protect your vitality. Before you embark on extra projects, contemplate whether you have the time, and leave space each week to recharge. A career lasts forty years, and nobody wins it in the first six months.","ja":"初めての仕事を始めるのはわくわくするが、怖いことでもある。初心者は不文律だらけの事務所に入り、それを破ることへの恐れが、単純な仕事さえ難しくする一種のためらいを生む。職業相談の専門家はいくつかの原則を勧めている。第一に、職務記述書に縛られていると感じないこと。問題に気づいたら丁寧にそう言うことだ。話すのを渋ると、たいていは関心がないと受け取られる。第二に、職場の礼儀作法を観察して学ぶこと。会議で誰が最初に話すか。意見の食い違いはどう扱われるか。第三に、2つの典型的な失敗を避けること。新人の中には、20年の経験を持つ同僚より自分のほうがわかっていると思い込む、うぬぼれた人がいる。逆に、ひどくびくびくして何にでも謝る人もいる。職業人らしさはその中間にある。実際的な習慣も大切だ。期待どおりに足りる給料はほとんどないので、最初の年はある程度の倹約を義務と考え、用心として毎月少しずつ貯金することだ。最後に、自分の活力を守ること。追加の仕事に乗り出す前に時間があるかどうかをよく考え、毎週、元気を取り戻すための余裕を残しておくこと。職業人生は40年続くものであり、最初の6か月で勝負が決まる人はいない。","words":[["novice","freq:novice"],["inhibition","freq:inhibition"],["constrained","freq:constrained"],["reluctance","freq:reluctance"],["etiquette","freq:etiquette"],["cocky","freq:cocky"],["edgy","freq:edgy"],["Professionalism","freq:professionalism"],["thrift","freq:thrift"],["obligatory","freq:obligatory"],["precaution","freq:precaution"],["vitality","freq:vitality"],["embark","freq:embark"],["contemplate","freq:contemplate"],["recharge","freq:recharge"]],"qs":[{"q":"How are newcomers advised to learn the workplace's unwritten rules?","o":["By reading their job description","By observing how colleagues behave","By asking their manager for a list","By speaking first in every meeting"],"a":1,"why":"learn the etiquette of the workplace by watching（観察して学ぶ）とある。"},{"q":"What financial advice is given for the first year?","o":["Borrow money for extra training.","Spend freely to reduce stress.","Ask for a higher salary at once.","Be careful with money and save a little each month."],"a":3,"why":"Treat a degree of thrift as obligatory とあり、saving a little each month（毎月少しずつ貯金する）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0076","page":"freq","group":"1001〜1100","kind":"包囲された要塞","text":"The fortress of Kalmar stood on a cliff above an abyss, and for three centuries it was thought impossible to capture. In the spring of 1644, an army of twelve thousand arrived to prove otherwise. The defenders, fewer than six hundred, were reliant on a single well and on food stored in the cellars. The first signs were ominous. The attackers brought a mammoth cannon that required forty horses to move, and on the second day they began to unleash a barrage against the north turret. Inside, conditions grew monstrous. The wounded lay in filth, and the garrison had to scrape moss from the walls to boil as soup. The commander knew that a direct fight would be futile. Instead, he sent small groups out at night to evade the enemy lines and burn their supplies. Each raid was a small thing, but its effects would ripple through the camp, where hunger was by then enormously worse than inside the walls. After eleven weeks, with winter approaching, the besieging army withdrew. The fortress had survived its greatest peril, though only ninety of its defenders were still alive to see the enemy leave.","ja":"カルマーの要塞は深い谷を見下ろす崖の上に立ち、3世紀の間、攻め落とすのは不可能だと考えられていた。1644年の春、1万2,000人の軍がそうではないことを証明しにやって来た。600人に満たない守備隊は、ただ一つの井戸と地下室に蓄えた食料に頼っていた。最初の兆しは不吉だった。攻撃側は動かすのに馬40頭を要する巨大な大砲を持ち込み、2日目には北の小塔に対して集中砲火を浴びせ始めた。内部の状況はひどいものになった。負傷者は汚物の中に横たわり、守備隊は壁からコケをこすり取って汁として煮なければならなかった。司令官は、正面からの戦いは無駄だと知っていた。その代わり、夜に小さな部隊を送り出して敵の戦線をすり抜けさせ、補給物資を焼かせた。一つひとつの襲撃は小さなものだったが、その影響は敵の野営地に波紋のように広がった。そこではその頃までに、飢えが城壁の中よりもはるかにひどくなっていたのだ。11週間後、冬が近づく中、包囲軍は撤退した。要塞は最大の危機を生き延びたが、敵が去るのを見届けるまで生きていた守備兵は90人だけだった。","words":[["abyss","freq:abyss"],["reliant","freq:reliant"],["ominous","freq:ominous"],["mammoth","freq:mammoth"],["unleash","freq:unleash"],["barrage","freq:barrage"],["turret","freq:turret"],["monstrous","freq:monstrous"],["filth","freq:filth"],["scrape","freq:scrape"],["futile","freq:futile"],["evade","freq:evade"],["ripple","freq:ripple"],["enormously","freq:enormously"],["peril","freq:peril"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did the commander choose to fight?","o":["By attacking the cannon directly","By sending small groups at night to destroy enemy supplies","By asking for a truce","By poisoning the well"],"a":1,"why":"sent small groups out at night to evade the enemy lines and burn their supplies（夜に小部隊を出して補給物資を焼かせた）とある。"},{"q":"Why did the besieging army finally leave?","o":["The fortress walls collapsed.","Their cannon was captured.","They were short of food and winter was coming.","A larger army arrived to help the defenders."],"a":2,"why":"野営地では hunger was ... enormously worse であり、with winter approaching, the besieging army withdrew とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0077","page":"freq","group":"1001〜1100","kind":"花屋の話","text":"Marta Lindqvist has run the flower stall at Central Station for thirty years, and she has seen habits change. In the 1990s, she says, it was commonplace for men to buy a bouquet on Friday evening, irrespective of the occasion. Today her customers are principally tourists and office workers buying for themselves. The stall is tiny, and therein lies its charm. A brass buzzer on the counter, never used, dates from the days when the station had a porter. Lindqvist is seventy-one but still agile enough to climb the ladder to the top shelf. Her skill often goes unnoticed. She can tell at a glance which flowers will last a week and which will fade by morning, and she refuses to sell the second kind at full price. She opens at six every morning, before the first trains arrive, and closes when the last flowers are gone. Any leftover stock goes to a nearby hospital at closing time. A reputable chain has twice offered to buy the stall. She has refused both times. \"They would keep my name on the sign,\" she says, \"and change everything behind it.\"","ja":"マルタ・リンドクビストは中央駅の花の売店を30年営んでおり、習慣が変わるのを見てきた。1990年代には、どんな機会かに関係なく、男性が金曜の夕方に花束を買うのはありふれたことだったと彼女は言う。今日、彼女の客は主に観光客と、自分のために買う会社員だ。売店はごく小さく、そこにこそその魅力がある。カウンターの上の、一度も使われない真ちゅうのブザーは、駅に荷物運びの係がいた時代のものだ。リンドクビストは71歳だが、一番上の棚まではしごを登れるほど今も機敏だ。彼女の技能はしばしば気づかれないままになる。どの花が1週間もち、どの花が朝までにしおれるかを一目で見分けることができ、後者を定価で売ることを拒む。彼女は毎朝6時、始発の列車が着く前に店を開け、最後の花がなくなると閉める。残った花は閉店時に近くの病院へ届けられる。評判のよいチェーン店が2度、売店の買い取りを申し出た。彼女は2度とも断った。「看板には私の名前を残して、その裏にあるものを全部変えてしまうでしょうから」と彼女は言う。","words":[["commonplace","freq:commonplace"],["bouquet","freq:bouquet"],["irrespective","freq:irrespective"],["principally","freq:principally"],["therein","freq:therein"],["buzzer","freq:buzzer"],["agile","freq:agile"],["unnoticed","freq:unnoticed"],["leftover","freq:leftover"],["reputable","freq:reputable"]],"qs":[{"q":"How have Lindqvist's customers changed?","o":["They now buy only on Fridays.","They are now mostly tourists and office workers buying for themselves.","They no longer use the station.","They prefer artificial flowers."],"a":1,"why":"Today her customers are principally tourists and office workers buying for themselves とある。"},{"q":"Why has she refused to sell the stall?","o":["The offers were too low.","She plans to give it to the hospital.","She believes the buyer would change everything except the name.","The station will not allow it."],"a":2,"why":"They would keep my name on the sign ... and change everything behind it（名前だけ残してすべてを変えてしまう）と言っている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0078","page":"freq","group":"1101〜1200","kind":"植民地支配の遺産","text":"A century after independence, the island republic of Meridia is still debating the legacy of imperialism. The colonization of the island began in 1790, when a trading company built a small outpost on the northern coast. Within fifty years, the company controlled the whole territory. It kept in place a feudal system of land ownership, imposed a nightly curfew on the towns, and employed officials to censor every newspaper. The annals of the period record a series of risings, each followed by punitive raids on the villages involved. Universal suffrage came only in 1921. Today the argument is about land. Descendants of the original farmers say they are the rightful owners of estates still held by foreign firms. A populist party has called for retribution in the form of seizure without payment. More moderate voices warn that this would have a crippling effect on exports. Last month, parliament passed a bipartisan bill that tries to find a middle way. Estates above a certain size will be bought by the state and divided. Whether the price offered is equitable is already being questioned in the courts, but for the first time in decades, both sides are at least talking.","ja":"独立から1世紀たった今も、島国メリディア共和国は帝国主義の遺産について議論を続けている。島の植民地化は1790年、ある貿易会社が北の海岸に小さな拠点を築いたときに始まった。50年のうちに、会社は領土全体を支配した。会社は土地所有の封建的な制度を維持し、町に毎晩の外出禁止令を課し、あらゆる新聞を検閲する役人を雇った。その時代の年代記は一連の蜂起を記録しており、そのたびに関わった村への懲罰的な襲撃が続いた。普通選挙権が実現したのは1921年になってからだ。今日の議論は土地をめぐるものだ。元の農民の子孫たちは、今も外国企業が保有する農園の正当な所有者は自分たちだと言う。ある大衆迎合的な政党は、補償なしの接収という形での報復を求めている。より穏健な人々は、それは輸出に壊滅的な影響を与えると警告する。先月、議会は中間の道を探ろうとする超党派の法案を可決した。一定の規模を超える農園は国が買い上げて分割する。提示される価格が公正かどうかはすでに裁判所で争われているが、数十年ぶりに、少なくとも双方が話し合っている。","words":[["imperialism","freq:imperialism"],["colonization","freq:colonization"],["outpost","freq:outpost"],["feudal","freq:feudal"],["curfew","freq:curfew"],["censor","freq:censor"],["annals","freq:annals"],["punitive","freq:punitive"],["suffrage","freq:suffrage"],["rightful","freq:rightful"],["populist","freq:populist"],["retribution","freq:retribution"],["crippling","freq:crippling"],["bipartisan","freq:bipartisan"],["equitable","freq:equitable"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is the main subject of today's argument in Meridia?","o":["Who should own the large estates","Whether to restore the curfew","When to hold the next election","How to rewrite the history books"],"a":0,"why":"Today the argument is about land（今日の議論は土地をめぐるもの）とあり、農園の所有者が争点である。"},{"q":"What does the new law do?","o":["It seizes all foreign property without payment.","It bans foreign firms from exporting.","It has the state buy and divide the largest estates.","It returns the island to the trading company."],"a":2,"why":"Estates above a certain size will be bought by the state and divided（一定規模を超える農園を国が買い上げて分割する）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0079","page":"freq","group":"1101〜1200","kind":"企業をめぐる訴訟","text":"The legal troubles of the Corvan Group grew more serious this week. The company, notoriously secretive about its finances, already faces an antitrust inquiry into its purchase of three rival firms. On Monday a former accountant filed an affidavit claiming that managers had hidden the depreciation of factory equipment in order to make profits look larger. He also alleges that the main beneficiary of several large payments was an undisclosed company owned by the chairman's brother. Corvan calls the claims shameless lies and has threatened to sue for defamation. Its lawyers insist on the legality of every payment and say that all of them were tax deductible. The firm's structure is so opaque, however, that even regulators have struggled to follow the money. A second case concerns a fire at a Corvan warehouse in which a guard died. Prosecutors have charged the site manager with manslaughter. The company itself faces only a misdemeanor charge, which would bring a fine, though a hefty one. Lawyers say the key issue in both cases will be attribution: who actually made the decisions. Corvan's shares have fallen by a third since January.","ja":"コーバン・グループの法的な問題は、今週さらに深刻になった。財務について秘密主義で悪名高い同社は、競合3社の買収をめぐってすでに独占禁止法上の調査を受けている。月曜には元会計担当者が宣誓供述書を提出し、経営幹部が利益を大きく見せるために工場設備の価値の下落を隠していたと主張した。彼はまた、いくつかの多額の支払いの主な受取人は、会長の兄が所有する公表されていない会社だったと申し立てている。コーバンはこれらの主張を恥知らずなうそだと呼び、名誉毀損で訴えると警告している。同社の弁護士たちは、どの支払いも合法であり、すべて税控除の対象だったと主張する。しかし、会社の構造があまりに不透明なため、規制当局でさえ金の流れを追うのに苦労してきた。2つ目の事件は、警備員が死亡したコーバンの倉庫の火事に関するものだ。検察は現場責任者を過失致死で起訴した。会社自体は軽犯罪の容疑を受けているだけで、科されるのは罰金だが、多額になる。弁護士たちによれば、どちらの事件でも鍵となる争点は、誰が実際に決定を下したのかという責任の帰属だ。コーバンの株価は1月以来、3分の1下落している。","words":[["notoriously","freq:notoriously"],["antitrust","freq:antitrust"],["affidavit","freq:affidavit"],["depreciation","freq:depreciation"],["beneficiary","freq:beneficiary"],["undisclosed","freq:undisclosed"],["shameless","freq:shameless"],["defamation","freq:defamation"],["legality","freq:legality"],["deductible","freq:deductible"],["opaque","freq:opaque"],["manslaughter","freq:manslaughter"],["misdemeanor","freq:misdemeanor"],["hefty","freq:hefty"],["attribution","freq:attribution"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does the former accountant claim?","o":["The company bought its rivals illegally.","Managers concealed falling equipment values to inflate profits.","The chairman started the warehouse fire.","Regulators accepted secret payments."],"a":1,"why":"managers had hidden the depreciation of factory equipment in order to make profits look larger とある。"},{"q":"What will be the central question in both cases?","o":["How large the fines should be","Whether the accountant can be trusted","How much the shares have fallen","Who was really responsible for the decisions"],"a":3,"why":"the key issue ... will be attribution: who actually made the decisions（誰が実際に決定を下したか）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0080","page":"freq","group":"1101〜1200","kind":"川辺の生態調査","text":"The river otter almost disappeared from the Dunmore valley in the 1970s. Today it is returning, and a team of biologists is trying to understand why. Their study area is a wooded stretch of river surrounded by otherwise arid hills. The otter is not endemic to the region, but it has lived there for thousands of years, and its presence shows that the water is clean. Counting the animals is difficult. The researchers place small plastic tubes along the bank, each lined with an adhesive strip that collects hair. In the laboratory, an electrode is used to separate DNA from each sample. The animals also respond to stimuli in unpredictable ways, which adds to the variability of the results. One finding is clear. Otters prefer banks of porous rock, where they can dig, and avoid areas where the soil has reached saturation after flooding. A map of the valley, with each contour line marked, shows their dens arranged almost like a constellation along the river. The biologists compare the otter to a canary in a mine; its role is analogous. One skeletal animal found last winter was a warning. To conserve the species, they say, the whole river must be protected.","ja":"カワウソは1970年代にダンモア谷からほとんど姿を消した。今日、カワウソは戻りつつあり、生物学者のチームがその理由を理解しようとしている。調査地は、ほかは乾燥した丘に囲まれた、木の茂った川の一区間だ。カワウソはこの地域の固有種ではないが、何千年もそこにすんできた動物で、その存在は水がきれいであることを示している。数を数えるのは難しい。研究者たちは岸に沿って小さなプラスチックの筒を置く。筒の内側には毛を集める粘着性の帯が張ってある。研究室では、電極を使って試料ごとにDNAを分離する。この動物は刺激に対して予測できない反応をすることもあり、それが結果のばらつきを大きくする。一つの発見ははっきりしている。カワウソは穴を掘ることのできる多孔質の岩の岸を好み、洪水の後に土が水で飽和した場所を避ける。等高線を一本ずつ記した谷の地図は、彼らの巣穴がほとんど星座のように川に沿って並んでいることを示している。生物学者たちはカワウソを炭鉱のカナリアにたとえる。その役割は似ているのだ。昨年の冬に見つかった、やせ細った1匹は警告だった。この種を保護するには川全体を守らなければならないと彼らは言う。","words":[["otter","freq:otter"],["wooded","freq:wooded"],["arid","freq:arid"],["endemic","freq:endemic"],["adhesive","freq:adhesive"],["electrode","freq:electrode"],["stimuli","freq:stimuli"],["variability","freq:variability"],["porous","freq:porous"],["saturation","freq:saturation"],["contour","freq:contour"],["constellation","freq:constellation"],["analogous","freq:analogous"],["skeletal","freq:skeletal"],["conserve","freq:conserve"]],"qs":[{"q":"How do the researchers count the otters?","o":["By catching them in nets","By collecting hair and analyzing its DNA","By following them at night","By asking local farmers"],"a":1,"why":"adhesive strip that collects hair とあり、研究室で DNA を分離するとある。"},{"q":"Where do otters prefer to make their dens?","o":["In banks of rock that they can dig into","In soil soaked by floods","In the dry hills","In plastic tubes"],"a":0,"why":"Otters prefer banks of porous rock, where they can dig（穴を掘れる多孔質の岩の岸を好む）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0081","page":"freq","group":"1101〜1200","kind":"俳優の自伝","text":"The actor Daniel Roth has written a memoir that is unusually honest about his early life. He describes a wretched childhood. His mother fell ill in his infancy, and an aunt became a kind of surrogate parent. By adolescence he was an angry boy with an inclination to fight, and he admits that he was downright obnoxious to anyone who tried to help. Teachers found his antics tiring. He still appears to resent one of them, who told him that his dream of acting was a delusion. The turning point came at seventeen, when a youth theater took him on tour. The work demanded stamina, with two performances a day, and it turned his energy inward for the first time. The later chapters are more subdued. Roth writes calmly about the hysteria that surrounded his first film, and about the loneliness that followed. He does not pretend that fame solved anything. The book ends not with a triumphant award ceremony but with a quiet scene: the actor, now fifty, sitting in his aunt's kitchen and finally thanking her. It is a modest ending, and it suits the book.","ja":"俳優ダニエル・ロスは、若い頃について異例なほど正直な回顧録を書いた。彼はみじめな子ども時代を描いている。母親は彼の幼年期に病に倒れ、おばが一種の親代わりになった。思春期になる頃には、彼はけんかをしたがる傾向のある怒れる少年になっており、助けようとする人には誰にでもまったく感じが悪かったと認めている。教師たちは彼のばかげた行動に手を焼いた。彼は今も、そのうちの一人に腹を立てているようだ。俳優になるという夢は妄想だと彼に言った教師である。転機は17歳のとき、ある青少年劇団が彼を巡業に連れて行ったときに訪れた。その仕事は1日2回の公演という持久力を要するもので、彼のエネルギーを初めて内側に向けさせた。後半の章はもっと抑えた調子だ。ロスは、最初の映画を取り巻いた熱狂と、その後に続いた孤独について、落ち着いて書いている。名声が何かを解決したふりはしない。この本は、勝ち誇った授賞式ではなく、静かな場面で終わる。50歳になった俳優が、おばの台所に座り、ついに彼女に感謝を伝える場面だ。控えめな結末だが、この本にふさわしい。","words":[["wretched","freq:wretched"],["infancy","freq:infancy"],["surrogate","freq:surrogate"],["adolescence","freq:adolescence"],["inclination","freq:inclination"],["downright","freq:downright"],["obnoxious","freq:obnoxious"],["antics","freq:antics"],["resent","freq:resent"],["delusion","freq:delusion"],["stamina","freq:stamina"],["inward","freq:inward"],["subdued","freq:subdued"],["hysteria","freq:hysteria"],["triumphant","freq:triumphant"]],"qs":[{"q":"What changed Roth at seventeen?","o":["A teacher praised his acting.","His mother recovered.","He went on tour with a youth theater.","He won his first award."],"a":2,"why":"The turning point came at seventeen, when a youth theater took him on tour（青少年劇団が巡業に連れて行った）とある。"},{"q":"How does the memoir end?","o":["With his first film","With a quiet moment of thanks to his aunt","With an award ceremony","With an argument with a teacher"],"a":1,"why":"sitting in his aunt's kitchen and finally thanking her（おばの台所で感謝を伝える）場面で終わる。"}]},
{"id":"f-0082","page":"freq","group":"1101〜1200","kind":"古典の新版","text":"This year marks the centennial of The Glass City, the novel in which Ivo Marten imagined a perfect society, and a new edition has been published to celebrate it. In a long preface, the editor explains why the book still matters. Marten's utopia was not a village of happy farmers. It was a cosmopolitan city of towers and moving streets, a futuristic vision that must have seemed absurd in 1926. The hallmark of his writing is its calm. He never shouts, and he avoids every rhetorical trick. The edition is exemplary in its care. It includes a synopsis of each chapter, notes on the archaic words Marten liked, and even a list explaining each acronym he invented for the city's many committees. The editor also reprints a short treatise in which Marten set out his political ideas. Here the picture is less comfortable. His perfect city has no place for the illiterate, and critics have long held divergent views on whether he was a democrat at all. Whatever the answer, the book has left a deep imprint on later writers, and this handsome volume shows why.","ja":"今年は、イボ・マルテンが完全な社会を思い描いた小説「ガラスの都市」の100周年に当たり、それを祝って新版が出版された。長い序文で、編者はこの本が今も重要である理由を説明している。マルテンの理想郷は、幸福な農民の村ではなかった。それは塔と動く通りを持つ国際的な都市であり、1926年にはばかげて見えたに違いない未来的な構想だった。彼の文章の特徴は落ち着きにある。彼は決して声を荒らげず、あらゆる修辞的な小細工を避ける。この版は行き届いた配慮の点で模範的だ。各章の概要、マルテンが好んだ古風な語についての注、さらには都市の多くの委員会のために彼が作り出した頭字語を一つずつ説明する一覧まで収めている。編者は、マルテンが自らの政治思想を述べた短い論文も再録している。ここでは、印象はあまり心地よくない。彼の完全な都市には読み書きのできない人の居場所がなく、彼がそもそも民主主義者だったのかについて、批評家たちは長く異なる見解を持ってきた。答えがどうであれ、この本は後の作家たちに深い跡を残しており、この立派な一冊がその理由を示している。","words":[["centennial","freq:centennial"],["preface","freq:preface"],["utopia","freq:utopia"],["cosmopolitan","freq:cosmopolitan"],["futuristic","freq:futuristic"],["hallmark","freq:hallmark"],["rhetorical","freq:rhetorical"],["exemplary","freq:exemplary"],["synopsis","freq:synopsis"],["archaic","freq:archaic"],["acronym","freq:acronym"],["treatise","freq:treatise"],["illiterate","freq:illiterate"],["divergent","freq:divergent"],["imprint","freq:imprint"]],"qs":[{"q":"What kind of ideal society did Marten imagine?","o":["A village of contented farmers","A modern, international city","An island without government","A country ruled by writers"],"a":1,"why":"a cosmopolitan city of towers and moving streets（塔と動く通りを持つ国際的な都市）とある。"},{"q":"Why is the picture \"less comfortable\" in his treatise?","o":["It shows that he copied other writers.","It is written in a shouting style.","It was never finished.","His perfect city excludes people who cannot read."],"a":3,"why":"His perfect city has no place for the illiterate（読み書きのできない人の居場所がない）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0083","page":"freq","group":"1101〜1200","kind":"観光地の変化","text":"Twenty years ago, the hill village of Castel Morra was a spartan place. It had one shop, one bar, and a rustic inn with six rooms. Then a magazine printed a photograph of the panorama from its church tower, an expansive view over three valleys, and everything changed. The first visitors were walkers. Growth was incremental, and the village adapted. But the past five years have seen a buildup of a different kind. An affluent crowd now arrives each weekend for gourmet dinners, and a colossal hotel has been built on the ridge. In August the car parks overflow by ten in the morning. Residents describe a pervasive sense that the place no longer belongs to them. A sizable number have sold their houses to investors, some of whom use predatory tactics, offering cash to elderly owners and pressing them to decide at once. The mayor is under attack from both sides. Hotel owners want him to loosen planning rules; residents want them tightened. Each new argument provides fodder for the national press, which brings still more visitors to see what the fuss is about.","ja":"20年前、丘の村カステル・モッラは質素な場所だった。店が1軒、酒場が1軒、そして6部屋の素朴な宿があった。その後、ある雑誌が教会の塔からの全景、3つの谷を見渡す広大な眺めの写真を載せ、すべてが変わった。最初の訪問者は徒歩旅行者だった。成長は少しずつで、村は適応した。しかしこの5年は、違う種類の増加が見られた。今では裕福な人々が毎週末、高級な夕食を目当てにやって来て、尾根には巨大なホテルが建った。8月には駐車場が午前10時までにあふれる。住民たちは、この場所がもう自分たちのものではないという感覚が広く行き渡っていると語る。かなりの数の住民が家を投資家に売った。投資家の中には、高齢の所有者に現金を示してその場で決めるよう迫るという、人を食い物にするやり方を使う者もいる。村長は両側から攻撃されている。ホテルの経営者は建築計画の規則を緩めるよう求め、住民は厳しくするよう求める。新しい論争が起こるたびに全国紙に格好の材料を与え、それが、騒ぎの中身を見ようとするさらに多くの訪問者を連れてくる。","words":[["spartan","freq:spartan"],["rustic","freq:rustic"],["panorama","freq:panorama"],["expansive","freq:expansive"],["incremental","freq:incremental"],["buildup","freq:buildup"],["affluent","freq:affluent"],["gourmet","freq:gourmet"],["colossal","freq:colossal"],["overflow","freq:overflow"],["pervasive","freq:pervasive"],["sizable","freq:sizable"],["predatory","freq:predatory"],["loosen","freq:loosen"],["fodder","freq:fodder"]],"qs":[{"q":"What first brought visitors to the village?","o":["A new hotel","A famous restaurant","A photograph of the view","A speech by the mayor"],"a":2,"why":"a magazine printed a photograph of the panorama from its church tower（教会の塔からの全景の写真）がきっかけだった。"},{"q":"Why is the mayor criticized from both sides?","o":["Hotel owners want fewer rules, while residents want stricter ones.","He sold his own house to investors.","He refused to speak to the press.","He built the hotel on the ridge."],"a":0,"why":"Hotel owners want him to loosen planning rules; residents want them tightened とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0084","page":"freq","group":"1101〜1200","kind":"西部の伝説","text":"The story of the bandit Elias Crane is still told in the mountain towns of the old frontier. According to legend, Crane began as a farmer. One spring a rich landowner diverted the river, and Crane's fields turned to dust. He could only rake dry earth where wheat had grown. The change in him was abrupt. He gathered a small band, and for six years they robbed the landowner's wagons, giving a share to poor families. Storytellers like to sprinkle the tale with heroic details. In one version, Crane manages to wrench a rifle from a guard without firing a shot. In another, he would never fumble a knot or miss a trail, even in the dark. The truth was probably less noble. Records show that his men were often murderous, and that he struggled to exert control over them. When a new sheriff arrived, determined to rein in the gang, several members betrayed their leader for a reward. Crane was hanged in 1881. His death did not end the story. It seemed instead to spawn a hundred songs, and his name is still carved on the bridge where he was captured.","ja":"盗賊イライアス・クレインの物語は、かつての辺境の山の町で今も語り継がれている。伝説によれば、クレインは農夫として出発した。ある春、裕福な地主が川の流れを変え、クレインの畑は砂ぼこりと化した。小麦が育っていた場所で、彼は乾いた土を熊手でかくことしかできなかった。彼の変わりようは突然だった。小さな一団を集め、6年間、地主の荷馬車を襲い、分け前を貧しい家族に与えた。語り手たちは、この話に英雄的な細部を振りまくのを好む。ある語りでは、クレインは一発も撃たずに警備の者から小銃をもぎ取る。別の語りでは、彼は暗闇の中でも結び目をしくじったり、道を見失ったりすることが決してなかったという。真実はおそらくそれほど気高くはなかった。記録によれば、彼の手下はしばしば残忍で、彼は手下を統制するのに苦労していた。一味を抑え込むと決意した新しい保安官が着任すると、数人の仲間が賞金目当てに頭を裏切った。クレインは1881年に絞首刑になった。彼の死は物語を終わらせなかった。むしろ100もの歌を生み出したようで、彼の名は今も、彼が捕らえられた橋に刻まれている。","words":[["bandit","freq:bandit"],["rake","freq:rake"],["abrupt","freq:abrupt"],["sprinkle","freq:sprinkle"],["wrench","freq:wrench"],["fumble","freq:fumble"],["murderous","freq:murderous"],["exert","freq:exert"],["rein","freq:rein"],["spawn","freq:spawn"]],"qs":[{"q":"According to the legend, why did Crane become an outlaw?","o":["A landowner's actions ruined his farm.","He lost a fight with a sheriff.","He was cheated by his own men.","He wanted to become famous in songs."],"a":0,"why":"a rich landowner diverted the river, and Crane's fields turned to dust（地主が川の流れを変え、畑がだめになった）とある。"},{"q":"What do the records suggest about the real Crane?","o":["He never robbed anyone.","He was hanged by mistake.","His men were violent and hard for him to control.","He gave all his money to the sheriff."],"a":2,"why":"his men were often murderous, and that he struggled to exert control over them とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0085","page":"freq","group":"1201〜1300","kind":"群島国家の統一問題","text":"The question of unification has dominated politics in the Sorel archipelago for thirty years. The northern and southern islands were separated under colonialism, when two rival powers divided the territory between them, and they have developed very differently since. The north is richer; much of the south is impoverished, and the socioeconomic gap grows wider each year. Last spring the dispute threatened to escalate into open conflict. After a unilateral declaration of independence by southern leaders, northern forces began a bombardment of the old citadel in the southern capital. The devastation was limited, but the populace was terrified, and a police crackdown on protesters followed in both regions. A ceasefire has held since June. Few people believe, however, that the matter is settled. A vote on the future of the islands is planned for next year, and it will be deeply divisive. Polls suggest that neither full unity nor full separation has majority support. Economists warn that a different danger may loom larger than war. If investment continues to fall, they say, both regions face a decline that will last for the foreseeable future, whatever flag flies over them.","ja":"統一の問題は、30年にわたってソレル群島の政治の中心にあり続けてきた。北の島々と南の島々は、対立する2つの大国が領土を分け合った植民地主義の時代に切り離され、それ以来、非常に異なる発展を遂げてきた。北のほうが豊かで、南の多くは貧困にあえいでおり、社会経済的な格差は年々広がっている。昨年の春、この争いは公然たる紛争へと激化するおそれがあった。南の指導者たちが一方的に独立を宣言した後、北の軍は南の首都にある古い城塞への砲撃を始めた。壊滅的な被害は限られていたが、民衆はおびえ、両地域で抗議者に対する警察の取り締まりが続いた。6月以降、停戦は保たれている。しかし、問題が決着したと信じる人はほとんどいない。島々の将来についての投票が来年予定されており、それは深い対立を生むだろう。世論調査によれば、完全な統一も完全な分離も過半数の支持を得ていない。経済学者たちは、別の危険が戦争よりも大きく迫っているかもしれないと警告する。投資が減り続ければ、どの旗が掲げられようと、両地域は当面続く衰退に直面すると彼らは言う。","words":[["unification","freq:unification"],["archipelago","freq:archipelago"],["colonialism","freq:colonialism"],["impoverished","freq:impoverished"],["socioeconomic","freq:socioeconomic"],["escalate","freq:escalate"],["unilateral","freq:unilateral"],["bombardment","freq:bombardment"],["citadel","freq:citadel"],["devastation","freq:devastation"],["populace","freq:populace"],["crackdown","freq:crackdown"],["divisive","freq:divisive"],["loom","freq:loom"],["foreseeable","freq:foreseeable"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the two groups of islands develop differently?","o":["They were divided between two powers in colonial times.","They speak unrelated languages.","The north has no farmland.","The south refused all trade."],"a":0,"why":"separated under colonialism, when two rival powers divided the territory（植民地時代に2つの大国が領土を分けた）とある。"},{"q":"What do economists fear more than war?","o":["A rise in population","A continued fall in investment","The result of the vote","Another declaration of independence"],"a":1,"why":"If investment continues to fall ... both regions face a decline（投資が減り続ければ両地域が衰退する）と警告している。"}]},
{"id":"f-0086","page":"freq","group":"1201〜1300","kind":"風刺雑誌の裁判","text":"A satirical magazine has won a legal battle that many expected it to lose. Two years ago, the magazine published a cartoon suggesting that a wealthy businessman was the mastermind behind a phony charity. He sued for libel, and his lawyers demanded damages so large that they would have closed the magazine. They also issued a subpoena for the editor's private notes, and one of them told reporters that she deserved incarceration. The editor, an outspoken critic of the rich, refused to hand anything over. In court, the businessman's team argued that the cartoon was written with malice and was intended to inflict lasting harm. They added a claim of slander, based on remarks she had made on the radio. The judge was not persuaded. Ridicule of the powerful, he said, is an old tradition, and the sums demanded were disproportionate. He described one of the claims as ludicrous. The businessman had sued ostensibly to protect his reputation, the judge noted, yet the charity's accounts told their own story. The victory is undeniable, but it was expensive. The magazine's legal costs were three times its annual profit, and readers are being asked to help.","ja":"ある風刺雑誌が、負けると多くの人が予想していた法廷闘争に勝った。2年前、この雑誌は、ある裕福な実業家がいんちきの慈善団体の首謀者であることをほのめかす漫画を掲載した。彼は名誉毀損で訴え、弁護士たちは雑誌を廃刊に追い込むほど多額の損害賠償を求めた。彼らは編集長の個人的なメモの提出を求める召喚状も出し、弁護士の一人は記者たちに、彼女は投獄に値すると語った。金持ちを遠慮なく批判する編集長は、何一つ引き渡すことを拒んだ。法廷で実業家側は、その漫画は悪意をもって描かれ、長く続く害を与えることを意図したものだと主張した。彼女がラジオでした発言に基づき、口頭での中傷という請求も加えた。裁判官は納得しなかった。権力者をあざけることは古い伝統であり、求められた金額は不釣り合いだと彼は述べた。請求の一つについては、ばかげていると表現した。実業家は表向きは評判を守るために訴えたが、慈善団体の会計がおのずと真相を語っていると裁判官は指摘した。勝利は否定できないが、高くついた。雑誌の訴訟費用は年間の利益の3倍で、読者に支援が呼びかけられている。","words":[["satirical","freq:satirical"],["mastermind","freq:mastermind"],["phony","freq:phony"],["libel","freq:libel"],["subpoena","freq:subpoena"],["incarceration","freq:incarceration"],["outspoken","freq:outspoken"],["malice","freq:malice"],["inflict","freq:inflict"],["slander","freq:slander"],["Ridicule","freq:ridicule"],["disproportionate","freq:disproportionate"],["ludicrous","freq:ludicrous"],["ostensibly","freq:ostensibly"],["undeniable","freq:undeniable"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the cartoon suggest about the businessman?","o":["He owned the magazine.","He was behind a fake charity.","He had avoided paying tax.","He had bribed a judge."],"a":1,"why":"the mastermind behind a phony charity（いんちきの慈善団体の首謀者）であるとほのめかした。"},{"q":"Why is the magazine asking its readers for help?","o":["It lost the case.","Its editor has been jailed.","It has been banned from radio.","Its legal costs were far greater than its profits."],"a":3,"why":"legal costs were three times its annual profit（訴訟費用が年間利益の3倍）だったためである。"}]},
{"id":"f-0087","page":"freq","group":"1201〜1300","kind":"記憶の研究","text":"A fifty-year-old teacher was brought to a hospital in Lyon last year with complete amnesia. He could speak, read, and play the piano, but he could not say who he was. Doctors at first suspected that he was intoxicated, then that he had suffered a stroke. Tests showed neither. His case has become the center of a study on memory and cognition. The research team has been able to pinpoint unusual activity in a small region of the brain that seems to inhibit the recall of personal events. They do not consider the condition pathological in the usual sense, since the tissue is healthy. Their conjecture is that a chemical imbalance is responsible. Blood samples were slightly acidic, and the patient had eaten large amounts of a food additive now under investigation. A causal link has not been proved. The man's recovery has been erratic. Some days he remembers his children; on others he has an involuntary fear of his own house. His sense of taste has changed too, and his palate rejects foods he once loved. Doctors call it a borderline case that fits no textbook. Studies of the developing embryo, they hope, may explain how the region forms.","ja":"昨年、50歳の教師が完全な記憶喪失の状態でリヨンの病院に運ばれた。彼は話すことも、読むことも、ピアノを弾くこともできたが、自分が誰なのかを言えなかった。医師たちは最初、彼が酔っているのではないかと疑い、次に脳卒中を起こしたのではないかと疑った。検査ではどちらでもなかった。彼の症例は、記憶と認知についての研究の中心になった。研究チームは、個人的な出来事を思い出すことを抑えているらしい脳の小さな領域に、異常な活動があることを正確に突き止めた。組織は健康なので、彼らはこの状態を通常の意味での病的なものとは考えていない。彼らの推測では、化学的な不均衡が原因だ。血液の試料はわずかに酸性で、患者は現在調査中のある食品添加物を大量に食べていた。因果関係は証明されていない。この男性の回復は不安定だ。子どもたちのことを覚えている日もあれば、自分の家に対して思わず恐怖を感じる日もある。味覚も変わり、かつて大好きだった食べ物を彼の舌は受けつけない。医師たちはこれを、どの教科書にも当てはまらない、どっちつかずの症例と呼ぶ。発達中の胚の研究が、その領域がどう形成されるかを説明してくれるかもしれないと、彼らは期待している。","words":[["amnesia","freq:amnesia"],["intoxicated","freq:intoxicated"],["cognition","freq:cognition"],["pinpoint","freq:pinpoint"],["inhibit","freq:inhibit"],["pathological","freq:pathological"],["conjecture","freq:conjecture"],["acidic","freq:acidic"],["additive","freq:additive"],["causal","freq:causal"],["erratic","freq:erratic"],["involuntary","freq:involuntary"],["palate","freq:palate"],["borderline","freq:borderline"],["embryo","freq:embryo"]],"qs":[{"q":"What could the patient not do when he arrived at the hospital?","o":["Read","Play the piano","Say who he was","Speak"],"a":2,"why":"he could not say who he was（自分が誰なのかを言えなかった）とある。"},{"q":"What do the researchers suspect is the cause?","o":["A chemical imbalance, possibly linked to something he ate","A stroke that damaged the tissue","Heavy drinking","An injury to his hands"],"a":0,"why":"Their conjecture is that a chemical imbalance is responsible とあり、a food additive を大量に食べていたとある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0088","page":"freq","group":"1201〜1300","kind":"中世の写本","text":"The monastery of St. Aldric owns one of the finest medieval books in Europe, and a recent study has revealed its strange history. The book is a collection of prayers, famous for the illumination of its pages in gold and blue. It was made around 1320 for a devout noblewoman whose pedigree is traced on the first page in an elaborate genealogy. Each chapter opens with an ornamental letter. One shows a bearded saint preparing to slay a dragon; another shows a king in regal robes kneeling to repent. The surprise lies in the margins. There, in tiny writing, an unknown monk recorded his doubts about the adherence of his brothers to their vows. He describes their lives as sinful and mocks the lofty language of the abbot. Such notes came close to heresy, and their author risked severe punishment. Why were they never removed? The study suggests that later monks simply did not look closely at a book they had placed on a pedestal as a holy treasure. The monastery has now agreed to the propagation of the whole text online, margins included.","ja":"聖オルドリック修道院は、ヨーロッパで最も見事な中世の本の一つを所蔵しており、最近の研究がその奇妙な歴史を明らかにした。その本は祈りの言葉を集めたもので、金と青によるページの彩飾で有名だ。1320年頃、ある信心深い貴族の女性のために作られたもので、彼女の血統は最初のページの手の込んだ家系図にたどられている。各章は装飾的な文字で始まる。一つは竜を退治しようとするひげを生やした聖人を、別の一つは王者らしい衣をまとってひざまずき、悔い改める王を描いている。驚きは余白にある。そこには、名前のわからない一人の修道士が、仲間の修道士たちが誓いを守っているかについての疑いを、小さな文字で書き留めていた。彼は仲間の生活を罪深いと述べ、修道院長の高尚な言葉づかいをあざけっている。こうした書き込みは異端に近く、書いた者は厳しい罰を受ける危険を冒していた。なぜそれらは消されなかったのか。この研究は、後の修道士たちが、聖なる宝として台座に祭り上げた本をただ詳しく見なかったのだと示唆している。修道院は今回、余白も含めた全文をネットで広く公開することに同意した。","words":[["illumination","freq:illumination"],["devout","freq:devout"],["pedigree","freq:pedigree"],["genealogy","freq:genealogy"],["ornamental","freq:ornamental"],["bearded","freq:bearded"],["slay","freq:slay"],["regal","freq:regal"],["repent","freq:repent"],["adherence","freq:adherence"],["sinful","freq:sinful"],["lofty","freq:lofty"],["heresy","freq:heresy"],["pedestal","freq:pedestal"],["propagation","freq:propagation"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was found in the margins of the book?","o":["A monk's criticisms of his fellow monks","A list of the noblewoman's debts","Drawings of dragons","Instructions for making gold paint"],"a":0,"why":"an unknown monk recorded his doubts about the adherence of his brothers to their vows とあり、彼らの生活を sinful と述べている。"},{"q":"Why does the study think the notes were never removed?","o":["The abbot agreed with them.","The writing was too small to erase.","Later monks treated the book as sacred and did not examine it closely.","The noblewoman's family protected them."],"a":2,"why":"later monks simply did not look closely at a book they had placed on a pedestal as a holy treasure とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0089","page":"freq","group":"1201〜1300","kind":"荒野の火事","text":"For three weeks last August, a wildfire burned across the high moor above the town of Garrow. Nobody knows what happened to ignite it. The summer had been dry, the grass was sparse, and a single spark from a car's hot radiator would have been enough. Seen from afar, the smoke looked like a second range of hills. The fire destroyed most of the pasture on which local farmers depend. Sheep were once plentiful here; by October, the market was in a slump, and several farms were struggling to stay afloat. Yet the moor is recovering faster than anyone expected. Rain in September seemed to awaken seeds that had lain in the soil for decades. Plants that had become a rarity are now common, and the burned ground is overrun with young heather. Birdwatchers have reported the sleek shape of a hawk not seen in the area since the 1960s. Ecologists say the fire has taught a hard lesson. The old practice of burning small patches each spring, abandoned thirty years ago, kept large fires away. Its return, they argue, is the one alteration to present policy that would make a real difference.","ja":"昨年の8月、3週間にわたって、ガロウの町の上にある高地の荒れ野で山火事が燃え続けた。何が火をつけたのかは誰にもわからない。夏は乾燥しており、草はまばらで、車の熱くなった放熱器からの火花一つで十分だっただろう。遠くから見ると、煙はもう一つの丘の連なりのように見えた。火事は地元の農家が頼る牧草地の大半を焼いた。かつてここには羊がたくさんいた。10月には市場は不振に陥り、いくつかの農場は倒産せずにいるのがやっとだった。それでも荒れ野は誰もが予想したより速く回復しつつある。9月の雨が、何十年も土の中に眠っていた種を目覚めさせたようだ。珍しくなっていた植物が今ではありふれており、焼けた地面は若いヒースで覆い尽くされている。野鳥観察者たちは、1960年代以来この地域で見られなかったタカのつややかな姿を報告している。生態学者たちは、この火事は厳しい教訓を与えたと言う。毎年春に小さな区画を焼くという、30年前にやめられた古い慣行が、大きな火事を遠ざけていたのだ。その復活こそ、現在の方針に加えるべき、本当の違いを生む唯一の変更だと彼らは主張している。","words":[["wildfire","freq:wildfire"],["moor","freq:moor"],["ignite","freq:ignite"],["sparse","freq:sparse"],["radiator","freq:radiator"],["afar","freq:afar"],["pasture","freq:pasture"],["plentiful","freq:plentiful"],["slump","freq:slump"],["afloat","freq:afloat"],["awaken","freq:awaken"],["rarity","freq:rarity"],["overrun","freq:overrun"],["sleek","freq:sleek"],["alteration","freq:alteration"]],"qs":[{"q":"How has the moor changed since the fire?","o":["It has become a desert.","Rare plants have returned and young heather has spread.","Farmers have planted new trees.","Sheep have increased in number."],"a":1,"why":"Plants that had become a rarity are now common, and the burned ground is overrun with young heather とある。"},{"q":"What do ecologists recommend?","o":["Banning cars from the moor","Moving the farms to the valley","Planting grass every spring","Bringing back the practice of burning small areas each year"],"a":3,"why":"The old practice of burning small patches each spring ... Its return（毎年春に小区画を焼く慣行の復活）を求めている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0090","page":"freq","group":"1201〜1300","kind":"名選手の引退","text":"When the tennis champion Irena Kovacs announced her retirement last week, even her greatest nemesis sent a message of praise. For fifteen years Kovacs was the most feared player in the world, though not the most gifted. Others had more natural agility. What set her apart was temperament. She competed fiercely, and she was adept at finding an opponent's weakness and using it without mercy. Her prowess on slow courts was unmatched. Off the court she had little charisma, and she treated the press with indifference, which did not make her popular. The intrigue surrounding her private life seemed to torment her, and in later years she gave no interviews at all. Injuries began to slow her noticeably after thirty. With hindsight, she admits, she should have rested more, but she had always believed that staying vigilant about fitness would be enough. Her final season was her worst. Younger players had learned to capitalize on her declining speed, and she no longer found it advantageous to play from the back of the court. She leaves with twenty-one major titles and, at last, the affection of the crowds.","ja":"テニスの女王イレーナ・コバチが先週引退を発表したとき、彼女の最大の宿敵でさえ称賛のメッセージを送った。15年の間、コバチは世界で最も恐れられた選手だったが、最も才能に恵まれた選手ではなかった。生まれつきの機敏さではほかの選手のほうが上だった。彼女を際立たせたのは気質だった。彼女は激しく戦い、相手の弱点を見つけてそれを容赦なく突くことに熟達していた。球足の遅いコートでの彼女の腕前は並ぶものがなかった。コートの外では人を引きつける魅力に乏しく、報道陣を無関心に扱ったので、人気は出なかった。私生活を取り巻く詮索は彼女を苦しめたようで、後年はまったく取材に応じなかった。30歳を過ぎると、けがが目に見えて彼女の動きを鈍らせ始めた。後から考えればもっと休むべきだったと彼女は認めるが、体調に気を配り続ければ十分だといつも信じていたのだ。最後のシーズンは最悪だった。若い選手たちは彼女の衰えた速さにつけ込むことを覚え、彼女にとってコートの後方から戦うことはもう有利ではなくなっていた。彼女は21の主要タイトルと、ようやく得た観衆の愛情とともに去る。","words":[["nemesis","freq:nemesis"],["agility","freq:agility"],["temperament","freq:temperament"],["fiercely","freq:fiercely"],["adept","freq:adept"],["prowess","freq:prowess"],["charisma","freq:charisma"],["indifference","freq:indifference"],["intrigue","freq:intrigue"],["torment","freq:torment"],["noticeably","freq:noticeably"],["hindsight","freq:hindsight"],["vigilant","freq:vigilant"],["capitalize","freq:capitalize"],["advantageous","freq:advantageous"]],"qs":[{"q":"What made Kovacs different from other players?","o":["Her natural speed","Her popularity with reporters","Her competitive character","Her height"],"a":2,"why":"What set her apart was temperament（彼女を際立たせたのは気質）とあり、激しく戦い相手の弱点を突いたとある。"},{"q":"What does she now say she should have done?","o":["Given more interviews","Taken more rest","Changed her coach","Retired at thirty"],"a":1,"why":"With hindsight, she admits, she should have rested more（もっと休むべきだった）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0091","page":"freq","group":"1201〜1300","kind":"流行と学校","text":"Every few years a new craze sweeps through schools, and teachers must decide how to respond. The latest is a card game that pupils trade and play at every break. To call it popular would be an understatement: in one school, nine children in ten own a set. Some heads have banned the cards as an undesirable distraction. Others take a different view. At Marsh Lane School, the head teacher, who has also been hostess to several parents' evenings on the subject, argues that a ban is senseless. Children will simply play in secret, she says, and conflict will be unavoidable. Her staff have found an ingenious alternative. They use the cards in supplementary maths lessons, asking pupils to calculate the odds of each move. The effect has been conspicuous. Attendance at the optional classes has doubled, and several children who disliked numbers now ask for harder problems. The head is realistic. In a year the game will be forgotten, she says, and behavior will revert to normal. Until then, she would rather use the children's enthusiasm than fight it.","ja":"数年ごとに新しい大流行が学校を席巻し、教師たちはどう対応するかを決めなければならない。最新のものは、生徒が休み時間のたびに交換して遊ぶカードゲームだ。人気があると言うのは控えめな表現だろう。ある学校では、10人中9人の子どもがひとそろい持っている。校長の中には、望ましくない気の散るものとしてカードを禁止した人もいる。別の見方をする人もいる。マーシュ・レーン校の校長は、この件について保護者向けの夕べの集まりを何度か主催してきた人でもあるが、禁止は無意味だと主張する。子どもたちは隠れて遊ぶだけで、対立は避けられなくなると彼女は言う。彼女の学校の教師たちは、独創的な代案を見つけた。補習の算数の授業でカードを使い、一手ごとの確率を計算するよう生徒に求めるのだ。効果は目立っている。任意参加の授業への出席は倍になり、数字が嫌いだった何人かの子どもが今ではもっと難しい問題を求めている。校長は現実的だ。1年もすればこのゲームは忘れられ、行動は普段どおりに戻るだろうと彼女は言う。それまでは、子どもたちの熱中と闘うより、それを利用したいというのだ。","words":[["craze","freq:craze"],["understatement","freq:understatement"],["undesirable","freq:undesirable"],["hostess","freq:hostess"],["senseless","freq:senseless"],["unavoidable","freq:unavoidable"],["ingenious","freq:ingenious"],["supplementary","freq:supplementary"],["conspicuous","freq:conspicuous"],["revert","freq:revert"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why does the head teacher at Marsh Lane oppose a ban?","o":["She thinks children would just play secretly and conflict would follow.","She earns money from the cards.","She invented the game herself.","The parents demanded that the cards stay."],"a":0,"why":"Children will simply play in secret ... and conflict will be unavoidable（隠れて遊ぶだけで対立が避けられなくなる）と言っている。"},{"q":"How do her teachers use the cards?","o":["As prizes for good behavior","As decorations in the hall","As material for extra maths lessons","As a way to raise money"],"a":2,"why":"They use the cards in supplementary maths lessons（補習の算数の授業で使う）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0092","page":"freq","group":"1301〜1400","kind":"反乱と国際社会","text":"The insurgency in the northern provinces of Kalenia is now in its fourth year, and the international response remains deeply contentious. An arms embargo imposed by the regional council was meant to act as a deterrent. In practice, weapons still cross the border, and the carnage in the mountain towns has grown worse. The rebels' last bastion is the city of Ormen. Government troops have surrounded it, but they have not attacked, and half a million civilians are living in limbo. Inside the city, armed vigilante groups have begun to intimidate anyone suspected of helping the army. Last week they opened fire to disperse a crowd that was asking for food. Outside powers are divided. Some governments want to bolster the army with training and equipment, arguing that extremism will spread if the rebels win. Others fear that overt support would only widen the war. A valiant group of local doctors has kept two hospitals open throughout, and their reports are the most reliable source of news. They describe a population that is exhausted rather than angry. A cohesive plan for peace has yet to appear. Until it does, the doctors say, each month of delay will be counted in lives.","ja":"カレニア北部諸州の反乱は4年目に入り、国際社会の対応は依然として激しい論争の的だ。地域評議会が課した武器の禁輸措置は、抑止力として働くはずだった。実際には武器は今も国境を越えており、山の町々での殺りくは悪化している。反乱軍の最後のとりではオルメン市だ。政府軍は市を包囲しているが攻撃はしておらず、50万人の民間人が宙ぶらりんの状態で暮らしている。市内では、武装した自警団が、軍を助けたと疑われる人を誰でも脅し始めた。先週、彼らは食料を求めていた群衆を追い散らすために発砲した。外部の国々の意見は割れている。反乱軍が勝てば過激主義が広がると主張し、訓練と装備で軍を強化したい政府もある。公然の支援は戦争を拡大させるだけだと恐れる政府もある。地元の勇敢な医師の一団がずっと2つの病院を開け続けており、彼らの報告が最も信頼できる情報源だ。彼らは、怒っているというより疲れ果てた住民の姿を伝えている。まとまりのある和平案はまだ現れていない。それが現れるまで、遅れた月日の一つひとつが人命で数えられることになると医師たちは言う。","words":[["insurgency","freq:insurgency"],["contentious","freq:contentious"],["embargo","freq:embargo"],["deterrent","freq:deterrent"],["carnage","freq:carnage"],["bastion","freq:bastion"],["limbo","freq:limbo"],["vigilante","freq:vigilante"],["intimidate","freq:intimidate"],["disperse","freq:disperse"],["bolster","freq:bolster"],["extremism","freq:extremism"],["overt","freq:overt"],["valiant","freq:valiant"],["cohesive","freq:cohesive"]],"qs":[{"q":"What has happened since the arms embargo was imposed?","o":["The rebels have surrendered.","Weapons have continued to arrive and the killing has increased.","The army has captured Ormen.","The border has been closed completely."],"a":1,"why":"weapons still cross the border, and the carnage in the mountain towns has grown worse とある。"},{"q":"Why do some governments oppose open support for the army?","o":["They think it would spread the war.","They support the rebels.","They cannot afford the equipment.","They doubt the doctors' reports."],"a":0,"why":"overt support would only widen the war（公然の支援は戦争を拡大させるだけ）と恐れている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0093","page":"freq","group":"1301〜1400","kind":"再審の決定","text":"A court has ordered a new trial for a man who has spent nine years in prison for an act of vandalism that ended in a death. The original case was simple. A fire was set in an empty school, a homeless man sleeping inside was killed, and an eyewitness identified the accused as the perpetrator. He was convicted and had to forfeit his small business to pay compensation. The appeal judges have now found his conviction unsafe, and possibly wrongful. The witness, it emerged, was a police informant who had been paid for earlier statements. One judge called it hypocritical for prosecutors to demand honesty from defendants while hiding such a fact. There was also evidence of perjury by a second witness. The court did not say that the police had acted in an unethical way, only that they had been negligent. It ruled that the right to an unbiased trial must take precedence over the wish for a quick result. Any delay, it added, was attributable to the prosecution, not the defense. The judges declined a request for intrusive new searches of the man's home. Whether the new trial will be conclusive is uncertain, since two witnesses have since died.","ja":"ある裁判所が、死者を出した破壊行為で9年間服役している男性の再審を命じた。元の事件は単純だった。空き校舎に火がつけられ、中で眠っていた家のない男性が死亡し、目撃者が被告を犯人だと名指しした。彼は有罪となり、賠償金を払うために小さな店を手放さなければならなかった。上訴審の裁判官たちは今回、彼の有罪判決は信頼できず、不当である可能性もあると認めた。その証人は、以前の供述について金を受け取っていた警察の情報提供者だったことが明らかになったのだ。ある裁判官は、検察がそのような事実を隠しながら被告に正直さを求めるのは偽善的だと述べた。2人目の証人による偽証の証拠もあった。裁判所は、警察が非倫理的に行動したとは言わず、怠慢だったとだけ述べた。そして、偏りのない裁判を受ける権利は、早く結果を出したいという望みに優先しなければならないと判断した。遅れはすべて弁護側ではなく検察側に起因するとも付け加えた。裁判官たちは、男性の自宅に立ち入る新たな捜索の求めを退けた。2人の証人がその後亡くなっているため、再審で決定的な結論が出るかどうかは不確かだ。","words":[["vandalism","freq:vandalism"],["eyewitness","freq:eyewitness"],["perpetrator","freq:perpetrator"],["forfeit","freq:forfeit"],["wrongful","freq:wrongful"],["informant","freq:informant"],["hypocritical","freq:hypocritical"],["perjury","freq:perjury"],["unethical","freq:unethical"],["negligent","freq:negligent"],["unbiased","freq:unbiased"],["precedence","freq:precedence"],["attributable","freq:attributable"],["intrusive","freq:intrusive"],["conclusive","freq:conclusive"]],"qs":[{"q":"What new fact emerged about the main witness?","o":["He had started the fire himself.","He was a paid police informant.","He had never seen the school.","He was related to the victim."],"a":1,"why":"a police informant who had been paid for earlier statements（以前の供述について金を受け取っていた警察の情報提供者）だった。"},{"q":"Why may the new trial fail to settle the matter?","o":["The school has been rebuilt.","The accused refuses to attend.","The judges disagree with one another.","Two witnesses are no longer alive."],"a":3,"why":"Whether the new trial will be conclusive is uncertain, since two witnesses have since died とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0094","page":"freq","group":"1301〜1400","kind":"がん治療の進歩","text":"Ten years ago, a diagnosis of malignant melanoma that had spread to other organs meant a prognosis of months, not years. A new generation of drugs has changed that. These treatments do not attack the tumor directly. They nurture the body's own immune cells and help them recognize cancer. In the best cases the improvement is measurable within weeks, and some patients have remained free of disease for a decade. The drugs are not a cure. About half of patients do not respond, and a relapse is common. Researchers are trying to understand why. One theory is that some people are deficient in a particular protein, and that without it the immune response begins to degenerate. Side effects are another problem. Most are reversible, but a few patients develop a depressive illness that doctors once dismissed as incidental. To optimize treatment, hospitals now rely on computers. The retrieval of records from thousands of earlier cases allows a rough approximation of how each new patient will respond. The exponential growth of such data may matter as much as any drug. Fear of cancer is primal, one doctor says, but it is no longer the same as hopelessness.","ja":"10年前、ほかの臓器に広がった悪性黒色腫という診断は、年単位ではなく月単位の見通しを意味した。新世代の薬がそれを変えた。これらの治療は腫瘍を直接攻撃しない。体自身の免疫細胞を育て、がんを見分けるのを助けるのだ。最良の場合、改善は数週間で目に見えて測れるほどになり、10年間病気のないままの患者もいる。この薬は完治させるものではない。患者の約半数は反応せず、再発もよくある。研究者たちはその理由を理解しようとしている。一つの説は、ある特定のたんぱく質が不足している人がいて、それがないと免疫反応が衰え始めるというものだ。副作用も問題だ。ほとんどは元に戻るが、少数の患者はうつ病を発症する。医師たちはかつて、それを付随的なものとして片づけていた。治療を最適化するために、病院は今ではコンピューターに頼っている。以前の数千例の記録を検索することで、新しい患者一人ひとりがどう反応するかを大まかに概算できる。そうしたデータの急激な増加は、どの薬にも劣らず重要かもしれない。がんへの恐怖は根源的なものだが、それはもう絶望と同じではないと、ある医師は言う。","words":[["malignant","freq:malignant"],["prognosis","freq:prognosis"],["nurture","freq:nurture"],["measurable","freq:measurable"],["relapse","freq:relapse"],["deficient","freq:deficient"],["degenerate","freq:degenerate"],["reversible","freq:reversible"],["depressive","freq:depressive"],["incidental","freq:incidental"],["optimize","freq:optimize"],["retrieval","freq:retrieval"],["approximation","freq:approximation"],["exponential","freq:exponential"],["primal","freq:primal"]],"qs":[{"q":"How do the new drugs work?","o":["They cut off the tumor's blood supply.","They help the body's immune cells to recognize cancer.","They replace damaged organs.","They remove a protein from the blood."],"a":1,"why":"They nurture the body's own immune cells and help them recognize cancer とある。"},{"q":"How do hospitals try to predict a patient's response?","o":["By comparing records from many earlier cases","By testing the drug on relatives","By measuring the patient's fear","By waiting for a relapse"],"a":0,"why":"The retrieval of records from thousands of earlier cases allows a rough approximation（以前の数千例の記録から概算する）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0095","page":"freq","group":"1301〜1400","kind":"音楽学校の発表会","text":"The annual concert of the city conservatory is usually a polite affair. This year's was different. The students chose a thematic program built around the idea of the double, and the result was the most interesting evening the hall has seen in years. It opened with a baroque suite, played on old instruments with a warm, mellow tone. Then came a new work for voices and narration, in which a singer and her antagonist, a figure seen only as a silhouette behind a screen, argue over the same song. Critics might characterize the piece as a farce, and it is certainly funny. But there is something uncanny about it too. In the final section, the two voices merge, and an eerie silence follows. The young composer avoids every simplistic effect. Her music moves easily between comedy and abstraction, and the performers showed remarkable versatility, changing roles and instruments without a pause. The evening closed with a string quartet by a student of nineteen. It was the embodiment of what a school like this exists for: skill placed in the service of an idea.","ja":"市立音楽学校の年に一度の演奏会は、たいていは行儀のよい催しだ。今年は違った。学生たちは「分身」という考えを軸にした主題別の演目を選び、その結果は、このホールがここ数年で経験した中で最も興味深い晩になった。演奏会は、古い楽器で温かくまろやかな音色で奏でられたバロックの組曲で始まった。次に、声と語りのための新作が続いた。そこでは一人の歌い手と、ついたての後ろの影としてしか見えない敵役とが、同じ歌をめぐって言い争う。批評家はこの作品を茶番と見なすかもしれないし、確かに滑稽だ。しかし、そこには不思議で不気味なものもある。最後の部分で2つの声は溶け合い、不気味な沈黙が続く。若い作曲家は、単純すぎる効果をことごとく避けている。彼女の音楽は喜劇と抽象の間を自在に行き来し、演奏者たちは、間を置かずに役と楽器を替えるという驚くべき多才さを見せた。晩は19歳の学生による弦楽四重奏曲で締めくくられた。それは、このような学校が何のために存在するのかを体現するものだった。一つの考えに奉仕する技能である。","words":[["conservatory","freq:conservatory"],["thematic","freq:thematic"],["baroque","freq:baroque"],["mellow","freq:mellow"],["narration","freq:narration"],["antagonist","freq:antagonist"],["silhouette","freq:silhouette"],["characterize","freq:characterize"],["farce","freq:farce"],["uncanny","freq:uncanny"],["eerie","freq:eerie"],["simplistic","freq:simplistic"],["abstraction","freq:abstraction"],["versatility","freq:versatility"],["embodiment","freq:embodiment"]],"qs":[{"q":"What idea was the program built around?","o":["The seasons","The double","Old instruments","Silence"],"a":1,"why":"a thematic program built around the idea of the double（「分身」という考えを軸にした演目）とある。"},{"q":"What impressed the reviewer about the performers?","o":["They played only baroque music.","They sang behind a screen all evening.","They switched roles and instruments with ease.","They wrote all the music themselves."],"a":2,"why":"remarkable versatility, changing roles and instruments without a pause（間を置かずに役と楽器を替える多才さ）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0096","page":"freq","group":"1301〜1400","kind":"ある一族の慈善","text":"The Hartwell family has been associated with philanthropy for four generations. Its patriarch, Josiah Hartwell, was the proprietor of a small ironworks and a pious member of a strict religious denomination. He believed that wealth was a trust, and he gave away a tenth of everything he earned. His ethos shaped the family. Hartwell children were taught to be modest, and one diary records a boy punished for the \"blasphemy\" of boasting about his father's money. A new history of the family, written by a descendant, is frank about its faults. Josiah could be smug about his own goodness, and he looked with disdain on neighbors who drank. He held a grudge against a brother for forty years. He was also oblivious to the conditions in his own factory, where children worked twelve hours a day. His wife, by contrast, appears as a meek but determined woman who persuaded him to build a school. She was more receptive to new ideas than he was. The family's gifts have been manifold: hospitals, libraries, a university. The author's conclusion is balanced. Generosity, she writes, does not cancel injustice, but neither should it be forgotten.","ja":"ハートウェル家は4世代にわたって慈善活動と結びつけられてきた。その家長ジョサイア・ハートウェルは、小さな製鉄所の所有者で、厳格な宗派の信心深い信者だった。彼は富を預かりものと信じ、稼いだものすべての10分の1を寄付した。彼の精神が一族を形作った。ハートウェル家の子どもたちは控えめであるよう教えられ、ある日記は、父親の金を自慢するという「冒涜」のために罰せられた少年のことを記している。子孫によって書かれた新しい一族の歴史は、その欠点について率直だ。ジョサイアは自分の善良さについて独りよがりになることがあり、酒を飲む隣人たちを軽蔑の目で見た。彼は40年間、弟に恨みを抱いていた。また、子どもたちが1日12時間働いていた自分の工場の状況にも気づいていなかった。対照的に、彼の妻は、おとなしいが意志の強い女性として描かれ、彼を説得して学校を建てさせた。彼女は彼よりも新しい考えを受け入れる姿勢があった。一族の寄付は多種多様だ。病院、図書館、大学である。著者の結論は均衡がとれている。気前のよさは不正を帳消しにはしないが、忘れられるべきでもないと彼女は書いている。","words":[["philanthropy","freq:philanthropy"],["patriarch","freq:patriarch"],["proprietor","freq:proprietor"],["pious","freq:pious"],["denomination","freq:denomination"],["ethos","freq:ethos"],["blasphemy","freq:blasphemy"],["descendant","freq:descendant"],["smug","freq:smug"],["disdain","freq:disdain"],["grudge","freq:grudge"],["oblivious","freq:oblivious"],["meek","freq:meek"],["receptive","freq:receptive"],["manifold","freq:manifold"]],"qs":[{"q":"What fault of Josiah's does the book mention concerning his factory?","o":["He paid his workers too much.","He closed it without warning.","He sold it to a rival.","He was unaware that children worked very long hours there."],"a":3,"why":"oblivious to the conditions in his own factory, where children worked twelve hours a day とある。"},{"q":"What is the author's conclusion?","o":["The family's gifts should be returned.","Generosity neither erases injustice nor deserves to be forgotten.","Josiah was a perfect man.","The family was never truly generous."],"a":1,"why":"Generosity ... does not cancel injustice, but neither should it be forgotten とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0097","page":"freq","group":"1301〜1400","kind":"再建を率いた経営者","text":"When Greta Solheim took over the Nordal furniture company, it was the underdog of its industry. Sales had been stagnant for a decade, and the mood in the factory was one of gloom. Solheim was regarded as a maverick. She had no supervisory experience in manufacturing, and her first decisions enraged the old managers. She cut the range of products by half and announced that there would be no redundancy among shop-floor workers, only in the head office. The first year was hectic. A frantic effort to redesign the best-selling chair nearly failed, and the future of the firm seemed to hinge on a single order from a hotel chain. It came through. Colleagues speak of her toughness, but also of the relish with which she solved practical problems. She would spend hours on the factory floor, treating design not as an adjunct to production but as part of it. The move to a new system of supply, which might have caused chaos, was seamless. Nordal is now the most profitable firm of its kind in the region. Solheim says the company's skill was always there. It had simply been buried under untold layers of management.","ja":"グレタ・ソルハイムがノルダル家具会社を引き継いだとき、同社は業界で勝ち目の薄い側だった。売上は10年間停滞し、工場の雰囲気は暗く沈んでいた。ソルハイムは型破りな人と見なされていた。製造業での監督職の経験がなく、彼女の最初の決定は古参の管理職を激怒させた。彼女は製品の種類を半分に減らし、現場の労働者には人員削減を行わず、本社でだけ行うと発表した。最初の年は慌ただしかった。最も売れている椅子を設計し直すという大慌ての取り組みは失敗しかけ、会社の将来はあるホテルチェーンからのたった一つの注文にかかっているように見えた。注文は取れた。同僚たちは彼女の強さを語るが、実際的な問題を解くときの彼女の喜びようについても語る。彼女は工場の現場で何時間も過ごし、設計を生産の付属物としてではなく、その一部として扱った。混乱を引き起こしかねなかった新しい供給の仕組みへの移行は、円滑に進んだ。ノルダルは今、その地域で同業の中で最も利益を上げている会社だ。ソルハイムは、会社の技能はずっとそこにあったと言う。計り知れないほど何層にも重なった管理の下に埋もれていただけなのだ。","words":[["underdog","freq:underdog"],["stagnant","freq:stagnant"],["gloom","freq:gloom"],["maverick","freq:maverick"],["supervisory","freq:supervisory"],["enraged","freq:enraged"],["redundancy","freq:redundancy"],["hectic","freq:hectic"],["frantic","freq:frantic"],["hinge","freq:hinge"],["toughness","freq:toughness"],["relish","freq:relish"],["adjunct","freq:adjunct"],["seamless","freq:seamless"],["untold","freq:untold"]],"qs":[{"q":"Where did Solheim cut jobs?","o":["On the factory floor","In the design team","In the head office","At the hotel chain"],"a":2,"why":"no redundancy among shop-floor workers, only in the head office（現場ではなく本社でだけ人員削減）とある。"},{"q":"According to Solheim, why had the company been failing?","o":["Its workers lacked skill.","Its skill was hidden beneath too much management.","Its furniture was too expensive.","Its factory was too small."],"a":1,"why":"the company's skill was always there ... buried under untold layers of management とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0098","page":"freq","group":"1301〜1400","kind":"火山探検の手記","text":"We had planned to reach the crater by noon, but the mountain had other ideas. The path, treacherous even in good weather, crossed a field of old lava that had cooled into a labyrinth of sharp ridges. Our bulky packs caught on every rock. Twice we lost the way and had to return to the last marker. At the edge of the crater we stopped. Far below, a lake of molten rock glowed orange, and now and then the mountain would fling a shower of sparks into the air. It was a pristine and terrible sight, a place where nothing living could last an hour. Our guide told us that an earlier expedition had come here in 1931 and vanished. Four men were thought to perish in a sudden eruption, and their names have almost passed into oblivion. We found one trace of them: a shred of canvas from a tent, pinned under a stone. We had arranged a rendezvous with the second team at the eastern rim at three. When they did not appear, we waited an hour, said little, and then began the long walk down in the fading light.","ja":"私たちは正午までに火口に着くつもりだったが、山には別の考えがあった。天気がよくても危険なその道は、冷えて鋭い尾根の迷路になった古い溶岩の原を横切っていた。かさばる荷物があらゆる岩に引っかかった。私たちは2度道を見失い、最後の目印まで戻らなければならなかった。火口の縁で私たちは立ち止まった。はるか下では溶けた岩の湖がだいだい色に輝き、時おり山は火花の雨を空中に放り上げた。それは手つかずの、恐ろしい光景で、生き物は何であれ1時間ともたない場所だった。案内人は、以前の探検隊が1931年にここへ来て姿を消したと話した。4人の男が突然の噴火で命を落としたと考えられており、その名前はほとんど忘れ去られている。私たちは彼らの痕跡を一つ見つけた。石の下に挟まった、テントの帆布の切れ端だ。私たちは3時に東の縁で第2班と落ち合う約束をしていた。彼らが現れなかったので、私たちは1時間待ち、ほとんど口をきかず、それから薄れていく光の中を長い下りの道についた。","words":[["treacherous","freq:treacherous"],["labyrinth","freq:labyrinth"],["bulky","freq:bulky"],["molten","freq:molten"],["fling","freq:fling"],["pristine","freq:pristine"],["perish","freq:perish"],["oblivion","freq:oblivion"],["shred","freq:shred"],["rendezvous","freq:rendezvous"]],"qs":[{"q":"What made the path so difficult?","o":["Deep snow","A river that had to be crossed","A field of old lava with sharp ridges","Thick forest"],"a":2,"why":"crossed a field of old lava that had cooled into a labyrinth of sharp ridges とある。"},{"q":"What trace of the 1931 expedition did the writer's group find?","o":["A piece of tent canvas","A diary","A marker with four names","A rope"],"a":0,"why":"a shred of canvas from a tent, pinned under a stone（石の下に挟まったテントの帆布の切れ端）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0099","page":"freq","group":"1401〜1500","kind":"解放運動の分裂","text":"The movement that won the emancipation of the Tarsi people was never as united as its songs suggest. A new history describes how close it came to destroying itself. In the 1950s the movement began to splinter. One faction, led by a former teacher, was adamant that change must come without bloodshed. Another argued that an unequal system built on force could be ended only by force, and it pursued that belief with fierce zeal. The rupture became public in 1958, when the two groups held separate meetings in the same town. For three years, sporadic fighting between them did more damage than the government, their real adversary, had ever managed. In the lawless hill districts, some of the violence was simply barbaric. What restored cohesion was an outsider. A respected judge agreed to act as mediator, and he was able to compel both sides to accept a common program by threatening to publish what he knew about each. The agreement left many questions unresolved, and the bitterness lasted for decades. But the author argues that without it, independence would have been delayed by a generation, and the price would have been higher still.","ja":"タルシの人々の解放を勝ち取った運動は、その歌が示すほど団結していたわけではなかった。新しい歴史書は、運動がどれほど自滅の寸前まで行ったかを描いている。1950年代、運動は分裂し始めた。元教師が率いる一派は、変革は流血なしに実現しなければならないと断固として主張した。別の一派は、力の上に築かれた不平等な制度は力によってしか終わらせられないと論じ、その信念を激しい熱意で追い求めた。亀裂は1958年、2つの集団が同じ町で別々の集会を開いたときに公然のものとなった。3年の間、両者の間の散発的な戦闘は、本当の敵である政府がそれまでに与えたどの打撃よりも大きな損害をもたらした。無法地帯となった丘陵地区では、暴力の一部はただ残忍だった。結束を取り戻したのは部外者だった。尊敬されていたある裁判官が調停者を務めることに同意し、それぞれについて知っていることを公表すると脅して、双方に共通の綱領を受け入れさせることができた。合意は多くの問題を未解決のまま残し、わだかまりは何十年も続いた。しかし著者は、それがなければ独立は一世代遅れ、代償はさらに大きくなっていただろうと論じている。","words":[["emancipation","freq:emancipation"],["splinter","freq:splinter"],["adamant","freq:adamant"],["bloodshed","freq:bloodshed"],["unequal","freq:unequal"],["zeal","freq:zeal"],["rupture","freq:rupture"],["sporadic","freq:sporadic"],["adversary","freq:adversary"],["lawless","freq:lawless"],["barbaric","freq:barbaric"],["cohesion","freq:cohesion"],["mediator","freq:mediator"],["compel","freq:compel"],["unresolved","freq:unresolved"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was the disagreement inside the movement?","o":["Whether to use violence to achieve change","Which town should host its meetings","Who should write its songs","Whether to accept foreign money"],"a":0,"why":"一派は without bloodshed を主張し、別の一派は only by force と論じた。"},{"q":"How did the judge bring the two sides together?","o":["He arrested their leaders.","He offered them government posts.","He moved the talks abroad.","He threatened to reveal what he knew about each of them."],"a":3,"why":"by threatening to publish what he knew about each（それぞれについて知っていることを公表すると脅して）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0100","page":"freq","group":"1401〜1500","kind":"引き渡しをめぐる争い","text":"The government has asked a foreign court to approve the extradition of a businessman accused of extortion on a grand scale. According to a dossier prepared by prosecutors, he used threats to harass shop owners in six cities, forcing them to pay for \"protection.\" Witnesses describe a pattern of coercion that lasted fifteen years. The man left the country two days before a planned roundup of his associates, which suggests that he had been warned. His lawyers have filed a writ to stop the transfer. They argue that he is ineligible for extradition because the alleged crimes are political, and that sending him home would cause undue hardship to his family. They also claim that the authorities continue to withhold key documents. Prosecutors call these arguments a mockery of the law. One of them remarked on the audacity of a man who claims to be a victim after years of scandalous wealth. The foreign court must now ascertain whether a fair trial is possible. If it refuses the request, the government will have little recourse. It could appeal, but the process might take five years, and several elderly witnesses may not live to give evidence.","ja":"政府は、大規模な恐喝で起訴された実業家の引き渡しを承認するよう、外国の裁判所に求めた。検察がまとめた関係書類によれば、彼は脅しを使って6つの都市の店主たちを悩ませ、「用心棒代」を払わせていた。証人たちは、15年続いた威圧の手口を語っている。この男は、仲間の一斉検挙が予定されていた日の2日前に出国しており、警告を受けていたことをうかがわせる。彼の弁護士たちは、移送を止めるための令状を申し立てた。彼らは、申し立てられた犯罪は政治的なものなので彼は引き渡しの対象にならないと主張し、本国に送れば家族に過度の苦しみを与えるとも言う。また、当局が重要な書類を出さずにいるとも主張している。検察はこれらの主張を法をあざ笑うものだと呼ぶ。検察官の一人は、けしからぬほどの富を何年も享受した後で被害者だと名乗る男の厚かましさを指摘した。外国の裁判所はこれから、公正な裁判が可能かどうかを確かめなければならない。要請が退けられれば、政府にはほとんど頼る手段がない。上訴はできるが、手続きに5年かかるかもしれず、何人かの高齢の証人は証言するまで生きていられないかもしれない。","words":[["extradition","freq:extradition"],["extortion","freq:extortion"],["dossier","freq:dossier"],["harass","freq:harass"],["coercion","freq:coercion"],["roundup","freq:roundup"],["writ","freq:writ"],["ineligible","freq:ineligible"],["undue","freq:undue"],["withhold","freq:withhold"],["mockery","freq:mockery"],["audacity","freq:audacity"],["scandalous","freq:scandalous"],["ascertain","freq:ascertain"],["recourse","freq:recourse"]],"qs":[{"q":"What suggests that the businessman had been warned?","o":["He hired new lawyers.","He left the country just before his associates were to be arrested.","He paid the shop owners back.","He wrote to the prosecutors."],"a":1,"why":"left the country two days before a planned roundup of his associates（仲間の一斉検挙の2日前に出国した）とある。"},{"q":"Why might an appeal fail to help the government?","o":["The foreign court is closed.","The dossier has been lost.","It could take so long that elderly witnesses may die.","The businessman's family opposes it."],"a":2,"why":"the process might take five years, and several elderly witnesses may not live to give evidence とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0101","page":"freq","group":"1401〜1500","kind":"希少疾患の治療","text":"Children born with Marren syndrome, a rare congenital disorder, cannot break down a natural toxin produced in digestion. Until recently the condition was fatal. The poison built up in the blood, bones became brittle, and most patients were too frail to walk by the age of ten. Damage to the nerves was irreversible. A treatment developed over the past decade has changed the outlook. It does not eradicate the disease. Instead, patients follow a strict regimen: a special diet, and a weekly infusion of an enzyme that the body cannot make. The enzyme is produced by fermentation, using bacteria, and then passed through several stages of purification. Without it, the toxin would slowly degrade the tissues. The treatment is corrective rather than curative, and it must continue for life. Doctors describe the illness with an image from physics. A healthy body, they say, constantly fights entropy; these children simply need help to do so. Problems remain. The enzyme does not diffuse easily into the brain, and some patients develop a compulsive need to eat foods that are dangerous for them. Even so, the first children treated are now entering university.","ja":"まれな先天性の病気であるマレン症候群を持って生まれた子どもは、消化の際に作られる天然の毒素を分解できない。最近まで、この病気は命に関わるものだった。毒が血液にたまり、骨はもろくなり、ほとんどの患者は10歳までに体が弱って歩けなくなった。神経の損傷は元に戻らなかった。過去10年で開発された治療法が見通しを変えた。それは病気を根絶するものではない。代わりに、患者は厳しい治療計画に従う。特別な食事と、体が作れない酵素の週1回の点滴である。酵素は細菌を使った発酵によって作られ、その後、何段階もの精製を経る。それがなければ、毒素が少しずつ組織を劣化させていく。この治療は完治させるものではなく症状を是正するもので、生涯続けなければならない。医師たちは物理学のたとえでこの病気を説明する。健康な体は絶えず無秩序と闘っており、この子どもたちはそのための助けを必要としているだけなのだという。問題は残っている。酵素は脳へは簡単に広がらず、一部の患者は自分にとって危険な食べ物をどうしても食べたくなる強迫的な欲求を抱くようになる。それでも、最初に治療を受けた子どもたちは今、大学に入ろうとしている。","words":[["congenital","freq:congenital"],["toxin","freq:toxin"],["brittle","freq:brittle"],["frail","freq:frail"],["irreversible","freq:irreversible"],["eradicate","freq:eradicate"],["regimen","freq:regimen"],["infusion","freq:infusion"],["fermentation","freq:fermentation"],["purification","freq:purification"],["degrade","freq:degrade"],["corrective","freq:corrective"],["entropy","freq:entropy"],["diffuse","freq:diffuse"],["compulsive","freq:compulsive"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does the treatment consist of?","o":["A single operation in childhood","A special diet and a weekly enzyme infusion","A vaccine given at birth","A drug that removes the toxin permanently"],"a":1,"why":"a special diet, and a weekly infusion of an enzyme that the body cannot make とある。"},{"q":"What problem remains with the treatment?","o":["The enzyme does not easily reach the brain.","The diet is too expensive.","The bacteria are dangerous.","Patients cannot attend university."],"a":0,"why":"The enzyme does not diffuse easily into the brain（酵素は脳へ簡単には広がらない）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0102","page":"freq","group":"1401〜1500","kind":"詩人の記念行事","text":"The city of Varn marked the centenary of the poet Ilse Maren last week with a commemoration that she would probably have disliked. Maren hated ceremony. She was an ardent enemy of every dogma, and in her lifetime she was called a barbarian by critics who preferred polite verse. The evening began with a prelude for solo cello and ended with a standing ovation for an actor who read her last poems. In between came speeches. The best was by a young scholar who has spent five years in archival research on Maren's notebooks. She showed how subtly the poet reworked her drafts, removing every word that explained too much. Maren's interest in the occult, often treated as a joke, turns out to have been serious, and some of her most grotesque images come from books on magic. The scholar's own study, just published, is highly readable. It presents Maren as the epitome of the difficult artist, and yet it finds serenity in the late work. Those final poems evoke a garden in winter. Many readers have found solace in them, and on this occasion, at least, the applause seemed deserved.","ja":"バルン市は先週、詩人イルゼ・マーレンの生誕100年を記念行事で祝ったが、彼女自身はおそらくそれを嫌っただろう。マーレンは儀式を憎んでいた。彼女はあらゆる教義の熱烈な敵であり、生前は、行儀のよい詩を好む批評家たちから野蛮人と呼ばれた。その晩は独奏チェロの前奏曲で始まり、彼女の最後の詩を朗読した俳優への総立ちの大喝采で終わった。その間には演説があった。最もよかったのは、マーレンのノートについて5年間、古文書の調査を続けてきた若い研究者の話だった。彼女は、詩人がいかに巧妙に下書きを練り直し、説明しすぎる言葉をことごとく削っていったかを示した。しばしば冗談として扱われてきたマーレンのオカルトへの関心は本気のものだったことがわかり、彼女の最も異様な比喩のいくつかは魔術の本から来ている。出版されたばかりの研究者自身の著書は、とても読みやすい。それはマーレンを気難しい芸術家の典型として描きながら、晩年の作品に静けさを見いだしている。それらの最後の詩は冬の庭を呼び起こす。多くの読者がそこに慰めを見いだしてきた。そして少なくともこの場では、拍手はふさわしいものに思えた。","words":[["commemoration","freq:commemoration"],["ardent","freq:ardent"],["dogma","freq:dogma"],["barbarian","freq:barbarian"],["prelude","freq:prelude"],["ovation","freq:ovation"],["archival","freq:archival"],["subtly","freq:subtly"],["occult","freq:occult"],["grotesque","freq:grotesque"],["readable","freq:readable"],["epitome","freq:epitome"],["serenity","freq:serenity"],["evoke","freq:evoke"],["solace","freq:solace"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the young scholar show about the poet's method?","o":["She wrote her poems in a single draft.","She carefully removed words that explained too much.","She copied images from other poets.","She refused to revise anything."],"a":1,"why":"removing every word that explained too much（説明しすぎる言葉をことごとく削った）とある。"},{"q":"What has research revealed about her interest in the occult?","o":["It was a joke she played on critics.","It began only in old age.","It was invented by her publisher.","It was genuine and influenced her images."],"a":3,"why":"turns out to have been serious とあり、異様な比喩のいくつかは魔術の本から来ているとある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0103","page":"freq","group":"1401〜1500","kind":"無人機による調査","text":"The Dallow range is one of the last unmapped regions of the continent. Its valleys are inaccessible by road, and the topography is so severe that earlier surveys covered only the periphery. Last summer a research team tried a new method. From a vantage point on the southern ridge, they launched an unmanned aircraft that could fly for twelve hours and photograph the ground in fine detail. The results were surprising. Cliffs almost perpendicular to the valley floor hid a series of lakes that appear on no map. On a ledge where eagles perch, the cameras found the wreckage of a small plane, a remnant of a flight that disappeared in 1962. Herds of feral goats, descended from animals abandoned by miners, abound on the upper slopes. The work was not easy. In the heat of the afternoon the valleys became an inferno, and rising air produced a mirage that confused the aircraft's sensors. Twice the team nearly lost it. The photographs also show that the soft upper rocks are beginning to crumble, exposing the granite bedrock beneath. A fuller survey is planned for next year.","ja":"ダロー山脈は、大陸に残る最後の未踏査地域の一つだ。谷には道路で行くことができず、地形があまりに険しいため、以前の調査は周辺部しか扱えなかった。昨年の夏、ある研究チームが新しい方法を試した。南の尾根の見晴らしのよい地点から、12時間飛行でき、地面を細部まで撮影できる無人機を飛ばしたのだ。結果は驚くべきものだった。谷底に対してほとんど垂直の崖が、どの地図にも載っていない一連の湖を隠していた。ワシが止まる岩棚では、カメラが小型機の残骸を見つけた。1962年に消息を絶った飛行の名残だ。鉱山労働者が置き去りにした動物の子孫である野生化したヤギの群れが、上のほうの斜面にたくさんいる。作業は簡単ではなかった。午後の暑さの中で谷は灼熱地獄となり、上昇する空気が蜃気楼を生んで機体の感知器を混乱させた。チームは2度、機体を失いかけた。写真はまた、上部の柔らかい岩が崩れ始め、その下の花こう岩の岩盤が露出しつつあることも示している。来年にはより本格的な調査が予定されている。","words":[["inaccessible","freq:inaccessible"],["topography","freq:topography"],["periphery","freq:periphery"],["vantage","freq:vantage"],["unmanned","freq:unmanned"],["perpendicular","freq:perpendicular"],["perch","freq:perch"],["wreckage","freq:wreckage"],["remnant","freq:remnant"],["feral","freq:feral"],["abound","freq:abound"],["inferno","freq:inferno"],["mirage","freq:mirage"],["crumble","freq:crumble"],["bedrock","freq:bedrock"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why had earlier surveys covered only the edges of the range?","o":["The region was too dangerous politically.","The terrain was too severe and there were no roads.","The lakes blocked the way.","Eagles attacked the surveyors."],"a":1,"why":"inaccessible by road とあり、the topography is so severe（地形があまりに険しい）ためである。"},{"q":"What caused problems for the aircraft in the afternoon?","o":["Strong rain","Low fuel","Heat that created false images for its sensors","Goats on the runway"],"a":2,"why":"rising air produced a mirage that confused the aircraft's sensors（上昇気流が蜃気楼を生み感知器を混乱させた）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0104","page":"freq","group":"1401〜1500","kind":"技術事業の失敗から","text":"The collapse of the Lumo tablet project is remembered in the industry as a fiasco. The device was meant to encompass everything a student needed: books, notes, and lessons. Two years and forty million dollars later, the company behind it was defunct. A former engineer has now written a frank account of what went wrong. The team, he says, was highly proficient, and nobody doubted its technical competency. The trouble lay elsewhere. The managers treated development as a sequential process, in which each stage had to be finished before the next began. Engineers who wanted to tinker with early models and refine them were told that time was the main constraint. Parts were designed to be interchangeable with a rival product, a choice that had an unintended effect: the tablet became slow. The most serious omission was the failure to consult teachers, who should have been the conduit between designers and children. By the time anyone asked them, it was too late to abort the launch. The engineer's conclusion is not bitter. The experience was fruitful, he writes, because every later product he worked on began with a visit to a classroom.","ja":"ルモ・タブレット計画の崩壊は、業界で大失敗として記憶されている。その機器は、生徒が必要とするすべて、つまり本もノートも授業も含むはずだった。2年と4,000万ドルを費やした後、背後にいた会社はなくなった。元技師が今回、何が悪かったのかを率直に記した。彼によれば、チームは非常に熟達しており、その技術的な能力を疑う人はいなかった。問題は別のところにあった。経営陣は開発を順を追った過程として扱い、各段階が終わってから次が始まるものとした。初期の試作機をいじって改良したい技師たちは、時間が最大の制約だと言われた。部品は競合製品と交換できるように設計されたが、その選択は意図しない結果を生んだ。タブレットの動作が遅くなったのだ。最も重大な手落ちは、教師に相談しなかったことだった。教師こそが設計者と子どもたちをつなぐ経路であるべきだった。誰かが教師に尋ねた頃には、発売を中止するには遅すぎた。技師の結論は恨みがましいものではない。その経験は実りの多いものだったと彼は書く。その後に関わったどの製品も、教室を訪ねることから始めたからだ。","words":[["fiasco","freq:fiasco"],["encompass","freq:encompass"],["defunct","freq:defunct"],["proficient","freq:proficient"],["competency","freq:competency"],["sequential","freq:sequential"],["tinker","freq:tinker"],["refine","freq:refine"],["constraint","freq:constraint"],["interchangeable","freq:interchangeable"],["unintended","freq:unintended"],["omission","freq:omission"],["conduit","freq:conduit"],["abort","freq:abort"],["fruitful","freq:fruitful"]],"qs":[{"q":"According to the engineer, what was the project's most serious mistake?","o":["The team lacked technical skill.","The device was too expensive.","Teachers were not consulted.","The parts were made by a rival."],"a":2,"why":"The most serious omission was the failure to consult teachers（教師に相談しなかったことが最も重大な手落ち）とある。"},{"q":"Why does he call the experience fruitful?","o":["It changed how he began every later project.","He was paid well.","The tablet was eventually relaunched.","He became the company's manager."],"a":0,"why":"every later product he worked on began with a visit to a classroom（その後の製品はすべて教室訪問から始めた）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0105","page":"freq","group":"1401〜1500","kind":"ボクシングの世界戦","text":"Nobody expected Saturday's title fight to last twelve rounds. The champion, Marco Reyes, has the physique of a heavier man and had won his last nine contests early. His challenger, a quiet twenty-three-year-old named Dev Anand, was thought to lack the power to trouble him. The predominant opinion among experts was that Reyes would clinch victory by the fourth round. They were wrong. From the first bell, Anand refused to collide with his opponent head-on. He moved, waited, and landed an assorted range of quick punches that did little damage but won points. Reyes, afflicted by a cut above the eye, began to tire. By the eighth round his temper started to fray, and on a whim he abandoned his careful plan and charged forward. It was the mistake Anand had been waiting for. The judges' decision was unanimous. In boxing, a result like this does not always denote a change of era, and Reyes has asked for a rematch. But Anand's preparation was exhaustive, and those who watched him train say that Saturday was no accident. The new champion said only that he was tired and wanted to go home.","ja":"土曜のタイトル戦が12ラウンドまで続くと予想した人はいなかった。王者マルコ・レイエスは一つ上の階級の選手のような体格をしており、直近の9試合を早い回で勝っていた。挑戦者である23歳の物静かなデブ・アナンドは、王者を苦しめるだけの力がないと思われていた。専門家の間で優勢だった意見は、レイエスが第4ラウンドまでに勝利を決めるというものだった。彼らは間違っていた。最初のゴングから、アナンドは相手と正面からぶつかることを拒んだ。彼は動き、待ち、大きな打撃にはならないが得点になる、さまざまな種類の素早いパンチを当てた。目の上の切り傷に苦しめられたレイエスは、疲れ始めた。第8ラウンドまでには彼の冷静さがすり切れ始め、彼は気まぐれに慎重な作戦を捨てて前に突進した。それこそアナンドが待っていたミスだった。判定は全員一致だった。ボクシングでは、こうした結果が必ずしも時代の交代を示すとは限らず、レイエスは再戦を求めている。しかしアナンドの準備は徹底的なもので、彼の練習を見た人々は、土曜の結果は偶然ではないと言う。新王者は、疲れたので家に帰りたいとだけ語った。","words":[["physique","freq:physique"],["predominant","freq:predominant"],["clinch","freq:clinch"],["collide","freq:collide"],["assorted","freq:assorted"],["afflicted","freq:afflicted"],["fray","freq:fray"],["whim","freq:whim"],["denote","freq:denote"],["exhaustive","freq:exhaustive"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did Anand fight the champion?","o":["He attacked head-on from the start.","He avoided direct clashes and scored with quick punches.","He relied on one powerful blow.","He tried to end the fight early."],"a":1,"why":"refused to collide with his opponent head-on とあり、quick punches で points を得たとある。"},{"q":"What mistake did Reyes make?","o":["He trained too little.","He refused a rematch.","He ignored the judges.","He gave up his plan and rushed forward."],"a":3,"why":"abandoned his careful plan and charged forward（慎重な作戦を捨てて突進した）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0106","page":"freq","group":"1501〜1600","kind":"分離独立の危機","text":"The threat of secession by the western provinces has pushed the republic of Halvar into its most precarious position since independence. The causes are old. For decades the west has complained that the redistribution of oil income favors the capital, and that the hierarchical structure of the army keeps its officers out of senior posts. Sectarian differences have sharpened the quarrel. Last month, a mutiny at a western barracks gave the government the pretext it needed. The president ordered the mobilization of reserve forces and threatened to revoke the region's limited self-rule. He also moved to expel two foreign diplomats accused of encouraging the rebels. Reports that the west has hired mercenary fighters remain unconfirmed. The effect on ordinary life has been severe. Emigration from the border districts has doubled, and the polarization of opinion has divided families. Church leaders have called for a cessation of all military movements while talks are arranged. Few observers expect a quick solution. A protracted crisis, they warn, could be worse than a short war, because it would allow both sides to arm themselves fully while the economy collapses around them.","ja":"西部諸州による分離独立の脅威は、ハルバール共和国を独立以来最も危うい立場に追い込んだ。原因は古くからある。何十年もの間、西部は、石油収入の再分配が首都に有利であり、軍の階層的な構造のせいで西部出身の将校が上級の地位に就けないと不満を訴えてきた。宗派の違いがその争いを鋭くした。先月、西部の兵舎での反乱が、政府に必要としていた口実を与えた。大統領は予備役の動員を命じ、この地域の限られた自治を取り消すと脅した。また、反乱者をあおったとされる外国の外交官2人を追放する措置に出た。西部が傭兵を雇ったという報道は、確認されていない。普通の暮らしへの影響は深刻だ。国境地区からの国外移住は倍になり、世論の二極化は家族を引き裂いている。教会の指導者たちは、協議が整う間、すべての軍事行動を停止するよう求めた。早期の解決を予想する観察者はほとんどいない。長引く危機は短い戦争より悪いかもしれないと彼らは警告する。経済が崩れていく中で、双方が完全に武装することを許すからだ。","words":[["secession","freq:secession"],["precarious","freq:precarious"],["redistribution","freq:redistribution"],["hierarchical","freq:hierarchical"],["Sectarian","freq:sectarian"],["mutiny","freq:mutiny"],["pretext","freq:pretext"],["mobilization","freq:mobilization"],["revoke","freq:revoke"],["expel","freq:expel"],["mercenary","freq:mercenary"],["Emigration","freq:emigration"],["polarization","freq:polarization"],["cessation","freq:cessation"],["protracted","freq:protracted"]],"qs":[{"q":"What has the west long complained about?","o":["The location of the capital","The share of oil income and exclusion from senior army posts","The number of foreign diplomats","The cost of emigration"],"a":1,"why":"the redistribution of oil income favors the capital とあり、将校が senior posts に就けないことも挙げている。"},{"q":"Why might a long crisis be worse than a short war?","o":["Both sides could arm fully while the economy fails.","Foreign armies would invade.","The churches would close.","The oil would run out."],"a":0,"why":"it would allow both sides to arm themselves fully while the economy collapses around them とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0107","page":"freq","group":"1501〜1600","kind":"医療法人の破綻","text":"The liquidation of the Sarvo Clinic Group was announced yesterday, ending one of the strangest business stories of recent years. The group ran forty private hospitals and was regarded as the undisputed leader in its field. Then a nurse began to speak. She told a newspaper that managers had ordered staff to record fictitious treatments in order to charge insurers. Reporters who managed to infiltrate a staff meeting heard the same thing. More serious claims followed. Three former patients were ready to attest that they had been operated on without need, and lawyers described a pattern of malpractice that one judge later called heinous. The founder, a secretive man who rarely gave interviews, denied any wrongdoing. He blamed misinformation spread by rivals. But documents showed that his own remuneration had tripled while the clinics lost money, and that a large payoff had been made to a former partner. The partner's wife, it emerged, had accused the founder of infidelity; the two matters were probably unrelated. Former staff describe him as manipulative and impossible to restrain. Creditors will receive about a third of what they are owed. The patients' cases will take far longer.","ja":"サルボ診療所グループの清算が昨日発表され、近年で最も奇妙な企業の物語の一つが終わった。このグループは40の私立病院を運営し、その分野で誰もが認める首位と見なされていた。そこへ一人の看護師が口を開いた。彼女はある新聞に、経営陣が保険会社に請求するために架空の治療を記録するよう職員に命じたと語った。職員会議にどうにか潜入した記者たちも同じことを聞いた。さらに深刻な主張が続いた。3人の元患者が、必要もないのに手術を受けたと証言する用意があり、弁護士たちは、後にある裁判官が極悪と呼んだ医療過誤の実態を述べた。めったに取材に応じない秘密主義の創業者は、不正行為を一切否定した。競争相手が広めた誤った情報のせいだと彼は言った。しかし書類は、診療所が赤字を出している間に彼自身の報酬が3倍になり、元共同経営者に多額の金が支払われていたことを示していた。その共同経営者の妻が創業者を不貞で非難していたことも明らかになったが、2つの件はおそらく無関係だ。元職員たちは、彼は人を巧みに操る人物で、抑えるのは不可能だったと語る。債権者は貸した額の約3分の1を受け取る。患者たちの訴訟は、はるかに長くかかるだろう。","words":[["liquidation","freq:liquidation"],["undisputed","freq:undisputed"],["fictitious","freq:fictitious"],["infiltrate","freq:infiltrate"],["attest","freq:attest"],["malpractice","freq:malpractice"],["heinous","freq:heinous"],["secretive","freq:secretive"],["wrongdoing","freq:wrongdoing"],["misinformation","freq:misinformation"],["remuneration","freq:remuneration"],["payoff","freq:payoff"],["infidelity","freq:infidelity"],["manipulative","freq:manipulative"],["restrain","freq:restrain"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the nurse reveal?","o":["The hospitals were about to be sold.","Staff were told to record treatments that never happened.","The founder was seriously ill.","Insurers had refused to pay."],"a":1,"why":"managers had ordered staff to record fictitious treatments in order to charge insurers とある。"},{"q":"What did the documents show about the founder?","o":["He had tried to save the clinics with his own money.","He had never owned the group.","He had warned regulators.","His pay rose sharply while the clinics were losing money."],"a":3,"why":"his own remuneration had tripled while the clinics lost money とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0108","page":"freq","group":"1501〜1600","kind":"農村の保健活動","text":"In the hill villages of the Marel district, the most important health worker is often the midwife. She delivers babies, gives advice on contraception, and treats the parasitic infections that are common where water is unclean. A project run by the regional university is trying to give these women better tools. One is a simple kit containing the antidote to snake venom, which loses its potency unless it is kept cold. Another is a small device that tests for hearing loss by measuring the response of the auditory nerve. Hearing problems are frequent in the district, and researchers suspect a pesticide used on the cotton fields. The chemical may remain latent in the body for years. The project also collects data. Each midwife carries a phone that can emit a signal to the nearest clinic, and the aggregation of thousands of reports has revealed patterns nobody had noticed. Injuries from a projectile, for example, rise sharply during the hunting season. The equipment is produced domestically and costs little. The pendulum of health policy, long tilted toward large hospitals, may be swinging back. The dissemination of such tools, the researchers argue, saves more lives per dollar than any new building. Even the hedgehog on the project's logo was drawn by a village child.","ja":"マレル地区の丘の村々では、最も重要な保健の担い手はしばしば助産師だ。彼女は赤ん坊を取り上げ、避妊について助言し、水が不潔な所でよく見られる寄生虫感染症を治療する。地域の大学が運営するある事業が、こうした女性たちにより良い道具を与えようとしている。一つは、ヘビの毒の解毒剤が入った簡単な道具箱だ。解毒剤は冷やしておかないと効力を失う。もう一つは、聴神経の反応を測ることで聴力の低下を調べる小さな機器だ。この地区では聴覚の問題が多く、研究者たちは綿畑で使われる農薬を疑っている。その化学物質は何年も体内に潜んでいるかもしれない。この事業はデータも集めている。助産師は一人ひとり、最寄りの診療所に信号を発することのできる電話を持っており、数千件の報告を集計することで、誰も気づいていなかった傾向が明らかになった。たとえば、発射物によるけがは狩猟の季節に急増する。機器は国内で生産され、費用はわずかだ。長く大病院のほうに傾いていた保健政策の振り子は、元へ戻りつつあるのかもしれない。こうした道具の普及は、どんな新しい建物よりも1ドルあたり多くの命を救うと研究者たちは主張する。事業のロゴのハリネズミでさえ、村の子どもが描いたものだ。","words":[["midwife","freq:midwife"],["contraception","freq:contraception"],["parasitic","freq:parasitic"],["antidote","freq:antidote"],["potency","freq:potency"],["auditory","freq:auditory"],["pesticide","freq:pesticide"],["latent","freq:latent"],["emit","freq:emit"],["aggregation","freq:aggregation"],["projectile","freq:projectile"],["domestically","freq:domestically"],["pendulum","freq:pendulum"],["dissemination","freq:dissemination"],["hedgehog","freq:hedgehog"]],"qs":[{"q":"What do researchers suspect is causing hearing problems in the district?","o":["Snake bites","Unclean water","Loud hunting weapons","A chemical used on cotton fields"],"a":3,"why":"researchers suspect a pesticide used on the cotton fields（綿畑で使われる農薬を疑っている）とある。"},{"q":"What has the collection of reports from midwives revealed?","o":["Patterns that had not been noticed before","A shortage of phones","That large hospitals are closing","That births are declining"],"a":0,"why":"the aggregation of thousands of reports has revealed patterns nobody had noticed とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0109","page":"freq","group":"1501〜1600","kind":"楽器職人の物語","text":"In a quaint workshop behind the cathedral, an artisan named Piero Santi has made violins for sixty years. He is eighty-four, a venerable figure in a trade that has few practitioners left. His instruments are known for their impeccable finish. Some players say they have a warmth that no modern factory can match. Santi follows no orthodoxy. He was trained in the classical method, but he has always been inventive, and one seminal change he made to the shape of the bridge has been widely copied. He speaks of his craft without sentiment. In a long monologue to a visitor, he will explain that a violin is a machine for moving air, and that sophistication lies in removing what is not needed. He does lament one thing: only three of the instruments he made in his first decade are still extant. The others were lost in a flood. Last month a young prodigy from Seoul played one of his violins in a concerto with the city orchestra. In the slow movement, soloist and strings moved in perfect unison. Santi, sitting in the third row, found the moment poignant. He said afterward that he had forgotten he had made the thing.","ja":"大聖堂の裏の古風で趣のある工房で、ピエロ・サンティという職人が60年間バイオリンを作ってきた。彼は84歳で、担い手がほとんど残っていない業界の尊敬すべき人物だ。彼の楽器は非の打ちどころのない仕上げで知られている。現代のどんな工場にもまねのできない温かみがあると言う演奏家もいる。サンティは正統とされる考え方に従わない。古典的な方法で訓練を受けたが、常に創意に富んでおり、駒の形に彼が加えた一つの独創的な変更は広くまねされてきた。彼は感傷を交えずに自分の技を語る。訪問者に向けた長い一人語りの中で、バイオリンは空気を動かす機械であり、洗練とは不要なものを取り除くことにあると説明する。彼が一つだけ嘆くことがある。最初の10年に作った楽器のうち、現存するのは3つだけなのだ。ほかは洪水で失われた。先月、ソウルから来た若い神童が、市のオーケストラとの協奏曲で彼のバイオリンの一つを弾いた。緩徐楽章では、独奏者と弦楽器が完全にそろって動いた。3列目に座っていたサンティは、その瞬間に胸を打たれた。後で彼は、あれを自分が作ったことを忘れていたと語った。","words":[["quaint","freq:quaint"],["artisan","freq:artisan"],["venerable","freq:venerable"],["impeccable","freq:impeccable"],["orthodoxy","freq:orthodoxy"],["inventive","freq:inventive"],["seminal","freq:seminal"],["monologue","freq:monologue"],["sophistication","freq:sophistication"],["lament","freq:lament"],["extant","freq:extant"],["prodigy","freq:prodigy"],["concerto","freq:concerto"],["unison","freq:unison"],["poignant","freq:poignant"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does Santi say sophistication means in making a violin?","o":["Adding more decoration","Taking away what is unnecessary","Using modern factory tools","Following the classical method exactly"],"a":1,"why":"sophistication lies in removing what is not needed（洗練とは不要なものを取り除くこと）とある。"},{"q":"What does he regret?","o":["That he never learned to play","That his bridge design was copied","That most of his earliest instruments were lost","That young players dislike his violins"],"a":2,"why":"only three of the instruments he made in his first decade are still extant. The others were lost in a flood とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0110","page":"freq","group":"1501〜1600","kind":"不安をめぐる随想","text":"Most of us have known a period when worry became a kind of fixation. We fret over a small mistake, brood on what others think, and lie awake with a visceral sense that something is wrong. Psychologists distinguish this ordinary angst from illness, but the line is not sharp. In her new book, Dr. Leila Hassan traces such feelings to our formative years. Children who received little reassurance, she argues, often grow into adults with an aversion to risk. She describes one patient whose stern paternal grandfather was intolerant of any failure. As an adult, the man took no gratification in success; each achievement only raised the standard. Hassan does not offer easy comfort. She is suspicious of advice that tells anxious people to think positively, and she notes with dismay how many of her patients have tried it and blamed themselves when it failed. Her own approach is modest. Name the fear, she says, in lucid and simple words. Do not treat a morbid thought as a prediction. Above all, resist apathy, which often looks like calm but is really a way of giving up. The book will not cure anyone. It may, however, make its readers kinder to themselves.","ja":"私たちのほとんどは、心配が一種の執着になった時期を経験している。小さな失敗をくよくよ悩み、他人がどう思うかを考え込み、何かがおかしいという本能的な感覚を抱えて眠れずに横になる。心理学者はこうした普通の不安を病気と区別するが、その境界ははっきりしていない。レイラ・ハッサン博士は新著の中で、そうした感情を人格が形成される時期までたどっている。あまり安心させてもらえなかった子どもは、危険を冒すことを強く嫌う大人に育つことが多いと彼女は論じる。彼女は、厳格な父方の祖父がどんな失敗にも不寛容だったという一人の患者を紹介している。大人になったその男性は、成功に何の満足も感じなかった。達成するたびに基準が上がるだけだったのだ。ハッサンは安易な慰めを差し出さない。不安を抱える人に前向きに考えよと言う助言を疑っており、患者の多くがそれを試し、うまくいかないと自分を責めたことを落胆とともに記している。彼女自身のやり方は控えめだ。恐れに、明快で簡単な言葉で名前を付けなさいと彼女は言う。病的な考えを予言として扱ってはいけない。何より、無気力に抗うこと。それはしばしば落ち着きのように見えるが、実はあきらめの一つの形なのだ。この本は誰も治さないだろう。しかし、読者を自分自身にもっと優しくするかもしれない。","words":[["fixation","freq:fixation"],["fret","freq:fret"],["brood","freq:brood"],["visceral","freq:visceral"],["angst","freq:angst"],["formative","freq:formative"],["reassurance","freq:reassurance"],["aversion","freq:aversion"],["paternal","freq:paternal"],["intolerant","freq:intolerant"],["gratification","freq:gratification"],["dismay","freq:dismay"],["lucid","freq:lucid"],["morbid","freq:morbid"],["apathy","freq:apathy"]],"qs":[{"q":"According to Hassan, what often produces adults who avoid risk?","o":["Too much praise in childhood","A childhood with little reassurance","A lack of formal education","Reading books about anxiety"],"a":1,"why":"Children who received little reassurance ... often grow into adults with an aversion to risk とある。"},{"q":"What does Hassan think of advice to \"think positively\"?","o":["She recommends it to all patients.","She believes it works for children only.","She invented it herself.","She distrusts it, since patients blame themselves when it fails."],"a":3,"why":"She is suspicious of advice ... と述べ、tried it and blamed themselves when it failed と記している。"}]},
{"id":"f-0111","page":"freq","group":"1501〜1600","kind":"山岳救助隊長の横顔","text":"Anja Kessler has led the mountain rescue team at Brunnhorn for twenty years, and she dislikes being asked about heroism. \"I am a realist,\" she says. \"Most of what we do is carrying things.\" Her colleagues tell a different story. They commend her judgment and her knack for reading weather that would hamper any other team. She has firsthand knowledge of every route on the mountain, and she can usually fathom where a lost climber has gone before the search begins. Ordinarily the work is routine: a twisted ankle, a walker caught by darkness. Now and then it is not. Last February her team spent nineteen hours bringing down two students from a ledge at three thousand meters. A rope hit a snag, and Kessler had to tweak the whole plan on the spot. She does not flatter the people she rescues. Many, she says, are barely literate in the basic rules of the mountains and believe that their energy is limitless. But she is merciful when it matters, and she never sends a bill. Asked what keeps her going, she mentions one thing. To see a family reunited at the hut door, she says, will uplift anyone.","ja":"アニャ・ケスラーはブルンホルンの山岳救助隊を20年率いており、英雄的行為について尋ねられるのを嫌う。「私は現実主義者です」と彼女は言う。「私たちがすることの大半は、物を運ぶことです」。同僚たちは違う話をする。彼らは彼女の判断力と、ほかのどの隊でも妨げになるような天候を読む才覚を称賛する。彼女はこの山のあらゆる経路を直接知っており、道に迷った登山者がどこへ行ったのかを、たいていは捜索が始まる前に見抜くことができる。通常、仕事は決まりきったものだ。くじいた足首、日没に追いつかれた歩行者。時にはそうでないこともある。昨年の2月、彼女の隊は標高3,000メートルの岩棚から学生2人を降ろすのに19時間を費やした。綱が思わぬ障害に引っかかり、ケスラーはその場で計画全体を微調整しなければならなかった。彼女は救助した人々にお世辞を言わない。多くは山の基本的な決まりをほとんど知らず、自分の体力は無限だと信じていると彼女は言う。しかし、肝心なときには慈悲深く、請求書を送ることは決してない。何が彼女を支えているのかと尋ねられ、彼女は一つのことを挙げる。小屋の戸口で家族が再会するのを見れば、誰でも気持ちが高まると彼女は言う。","words":[["heroism","freq:heroism"],["realist","freq:realist"],["commend","freq:commend"],["knack","freq:knack"],["hamper","freq:hamper"],["firsthand","freq:firsthand"],["fathom","freq:fathom"],["Ordinarily","freq:ordinarily"],["snag","freq:snag"],["tweak","freq:tweak"],["flatter","freq:flatter"],["literate","freq:literate"],["limitless","freq:limitless"],["merciful","freq:merciful"],["uplift","freq:uplift"]],"qs":[{"q":"What do Kessler's colleagues praise?","o":["Her judgment and her ability to read the weather","Her skill at writing reports","Her speed at climbing","Her talks to students"],"a":0,"why":"They commend her judgment and her knack for reading weather（判断力と天候を読む才覚を称賛する）とある。"},{"q":"What does she say about many of the people she rescues?","o":["They pay too little.","They are experienced climbers.","They know little about basic mountain rules and overestimate their strength.","They refuse to follow her team."],"a":2,"why":"barely literate in the basic rules of the mountains and believe that their energy is limitless とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0112","page":"freq","group":"1501〜1600","kind":"古い物語の読み直し","text":"The folk tale of the two brothers is known in disparate forms across half the world. In the best-known version, a younger brother sets out to avenge his father, who has been killed by a giant. The story is often told to children, though parts of it are gruesome. The giant's treatment of his prisoners is inhuman, and the scene in which the hero must crouch all night in a pit of bones is hard to forget. Scholars frown on attempts to soften such details. The darkness, they argue, is the point. To the people who first told the tale, a world without sudden death was unthinkable, and stories were a way of facing it. A new study adds an interesting idea. In many versions the elder brother is the bane of the family, lazy and cruel. Yet at the end he is forgiven, usually to the detriment of the plot. The author suggests that this ending was added later, when listeners no longer wanted a dismal conclusion. The older version, in which only one brother returns, survives in a single manuscript, and it is far the stronger tale.","ja":"2人の兄弟の民話は、世界の半分にわたって、まったく異なる形で知られている。最もよく知られた語りでは、弟が、巨人に殺された父の復讐をするために旅立つ。この物語はよく子どもに語られるが、部分的にはむごたらしい。巨人による捕虜の扱いは非人間的で、主人公が骨だらけの穴の中に一晩中しゃがんでいなければならない場面は忘れがたい。学者たちは、こうした細部を和らげようとする試みに眉をひそめる。暗さこそが要点なのだと彼らは主張する。この物語を最初に語った人々にとって、突然の死のない世界は考えられないものであり、物語はそれに向き合う方法だった。新しい研究は興味深い考えを付け加えている。多くの語りでは、兄は怠け者で残酷な、一家の災いのもとだ。それでも最後に彼は赦される。たいていは筋を損なう形で。著者は、この結末は、聞き手がもう惨めな結末を望まなくなった後の時代に加えられたものだと示唆する。兄弟の一人しか帰らない古い語りは、ただ一つの写本に残っており、物語としてははるかに力強い。","words":[["disparate","freq:disparate"],["avenge","freq:avenge"],["gruesome","freq:gruesome"],["inhuman","freq:inhuman"],["crouch","freq:crouch"],["frown","freq:frown"],["unthinkable","freq:unthinkable"],["bane","freq:bane"],["detriment","freq:detriment"],["dismal","freq:dismal"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why do scholars oppose softening the tale's dark details?","o":["Children enjoy them.","They believe the darkness is the purpose of the story.","The details are historically accurate.","The manuscript forbids changes."],"a":1,"why":"The darkness, they argue, is the point（暗さこそが要点）とある。"},{"q":"What does the new study suggest about the forgiving ending?","o":["It is the oldest part of the tale.","It was copied from another country.","It appears in only one manuscript.","It was added later for listeners who wanted a happier conclusion."],"a":3,"why":"this ending was added later, when listeners no longer wanted a dismal conclusion とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0113","page":"freq","group":"1601〜1700","kind":"休戦協定の内幕","text":"The armistice that ended the Border War in 1953 is usually presented as a triumph of diplomacy. Papers released last month tell a more troubled story. By the autumn of that year, both armies were exhausted. A horde of refugees had filled the southern towns, and commanders feared a calamity if fighting continued through the winter. The talks, held under the auspices of a neutral government, nearly failed at once. The northern delegation issued an ultimatum: either the southern army would dismantle its forts along the river, or the war would go on. A renegade general in the south then seized an armory and threatened to fight alone, promising the annihilation of anyone who signed. His own government had to denounce him publicly before talks could resume. What saved the agreement was the fragmentation of opinion within each side. Hard-line leaders had lost their clout, and moderate officers, men whose valor in battle nobody could question, were willing to compromise. Their views began to converge on a simple exchange of prisoners and a fixed border. The enactment of the treaty took three more months. The papers show that it was signed with relief rather than joy.","ja":"1953年に国境戦争を終わらせた休戦協定は、たいてい外交の勝利として紹介される。先月公開された文書は、もっと苦難に満ちた物語を語っている。その年の秋までに、両軍は疲れ果てていた。難民の大群が南部の町々を埋め、司令官たちは、戦闘が冬を越して続けば大惨事になると恐れた。中立国政府の後援のもとで行われた協議は、すぐに失敗しかけた。北の代表団は最後通告を突きつけた。南の軍が川沿いのとりでを解体するか、さもなければ戦争は続く、というものだ。すると南の離反した将軍が兵器庫を占拠して単独で戦うと脅し、署名する者は誰でも全滅させると言い放った。協議を再開するには、彼自身の政府が公然と彼を非難しなければならなかった。合意を救ったのは、双方の内部で意見が分裂していたことだった。強硬派の指導者は影響力を失っており、戦場での勇敢さを誰も疑えない穏健な将校たちが、妥協する意思を持っていた。彼らの考えは、単純な捕虜交換と国境の確定へとまとまり始めた。条約の成立にはさらに3か月かかった。文書は、それが喜びよりも安堵とともに署名されたことを示している。","words":[["armistice","freq:armistice"],["horde","freq:horde"],["calamity","freq:calamity"],["auspices","freq:auspices"],["ultimatum","freq:ultimatum"],["dismantle","freq:dismantle"],["renegade","freq:renegade"],["armory","freq:armory"],["annihilation","freq:annihilation"],["denounce","freq:denounce"],["fragmentation","freq:fragmentation"],["clout","freq:clout"],["valor","freq:valor"],["converge","freq:converge"],["enactment","freq:enactment"]],"qs":[{"q":"What almost ended the talks after the northern demand?","o":["A general in the south seized weapons and threatened to fight on.","The neutral government withdrew.","Refugees attacked the delegations.","Winter arrived early."],"a":0,"why":"A renegade general in the south then seized an armory and threatened to fight alone とある。"},{"q":"What finally made agreement possible?","o":["A decisive battle","A new ultimatum from the south","Moderate officers on both sides were ready to compromise.","The refugees returned home."],"a":2,"why":"Hard-line leaders had lost their clout, and moderate officers ... were willing to compromise とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0114","page":"freq","group":"1601〜1700","kind":"研究不正の調査","text":"A university inquiry has found a senior professor guilty of plagiarism and of misleading the public about his research. The case began when a student noticed an inconsistency between two of his papers. Passages in the second matched, almost word for word, an article by a foreign scientist. The professor's first defense was that the similarity was accidental and the accusation unfounded. He later blamed an assistant, whom the inquiry describes as an unwilling accomplice. Investigators also examined how the university had missed the problem. They found a loophole in its rules: papers written with outside partners were not checked. There had been a leakage of confidential data as well, because the laboratory's firewall had not been updated in four years. The report rejects the idea that the errors were merely logistical. It speaks plainly of deceit, and it calls several of his published claims erroneous. The professor says that all his partnerships were consensual and properly recorded. The university has accepted every recommendation. Honesty, the report concludes, is a prerequisite for research, and staff must now have the requisite training. Critics note that the upkeep of such systems costs money, and that the university's recent diversification into commercial work may have encouraged haste.","ja":"ある大学の調査が、上級教授を盗用と、自分の研究について世間を誤解させたことで有責と認めた。この件は、ある学生が彼の2本の論文の間の矛盾に気づいたときに始まった。2本目の中の文章は、外国の科学者の論文とほとんど一語一語一致していた。教授の最初の弁明は、類似は偶然で、告発には根拠がないというものだった。後に彼は助手のせいにしたが、調査はその助手を不本意な共犯者と表現している。調査官たちは、大学がなぜこの問題を見逃したのかも調べた。彼らは規則の抜け穴を見つけた。外部の協力者と書いた論文は点検されていなかったのだ。研究室のファイアウォールが4年間更新されていなかったため、機密データの漏洩も起きていた。報告書は、誤りが単に段取り上のものだったという考えを退けている。はっきりと欺きについて語り、彼の公表した主張のいくつかを誤りだとしている。教授は、自分の協力関係はすべて合意の上で、適切に記録されていたと言う。大学はすべての勧告を受け入れた。正直さは研究の前提条件であり、職員は今後、必要な研修を受けなければならないと報告書は結論づけている。批判する人々は、そうした仕組みの維持には金がかかること、そして大学が最近、商業的な仕事へと多角化したことが性急さを助長したかもしれないことを指摘している。","words":[["plagiarism","freq:plagiarism"],["inconsistency","freq:inconsistency"],["unfounded","freq:unfounded"],["accomplice","freq:accomplice"],["loophole","freq:loophole"],["leakage","freq:leakage"],["firewall","freq:firewall"],["logistical","freq:logistical"],["deceit","freq:deceit"],["erroneous","freq:erroneous"],["consensual","freq:consensual"],["prerequisite","freq:prerequisite"],["requisite","freq:requisite"],["upkeep","freq:upkeep"],["diversification","freq:diversification"]],"qs":[{"q":"How was the problem first discovered?","o":["A foreign scientist complained.","A student noticed that two papers did not agree.","The assistant confessed.","The firewall recorded an attack."],"a":1,"why":"a student noticed an inconsistency between two of his papers とある。"},{"q":"What weakness did investigators find in the university's rules?","o":["Students were not allowed to read papers.","Professors could not work with outside partners.","Assistants were never trained.","Papers written with outside partners went unchecked."],"a":3,"why":"papers written with outside partners were not checked（外部協力者との論文は点検されていなかった）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0115","page":"freq","group":"1601〜1700","kind":"砂漠の夜の生き物","text":"The Sarn Desert seems lifeless by day. To understand it, you must wait for darkness. Most of its animals are nocturnal, and as the heat fades they ascend from burrows that reach deep into the cooler strata of rock. Dr. Hana Idris has studied the desert for twelve years. Her main subject is a small reptile, a lizard with luminous spots along its back whose purpose is still unknown. To catch one, she sets a gentle snare of thread beside a secluded pool, one of the few places where water continues to trickle through the dry season. Her work combines biology with meteorology. By placing a rainfall map as an overlay on her records, she has been able to discern a pattern. The lizards breed only after recurrent storms, which occur perhaps once in six years. In between, the population appears almost homogeneous in age. She worries about a new threat. A fungal blight has killed many of the desert shrubs, and the insects that depend on them are disappearing. Several lizards she has caught show signs of malnutrition. The desert, she says, has survived a great deal, but it has never had to change this fast.","ja":"サーン砂漠は、昼間は生き物がいないように見える。それを理解するには、暗くなるのを待たなければならない。そこの動物の大半は夜行性で、暑さが引くにつれて、より涼しい岩の地層の奥深くまで届く巣穴から上がってくる。ハナ・イドリス博士は12年間この砂漠を研究してきた。主な研究対象は小さなは虫類で、背中に沿って光る斑点を持つトカゲだ。その斑点の役割はまだわかっていない。1匹を捕まえるために、彼女は人里離れた水たまりのそばに糸の穏やかなわなを仕掛ける。そこは乾季を通じて水がちょろちょろ流れ続ける数少ない場所の一つだ。彼女の研究は生物学と気象学を組み合わせている。自分の記録の上に降雨量の地図を重ねることで、彼女はある傾向を見分けることができた。このトカゲは、おそらく6年に一度起こる、繰り返しの嵐の後にしか繁殖しない。その合間には、個体群の年齢はほとんど同質に見える。彼女は新しい脅威を心配している。菌による胴枯れ病が砂漠の低木の多くを枯らし、それに頼る昆虫が姿を消しつつある。彼女が捕まえたトカゲの何匹かは栄養失調の兆候を見せている。砂漠は多くのことを生き延びてきたが、これほど速く変わらなければならなかったことは一度もないと彼女は言う。","words":[["nocturnal","freq:nocturnal"],["ascend","freq:ascend"],["strata","freq:strata"],["reptile","freq:reptile"],["luminous","freq:luminous"],["snare","freq:snare"],["secluded","freq:secluded"],["trickle","freq:trickle"],["meteorology","freq:meteorology"],["overlay","freq:overlay"],["discern","freq:discern"],["recurrent","freq:recurrent"],["homogeneous","freq:homogeneous"],["blight","freq:blight"],["malnutrition","freq:malnutrition"]],"qs":[{"q":"What pattern did Dr. Idris find in the lizards' breeding?","o":["They breed every spring.","They breed only after repeated storms.","They breed in the hottest months.","They never breed near water."],"a":1,"why":"The lizards breed only after recurrent storms（繰り返しの嵐の後にしか繁殖しない）とある。"},{"q":"Why are some lizards showing signs of malnutrition?","o":["A disease has killed shrubs, and the insects they eat are vanishing.","The pools have dried up.","Tourists are feeding them.","The burrows have collapsed."],"a":0,"why":"A fungal blight has killed many of the desert shrubs, and the insects that depend on them are disappearing とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0116","page":"freq","group":"1601〜1700","kind":"歴史映画の批評","text":"The House of Varen is a film about an aristocratic family in decline, and it has all the grandeur that the subject invites: candlelit halls, long corridors, and snow falling on a ruined garden. Its cinematography is superb. Every frame could hang in a gallery. The story is harder to summarize. The director refuses to follow a simple chronology, and the first hour moves between three periods with only cryptic hints to guide us. The mother, played with great restraint, remains an enigma to the end. At the center is a seductive stranger who arrives to recount a secret about the family's past. The dialogue is the film's weakness. The servants speak a lively vernacular, but the family talk in the jargon of philosophy, and their metaphysical debates slow the action. Comedy is clearly not the director's forte. Still, the final scene is unforgettable. On a commemorative evening for the dead father, the last guests leave, and the daughter closes the shutters one by one. It is a bittersweet image of an ending that is also a release. The film asks for patience, and for those who give it, the reward is considerable.","ja":"「ヴァーレン家」は没落していく貴族の一家を描いた映画で、その題材が招く壮大さをすべて備えている。ろうそくに照らされた広間、長い廊下、荒れた庭に降る雪。撮影技術は見事だ。どの画面も画廊に掛けられるほどである。物語は要約しにくい。監督は単純な年代順に従うことを拒み、最初の1時間は、謎めいた手がかりだけを頼りに3つの時代の間を行き来する。非常に抑えた演技で演じられる母親は、最後まで謎のままだ。中心にいるのは、一家の過去についての秘密を語るためにやって来る魅惑的なよそ者である。台詞がこの映画の弱点だ。使用人たちは生き生きとした土地の言葉を話すが、家族は哲学の専門用語で語り、その形而上学的な議論が話の進みを遅くする。喜劇は明らかにこの監督の得意分野ではない。それでも、最後の場面は忘れがたい。亡き父をしのぶ記念の晩、最後の客が去り、娘がよろい戸を一つずつ閉めていく。それは、終わりであると同時に解放でもあるものの、ほろ苦い映像だ。この映画は忍耐を求めるが、それを差し出す人にとって、見返りは相当なものだ。","words":[["aristocratic","freq:aristocratic"],["grandeur","freq:grandeur"],["cinematography","freq:cinematography"],["summarize","freq:summarize"],["chronology","freq:chronology"],["cryptic","freq:cryptic"],["enigma","freq:enigma"],["seductive","freq:seductive"],["recount","freq:recount"],["vernacular","freq:vernacular"],["jargon","freq:jargon"],["metaphysical","freq:metaphysical"],["forte","freq:forte"],["commemorative","freq:commemorative"],["bittersweet","freq:bittersweet"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why is the story hard to follow at first?","o":["It moves between different periods with few clear signs.","The actors speak too quietly.","The film has no ending.","The servants narrate it."],"a":0,"why":"The director refuses to follow a simple chronology とあり、only cryptic hints で3つの時代を行き来するとある。"},{"q":"What does the reviewer consider the film's weakness?","o":["The photography","The final scene","The dialogue of the family","The mother's performance"],"a":2,"why":"The dialogue is the film's weakness とあり、家族の metaphysical debates が話を遅くするとある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0117","page":"freq","group":"1601〜1700","kind":"二人の研究者","text":"The partnership between Sofia Brandt and Emil Okafor produced some of the most important physics of the century, and it was a study in contrasts. Brandt was conscientious to a fault. Her notebooks were meticulous, and her modesty was so great that she twice refused prizes. Colleagues thought her timid, even submissive, until they heard her defend a result. Okafor was the opposite: forceful, abrasive in argument, and a fanatic for speed. He found her caution maddening, and he called her objections frivolous more than once. Yet each supplied what the other lacked. His ingenuity produced ideas faster than anyone could test them, and her patience found the errors. A former student recalls the day their great experiment finally worked. Okafor was giddy with euphoria and wanted to announce it at once. Brandt insisted on three more weeks of checks. She was right; a mindless error in the wiring had flattered the first result. Some colleagues were envious of their success, and rumors about quarrels were common. Those who knew them say the quarrels were real but never personal. Both, in their different ways, were virtuous scientists, loyal above all to the evidence.","ja":"ソフィア・ブラントとエミル・オカフォーの協力関係は、その世紀で最も重要な物理学のいくつかを生み出した。そしてそれは対照の見本だった。ブラントは度が過ぎるほど念入りだった。彼女のノートは細心で、謙遜のあまり2度も賞を辞退した。同僚たちは彼女を臆病で、従順でさえあると思っていた。彼女が結果を擁護するのを聞くまでは。オカフォーは正反対だった。力強く、議論ではとげとげしく、速さの狂信者だった。彼は彼女の慎重さに腹を立て、彼女の異議をくだらないと呼んだことも一度ではなかった。それでも、それぞれが相手に欠けているものを補った。彼の創意工夫は誰にも検証しきれない速さで考えを生み出し、彼女の忍耐が誤りを見つけた。ある元学生は、2人の大きな実験がついに成功した日を覚えている。オカフォーは幸福感に浮かれ、すぐに発表したがった。ブラントはさらに3週間の点検を主張した。彼女が正しかった。配線の不注意な誤りが、最初の結果を実際より良く見せていたのだ。2人の成功をうらやむ同僚もおり、けんかのうわさはよくあった。2人を知る人々は、けんかは本物だったが、決して個人的なものではなかったと言う。2人とも、それぞれのやり方で高潔な科学者であり、何よりも証拠に忠実だった。","words":[["conscientious","freq:conscientious"],["meticulous","freq:meticulous"],["modesty","freq:modesty"],["timid","freq:timid"],["submissive","freq:submissive"],["forceful","freq:forceful"],["abrasive","freq:abrasive"],["fanatic","freq:fanatic"],["frivolous","freq:frivolous"],["ingenuity","freq:ingenuity"],["giddy","freq:giddy"],["euphoria","freq:euphoria"],["mindless","freq:mindless"],["envious","freq:envious"],["virtuous","freq:virtuous"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did the two scientists complement each other?","o":["He produced ideas quickly, and she patiently found the errors.","She raised money, and he did the experiments.","He wrote the papers, and she gave the lectures.","She worked fast, and he checked her notes."],"a":0,"why":"His ingenuity produced ideas faster than anyone could test them, and her patience found the errors とある。"},{"q":"What happened when their great experiment first seemed to work?","o":["They announced it immediately.","Okafor refused to believe it.","Brandt insisted on more checks and found an error.","The laboratory was closed."],"a":2,"why":"Brandt insisted on three more weeks of checks. She was right; a mindless error in the wiring ... とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0118","page":"freq","group":"1601〜1700","kind":"貧困地区の女性たち","text":"In the largest slum of the capital, a group of women has spent ten years building something that outsiders said was impossible. The district is poor, and its culture is strongly patriarchal. Women who worked outside the home were once described in derogatory terms, and neighbors would shun a family whose daughter stayed at school. The effects were insidious. Girls married young, and the local economy remained sluggish because half its talent was unused. The change began with a savings club of twelve members. They met weekly to formulate small plans: a shared sewing machine, a stall at the market. Familial opposition was strong at first. One founder recalls the pitiful sum they started with, less than a week's wages between them. What mattered more was intangible. Each success was an affirmation that they could manage their own affairs, and the club's meetings proved conducive to other ambitions. Members began to enlighten one another about health, law, and schooling. Today there is a plethora of such clubs, with nine thousand members. A plan for a women's bank, first discussed in 2015, finally came to fruition this spring. Its first loan paid for a daughter's university fees.","ja":"首都最大のスラムで、ある女性たちの集まりが10年をかけて、部外者が不可能だと言ったものを築いてきた。この地区は貧しく、その文化は強く家父長制的だ。家の外で働く女性はかつて軽蔑的な言葉で呼ばれ、娘が学校に通い続ける家族を近所の人々は避けたものだった。その影響は知らぬ間に進行した。少女たちは若くして結婚し、才能の半分が使われなかったために地元の経済は停滞したままだった。変化は12人の会員による貯蓄の会から始まった。彼女たちは毎週集まって小さな計画を立てた。共用のミシン、市場の売店。最初、家族の反対は強かった。創設者の一人は、始めたときの哀れなほどわずかな金額を覚えている。全員合わせても1週間分の賃金に満たなかった。もっと大切だったのは目に見えないものだった。一つひとつの成功が、自分たちのことは自分たちで管理できるという確認であり、会の集まりはほかの志を育てる助けになった。会員たちは健康や法律、学校教育について互いに教え合い始めた。今日、こうした会はあり余るほどあり、会員は9,000人いる。2015年に初めて話し合われた女性のための銀行の計画は、この春ついに実現した。最初の融資は、ある娘の大学の学費に充てられた。","words":[["slum","freq:slum"],["patriarchal","freq:patriarchal"],["derogatory","freq:derogatory"],["shun","freq:shun"],["insidious","freq:insidious"],["sluggish","freq:sluggish"],["formulate","freq:formulate"],["Familial","freq:familial"],["pitiful","freq:pitiful"],["intangible","freq:intangible"],["affirmation","freq:affirmation"],["conducive","freq:conducive"],["enlighten","freq:enlighten"],["plethora","freq:plethora"],["fruition","freq:fruition"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did the change in the district begin?","o":["With a government grant","With a new school for girls","With a small savings club","With a protest march"],"a":2,"why":"The change began with a savings club of twelve members（12人の貯蓄の会から始まった）とある。"},{"q":"What does the passage say mattered more than money?","o":["The confidence each success gave the women","The support of their husbands","The size of the market","The help of outsiders"],"a":0,"why":"What mattered more was intangible. Each success was an affirmation that they could manage their own affairs とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0119","page":"freq","group":"1601〜1700","kind":"村の夏至祭","text":"On the shortest night of the year, the village of Skarholm keeps a vigil that is older than its church. At dusk the villagers gather on the hill and light a fire that must burn, uninterrupted, until dawn. Everyone is expected to partake in the work of feeding it. Children carry sticks from a hoard of wood collected since spring, and the old people sing. According to legend, the fire keeps away an imp who comes to seduce travelers from the road and lead them into the marsh. Whether anyone still believes this is a moot point. What is certain is that the custom has survived everything, including a whirlwind in 1904 that scattered the fire across the hillside. I joined the villagers last June. Toward two in the morning, when the cold had begun to puncture even the thickest coat, an old farmer handed me a cup of hot milk and pointed east. \"Day is nigh,\" he said. An hour later, the first light touched the sea, the singing stopped, and for a long moment nobody spoke. Then the fire was allowed to die, and the village went home to sleep.","ja":"1年で最も夜の短い日、スカルホルムの村は、その教会よりも古い寝ずの番を守る。夕暮れに村人たちは丘に集まり、夜明けまで途切れることなく燃え続けなければならない火をたく。火にまきをくべる仕事には、誰もが加わることになっている。子どもたちは春から集めてきた木の蓄えから枝を運び、年寄りたちは歌う。伝説によれば、その火は、旅人を誘惑して道からそらし、沼地へ連れ込みに来る小鬼を遠ざけるのだという。今もそれを信じている人がいるかどうかは、議論の余地のある点だ。確かなのは、この慣習があらゆるものを生き延びてきたことだ。火を丘の斜面一面にまき散らした1904年の旋風も含めて。私は昨年の6月、村人たちに加わった。午前2時頃、寒さが最も厚いコートさえ突き通し始めたとき、年老いた農夫が私に熱い牛乳の入ったカップを手渡し、東を指さした。「夜明けは近い」と彼は言った。1時間後、最初の光が海に触れ、歌は止まり、長い間、誰も口をきかなかった。それから火は消えるに任され、村人たちは眠りに家へ帰った。","words":[["vigil","freq:vigil"],["uninterrupted","freq:uninterrupted"],["partake","freq:partake"],["hoard","freq:hoard"],["imp","freq:imp"],["seduce","freq:seduce"],["moot","freq:moot"],["whirlwind","freq:whirlwind"],["puncture","freq:puncture"],["nigh","freq:nigh"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is the fire said to protect against?","o":["Storms from the sea","A creature that lures travelers into the marsh","Thieves from other villages","The cold of winter"],"a":1,"why":"the fire keeps away an imp who comes to seduce travelers from the road and lead them into the marsh とある。"},{"q":"What happened when the first light appeared?","o":["The villagers lit a second fire.","The children ran down the hill.","The farmer began to sing.","The singing stopped and everyone fell silent."],"a":3,"why":"the singing stopped, and for a long moment nobody spoke とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0120","page":"freq","group":"1701〜1800","kind":"独裁政権の崩壊","text":"For twenty-two years, General Otar Meskhi ruled the republic of Dalnia as a totalitarian state. He would wield power through a paramilitary police that answered only to him, and any writer judged subversive disappeared. Foreign governments, anxious to keep the peace, chose to appease him. The end came quickly. In the spring of 1989, an insurgent movement in the mountains began to win support in the towns. Meskhi ordered an onslaught against three villages, and the public outcry reached even his own officers. A breakaway group within the army refused to fire on a crowd in the capital. Within a week, the general's guards had been persuaded to disarm. What followed was not simple. Reactionary members of the old regime tried to assassinate the new prime minister, and for a time it seemed that one conqueror would merely replace another. The young government managed to repel two attempts to seize the radio station. Historians still argue about why the system fell so fast. The most persuasive answer is the plainest. A state that depends on fear, one of them writes, has no defense once enough people become defiant on the same day.","ja":"22年の間、オタール・メスヒ将軍はダルニア共和国を全体主義国家として支配した。彼は自分にだけ従う準軍事的な警察を通じて権力を振るい、体制を覆そうとしていると判断された作家は誰でも姿を消した。平和を保ちたい外国政府は、彼に譲歩することを選んだ。終わりは速くやって来た。1989年の春、山岳地帯の反乱運動が町々で支持を得始めた。メスヒは3つの村への猛攻撃を命じ、世間の激しい抗議は彼自身の将校たちにまで届いた。軍内部の離脱した一派が、首都で群衆に発砲することを拒んだ。1週間のうちに、将軍の護衛たちは説得されて武装を解いた。その後は単純ではなかった。旧政権の反動的な人々が新しい首相を暗殺しようとし、一時は、ある征服者が別の征服者に取って代わるだけに見えた。若い政府は、放送局を占拠しようとする2度の試みを撃退することができた。歴史家たちは、この体制がなぜこれほど速く倒れたのかを今も議論している。最も説得力のある答えは最も単純なものだ。恐怖に頼る国家は、十分な数の人々が同じ日に反抗的になれば、身を守るすべがないと、歴史家の一人は書いている。","words":[["totalitarian","freq:totalitarian"],["wield","freq:wield"],["paramilitary","freq:paramilitary"],["subversive","freq:subversive"],["appease","freq:appease"],["insurgent","freq:insurgent"],["onslaught","freq:onslaught"],["outcry","freq:outcry"],["breakaway","freq:breakaway"],["disarm","freq:disarm"],["Reactionary","freq:reactionary"],["assassinate","freq:assassinate"],["conqueror","freq:conqueror"],["repel","freq:repel"],["defiant","freq:defiant"]],"qs":[{"q":"What turned the general's own officers against him?","o":["A foreign invasion","The attack he ordered on three villages","A cut in their pay","The death of the prime minister"],"a":1,"why":"Meskhi ordered an onslaught against three villages, and the public outcry reached even his own officers とある。"},{"q":"What explanation for the regime's fall does the passage find most convincing?","o":["The army was too small.","Foreign governments withdrew support.","A state built on fear collapses when enough people defy it at once.","The general became ill."],"a":2,"why":"A state that depends on fear ... has no defense once enough people become defiant on the same day とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0121","page":"freq","group":"1701〜1800","kind":"合併交渉の失敗","text":"The collapse of the planned merger between two shipping firms, Aldane and Costra, has been called the business debacle of the year. On paper, the deal was attractive. Both companies came from old mercantile families, and analysts predicted a synergy worth a whopping two billion dollars. Almost every term was negotiable. Then the difficulties began. Costra's board had made an unsolicited offer, and Aldane's directors suspected a ploy to learn their prices. They agreed to talk only on the presumption that confidential figures would not be shared. Costra then asked for preferential access to Aldane's ports, which regulators said was not permissible. Market conditions turned unfavorable in the autumn, and the board decided to defer its decision. The final blow was unexpected. An inquest into the death of a sailor on a Costra ship heard evidence of indecent haste in safety checks, and Aldane's chairman announced that his firm could not affirm its confidence in its partner. Costra's shares fell by a fifth. Both companies now say that they will continue alone. Neither will admit how much the eighteen months of talks have cost.","ja":"海運会社2社、オルデインとコストラの間で計画されていた合併の破談は、今年の実業界の大失敗と呼ばれている。書類上、この取引は魅力的だった。両社とも古い商人の一族に由来し、専門家は20億ドルという途方もない相乗効果を予測していた。ほとんどすべての条件に交渉の余地があった。そこから困難が始まった。コストラの取締役会は頼まれもしない申し出をしており、オルデインの取締役たちは、自社の価格を探るための策略ではないかと疑った。彼らは、機密の数字は共有しないという前提でのみ話し合いに応じた。次にコストラはオルデインの港への優先的な利用権を求めたが、規制当局はそれは許されないと述べた。秋には市場の状況が不利になり、取締役会は決定を延期することにした。最後の一撃は予想外だった。コストラの船での船員の死についての死因審問で、安全点検が不適切なほど急いで行われていたという証拠が示され、オルデインの会長は、自社は相手への信頼を確認できないと発表した。コストラの株価は5分の1下落した。両社は今、単独で事業を続けると言っている。18か月の交渉にどれだけの費用がかかったかは、どちらも認めようとしない。","words":[["debacle","freq:debacle"],["mercantile","freq:mercantile"],["synergy","freq:synergy"],["whopping","freq:whopping"],["negotiable","freq:negotiable"],["unsolicited","freq:unsolicited"],["ploy","freq:ploy"],["presumption","freq:presumption"],["preferential","freq:preferential"],["permissible","freq:permissible"],["unfavorable","freq:unfavorable"],["defer","freq:defer"],["inquest","freq:inquest"],["indecent","freq:indecent"],["affirm","freq:affirm"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why were Aldane's directors suspicious of the offer?","o":["They feared it was a trick to learn their prices.","They thought the price was too high.","They disliked the regulators.","They had already agreed to another merger."],"a":0,"why":"Aldane's directors suspected a ploy to learn their prices とある。"},{"q":"What finally ended the talks?","o":["A fall in shipping prices","A dispute between two families","A new law on ports","Evidence about safety checks that emerged at an inquest"],"a":3,"why":"An inquest ... heard evidence of indecent haste in safety checks、その後会長が信頼を確認できないと発表した。"}]},
{"id":"f-0122","page":"freq","group":"1701〜1800","kind":"渡り鳥の研究","text":"Each October, a vast expanse of marsh on the northern coast fills with geese. They are migratory birds, arriving from the Arctic in flocks so large that the sky seems to move. From the panoramic windows of the research station, scientists count them as they swoop down to feed. The work began in 1958, and its records are among the longest in Europe. They allow researchers to reconstruct how the timing of migration has changed. On frosty mornings the birds once arrived in the first week of the month. They now come almost three weeks later, and the dates correlate closely with climatic data from the Arctic. The station's methods are simple. A few birds are caught, weighed, and marked with a translucent ring. Blood samples are stored in a flask of cold liquid. The nomenclature of the subspecies is still debated, and a recurrence of an old argument divides the team each year. There are dangers. A ferocious storm in 2013 flooded the marsh, and the fragrant reed beds took years to recover. The director says there is no alchemy in the work. Its value lies in doing the same thing, carefully, for sixty years.","ja":"毎年10月、北の海岸の広大な湿地はガンでいっぱいになる。彼らは渡り鳥で、空が動いているように見えるほど大きな群れをなして北極圏からやって来る。観測所の全景を見渡せる窓から、科学者たちは餌を食べに急降下してくる鳥を数える。この仕事は1958年に始まり、その記録はヨーロッパで最も長いものの一つだ。その記録のおかげで、研究者は渡りの時期がどう変わってきたかを再現できる。かつて鳥たちは、霜の降りた朝、その月の最初の週に到着していた。今ではほぼ3週間遅く来るようになり、その日付は北極圏の気候のデータと密接に相関している。観測所の方法は単純だ。数羽を捕まえて体重を量り、半透明の足輪で印を付ける。血液の試料は冷たい液体を入れた瓶に保存する。亜種の命名法は今も議論されており、古い論争の再燃が毎年チームを二分する。危険もある。2013年の猛烈な嵐は湿地を水浸しにし、香りのよいアシ原は回復に何年もかかった。所長は、この仕事に錬金術のような秘密はないと言う。その価値は、同じことを注意深く60年間続けることにあるのだ。","words":[["expanse","freq:expanse"],["migratory","freq:migratory"],["panoramic","freq:panoramic"],["swoop","freq:swoop"],["reconstruct","freq:reconstruct"],["frosty","freq:frosty"],["correlate","freq:correlate"],["climatic","freq:climatic"],["translucent","freq:translucent"],["flask","freq:flask"],["nomenclature","freq:nomenclature"],["recurrence","freq:recurrence"],["ferocious","freq:ferocious"],["fragrant","freq:fragrant"],["alchemy","freq:alchemy"]],"qs":[{"q":"What have the station's long records shown?","o":["The geese now arrive about three weeks later than before.","The number of geese has doubled.","The geese no longer come from the Arctic.","The marsh is getting larger."],"a":0,"why":"They now come almost three weeks later（今ではほぼ3週間遅く来る）とある。"},{"q":"According to the director, where does the value of the work lie?","o":["In new and expensive equipment","In repeating the same careful work over many years","In settling the debate about subspecies","In protecting the station from storms"],"a":1,"why":"Its value lies in doing the same thing, carefully, for sixty years とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0123","page":"freq","group":"1701〜1800","kind":"校長の退任","text":"When Margaret Lindley became head of Ashgrove School, it was a place of rigid conformity. Pupils wore gray, sat in rows, and were told that individuality was a form of bad manners. Lindley, who retires this summer after thirty years, changed all that. Colleagues describe her demeanor as courteous and agreeable, and visitors often mistook her gentleness for weakness. They were wrong. She could be assertive when the school's interests required it, and governors found her a crafty negotiator. She was also a staunch defender of her staff. Her belief was that every child has an aptitude for something, and that a teacher's vocation is to find it. Under her, the school added workshops, a theater, and a garden. Results improved, though she never treated them as the main point. Former pupils remember small things: her bubbly laugh in the corridor, or the gallant way she once carried a nervous boy's violin onto the stage. She has refused a public ceremony, saying that she feels unworthy of such attention. Having risen from obscurity to eminence in her profession, she plans to return, quite happily, to a cottage and a large dog.","ja":"マーガレット・リンドリーがアッシュグローブ校の校長になったとき、そこは厳格に型にはめる場所だった。生徒は灰色の服を着て列に並んで座り、個性は無作法の一種だと教えられていた。30年を経てこの夏に退職するリンドリーは、そのすべてを変えた。同僚たちは彼女の物腰を礼儀正しく感じがよいと表現し、訪問者はしばしば彼女の優しさを弱さと取り違えた。それは間違いだった。学校の利益が求めるときには彼女ははっきり主張することができ、理事たちは彼女を抜け目のない交渉相手だと知った。彼女はまた、教職員の筋金入りの擁護者でもあった。彼女の信念は、どの子にも何かの適性があり、それを見つけるのが教師の天職だというものだった。彼女のもとで、学校には作業場、劇場、庭が加わった。成績は上がったが、彼女はそれを主眼として扱うことは決してなかった。かつての生徒たちは小さなことを覚えている。廊下での彼女の陽気な笑い声や、緊張した少年のバイオリンを彼女が堂々と舞台まで運んでくれたことなどだ。彼女は、そのような注目には値しないと感じると言って、公の式典を断った。無名からこの職業での名声へと上り詰めた彼女は、小さな家と大きな犬のもとへ、実に幸せそうに戻るつもりだ。","words":[["conformity","freq:conformity"],["individuality","freq:individuality"],["demeanor","freq:demeanor"],["courteous","freq:courteous"],["agreeable","freq:agreeable"],["assertive","freq:assertive"],["crafty","freq:crafty"],["staunch","freq:staunch"],["aptitude","freq:aptitude"],["vocation","freq:vocation"],["bubbly","freq:bubbly"],["gallant","freq:gallant"],["unworthy","freq:unworthy"],["obscurity","freq:obscurity"],["eminence","freq:eminence"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did visitors often misunderstand about Lindley?","o":["They thought her gentle manner meant she was weak.","They thought she disliked children.","They thought she wanted to leave.","They thought she ignored exam results."],"a":0,"why":"visitors often mistook her gentleness for weakness（優しさを弱さと取り違えた）とある。"},{"q":"What was her central belief as a teacher?","o":["Good results matter most.","Pupils should all dress alike.","Rules should never change.","Every child is good at something, and teachers must discover it."],"a":3,"why":"every child has an aptitude for something, and that a teacher's vocation is to find it とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0124","page":"freq","group":"1701〜1800","kind":"仕立て屋の回想録","text":"The memoir of the tailor Giorgio Santoro, published this month, is a rambling but delightful book. Santoro made bespoke suits for actors, kings, and at least one hermit who lived in a cave and paid in gold coins. He tells one anecdote after another, and the reader soon learns not to expect a straight line. His real subject is taste. A good suit, he writes, should be suggestive rather than loud; it should hint at the man inside. He had an almost ethereal sensibility for cloth and could tell a wool's origin by touch. The chapters on his customers are sharper. One is a caricature of a vain politician who demanded a new jacket every week. Another, written with unparalleled tenderness, describes his platonic friendship with a famous singer, whom he dressed for forty years. Santoro makes only an oblique reference to his own poverty as a child. He prefers to speak of refinement, by which he means patience. When his shop chose to unveil its last collection, there was no fanfare, only a note in the window. Critics have reviewed the book favorably, and rightly so.","ja":"今月出版された仕立て屋ジョルジョ・サントーロの回顧録は、とりとめがないが楽しい本だ。サントーロは俳優や王たち、そして洞窟に住んで金貨で支払った少なくとも一人の隠者のために、注文仕立ての背広を作った。彼は逸話を次から次へと語り、読者はまもなく、まっすぐな筋を期待しないことを覚える。彼の本当の主題は趣味のよさだ。良い背広は派手であるよりも、それとなく感じさせるものであるべきで、中にいる人をほのめかすべきだと彼は書く。彼は布地に対してほとんどこの世のものとは思えない感性を持ち、触っただけで羊毛の産地がわかった。客についての章はもっと鋭い。一つは、毎週新しい上着を要求した見えっ張りの政治家の戯画だ。もう一つは、比類のない優しさで書かれており、彼が40年間服を仕立てた有名な歌手との純粋に精神的な友情を描いている。サントーロは、子どもの頃の自分の貧しさには遠回しに触れるだけだ。彼は洗練について語ることを好む。それは彼にとって忍耐を意味する。彼の店が最後のコレクションを公開することにしたとき、派手な宣伝はなく、窓に一枚の知らせがあっただけだった。批評家たちはこの本を好意的に評しているが、それは当然だ。","words":[["rambling","freq:rambling"],["bespoke","freq:bespoke"],["hermit","freq:hermit"],["anecdote","freq:anecdote"],["suggestive","freq:suggestive"],["ethereal","freq:ethereal"],["sensibility","freq:sensibility"],["caricature","freq:caricature"],["unparalleled","freq:unparalleled"],["platonic","freq:platonic"],["oblique","freq:oblique"],["refinement","freq:refinement"],["unveil","freq:unveil"],["fanfare","freq:fanfare"],["favorably","freq:favorably"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does Santoro believe a good suit should do?","o":["Attract as much attention as possible","Hint quietly at the person wearing it","Follow the latest fashion","Cost as little as possible"],"a":1,"why":"should be suggestive rather than loud; it should hint at the man inside とある。"},{"q":"How does he treat his own childhood poverty in the book?","o":["He describes it in detail.","He denies it.","He mentions it only indirectly.","He blames his customers for it."],"a":2,"why":"makes only an oblique reference to his own poverty as a child（遠回しに触れるだけ）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0125","page":"freq","group":"1701〜1800","kind":"移民と農村","text":"A century ago, the plains of the Marrow valley were settled by farmers from across the ocean. The transatlantic journey took three weeks, and most arrived with little money. The society they built was agrarian and proud. Its history is often told as a simple story of assimilation, in which newcomers gave up their customs and became like everyone else. A new study calls this a fallacy. The settlers did not simply blend in. Their stewardship of the land, learned in a colder climate, changed local farming, and their churches became centers of political agitation. Life was hard. Housing in the early years was deplorable, and rowdy Saturday nights in the market towns worried the authorities. To equate the settlers with trouble, however, would be an absurdity. They chose to augment their small incomes with crafts, and their cooperative shops helped to prolong the life of villages that would otherwise have died. Becoming part of a new country did not entail forgetting the old one. The study ends with a proposal to revamp the valley's museum, whose present displays the author calls a feeble version of a remarkable history.","ja":"1世紀前、マロー谷の平原には海の向こうから来た農民が入植した。大西洋を渡る旅は3週間かかり、ほとんどの人はわずかな金しか持たずに到着した。彼らが築いた社会は農業中心で、誇り高かった。その歴史はしばしば、新参者が自分たちの習慣を捨ててほかの皆と同じようになったという、単純な同化の物語として語られる。新しい研究はこれを誤った考えだと呼ぶ。入植者たちは単に溶け込んだのではなかった。より寒い気候で身につけた彼らの土地の管理の仕方は地元の農業を変え、彼らの教会は政治的な扇動の中心になった。生活は厳しかった。初期の住宅はひどいもので、市場町での騒々しい土曜の夜は当局を心配させた。しかし、入植者を厄介ごとと同一視するのはばかげている。彼らは工芸でわずかな収入を増やすことを選び、彼らの協同組合の店は、そうでなければ消えていたはずの村々の命を延ばす助けになった。新しい国の一員になることは、古い国を忘れることを伴わなかった。この研究は、谷の博物館を刷新する提案で終わっている。著者は現在の展示を、驚くべき歴史の説得力のない見せ方だと呼んでいる。","words":[["transatlantic","freq:transatlantic"],["agrarian","freq:agrarian"],["assimilation","freq:assimilation"],["fallacy","freq:fallacy"],["stewardship","freq:stewardship"],["agitation","freq:agitation"],["deplorable","freq:deplorable"],["rowdy","freq:rowdy"],["equate","freq:equate"],["absurdity","freq:absurdity"],["augment","freq:augment"],["prolong","freq:prolong"],["entail","freq:entail"],["revamp","freq:revamp"],["feeble","freq:feeble"]],"qs":[{"q":"What common view of the settlers does the study reject?","o":["That they arrived with little money","That they simply gave up their customs and blended in","That they were farmers","That they built churches"],"a":1,"why":"a simple story of assimilation を a fallacy と呼び、The settlers did not simply blend in とある。"},{"q":"How did the settlers help the villages survive?","o":["By closing the market towns","By moving to the cities","By selling their land","By running cooperative shops and adding craft income"],"a":3,"why":"augment their small incomes with crafts, and their cooperative shops helped to prolong the life of villages とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0126","page":"freq","group":"1701〜1800","kind":"夜の市場","text":"The night market of Old Harbor opens at ten and closes before dawn. To reach it, visitors must take a detour through the back streets, since the main road is closed after dark. The market began as a clandestine affair. During the years of rationing, traders met here in secret, and customers learned to fend for themselves among the stalls. Something of that atmosphere remains. The lanes are narrow, and every corner is filled with clutter: baskets, broken clocks, a deformed bicycle that nobody has ever bought. Tourists mingle with fishermen and night-shift nurses. A brisk trade is done in hot soup. The traders are a mixed group. One old woman, thought by newcomers to be deranged, simply prefers talking to her parrots. Another will gently nudge you toward the best fruit while pretending to rearrange it. By four, the crowd thins. Stallholders pack their goods, the lamps go out one by one, and the district sinks back into slumber. An hour later, nothing remains except a few crates and the smell of ginger. The council has twice tried to move the market indoors, and has twice thought better of it.","ja":"オールド・ハーバーの夜市は10時に開き、夜明け前に閉まる。そこへ行くには、暗くなると大通りが閉鎖されるので、訪問者は裏通りを抜ける回り道をしなければならない。この市場は秘密のものとして始まった。配給の時代、商人たちはここでひそかに集まり、客は売店の間で自力でやっていくことを覚えた。その雰囲気は今もいくらか残っている。路地は狭く、あらゆる隅が散らかった物でいっぱいだ。かご、壊れた時計、誰も買ったことのない変形した自転車。観光客が漁師や夜勤の看護師と入り混じる。熱いスープは活発に売れていく。商人たちは雑多な顔ぶれだ。ある老女は、新顔からは正気を失っていると思われているが、単にオウムと話すほうが好きなだけだ。別の商人は、並べ直すふりをしながら、一番いい果物のほうへあなたをそっと促す。4時には人混みがまばらになる。売り手たちは品物を片づけ、明かりが一つずつ消え、この地区は再び眠りに沈む。1時間後には、いくつかの木箱とショウガの匂いのほかは何も残らない。市議会は2度、市場を屋内に移そうとし、2度とも考え直した。","words":[["detour","freq:detour"],["clandestine","freq:clandestine"],["fend","freq:fend"],["clutter","freq:clutter"],["deformed","freq:deformed"],["mingle","freq:mingle"],["brisk","freq:brisk"],["deranged","freq:deranged"],["nudge","freq:nudge"],["slumber","freq:slumber"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did the market begin?","o":["As a tourist attraction","As a secret meeting place for traders during rationing","As a fish auction","As a festival held once a year"],"a":1,"why":"The market began as a clandestine affair. During the years of rationing, traders met here in secret とある。"},{"q":"What has the council decided about moving the market indoors?","o":["It has moved it.","It will close it.","It has twice considered the idea and dropped it.","It has asked tourists to vote."],"a":2,"why":"has twice tried to move the market indoors, and has twice thought better of it（2度とも考え直した）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0127","page":"freq","group":"1801〜1900","kind":"和平への下準備","text":"Thirty years after the insurrection that divided the island of Kerra, there are signs of a thaw. The northern enclave, which has governed itself since the fighting, last week agreed to exploratory talks with the southern government. A retired bishop is acting as intermediary. The groundwork was laid quietly over two years. Each side took small steps. The south stopped its practice of deciding to deport northerners found without papers, and the north promised to renounce the use of force. A transnational watchdog was allowed to visit prisons on both sides, ending decades in which abuses were committed with impunity. Old animosity has not vanished. When southern veterans tried to mobilize a march along the dividing line last month, northern leaders called it a provocation, and the talks nearly collapsed before they began. The hardest question is disarmament. Thousands of weapons remain hidden in farms and cellars, and neither side trusts the other to give them up first. The bishop is cautious. He told reporters that he had seen too many false dawns to make promises. But he added that for the first time, the young people on both sides seem tired of their parents' war.","ja":"ケラ島を二分した反乱から30年、雪解けの兆しがある。戦闘以来自治を続けてきた北部の孤立地域が先週、南の政府との予備的な協議に同意した。引退した司教が仲介者を務めている。土台は2年かけて静かに築かれた。双方が小さな一歩を踏み出した。南は、書類を持たずに見つかった北の住民を国外追放にするという慣行をやめ、北は武力の行使を放棄すると約束した。国境を越えて活動する監視機関が双方の刑務所を訪れることを許され、虐待が罰を受けずに行われてきた数十年が終わった。古い敵意は消えていない。先月、南の退役軍人たちが分断線に沿った行進に人を動員しようとすると、北の指導者たちはそれを挑発と呼び、協議は始まる前に崩れかけた。最も難しい問題は武装解除だ。数千の武器が農場や地下室に隠されたままで、どちらの側も相手が先に手放すとは信じていない。司教は慎重だ。偽りの夜明けをあまりに多く見てきたので約束はできないと、彼は記者たちに語った。しかし、初めて双方の若者が親たちの戦争にうんざりしているように見えるとも付け加えた。","words":[["insurrection","freq:insurrection"],["thaw","freq:thaw"],["enclave","freq:enclave"],["exploratory","freq:exploratory"],["intermediary","freq:intermediary"],["groundwork","freq:groundwork"],["deport","freq:deport"],["renounce","freq:renounce"],["transnational","freq:transnational"],["watchdog","freq:watchdog"],["impunity","freq:impunity"],["animosity","freq:animosity"],["mobilize","freq:mobilize"],["provocation","freq:provocation"],["disarmament","freq:disarmament"]],"qs":[{"q":"What steps did the two sides take before the talks?","o":["The south stopped expelling northerners, and the north promised not to use force.","Both sides exchanged all prisoners.","The north joined the southern government.","Both sides destroyed their weapons."],"a":0,"why":"The south stopped ... deport northerners ..., and the north promised to renounce the use of force とある。"},{"q":"What is the most difficult issue?","o":["Who will act as intermediary","Getting hidden weapons given up","Where the talks will be held","How to rebuild the prisons"],"a":1,"why":"The hardest question is disarmament とあり、どちらも相手が先に手放すと信じていないとある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0128","page":"freq","group":"1801〜1900","kind":"美術商の裁判","text":"The trial of the art dealer Lucien Marot ended yesterday in a verdict that surprised nobody. Marot, who liked to tout his gallery as the most trusted in the city, was found guilty of selling at least thirty paintings that he knew to be a forgery. Prosecutors did not allege that he painted them himself. His crime was to condone the fraud and profit from it. For years he displayed the pictures among his genuine wares, using a clever gimmick: each came with a letter from a \"family\" that had supposedly owned it for generations. The defense tried to deflect blame onto a restorer, and put an innocent slant on every document. The judges did not concur. They called Marot's evidence an unqualified failure and ordered restitution to the buyers. That may prove difficult. The gallery is close to insolvency after years of stagnation in the art market. Marot's lawyers describe the sentence as an unjust imposition on a sick man. Few are sympathetic. One buyer remarked that an invitation to Marot's private dinners, once considered a perk of being rich, now looks like the most expensive meal he ever ate.","ja":"美術商ルシアン・マロの裁判は昨日、誰も驚かない評決で終わった。自分の画廊を市で最も信頼できると盛んに宣伝していたマロは、偽造品だと知っていた絵を少なくとも30点売ったとして有罪となった。検察は、彼が自分でそれらを描いたとは主張しなかった。彼の罪は、詐欺を容認し、そこから利益を得たことだった。何年もの間、彼はそれらの絵を本物の商品の中に並べ、巧妙な仕掛けを使った。どの絵にも、何世代もそれを所有してきたとされる「一族」からの手紙が付いていたのだ。弁護側は責任を修復家にそらそうとし、あらゆる書類に無実を思わせる偏った解釈を加えた。裁判官たちは同意しなかった。彼らはマロの証言を文句なしの失敗と呼び、買い手への賠償を命じた。それは難しいかもしれない。美術市場の長年の停滞の後、画廊は破産寸前だからだ。マロの弁護士たちは、この判決を病人に対する不当な押しつけだと述べる。同情する人はほとんどいない。ある買い手は、かつては金持ちであることの特典と考えられていたマロの私的な夕食会への招待が、今では自分が食べた中で最も高くついた食事に見えると語った。","words":[["tout","freq:tout"],["forgery","freq:forgery"],["allege","freq:allege"],["condone","freq:condone"],["wares","freq:wares"],["gimmick","freq:gimmick"],["deflect","freq:deflect"],["slant","freq:slant"],["concur","freq:concur"],["unqualified","freq:unqualified"],["restitution","freq:restitution"],["insolvency","freq:insolvency"],["stagnation","freq:stagnation"],["imposition","freq:imposition"],["perk","freq:perk"]],"qs":[{"q":"What exactly was Marot found guilty of?","o":["Painting fake pictures himself","Selling paintings he knew were fakes","Stealing from a restorer","Writing false reviews"],"a":1,"why":"selling at least thirty paintings that he knew to be a forgery とある。"},{"q":"Why may the buyers not get their money back?","o":["The paintings have been destroyed.","The judges refused to order payment.","The gallery is nearly bankrupt.","Marot has left the country."],"a":2,"why":"The gallery is close to insolvency（破産寸前）であるため難しいかもしれないとある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0129","page":"freq","group":"1801〜1900","kind":"医学の俗説","text":"Much of what people believe about health rests on anecdotal evidence. A friend's illness went into remission after she gave up coffee; a neighbor swears by a week of total abstinence from food. Dr. Petra Nowak, a physician who also holds a degree in zoology, has written a book to counteract such claims. She begins with spas. For centuries, patients have bathed in springs rich in sulphur, believing that the water cures everything from infertility to gout. Careful studies, she shows, find no benefit beyond relaxation. She is equally hard on modern fashions. The idea that a month of sobriety \"resets\" the liver has, she says, no scientific equivalence to real treatment. Nowak explains how false beliefs spread. The dispersion of a rumor online is far faster than its correction, and the inertia of habit does the rest. She compares the process to an infestation: once established, an idea is hard to remove. The final chapter turns to serious matters. She tries to unravel the confused public debate on euthanasia, and she warns that feelings on the subject are as flammable as dry grass. Her advice throughout is simple. Ask for the evidence, and ask who paid for it.","ja":"人々が健康について信じていることの多くは、個別の体験談に基づいている。友人がコーヒーをやめたら病気の症状が落ち着いた。隣人は1週間の完全な絶食が効くと言い張る。動物学の学位も持つ医師のペトラ・ノバク博士は、こうした主張に対抗するために本を書いた。彼女は温泉から始める。何世紀もの間、患者たちは硫黄に富む泉につかり、その水が不妊から痛風まで何でも治すと信じてきた。注意深い研究は、くつろぎ以上の効果を見つけていないと彼女は示す。彼女は現代の流行にも同じくらい厳しい。1か月の禁酒が肝臓を「初期化する」という考えは、本当の治療と科学的に同等とは言えないと彼女は言う。ノバクは、誤った信念がどう広まるかを説明する。ネットでのうわさの拡散はその訂正よりはるかに速く、あとは習慣の惰性が片づけてしまう。彼女はこの過程を害虫のまん延にたとえる。いったん根づくと、考えは取り除きにくいのだ。最終章は深刻な問題に向かう。彼女は安楽死についての混乱した世間の議論を解きほぐそうとし、この主題についての感情は乾いた草のように燃えやすいと警告する。彼女の助言は一貫して単純だ。証拠を求め、その費用を誰が出したのかを尋ねよ、というものだ。","words":[["anecdotal","freq:anecdotal"],["remission","freq:remission"],["abstinence","freq:abstinence"],["zoology","freq:zoology"],["counteract","freq:counteract"],["sulphur","freq:sulphur"],["infertility","freq:infertility"],["sobriety","freq:sobriety"],["equivalence","freq:equivalence"],["dispersion","freq:dispersion"],["inertia","freq:inertia"],["infestation","freq:infestation"],["unravel","freq:unravel"],["euthanasia","freq:euthanasia"],["flammable","freq:flammable"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does Nowak say studies show about bathing in sulphur springs?","o":["It cures infertility.","It has no benefit except relaxation.","It damages the liver.","It works only in winter."],"a":1,"why":"find no benefit beyond relaxation（くつろぎ以上の効果を見つけていない）とある。"},{"q":"Why are false beliefs hard to correct, according to the book?","o":["Doctors refuse to discuss them.","People cannot read scientific papers.","Books about health are too expensive.","Rumors spread much faster than corrections, and habit keeps them alive."],"a":3,"why":"The dispersion of a rumor online is far faster than its correction, and the inertia of habit does the rest とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0130","page":"freq","group":"1801〜1900","kind":"古い写本の展示","text":"The exhibition now open at the National Library brings together sixty medieval manuscripts, some recently unearthed in a monastery cellar. The most beautiful is a book of hours with an ornate cover of silver and enamel. It was made for a queen and consecrated by a bishop whose name appears on the first page. To modern eyes, much of the content is esoteric. The texts follow the scholastic method, in which every question is divided into smaller ones, and an untrained reader may find them incomprehensible. The curators have responded with finesse. Each case explains one facet of medieval thought in plain language, and short films show how the books were made. One display goes off on a tangent about the price of blue ink; it is the most popular in the show. The exhibition avoids the temptation to perpetuate old myths. These writers did not believe the earth was flat, and their debates about free will still resonate. A few passages seem almost prophetic. The books embody an epoch that took ideas seriously, and this is unquestionably the best chance in a generation to see them.","ja":"国立図書館で現在開かれている展覧会は、60点の中世の写本を集めている。その一部は最近、修道院の地下室で発掘されたものだ。最も美しいのは、銀と七宝の凝った装飾の表紙を持つ時祷書である。それはある王妃のために作られ、最初のページに名前が記された司教によって聖別された。現代の目には、内容の多くは難解だ。本文は、あらゆる問いをより小さな問いに分けていくスコラ学の方法に従っており、訓練を受けていない読者には理解できないかもしれない。学芸員たちは巧みに対応した。展示ケースの一つひとつが中世の思想の一側面を平易な言葉で説明し、短い映像が本の作られ方を見せる。ある展示は青いインクの値段についてわき道にそれているが、それがこの展覧会で最も人気がある。展覧会は、古い俗説を永続させるという誘惑を避けている。これらの書き手たちは地球が平らだとは信じておらず、自由意志についての彼らの議論は今も心に響く。いくつかの文章はほとんど予言的に思える。これらの本は、考えを真剣に受け止めた一つの時代を体現しており、今回は疑いなく、それらを見る一世代に一度の最良の機会だ。","words":[["unearthed","freq:unearthed"],["ornate","freq:ornate"],["consecrated","freq:consecrated"],["esoteric","freq:esoteric"],["scholastic","freq:scholastic"],["incomprehensible","freq:incomprehensible"],["finesse","freq:finesse"],["facet","freq:facet"],["tangent","freq:tangent"],["perpetuate","freq:perpetuate"],["resonate","freq:resonate"],["prophetic","freq:prophetic"],["embody","freq:embody"],["epoch","freq:epoch"],["unquestionably","freq:unquestionably"]],"qs":[{"q":"How have the curators helped visitors understand the difficult texts?","o":["By translating every manuscript","By removing the hardest books","By explaining ideas simply and showing short films","By hiring medieval scholars as guides"],"a":2,"why":"Each case explains one facet of medieval thought in plain language, and short films show how the books were made とある。"},{"q":"What old myth does the exhibition refuse to repeat?","o":["That medieval writers thought the earth was flat","That the queen wrote the book herself","That blue ink was cheap","That monks could not read"],"a":0,"why":"These writers did not believe the earth was flat とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0131","page":"freq","group":"1801〜1900","kind":"指揮者の評伝","text":"The conductor Anton Ravel was one of the great musicians of his age and, by most accounts, one of its most difficult men. A new biography does not hide this. Ravel had a perverse need to quarrel. He treated orchestras with a cavalier contempt, and his rehearsals were described by one violinist as sadistic. His notoriety grew with every tour. He traveled with an entourage of assistants whom he dismissed regularly, and a single slur from him could end a young player's career. Yet the author finds another man behind the legend. Ravel's courtship of his wife, conducted through four hundred letters, shows tenderness and wit. He valued her companionship above everything, and after her death he lost his zest for work. His one indulgence was chess, which he played badly. In old age he showed something like repentance. He gave impromptu lessons to students without payment and apologized, in writing, to several musicians he had wronged. He never became complacent about his art. On the night before he died, he was studying a score with perfect composure. The idea that such a man was simply a monster, the author concludes, is laughable.","ja":"指揮者アントン・ラベルは、その時代の偉大な音楽家の一人であり、多くの証言によれば、最も気難しい男の一人でもあった。新しい伝記はそれを隠さない。ラベルには、けんかをせずにいられないひねくれた欲求があった。彼はオーケストラをぞんざいな軽蔑をもって扱い、ある バイオリン奏者は彼のリハーサルを加虐的だと表現した。彼の悪評は巡業のたびに高まった。彼は助手たちの取り巻きを連れて旅をし、定期的に首にした。彼のたった一言の中傷が、若い奏者の経歴を終わらせることもあった。それでも著者は、伝説の背後に別の男を見いだす。400通の手紙を通じて行われた妻への求愛は、優しさと機知を示している。彼は何よりも彼女がそばにいてくれることを大切にし、彼女の死後は仕事への熱意を失った。彼の唯一の道楽はチェスで、腕は下手だった。老年には、悔い改めに近いものを見せた。学生たちに無報酬で即興の指導をし、自分が不当に扱った何人かの音楽家に書面で謝罪した。彼は自分の芸術について決して慢心しなかった。亡くなる前の晩、彼はまったく落ち着いて楽譜を研究していた。そのような男が単なる怪物だったという考えは、ばかばかしいと著者は結論づけている。","words":[["perverse","freq:perverse"],["cavalier","freq:cavalier"],["sadistic","freq:sadistic"],["notoriety","freq:notoriety"],["entourage","freq:entourage"],["slur","freq:slur"],["courtship","freq:courtship"],["companionship","freq:companionship"],["zest","freq:zest"],["indulgence","freq:indulgence"],["repentance","freq:repentance"],["impromptu","freq:impromptu"],["complacent","freq:complacent"],["composure","freq:composure"],["laughable","freq:laughable"]],"qs":[{"q":"What do Ravel's letters to his wife reveal?","o":["A tender and witty side of him","His plans to retire","His hatred of orchestras","His skill at chess"],"a":0,"why":"shows tenderness and wit（優しさと機知を示している）とある。"},{"q":"How did he change in old age?","o":["He stopped studying music.","He became richer.","He quarreled more often.","He taught for free and apologized to people he had treated badly."],"a":3,"why":"gave impromptu lessons to students without payment and apologized, in writing, to several musicians he had wronged とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0132","page":"freq","group":"1801〜1900","kind":"汚染地帯を歩く","text":"The road to the old mining town of Verkhova is an arduous one. For the last forty kilometers it crosses a wasteland of gray earth, where a plume of yellow dust rises behind every vehicle. The mine closed in 1994, and the town has been desolate ever since. We reached it at noon. The smell from the waste ponds was repulsive, and I felt my companion recoil as we passed the first one. The pollution here is the scourge of the whole district. Chemicals have spread into the contiguous farmland, and the damage to the river is grievous. A few families remain. They live in makeshift houses built from old doors and sheets of metal, and they grow vegetables in soil they know to be poisoned. The sense of abandonment is palpable. One man showed us a fence entangled with wire where the school had stood. Fire, he said, had seemed to devour the building in an hour. The government has promised a cleanup for twenty years. Meanwhile the only road out is congested each morning with trucks carrying away what metal remains, and crossing the ponds on foot is a perilous business.","ja":"古い鉱山の町ベルホバへの道は、骨の折れるものだ。最後の40キロは灰色の土の荒れ地を横切り、そこではどの車の後ろにも黄色い砂ぼこりの柱が立ち上る。鉱山は1994年に閉鎖され、町はそれ以来荒れ果てたままだ。私たちは正午に着いた。廃棄物の池からの臭いはひどく不快で、最初の池のそばを通ったとき、連れが後ずさりするのを感じた。ここの汚染は地区全体の災いのもとだ。化学物質は隣接する農地に広がり、川への被害は重大である。数家族が残っている。彼らは古いドアや金属の板で建てた間に合わせの家に住み、毒されていると知っている土で野菜を育てている。見捨てられたという感覚ははっきり感じられる。ある男性は、学校が建っていた場所の、針金の絡まった柵を見せてくれた。火は1時間で建物をのみ込んだように見えたと彼は言った。政府は20年間、浄化を約束してきた。その間、外へ出るただ一本の道は、残った金属を運び去るトラックで毎朝混雑しており、池を徒歩で渡るのは危険なことだ。","words":[["arduous","freq:arduous"],["wasteland","freq:wasteland"],["plume","freq:plume"],["desolate","freq:desolate"],["repulsive","freq:repulsive"],["recoil","freq:recoil"],["scourge","freq:scourge"],["contiguous","freq:contiguous"],["grievous","freq:grievous"],["makeshift","freq:makeshift"],["palpable","freq:palpable"],["entangled","freq:entangled"],["devour","freq:devour"],["congested","freq:congested"],["perilous","freq:perilous"]],"qs":[{"q":"What has happened to the land around the town?","o":["It has been turned into a park.","Chemicals have spread into nearby farmland and harmed the river.","It has been sold to farmers.","It is covered with new houses."],"a":1,"why":"Chemicals have spread into the contiguous farmland, and the damage to the river is grievous とある。"},{"q":"How do the remaining families live?","o":["In improvised houses, growing food in poisoned soil","In houses provided by the government","In the old school building","In a camp outside the district"],"a":0,"why":"makeshift houses ... and they grow vegetables in soil they know to be poisoned とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0133","page":"freq","group":"1801〜1900","kind":"朝食と習慣","text":"A plump grapefruit, cut in half and sprinkled with sugar, was once the standard start to the day in many households. Today it has almost disappeared from the breakfast table. Food historians like to study such changes, because they show how taste and habit intersect. The gist of their argument is simple. People do not choose food freely. They eat what advertising, price, and fashion place in front of them. In the 1970s, fruit growers managed to devise a campaign that made grapefruit seem modern. Twenty years later, the same fruit looked tacky, a relic of failed diets. There is some divergence among experts about what comes next. Some envision a return to simple fruit as concern about sugar continues to deepen. Others think the idea of a fixed breakfast is becoming nonexistent, replaced by snacks eaten on the way to work. Whatever happens, the historians say, the lesson of the grapefruit is clear. A food can go from luxury to habit to joke within a single lifetime, and its taste has very little to do with it. The same story could be told of a dozen other foods.","ja":"半分に切って砂糖を振りかけた、丸々としたグレープフルーツは、かつて多くの家庭で一日の標準的な始まりだった。今日では朝食の食卓からほとんど姿を消した。食の歴史家はこうした変化を研究するのを好む。味の好みと習慣がどう交わるかを示すからだ。彼らの主張の要点は単純だ。人は食べ物を自由に選んでいるのではない。広告と価格と流行が目の前に置くものを食べるのだ。1970年代、果物の生産者たちは、グレープフルーツを現代的に見せる宣伝活動を考案することができた。20年後、同じ果物は安っぽく見え、失敗したダイエットの遺物になっていた。次に何が来るかについて、専門家の間にはいくらか見解の相違がある。砂糖への懸念が深まり続ける中、簡素な果物への回帰を思い描く人もいる。決まった朝食という考え自体が、通勤途中に食べる軽食に取って代わられ、存在しなくなりつつあると考える人もいる。何が起ころうと、グレープフルーツの教訓は明らかだと歴史家たちは言う。ある食べ物は、ぜいたく品から習慣へ、そして笑いの種へと、一人の人間の一生のうちに変わりうる。そして、その味はそれとほとんど関係がない。同じ話は、ほかの十数種類の食べ物についても語ることができる。","words":[["plump","freq:plump"],["grapefruit","freq:grapefruit"],["intersect","freq:intersect"],["gist","freq:gist"],["devise","freq:devise"],["tacky","freq:tacky"],["divergence","freq:divergence"],["envision","freq:envision"],["deepen","freq:deepen"],["nonexistent","freq:nonexistent"]],"qs":[{"q":"According to food historians, what mainly decides what people eat?","o":["Their free choice","Advertising, price, and fashion","Advice from doctors","The season of the year"],"a":1,"why":"They eat what advertising, price, and fashion place in front of them とある。"},{"q":"What is the \"lesson of the grapefruit\"?","o":["Sugar should be avoided.","Breakfast is the most important meal.","Fruit growers cannot influence taste.","A food's status can change greatly in one lifetime, regardless of its taste."],"a":3,"why":"A food can go from luxury to habit to joke within a single lifetime, and its taste has very little to do with it とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0134","page":"freq","group":"1901〜2000","kind":"国境紛争の背景","text":"The border war between Ardesh and Kovar has entered its third year, and neither side seems able to end it. The conflict has become a struggle of attrition. Each month a few villages change hands, at great cost, and the lines hardly move. Its origins lie in an old grievance. Ardesh has never accepted the loss of the river province in 1947, and its leaders speak of redress as a national duty. What began as a political claim has turned into something closer to a vendetta. Soldiers wear the insignia of units destroyed in earlier wars, and the dead are honored in the language of martyrdom. Rumors are rife on both sides, and they spread unchecked. When Kovar decided to sever diplomatic relations last spring, there was uproar in its own parliament, where a hitherto loyal party accused the government of closing the last door to peace. Outside powers watch with concern. The geopolitical stakes are high, since the province controls the only pass through the mountains. Analysts believe that the fighting will intensify before it fades. The cost is already unsustainable for both economies. Public anger, they suggest, has not yet reached its zenith.","ja":"アルデシュとコバルの国境戦争は3年目に入り、どちらの側も終わらせることができないように見える。紛争は消耗戦になった。毎月、いくつかの村が大きな犠牲を払って奪い合われ、戦線はほとんど動かない。その起源は古い不満にある。アルデシュは1947年に川沿いの州を失ったことを決して受け入れておらず、指導者たちはその是正を国民の義務として語る。政治的な要求として始まったものは、長年の復讐の抗争に近いものへと変わった。兵士たちは以前の戦争で壊滅した部隊の記章を着け、死者は殉教の言葉でたたえられる。双方でうわさが広まり、抑えられることなく拡散している。昨年の春、コバルが外交関係を断つことを決めたとき、自国の議会で大騒ぎが起きた。それまで政府に忠実だったある党が、政府は和平への最後の扉を閉ざしたと非難したのだ。外部の国々は懸念を持って見守っている。その州は山脈を抜ける唯一の峠を押さえているので、地政学上の利害は大きい。専門家たちは、戦闘は収まる前にさらに激しくなると考えている。その負担は、すでに双方の経済にとって持続不可能だ。世間の怒りはまだ頂点に達していないと、彼らは示唆している。","words":[["attrition","freq:attrition"],["grievance","freq:grievance"],["redress","freq:redress"],["vendetta","freq:vendetta"],["insignia","freq:insignia"],["martyrdom","freq:martyrdom"],["rife","freq:rife"],["unchecked","freq:unchecked"],["sever","freq:sever"],["uproar","freq:uproar"],["hitherto","freq:hitherto"],["geopolitical","freq:geopolitical"],["intensify","freq:intensify"],["unsustainable","freq:unsustainable"],["zenith","freq:zenith"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is the origin of the conflict?","o":["A dispute over trade","The loss of a province that one country never accepted","A quarrel between two parties","An attack on a mountain pass"],"a":1,"why":"Ardesh has never accepted the loss of the river province in 1947 とある。"},{"q":"Why are outside powers concerned?","o":["The province controls the only route through the mountains.","Both countries have asked them for money.","The war has reached their borders.","Their own soldiers are fighting."],"a":0,"why":"the province controls the only pass through the mountains（唯一の峠を押さえている）ためである。"}]},
{"id":"f-0135","page":"freq","group":"1901〜2000","kind":"買収後の会社","text":"Eighteen months after the buyout of Pellam Stores by the tycoon Rex Havering, the mood inside the company is grim. Havering promised to diversify the business and to rectify what he called decades of lazy management. Staff say the reality has been different. To meet strict budgetary targets, he has cut four hundred jobs. Those who left received severance pay well below the industry standard, and promotion no longer depends on seniority. Managers describe a climate of burnout. One told reporters that every decision now requires his personal approval, which he treats as a formality only when it suits him. Havering's defenders point out that retail is a cyclical business and that sales were falling before he arrived. They add a caveat: the full figures have not been published. Critics reply that he has used a holding company to circumvent rules on reporting. A former collaborator has accused him of trying to procure confidential data from rivals, a charge he denies. Meanwhile, an unusual solicitation has reached shareholders. A group of former employees is asking them to vote against his re-election as chairman. Few expect it to succeed, but the meeting will not be a quiet one.","ja":"実業界の大物レックス・ヘイバリングによるペラム・ストアーズの買収から18か月、社内の雰囲気は暗い。ヘイバリングは事業を多角化し、彼の言う数十年の怠慢な経営を正すと約束した。従業員は、現実は違ったと言う。厳しい予算上の目標を達成するため、彼は400人の職を削った。去った人々は業界の標準をかなり下回る退職手当しか受け取らず、昇進はもう年功に基づかなくなった。管理職たちは燃え尽きの空気を語る。ある管理職は記者たちに、今ではあらゆる決定に彼個人の承認が必要で、彼は都合のよいときだけそれを形式的な手続きとして扱うと語った。ヘイバリングを擁護する人々は、小売業は景気の波に左右される商売であり、売上は彼が来る前から落ちていたと指摘する。彼らはただし書きを付け加える。完全な数字は公表されていないのだ。批判する人々は、彼が持ち株会社を使って報告の規則をすり抜けたと反論する。かつての協力者は、彼が競合他社から機密データを入手しようとしたと告発したが、彼は否定している。その間に、珍しい勧誘が株主に届いた。元従業員の集まりが、彼の会長再任に反対票を投じるよう求めているのだ。成功すると予想する人はほとんどいないが、株主総会は静かなものにはならないだろう。","words":[["buyout","freq:buyout"],["tycoon","freq:tycoon"],["diversify","freq:diversify"],["rectify","freq:rectify"],["budgetary","freq:budgetary"],["severance","freq:severance"],["seniority","freq:seniority"],["burnout","freq:burnout"],["formality","freq:formality"],["cyclical","freq:cyclical"],["caveat","freq:caveat"],["circumvent","freq:circumvent"],["collaborator","freq:collaborator"],["procure","freq:procure"],["solicitation","freq:solicitation"]],"qs":[{"q":"What has changed for staff since the takeover?","o":["Jobs have been cut and promotion is no longer based on length of service.","Salaries have doubled.","Every manager has been replaced.","The stores have been renamed."],"a":0,"why":"he has cut four hundred jobs とあり、promotion no longer depends on seniority とある。"},{"q":"What are former employees asking shareholders to do?","o":["Sell their shares","Publish the full figures","Vote against Havering's re-election as chairman","Close the company"],"a":2,"why":"asking them to vote against his re-election as chairman とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0136","page":"freq","group":"1901〜2000","kind":"感染症の追跡","text":"When three farm workers in the Ostra valley fell ill within a week, local doctors suspected food poisoning. Laboratory tests pointed instead to a pathogen never before seen in the region. The first fatality came five days later. Public health teams moved quickly. They established that the disease has an incubation period of about ten days, and that it enters the body through small cuts, usually on the hands. One surgeon noticed that infection began at the proximal end of a wound and spread along the arm. In severe cases the tissue cannot regenerate, and an incision must be made to remove it. The investigators were joined by an unexpected expert: an anthropologist who had studied farming customs in the valley. She explained that workers cleaned their tools with river water, which helped the bacteria's adhesion to the metal. Simple changes followed. Gloves were distributed, and tools were boiled. The outbreak was preventable, the final report concludes, and its control required only rudimentary equipment. It warns, however, that the health service has been running on autopilot. Vigilance against emergent diseases had been allowed to weaken, and the singularity of this case should not be taken as comfort. Nobody suffered permanent mutilation, but that was luck.","ja":"オストラ谷で農場労働者3人が1週間のうちに発病したとき、地元の医師たちは食中毒を疑った。ところが検査は、この地域でこれまで見られたことのない病原体を指し示した。最初の死者は5日後に出た。公衆衛生のチームは素早く動いた。彼らは、この病気の潜伏期間が約10日であること、そして通常は手にある小さな切り傷から体に入ることを突き止めた。ある外科医は、感染が傷の体の中心に近い側の端から始まり、腕に沿って広がることに気づいた。重い場合には組織は再生できず、切開して取り除かなければならない。調査には予想外の専門家が加わった。この谷の農業の慣習を研究していた人類学者だ。彼女は、労働者たちが道具を川の水で洗っており、それが細菌の金属への付着を助けていたと説明した。簡単な変更が続いた。手袋が配られ、道具は煮沸された。この流行は予防できたもので、その抑え込みに必要だったのは初歩的な装備だけだったと最終報告書は結論づけている。しかし報告書は、保健サービスが惰性で動いてきたと警告する。新たに現れる病気への警戒は弱まるに任されており、今回の事例の特異さを安心材料と受け取ってはならないという。誰も体の一部を永久に失うことはなかったが、それは運がよかっただけだ。","words":[["pathogen","freq:pathogen"],["fatality","freq:fatality"],["incubation","freq:incubation"],["proximal","freq:proximal"],["regenerate","freq:regenerate"],["incision","freq:incision"],["anthropologist","freq:anthropologist"],["adhesion","freq:adhesion"],["preventable","freq:preventable"],["rudimentary","freq:rudimentary"],["autopilot","freq:autopilot"],["Vigilance","freq:vigilance"],["emergent","freq:emergent"],["singularity","freq:singularity"],["mutilation","freq:mutilation"]],"qs":[{"q":"How does the disease enter the body?","o":["Through contaminated food","Through small cuts, usually on the hands","Through the air","Through insect bites"],"a":1,"why":"it enters the body through small cuts, usually on the hands とある。"},{"q":"What did the anthropologist contribute?","o":["She identified the bacteria in the laboratory.","She treated the first patients.","She wrote the final report.","She explained a tool-cleaning custom that helped the bacteria spread."],"a":3,"why":"workers cleaned their tools with river water, which helped the bacteria's adhesion to the metal とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0137","page":"freq","group":"1901〜2000","kind":"脚本家の講演","text":"At a festival in Edinburgh last week, the screenwriter Mira Castell gave a talk that was both funny and wise. She began with a fable. A king, she said, once asked for a story that had never been told. Every writer in the land failed, because every plot they offered was already a cliche. Castell's point was that originality is a utopian goal. All stories borrow. The interplay between old patterns and new voices is what gives a film its life. She was scathing about pretentious films that confuse slowness with depth. The allure of cinema, she argued, lies in simple things: a face, a secret, a journey. Her own best-known script, about a doctor who believes in reincarnation, began as a joke about a magic elixir. She described the moment of epiphany when she saw that the joke was really a story about grief. Castell also spoke about authorship. A script, she said, has a complicated provenance; fifty people change it before it reaches the screen, and the writer's name in the credits is almost an abbreviation for all of them. She dislikes the exalted language used about writers. The audience found her enchanting, and the questions went on for an hour.","ja":"先週エディンバラの映画祭で、脚本家ミラ・カステルは、面白くて賢明な講演をした。彼女は寓話から始めた。昔、ある王が、まだ誰も語ったことのない物語を求めた。国中の作家がことごとく失敗した。彼らが差し出した筋はどれも、すでに使い古されたものだったからだ。カステルの言いたいことは、独創性とは理想郷のような目標だということだった。すべての物語は借りている。古い型と新しい声の相互作用こそが、映画に命を与えるのだ。遅さを深さと取り違える気取った映画に、彼女は痛烈だった。映画の魅力は単純なものにあると彼女は論じた。顔、秘密、旅である。彼女自身の最もよく知られた脚本は、生まれ変わりを信じる医師についてのもので、魔法の霊薬についての冗談として始まった。その冗談が本当は悲しみについての物語だと気づいた、ひらめきの瞬間を彼女は語った。カステルは作者であることについても話した。脚本には複雑な来歴がある。画面に届くまでに50人がそれを変えるのであり、クレジットに載る脚本家の名前は、ほとんどその全員の略称のようなものだと彼女は言った。彼女は作家について使われる高尚な言葉を嫌う。聴衆は彼女に魅了され、質疑は1時間続いた。","words":[["screenwriter","freq:screenwriter"],["fable","freq:fable"],["cliche","freq:cliche"],["utopian","freq:utopian"],["interplay","freq:interplay"],["pretentious","freq:pretentious"],["allure","freq:allure"],["reincarnation","freq:reincarnation"],["elixir","freq:elixir"],["epiphany","freq:epiphany"],["authorship","freq:authorship"],["provenance","freq:provenance"],["abbreviation","freq:abbreviation"],["exalted","freq:exalted"],["enchanting","freq:enchanting"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was the point of Castell's fable?","o":["Kings make poor critics.","Complete originality is impossible.","Writers should avoid old stories.","Films are better than books."],"a":1,"why":"originality is a utopian goal. All stories borrow とある。"},{"q":"What does she say about the writer's name in the credits?","o":["It stands for the many people who shaped the script.","It should appear first.","It is usually misspelled.","It guarantees a film's quality."],"a":0,"why":"the writer's name in the credits is almost an abbreviation for all of them（全員の略称のようなもの）とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0138","page":"freq","group":"1901〜2000","kind":"探検家の人物像","text":"Captain Elsa Thorne led seven expeditions to the Antarctic, and those who served under her never forgot the experience. She was unorthodox in every way. She chose her crews for character rather than strength, and she had a foresight about weather that seemed close to magic. Her manner was curt. She disliked small talk, refused to socialize in port, and was known to be cranky before breakfast. Yet she built a rapport with her men that outlasted the voyages. She had no patience for any clique on board, and she broke up groups that kept to themselves. Diaries from the expeditions show the other side of her. In the worst months, when one sailor grew distraught and another sank into a silent alienation, she sat with them for hours. She was stoic about her own injuries, and her fortitude became a legend. She also had a mischievous streak. One entry describes her watching a young officer strut about the deck in a new coat, and then quietly tying his bootlaces together. Her only weakness was a propensity to take risks alone. After one such episode, there was an angry outburst from her first mate. She listened, nodded, and changed nothing.","ja":"エルサ・ソーン隊長は南極への探検を7回率い、彼女のもとで働いた人々はその経験を決して忘れなかった。彼女はあらゆる点で型破りだった。乗組員を体力ではなく人柄で選び、天候について魔法に近いと思えるほどの先見の明を持っていた。彼女の態度はそっけなかった。世間話を嫌い、港で人と付き合うことを拒み、朝食前は不機嫌なことで知られていた。それでも彼女は、航海が終わった後も続く信頼関係を部下との間に築いた。船内の派閥には我慢がならず、内輪で固まる集団を解散させた。探検の日記は彼女の別の面を示している。最悪の数か月、一人の船員が取り乱し、別の一人が黙り込んで孤立していったとき、彼女は何時間も彼らのそばに座っていた。自分のけがには平然としており、その不屈の精神は伝説になった。いたずら好きな一面もあった。ある日記の記述は、若い士官が新しいコートを着て甲板を気取って歩くのを彼女が眺め、それからそっと彼の靴ひもを結び合わせた様子を描いている。彼女の唯一の弱点は、一人で危険を冒す傾向だった。そうした出来事の後、一等航海士が怒りを爆発させたことがある。彼女は耳を傾け、うなずき、何も変えなかった。","words":[["unorthodox","freq:unorthodox"],["foresight","freq:foresight"],["curt","freq:curt"],["socialize","freq:socialize"],["cranky","freq:cranky"],["rapport","freq:rapport"],["clique","freq:clique"],["distraught","freq:distraught"],["alienation","freq:alienation"],["stoic","freq:stoic"],["fortitude","freq:fortitude"],["mischievous","freq:mischievous"],["strut","freq:strut"],["propensity","freq:propensity"],["outburst","freq:outburst"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did Thorne choose her crews?","o":["By physical strength","By family connections","By character","By age"],"a":2,"why":"She chose her crews for character rather than strength とある。"},{"q":"What do the diaries show about her?","o":["She spent long hours comforting men who were suffering.","She avoided the crew in bad months.","She kept no records herself.","She often lost her temper."],"a":0,"why":"when one sailor grew distraught ..., she sat with them for hours とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0139","page":"freq","group":"1901〜2000","kind":"海辺の町の秋","text":"In October the tourists leave Port Selwyn, and the town returns to itself. I walked along the promenade on my first morning there. A fine drizzle was falling, and mist lay like a shroud over the harbor. Dead leaves would swirl around the empty bandstand and settle again. Beyond the last houses, a path climbs a steep incline to the cliffs. It is not uncharted country, but it feels so. The only building is a forsaken chapel, its roof gone, and the only sound was the grunt of a seal on the rocks below. At the top, the land falls away into a ravine filled with ferns. I sat there for an hour in contemplation, and felt something close to rapture when the sun broke through. The town itself is plain. Its hotels are closed in autumn, and the weather can be atrocious. But there is a harmonious quality to its gray streets, and the people have the easy manner of those who expect no visitors. In the bar that evening, a nameless fisherman bought me a drink and asked nothing in return. We were, he said, kindred spirits: two people with nowhere else to be.","ja":"10月になると観光客はポート・セルウィンを去り、町は本来の姿に戻る。私はそこでの最初の朝、遊歩道を歩いた。細かい霧雨が降り、霧が覆いのように港にかかっていた。枯れ葉が誰もいない野外音楽堂の周りで渦を巻き、また落ち着いた。最後の家々の先で、小道が急な坂を登って崖へ向かう。そこは未知の土地ではないが、そう感じられる。唯一の建物は屋根のなくなった見捨てられた礼拝堂で、唯一の音は下の岩にいるアザラシのうなり声だった。頂上では、土地がシダに埋もれた峡谷へと落ち込んでいる。私はそこに1時間座って物思いにふけり、日が差したとき、歓喜に近いものを感じた。町そのものは地味だ。秋にはホテルが閉まり、天気はひどいこともある。しかし、その灰色の通りには調和のとれた趣があり、人々は訪問者を期待しない人特有の気楽な物腰をしている。その晩、酒場で、名も知らぬ漁師が一杯おごってくれて、何の見返りも求めなかった。私たちは気の合う仲間だと彼は言った。ほかに行く所のない2人なのだと。","words":[["promenade","freq:promenade"],["drizzle","freq:drizzle"],["shroud","freq:shroud"],["swirl","freq:swirl"],["incline","freq:incline"],["uncharted","freq:uncharted"],["forsaken","freq:forsaken"],["grunt","freq:grunt"],["ravine","freq:ravine"],["contemplation","freq:contemplation"],["rapture","freq:rapture"],["atrocious","freq:atrocious"],["harmonious","freq:harmonious"],["nameless","freq:nameless"],["kindred","freq:kindred"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the writer find at the top of the cliffs?","o":["A lighthouse","A hotel","A ravine filled with ferns","A fishing boat"],"a":2,"why":"the land falls away into a ravine filled with ferns とある。"},{"q":"How does the writer describe the townspeople?","o":["Relaxed, like people who expect no visitors","Unfriendly toward strangers","Busy preparing for tourists","Eager to sell souvenirs"],"a":0,"why":"the easy manner of those who expect no visitors とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0140","page":"freq","group":"1901〜2000","kind":"手紙の偽造事件","text":"For forty years, a set of letters in the Hargrove Library was accepted as the work of a famous poet. They are, we now know, a fake. The discovery was made by a student who had been given the chore of copying them. She noticed that the handwriting, though almost indistinguishable from the poet's, never varied. Real handwriting changes with mood and haste; this did not. The forger's ruse had been simple and nifty. He used genuine paper from the right decade and copied phrases from published poems. What he could not copy was the unspoken knowledge that real friends share. The letters explain too much. The library's old catalog, a cumbersome set of forty volumes, shows that doubts were raised in 1961 and ignored. The director has accepted the finding wholeheartedly, saying that it would be intolerable to defend a mistake out of pride. The episode is undeniably embarrassing. It may also be useful. The library plans an exhibition that will place the fakes beside real letters, to help visitors sharpen their own eyes. The student, for her part, has been offered a job.","ja":"40年の間、ハーグローブ図書館にある一連の手紙は、ある有名な詩人の書いたものとして受け入れられてきた。それらは偽物だと、今ではわかっている。発見したのは、それらを書き写すという雑用を任された学生だった。彼女は、その筆跡が詩人のものとほとんど区別がつかないにもかかわらず、まったく変化しないことに気づいた。本物の筆跡は気分や急ぎ具合で変わるが、これは変わらなかった。偽造者の計略は単純で気の利いたものだった。彼は適切な年代の本物の紙を使い、出版された詩から言い回しを写した。彼が写せなかったのは、本当の友人同士が共有する暗黙の知識だった。その手紙は説明しすぎているのだ。40巻から成る扱いにくい図書館の古い目録は、1961年に疑いが出され、無視されたことを示している。館長はこの発見を全面的に受け入れ、自尊心から誤りを擁護することは耐えられないと述べた。この出来事は明らかに恥ずかしいものだ。しかし役に立つかもしれない。図書館は、来館者が自分の目を鋭くする助けになるよう、偽物を本物の手紙の横に並べる展示を計画している。その学生はといえば、職を提示された。","words":[["chore","freq:chore"],["indistinguishable","freq:indistinguishable"],["ruse","freq:ruse"],["nifty","freq:nifty"],["unspoken","freq:unspoken"],["cumbersome","freq:cumbersome"],["wholeheartedly","freq:wholeheartedly"],["intolerable","freq:intolerable"],["undeniably","freq:undeniably"],["sharpen","freq:sharpen"]],"qs":[{"q":"What made the student suspect the letters were fake?","o":["The paper was too new.","The handwriting never changed at all.","The poet's name was misspelled.","The catalog listed them twice."],"a":1,"why":"the handwriting ... never varied（筆跡がまったく変化しない）ことに気づいた。"},{"q":"What does the library plan to do?","o":["Destroy the letters","Close the catalog","Hide the discovery","Show the fakes next to real letters in an exhibition"],"a":3,"why":"an exhibition that will place the fakes beside real letters とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0141","page":"freq","group":"2001〜2100","kind":"条約批准の攻防","text":"The ratification of the Northern Treaty, which would end a decade of standoff between two rival blocs, is still in doubt. The treaty was signed in June after a small neutral state agreed to mediate. It provides for the resettlement of forty thousand displaced families and a shared authority over the river. Supporters say it will avert another war. Opponents see it as surrender. In the larger of the two countries, nationalist deputies accuse the government of treachery and of abandoning its hegemony in the region. They reiterate, at every session, that no foreign judge should rule on the border. The prime minister has remained steadfast. Inaction, she argues, is the more dangerous course, since the frontier towns bear the brunt of every crisis and half their young people are jobless. Whether she has the votes is debatable. A burgeoning protest movement in the capital has made some of her allies nervous, and the memory of the political upheaval of 2019 is fresh. The vote is set for next Thursday. If the treaty fails, diplomats say, it will not be offered again in this generation.","ja":"対立する2つの陣営の10年に及ぶにらみ合いを終わらせるはずの北方条約の批准は、まだ見通せない。条約は、ある小さな中立国が調停を引き受けた後、6月に署名された。それは住む場所を追われた4万家族の再定住と、川についての共同の管理機関を定めている。支持者たちは、それが新たな戦争を防ぐと言う。反対派はそれを降伏と見る。2国のうち大きいほうの国では、民族主義の議員たちが、政府は裏切りを働き、地域での覇権を放棄したと非難している。彼らは会期のたびに、外国の裁判官が国境について判断すべきではないと繰り返し述べる。首相は揺るがない姿勢を保っている。何もしないほうが危険な道だと彼女は主張する。国境の町々はあらゆる危機の矢面に立たされ、若者の半分が失業しているからだ。彼女に必要な票があるかどうかは議論の余地がある。首都で急速に広がる抗議運動が彼女の同盟者の一部を不安にさせており、2019年の政治的激変の記憶も新しい。採決は来週の木曜に予定されている。条約が否決されれば、この世代のうちに再び提案されることはないだろうと外交官たちは言う。","words":[["ratification","freq:ratification"],["standoff","freq:standoff"],["mediate","freq:mediate"],["resettlement","freq:resettlement"],["avert","freq:avert"],["treachery","freq:treachery"],["hegemony","freq:hegemony"],["reiterate","freq:reiterate"],["steadfast","freq:steadfast"],["Inaction","freq:inaction"],["brunt","freq:brunt"],["jobless","freq:jobless"],["debatable","freq:debatable"],["burgeoning","freq:burgeoning"],["upheaval","freq:upheaval"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why does the prime minister say doing nothing is more dangerous?","o":["The border towns suffer most in every crisis.","The neutral state will withdraw.","The river is drying up.","The treaty will expire on Thursday."],"a":0,"why":"the frontier towns bear the brunt of every crisis and half their young people are jobless とある。"},{"q":"What do nationalist deputies object to?","o":["The cost of resettlement","The location of the vote","A foreign judge deciding questions about the border","The size of the protest movement"],"a":2,"why":"no foreign judge should rule on the border と繰り返し述べている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0142","page":"freq","group":"2001〜2100","kind":"密輸事件の判決","text":"A judgment handed down yesterday may become an important case in the country's jurisprudence. It concerned a fisherman accused of carrying contraband across the bay. The evidence was circumstantial. Police had placed a cordon around the harbor after a tip, and they found forty boxes of untaxed cigarettes in a shed that the man sometimes used. He was arrested after an altercation with an officer. The trial court convicted him. On appeal, the judges took a different view. The search, they said, had not been justifiable, because the warrant named another building. They also criticized a serious blunder: the officer who acted as custodian of the boxes had left them unguarded for two days. Any linkage between the accused and the goods was therefore inconclusive. A neighbor had been willing to vouch for his character, and the court noted that his long detention in a crowded cell had been inhumane. The ruling does not mean that courts will now be lenient with smugglers. It means that rules of evidence apply to everyone. The man has become a claimant himself. He is seeking compensation, including the return of his allotment of fishing permits.","ja":"昨日言い渡されたある判決は、この国の法体系における重要な判例になるかもしれない。それは、湾を越えて密輸品を運んだとして起訴された漁師に関するものだった。証拠は状況証拠だった。警察は通報を受けて港の周りに非常線を張り、その男が時々使っていた小屋から課税されていない紙巻きたばこ40箱を見つけた。彼は警官との口論の後に逮捕された。一審の裁判所は彼を有罪とした。上訴審で、裁判官たちは異なる見方をした。令状には別の建物が記されていたので、その捜索は正当と認められなかったと彼らは述べた。重大な失態も批判した。箱の管理を任された警官が、それを2日間見張りなしで放置していたのだ。したがって、被告と品物との関連は決定的ではなかった。ある隣人は彼の人柄を保証する用意があり、裁判所は、混み合った房での長い拘留が非人道的だったと指摘した。この判決は、裁判所が今後密輸業者に寛大になるという意味ではない。証拠の規則は誰にでも適用されるという意味だ。男性は今度は自分が請求者になった。割り当てられていた漁業許可の返還を含む補償を求めている。","words":[["jurisprudence","freq:jurisprudence"],["contraband","freq:contraband"],["circumstantial","freq:circumstantial"],["cordon","freq:cordon"],["altercation","freq:altercation"],["justifiable","freq:justifiable"],["blunder","freq:blunder"],["custodian","freq:custodian"],["linkage","freq:linkage"],["inconclusive","freq:inconclusive"],["vouch","freq:vouch"],["inhumane","freq:inhumane"],["lenient","freq:lenient"],["claimant","freq:claimant"],["allotment","freq:allotment"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the appeal judges find the search unjustified?","o":["The police had no tip.","The warrant named a different building.","The fisherman was not present.","The harbor was outside the city."],"a":1,"why":"the warrant named another building（令状には別の建物が記されていた）ためである。"},{"q":"What mistake did the police make with the boxes?","o":["They sold them.","They opened them in public.","They burned them too soon.","They left them unguarded for two days."],"a":3,"why":"had left them unguarded for two days とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0143","page":"freq","group":"2001〜2100","kind":"干拓地の将来","text":"The Marren flats were created eighty years ago by one of the largest reclamation projects in Europe. Engineers drained a shallow sea at the confluence of two rivers and turned it into farmland. Today the land is in trouble. The depletion of groundwater has caused the soil to sink, and salt is returning. Scientists disagree about what to do. Some argue that the area will not remain habitable for more than fifty years and that preventative action should begin now. The region lies near a tectonic fault, and an earthquake could cause an instantaneous failure of the old sea walls. Others see an opportunity. If part of the land were flooded again, the marshes would return, and with them the avian life that vanished in the 1940s. The dispersal of seeds by birds would restore the plant cover within a decade. Farmers are not persuaded. They point to the degeneration of other flooded areas into mud, and to the extermination of crops by salt. One problem is local. Morning condensation on the sea walls has caused rust, and any amplification of the tides would test them severely. Tempers are inflamed. A sedentary population, on the land for three generations, does not move easily.","ja":"マレン干拓地は80年前、ヨーロッパ最大級の埋め立て事業の一つによって造られた。技師たちは2つの川の合流点にある浅い海を干拓し、農地に変えた。今日、その土地は困難に陥っている。地下水の枯渇が地盤の沈下を引き起こし、塩が戻りつつある。科学者たちはどうすべきかについて意見が分かれている。この一帯は50年以上は住める状態を保てず、予防的な行動を今始めるべきだと主張する人もいる。この地域は地殻の断層の近くにあり、地震が起これば古い防潮堤が一瞬で崩れるおそれがある。好機と見る人もいる。土地の一部を再び水没させれば湿地が戻り、それとともに1940年代に姿を消した鳥類も戻るだろう。鳥による種子の散布が、10年以内に植生を回復させるはずだ。農家は納得していない。彼らは、ほかの水没地が泥地へと劣化したこと、そして塩によって作物が全滅したことを挙げる。一つの問題はこの土地特有のものだ。防潮堤の朝の結露がさびを生じさせており、潮位が少しでも増幅されれば堤は厳しく試されることになる。人々の感情は高ぶっている。3世代にわたってその土地に定住してきた住民は、簡単には動かない。","words":[["reclamation","freq:reclamation"],["confluence","freq:confluence"],["depletion","freq:depletion"],["habitable","freq:habitable"],["preventative","freq:preventative"],["tectonic","freq:tectonic"],["instantaneous","freq:instantaneous"],["avian","freq:avian"],["dispersal","freq:dispersal"],["degeneration","freq:degeneration"],["extermination","freq:extermination"],["condensation","freq:condensation"],["amplification","freq:amplification"],["inflamed","freq:inflamed"],["sedentary","freq:sedentary"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is causing the land to sink?","o":["The weight of new buildings","The loss of groundwater","Frequent earthquakes","The return of birds"],"a":1,"why":"The depletion of groundwater has caused the soil to sink とある。"},{"q":"What benefit do some scientists see in flooding part of the land again?","o":["Marshes and bird life would return.","Farming would become easier.","The sea walls would be strengthened.","The fault would become stable."],"a":0,"why":"the marshes would return, and with them the avian life that vanished in the 1940s とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0144","page":"freq","group":"2001〜2100","kind":"近代詩の読み方","text":"The poems of Anna Reiss are often described as difficult, and readers who try to decipher them line by line usually give up. A new guide suggests another approach. Reiss was a leading figure of modernism, and her work must be read in a contextual way, against the music and painting of her time. The author begins with sound. Reiss wrote for the ear, and the cadence of her lines has a hypnotic quality that survives even in translation. He then asks readers to delve into her favorite subject, the duality of the self. In poem after poem, a calm speaker is followed by a ghostly second voice that contradicts her. Beneath the veneer of control lies fear. The guide is strongest on stylistic detail. It shows how an apparently effortless phrase was the product of thirty drafts, and how she turned a proverbial saying inside out. Her late sequence about a ruined house, with its stately rhythm, is unmatched in the poetry of the century. The author claims too much when he calls reading Reiss a transformative experience. But he is right that patience is rewarded, and his book is the best place to begin.","ja":"アンナ・ライスの詩はしばしば難しいと言われ、一行ずつ解読しようとする読者はたいていあきらめてしまう。新しい手引書は別の方法を提案している。ライスは近代主義の指導的な人物であり、その作品は、同時代の音楽や絵画に照らして、文脈に即して読まなければならないという。著者は音から始める。ライスは耳のために書いており、彼女の詩行の抑揚には、翻訳でも残る、うっとりさせる質がある。次に著者は、彼女の好んだ主題である自己の二重性を掘り下げるよう読者に求める。詩から詩へと、落ち着いた語り手の後に、それに反論する幽霊のような第二の声が続く。抑制といううわべの下には恐れがある。この手引書は文体上の細部について最も優れている。一見楽々と書かれたような言い回しが30回の推敲の産物だったこと、そして彼女がことわざの言い回しをどう裏返したかを示している。堂々としたリズムを持つ、荒れ果てた家についての晩年の連作は、その世紀の詩の中で比類がない。ライスを読むことを人生を変える体験と呼ぶとき、著者は言いすぎている。しかし、忍耐は報われるという点で彼は正しく、彼の本は読み始めるのに最良の場所だ。","words":[["decipher","freq:decipher"],["modernism","freq:modernism"],["contextual","freq:contextual"],["cadence","freq:cadence"],["hypnotic","freq:hypnotic"],["delve","freq:delve"],["duality","freq:duality"],["ghostly","freq:ghostly"],["veneer","freq:veneer"],["stylistic","freq:stylistic"],["effortless","freq:effortless"],["proverbial","freq:proverbial"],["stately","freq:stately"],["unmatched","freq:unmatched"],["transformative","freq:transformative"]],"qs":[{"q":"How does the guide suggest reading Reiss?","o":["By translating her poems first","By reading only her late work","In relation to the music and painting of her time","Line by line, with a dictionary"],"a":2,"why":"must be read in a contextual way, against the music and painting of her time とある。"},{"q":"What does the guide show about her apparently easy phrases?","o":["They were the result of many drafts.","They were borrowed from other poets.","They were written quickly.","They were added by editors."],"a":0,"why":"an apparently effortless phrase was the product of thirty drafts とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0145","page":"freq","group":"2001〜2100","kind":"棋士の素顔","text":"Viktor Laine, who became world chess champion at twenty-two, is one of the strangest figures the game has produced. As a child he was brilliant and impossible. A lost game would end in a tantrum, and he treated weaker opponents with open scorn. Teachers found him antisocial. He studied diligently, twelve hours a day, and seemed to need nothing else. Success did not soften him. He was touchy about criticism and rabid in his dislike of journalists, whom he considered inept. He was also deeply superstitious. He would not play without a particular pen, and one quirk, tapping the table three times before each move, drove opponents to distraction. Yet people who knew him well describe an endearing side. He was idealistic about the game, which he regarded as an art, and he gave away most of his prize money. A famous rival called him the most skillful defender in history. In middle age he withdrew to a monastery, where he was said to have taken a vow of chastity and silence as penance for his early arrogance. The cynicism of the press about this retreat was predictable. Friends say that he had simply found a place where nobody asked him to win.","ja":"22歳でチェスの世界王者になったビクトル・ライネは、この競技が生んだ最も風変わりな人物の一人だ。子どもの頃、彼は才気にあふれ、手に負えなかった。負けた対局はかんしゃくで終わり、弱い相手をあからさまな軽蔑をもって扱った。教師たちは彼を非社交的だと感じた。彼は1日12時間、熱心に研究し、ほかには何も必要としないように見えた。成功しても彼は丸くならなかった。批判には怒りっぽく、記者を嫌うことにかけては狂信的で、彼らを無能と見なしていた。彼はまた、ひどく迷信深かった。特定のペンがなければ対局せず、一手ごとに机を3回たたくという奇妙な癖は相手の気を散らした。それでも、彼をよく知る人々は愛すべき一面を語る。彼はこの競技について理想主義的で、それを芸術と見なしており、賞金の大半を人に与えた。ある有名な好敵手は、彼を史上最も巧みな守り手と呼んだ。中年になると彼は修道院に引きこもり、若い頃の傲慢さへの償いとして、純潔と沈黙の誓いを立てたと言われた。この隠遁に対する報道陣の冷笑的な態度は予想どおりだった。友人たちは、彼は誰からも勝てと言われない場所を見つけただけだと言う。","words":[["tantrum","freq:tantrum"],["scorn","freq:scorn"],["antisocial","freq:antisocial"],["diligently","freq:diligently"],["touchy","freq:touchy"],["rabid","freq:rabid"],["inept","freq:inept"],["superstitious","freq:superstitious"],["quirk","freq:quirk"],["endearing","freq:endearing"],["idealistic","freq:idealistic"],["skillful","freq:skillful"],["chastity","freq:chastity"],["penance","freq:penance"],["cynicism","freq:cynicism"]],"qs":[{"q":"What habit of Laine's disturbed his opponents?","o":["Humming during games","Arriving late","Tapping the table before each move","Refusing to shake hands"],"a":2,"why":"one quirk, tapping the table three times before each move, drove opponents to distraction とある。"},{"q":"How do friends explain his withdrawal to a monastery?","o":["He had lost his title.","He had found a place where he was not expected to win.","He wanted to write a book.","He was hiding from the police."],"a":1,"why":"he had simply found a place where nobody asked him to win とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0146","page":"freq","group":"2001〜2100","kind":"遊牧民と祭り","text":"For most of the year, the Dareh people lead a nomadic life, moving their herds between the high pastures and the plain. Their only fixed abode is a winter camp beside a tranquil lake. Once every three years, all the families gather there for a festival that celebrates kinship. I was allowed to attend last spring, an unwelcome guest at first and then, by degrees, a tolerated one. The ground was soggy after rain, and tents covered the shore. On the second day there was a commotion. Two hundred horses, frightened by thunder, broke loose, and the stampede nearly reached a group of unsuspecting children. Nobody was hurt, which the elders found inexplicable and I found miraculous. The Dareh are acutely aware that their way of life is under threat. A plan to build a road through the pastures is, to them, an abomination. Officials call their objections preposterous and speak of progress. What I found most aggravating was the assumption that people who own little must want more. One old woman put it simply. A herder who follows the road, she said, will go astray; one who follows the grass will always find the way home.","ja":"1年の大半、ダレの人々は遊牧生活を送り、高地の牧草地と平原の間で家畜の群れを移動させる。彼らの唯一の定まった住まいは、静かな湖のそばの冬の野営地だ。3年に一度、すべての家族がそこに集まり、親族のきずなを祝う祭りを開く。私は昨年の春、参加を許された。最初は歓迎されない客で、それから少しずつ、大目に見てもらえる客になった。地面は雨の後でぬかるみ、天幕が岸辺を覆っていた。2日目に騒ぎが起きた。雷におびえた200頭の馬が逃げ出し、その集団暴走は何も知らない子どもたちの一団にあと少しで達するところだった。誰もけがをしなかった。長老たちはそれを説明のつかないことだと感じ、私は奇跡的だと感じた。ダレの人々は、自分たちの生き方が脅かされていることを強く意識している。牧草地を貫く道路を建設する計画は、彼らにとって忌まわしいものだ。役人たちは彼らの異議をばかげていると呼び、進歩について語る。私が最もいら立ちを覚えたのは、持ち物の少ない人々はもっと欲しがっているに違いないという思い込みだった。ある老女はそれを簡潔に言い表した。道路に従う牧夫は道に迷うが、草に従う者はいつも家への道を見つける、と彼女は言った。","words":[["nomadic","freq:nomadic"],["abode","freq:abode"],["tranquil","freq:tranquil"],["kinship","freq:kinship"],["unwelcome","freq:unwelcome"],["soggy","freq:soggy"],["commotion","freq:commotion"],["stampede","freq:stampede"],["unsuspecting","freq:unsuspecting"],["inexplicable","freq:inexplicable"],["acutely","freq:acutely"],["abomination","freq:abomination"],["preposterous","freq:preposterous"],["aggravating","freq:aggravating"],["astray","freq:astray"]],"qs":[{"q":"What caused the commotion on the second day?","o":["A fight between families","Horses frightened by thunder ran loose","The arrival of officials","A flood from the lake"],"a":1,"why":"Two hundred horses, frightened by thunder, broke loose とある。"},{"q":"What assumption annoyed the writer most?","o":["That nomads dislike festivals","That the road would never be built","That herders cannot read","That people with few possessions must want more"],"a":3,"why":"the assumption that people who own little must want more とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0147","page":"freq","group":"2001〜2100","kind":"体操選手の復帰","text":"Two years ago, the gymnast Lena Fischer fell from the trampoline during a national final and broke her ankle. It was the third serious injury of her career, and some commentators began to speak of a jinx. Fischer does not believe in such things. Her fall, she says, was no fluke of bad luck but the result of trying to cram too much training into too few weeks. Her return has been slow. Doctors warned that scar tissue might impede her movement, and for months she could not stoop to tie her shoes without pain. What helped was a change of method. Her new coach likes to innovate. Instead of repeating full routines, she practices short sequences with close attention to situational judgment: where she is in the air, and what can still be corrected. The closeness between coach and athlete is obvious to anyone who watches them work. Next month Fischer will run the gauntlet of selection for the world championships, facing judges who have not seen her compete since the accident. She says she is not nervous. Having learned to walk again, she finds that a difficult landing no longer frightens her.","ja":"2年前、体操選手レナ・フィッシャーは全国大会の決勝中にトランポリンから落ち、足首を骨折した。それは彼女の経歴で3度目の大けがで、一部の解説者はジンクスについて語り始めた。フィッシャーはそういうものを信じない。自分の転落は不運のまぐれではなく、あまりに少ない週数にあまりに多くの練習を詰め込もうとした結果だと彼女は言う。復帰はゆっくりだった。医師たちは瘢痕組織が動きを妨げるかもしれないと警告し、何か月もの間、彼女は痛みなしにはかがんで靴ひもを結ぶこともできなかった。助けになったのは方法の変更だった。彼女の新しいコーチは新しいことを取り入れるのが好きだ。演技全体を繰り返す代わりに、彼女は短い連続技を練習し、状況に応じた判断に細心の注意を払う。空中で自分がどこにいるか、まだ何を修正できるかである。コーチと選手の親密さは、2人の練習を見る人なら誰にでも明らかだ。来月フィッシャーは、世界選手権の選考という厳しい試練に臨み、事故以来彼女の演技を見ていない審判たちと向き合う。彼女は緊張していないと言う。もう一度歩くことを覚えた今、難しい着地はもう怖くないのだという。","words":[["trampoline","freq:trampoline"],["jinx","freq:jinx"],["fluke","freq:fluke"],["cram","freq:cram"],["impede","freq:impede"],["stoop","freq:stoop"],["innovate","freq:innovate"],["situational","freq:situational"],["closeness","freq:closeness"],["gauntlet","freq:gauntlet"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does Fischer say caused her fall?","o":["A faulty trampoline","Bad luck","Trying to fit too much training into too little time","A mistake by her coach"],"a":2,"why":"the result of trying to cram too much training into too few weeks とある。"},{"q":"How has her training changed?","o":["She practices short sequences and focuses on judgment in the air.","She repeats full routines more often.","She trains without a coach.","She has given up the trampoline."],"a":0,"why":"she practices short sequences with close attention to situational judgment とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0148","page":"freq","group":"2101〜2200","kind":"分離運動と政府の誤算","text":"The decision by the government of Valmeria to ban the main separatist party was meant to be a clever gambit. It has proved a serious mistake. The ban was issued by edict, without a vote in parliament. Ministers accused the party's leaders of conspiring with foreign powers and of trying to incite violence. They hoped to neutralize a movement that had been gaining support, and to find a scapegoat for the weak economy. The plan was quick to backfire. Within days, the party's support doubled. Its leader, who had seemed close to retirement, became untouchable overnight, and a resolute crowd of fifty thousand filled the regional capital. The government's own coalition is now in a shambles. Two ministers have resigned, and one accused the prime minister of cowardice for refusing to debate the matter. Constitutional lawyers say the moment is a momentous one. For the first time, the court may force a government to relinquish a power it has claimed for itself. Some see hope in the crisis. A citizens' assembly, chosen by lot, has been proposed to discuss the region's future. Such a participatory approach has never been tried here, and after this month, few believe that the old methods can continue.","ja":"バルメリア政府が主要な分離独立派政党を禁止した決定は、巧妙な一手になるはずだった。それは重大な誤りだったとわかった。禁止は議会での採決なしに、布告によって出された。閣僚たちは、党の指導者たちが外国勢力と共謀し、暴力を扇動しようとしていると非難した。彼らは、支持を伸ばしていた運動を無力化し、低迷する経済の責任を負わせる身代わりを見つけることを期待していた。計画はすぐに裏目に出た。数日のうちに党の支持は倍になった。引退間近に見えていた党首は一夜にして手出しのできない存在になり、決意を固めた5万人の群衆が地方の中心都市を埋めた。政府自身の連立は今、大混乱に陥っている。2人の閣僚が辞任し、その一人は、この件の討論を拒んだ首相を臆病だと非難した。憲法学者たちは、今は重大な時だと言う。政府が自らのものだと主張してきた権限を手放すよう、裁判所が初めて強いるかもしれないのだ。この危機に希望を見る人もいる。この地域の将来を話し合うために、くじで選ばれる市民会議が提案されている。そのような参加型の方法はここでは試されたことがなく、今月の出来事の後では、古いやり方を続けられると信じる人はほとんどいない。","words":[["separatist","freq:separatist"],["gambit","freq:gambit"],["edict","freq:edict"],["conspiring","freq:conspiring"],["incite","freq:incite"],["neutralize","freq:neutralize"],["scapegoat","freq:scapegoat"],["backfire","freq:backfire"],["untouchable","freq:untouchable"],["resolute","freq:resolute"],["shambles","freq:shambles"],["cowardice","freq:cowardice"],["momentous","freq:momentous"],["relinquish","freq:relinquish"],["participatory","freq:participatory"]],"qs":[{"q":"What happened after the party was banned?","o":["Its support fell sharply.","Its support doubled within days.","Its leader fled abroad.","Parliament approved the ban."],"a":1,"why":"Within days, the party's support doubled とある。"},{"q":"What new idea has been proposed for the region?","o":["A military government","A second ban","A treaty with foreign powers","An assembly of citizens chosen by lot"],"a":3,"why":"A citizens' assembly, chosen by lot, has been proposed とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0149","page":"freq","group":"2101〜2200","kind":"ある被告の言い分","text":"The trial of a haulage company owner ended this week with a custodial sentence of four years. He had been charged with using his trucks to smuggle fuel across the border and so avoid a surcharge introduced last year. The prosecution called it an egregious case. Over eighteen months, they said, the company had cheated the state of millions while its honest rivals struggled. The defendant's explanations were brazen. He claimed that his drivers had acted unknowingly, and that he had been in hospital on the key dates. This alibi proved flimsy. Records showed that he had been treated for intoxication on one night only. He then argued that the customs raid on his depot was an act of trespass, and that the seizure of his records was unwarranted. The judge rejected both points. She noted that he was already a delinquent taxpayer, pursued by more than one creditor, and that he had described an inspector in court as a fiend. A disqualification from running any company for ten years was added to the sentence. Outside the court, his lawyer said that an appeal was certain. Few who had followed the trial expected it to succeed.","ja":"ある運送会社の経営者の裁判は今週、4年の拘禁刑で終わった。彼は、自社のトラックを使って国境を越えて燃料を密輸し、昨年導入された追加料金を逃れたとして起訴されていた。検察はこれを目に余る事件と呼んだ。18か月にわたり、この会社は国から数百万をだまし取り、その間、誠実な競合他社は苦しんでいたと検察は述べた。被告の説明は厚かましいものだった。運転手たちは知らずに行動したのであり、自分は肝心の日には入院していたと彼は主張した。このアリバイは薄弱だとわかった。記録は、彼が酩酊の治療を受けたのは一晩だけだったことを示していた。次に彼は、税関による車庫への立ち入りは不法侵入に当たり、記録の押収は不当だと主張した。裁判官はどちらの主張も退けた。彼女は、彼がすでに税金を滞納しており、複数の債権者に追われていること、そして法廷で検査官を悪魔呼ばわりしたことを指摘した。刑には、10年間いかなる会社の経営にも携われないという資格剥奪が加えられた。法廷の外で、彼の弁護士は上訴は確実だと述べた。裁判を追ってきた人で、それが成功すると予想した人はほとんどいなかった。","words":[["custodial","freq:custodial"],["smuggle","freq:smuggle"],["surcharge","freq:surcharge"],["egregious","freq:egregious"],["brazen","freq:brazen"],["unknowingly","freq:unknowingly"],["alibi","freq:alibi"],["flimsy","freq:flimsy"],["intoxication","freq:intoxication"],["trespass","freq:trespass"],["unwarranted","freq:unwarranted"],["delinquent","freq:delinquent"],["creditor","freq:creditor"],["fiend","freq:fiend"],["disqualification","freq:disqualification"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the defendant's alibi fail?","o":["Hospital records covered only one night.","His drivers contradicted him.","The hospital had closed.","He admitted that it was false."],"a":0,"why":"Records showed that he had been treated for intoxication on one night only とある。"},{"q":"What was added to his prison sentence?","o":["A fine equal to the unpaid surcharge","The loss of his trucks","A ten-year ban on running companies","An order to apologize to the inspector"],"a":2,"why":"A disqualification from running any company for ten years was added とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0150","page":"freq","group":"2101〜2200","kind":"地下の生命","text":"Two kilometers beneath the surface of the earth, in water that has not seen sunlight for millions of years, there is life. The discovery of these subterranean bacteria has changed our ideas about where living things can exist. They survive without oxygen, in corrosive water hot enough to burn skin. Their energy comes not from the sun but from the natural fission of radioactive elements in the rock. Genomic studies show that they divide perhaps once in a thousand years. To a biologist, the most salient fact is their patience. The research is difficult. Samples must be sealed with epoxy the moment they are taken, and the cell walls have an elasticity that makes them hard to break open. There is no observable activity for months. Proving causation, that the bacteria really live on radiation and not on something else, took a decade. The findings matter beyond geology. If life can exist here, it may exist under the ice of distant moons, and the search for extraterrestrial organisms has gained new energy. Nobody expects sentient beings. But the eradication of a single old assumption, that life needs light, may prove one of the larger steps humankind has taken. The cold is no affliction to such creatures, and their endurance seems almost superhuman.","ja":"地表から2キロ下、何百万年も日光を見ていない水の中に、生命がいる。こうした地下の細菌の発見は、生き物がどこに存在できるかについての私たちの考えを変えた。それらは酸素なしで、皮膚をやけどさせるほど熱い腐食性の水の中で生きている。そのエネルギーは太陽からではなく、岩の中の放射性元素の自然な核分裂から来る。ゲノムの研究は、それらがおそらく1,000年に一度しか分裂しないことを示している。生物学者にとって最も顕著な事実は、その辛抱強さだ。研究は難しい。試料は採取した瞬間にエポキシ樹脂で密封しなければならず、細胞壁には弾力性があって壊して開けにくい。何か月も、目に見える活動はない。因果関係、つまり細菌が本当に放射線で生きていて、ほかの何かで生きているのではないことを証明するのに10年かかった。この発見は地質学を超えて重要だ。ここに生命が存在できるなら、遠い衛星の氷の下にも存在するかもしれず、地球外生物の探索は新たな勢いを得た。感覚を持つ存在を期待する人はいない。しかし、生命には光が必要だというたった一つの古い思い込みを根絶したことは、人類が踏み出した大きな歩みの一つになるかもしれない。寒さはそうした生き物にとって苦しみではなく、その耐久力はほとんど超人的に思える。","words":[["subterranean","freq:subterranean"],["corrosive","freq:corrosive"],["fission","freq:fission"],["Genomic","freq:genomic"],["salient","freq:salient"],["epoxy","freq:epoxy"],["elasticity","freq:elasticity"],["observable","freq:observable"],["causation","freq:causation"],["extraterrestrial","freq:extraterrestrial"],["sentient","freq:sentient"],["eradication","freq:eradication"],["humankind","freq:humankind"],["affliction","freq:affliction"],["superhuman","freq:superhuman"]],"qs":[{"q":"Where do these bacteria get their energy?","o":["From sunlight","From the fission of radioactive elements in rock","From oxygen in the water","From other living things"],"a":1,"why":"from the natural fission of radioactive elements in the rock とある。"},{"q":"Why does the discovery matter beyond geology?","o":["It suggests life might exist under the ice of distant moons.","It proves that bacteria can think.","It shows that the earth is cooling.","It makes mining safer."],"a":0,"why":"If life can exist here, it may exist under the ice of distant moons とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0151","page":"freq","group":"2101〜2200","kind":"小さな出版社の成功","text":"When Tessa Bloom founded Lantern Press ten years ago, friends told her that the idea was hopeless. She wanted to publish books for children that respected their intelligence. The market, she was told, rewarded mediocrity. Today her fledgling company has a readership of two million and a shelf of prizes. Bloom's approach owes much to her years as a teacher. She has strong views on pedagogy, and she believes that a story should conjure a world rather than explain one. Her authors are asked to cut every snippet of moral advice. The craftsmanship of the books is remarkable. Each is printed on heavy paper, with copious illustrations, and the centerpiece of every volume is a map. Success has brought the limelight, which she dislikes. She remains an enigmatic figure who rarely appears in public, and the adoration of young readers seems to puzzle her. Last year a philanthropist offered to pay for the refurbishment of an old church as a children's library. Bloom accepted on one condition: that her name should not appear on it. The library opened in May, beneath a spire that can be seen across the town.","ja":"テッサ・ブルームが10年前にランタン・プレスを設立したとき、友人たちはその考えは見込みがないと言った。彼女は、子どもの知性を尊重する子ども向けの本を出版したかった。市場は凡庸さに報いるのだと彼女は言われた。今日、彼女の発足したばかりだった会社は200万人の読者と、棚いっぱいの賞を持っている。ブルームのやり方は、教師だった年月に多くを負っている。彼女は教授法について強い考えを持ち、物語は世界を説明するのではなく、世界を呼び出すべきだと信じている。彼女の著者たちは、道徳的な助言の断片をことごとく削るよう求められる。本の職人技は見事だ。どの本も厚い紙に印刷され、豊富な挿絵があり、どの巻でも目玉は地図だ。成功は脚光をもたらしたが、彼女はそれを嫌う。彼女は今もめったに公の場に姿を見せない謎めいた人物であり、幼い読者たちの熱愛は彼女を戸惑わせるようだ。昨年、ある慈善家が、古い教会を子ども図書館として改装する費用を出すと申し出た。ブルームは一つの条件を付けて受け入れた。自分の名前をそこに出さないことだ。図書館は5月、町中から見える尖塔の下で開館した。","words":[["mediocrity","freq:mediocrity"],["fledgling","freq:fledgling"],["readership","freq:readership"],["pedagogy","freq:pedagogy"],["conjure","freq:conjure"],["snippet","freq:snippet"],["craftsmanship","freq:craftsmanship"],["copious","freq:copious"],["centerpiece","freq:centerpiece"],["limelight","freq:limelight"],["enigmatic","freq:enigmatic"],["adoration","freq:adoration"],["philanthropist","freq:philanthropist"],["refurbishment","freq:refurbishment"],["spire","freq:spire"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does Bloom ask her authors to remove?","o":["Maps","Illustrations","Any piece of moral advice","Difficult words"],"a":2,"why":"Her authors are asked to cut every snippet of moral advice とある。"},{"q":"On what condition did she accept the philanthropist's offer?","o":["That the library be free","That her name not appear on it","That only her books be stocked","That the church be rebuilt first"],"a":1,"why":"on one condition: that her name should not appear on it とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0152","page":"freq","group":"2101〜2200","kind":"交渉人の教え","text":"Dr. Nadia Rahimi spent thirty years negotiating the release of hostages, and her new book distills what she learned. It is a shrewd and unsentimental work. Rahimi begins by trying to dispel a myth. Negotiators, she says, are not smooth talkers. The best are astute listeners who can remain cordial with people they loathe. She warns against a condescending tone, which hostage-takers detect at once, and against wishful thinking about quick results. In actuality, most cases take weeks. The quality she values most is tenacity. A kidnapper may be fickle, changing demands by the hour, and the negotiator must grapple with each change without anger. Rahimi is honest about the cost. She describes the pang she still feels over an untimely death in 1998, when a deal collapsed an hour before the release. For years afterward she felt a compulsion to reread the transcripts, searching for her mistake. She does not revel in her successes. The book ends with a simple rule. Never promise what you cannot deliver, she writes, because the other side remembers everything, and so, in the end, will you.","ja":"ナディア・ラヒミ博士は30年間、人質の解放交渉に携わり、新著はそこで学んだことの精髄をまとめている。それは抜け目がなく、感傷を排した本だ。ラヒミはまず、一つの俗説を払いのけようとする。交渉人は口のうまい人ではないと彼女は言う。最良の交渉人は、ひどく嫌っている相手にも友好的でいられる、鋭い聞き手なのだ。彼女は、人質を取る者がすぐに見抜く、人を見下すような口調を戒め、早い結果についての希望的観測も戒める。実際には、ほとんどの事件は数週間かかる。彼女が最も重んじる資質は粘り強さだ。誘拐犯は気まぐれで、1時間ごとに要求を変えるかもしれず、交渉人は怒ることなく一つひとつの変化に取り組まなければならない。ラヒミは代償について正直だ。1998年、解放の1時間前に取引が崩れて起きた早すぎる死について、今も感じる胸のうずきを彼女は記している。その後何年も、彼女は自分の誤りを探して記録を読み返さずにいられない衝動を感じた。彼女は自分の成功に酔いしれない。本は単純な規則で終わる。果たせないことを決して約束するな、と彼女は書く。相手側はすべてを覚えているし、最後には自分も覚えていることになるからだ。","words":[["shrewd","freq:shrewd"],["dispel","freq:dispel"],["astute","freq:astute"],["cordial","freq:cordial"],["loathe","freq:loathe"],["condescending","freq:condescending"],["wishful","freq:wishful"],["actuality","freq:actuality"],["tenacity","freq:tenacity"],["fickle","freq:fickle"],["grapple","freq:grapple"],["pang","freq:pang"],["untimely","freq:untimely"],["compulsion","freq:compulsion"],["revel","freq:revel"]],"qs":[{"q":"According to Rahimi, what are the best negotiators like?","o":["Smooth and persuasive talkers","Careful listeners who stay polite with people they dislike","Quick decision-makers","People who make many promises"],"a":1,"why":"The best are astute listeners who can remain cordial with people they loathe とある。"},{"q":"What is the rule at the end of the book?","o":["Never promise what you cannot deliver.","Always demand a quick result.","Change your demands every hour.","Avoid reading old transcripts."],"a":0,"why":"Never promise what you cannot deliver とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0153","page":"freq","group":"2101〜2200","kind":"廃村の動物たち","text":"The village of Kellmoor was abandoned in 1962, when the mine closed and the last families left. Sixty years on, it belongs to the animals. I visited at dusk in March. The road was overgrown, and the first houses were derelict, their roofs fallen in. A murky pond lay where the square had been, and the stench of rotting leaves hung over it. Yet the place was full of life. Deer come down from the hills to forage in the old gardens, where apple trees still sprout new branches each spring. Badgers have dug a burrow beneath the church. Along the jagged remains of the mine wall, I counted eleven kinds of bird. For the people who lived here, life was a matter of subsistence, and the work was strenuous. For the animals, the ruins offer shelter and a tolerable winter. As darkness fell, I saw a flicker of movement in the chapel window: an owl, leaving to hunt. There was a flurry of wings, then silence. Some would call an owl at nightfall a bad omen. To me it seemed a glimmer of something hopeful, proof that a place can be lost to us and still not be lost.","ja":"ケルムーアの村は1962年、鉱山が閉鎖され、最後の家族が去ったときに放棄された。60年たった今、そこは動物たちのものだ。私は3月の夕暮れに訪れた。道は草木に覆われ、最初の家々は荒れ果て、屋根は落ちていた。広場があった場所には濁った池があり、腐った葉の悪臭がその上に漂っていた。それでもその場所は生命に満ちていた。シカが丘から下りてきて古い庭で食べ物を探す。そこではリンゴの木が今も毎年春に新しい枝を芽吹かせる。アナグマは教会の下に巣穴を掘った。鉱山の壁のぎざぎざの残骸に沿って、私は11種類の鳥を数えた。ここに住んでいた人々にとって、生活は生きていくのがやっとのもので、仕事は激しかった。動物たちにとって、廃墟は隠れ家と、どうにか耐えられる冬を与えてくれる。暗くなると、礼拝堂の窓にちらりと動くものが見えた。狩りに出ていくフクロウだった。慌ただしい羽ばたきがあり、それから静寂が訪れた。日暮れのフクロウを悪い前兆と呼ぶ人もいるだろう。私には、それは何か希望のあるもののかすかな光に思えた。ある場所が私たちから失われても、なお失われてはいないことの証しである。","words":[["overgrown","freq:overgrown"],["derelict","freq:derelict"],["murky","freq:murky"],["stench","freq:stench"],["forage","freq:forage"],["sprout","freq:sprout"],["burrow","freq:burrow"],["jagged","freq:jagged"],["subsistence","freq:subsistence"],["strenuous","freq:strenuous"],["tolerable","freq:tolerable"],["flicker","freq:flicker"],["flurry","freq:flurry"],["omen","freq:omen"],["glimmer","freq:glimmer"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why was the village abandoned?","o":["A flood destroyed it.","The mine closed.","The animals drove people out.","The church collapsed."],"a":1,"why":"abandoned in 1962, when the mine closed and the last families left とある。"},{"q":"How does the writer feel about seeing the owl?","o":["Frightened by a bad sign","Angry at the ruin","Bored by the silence","Hopeful that the place is still alive"],"a":3,"why":"To me it seemed a glimmer of something hopeful とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0154","page":"freq","group":"2101〜2200","kind":"嵐の夜の救助","text":"The night of the storm was, by any measure, fraught with danger. At two in the morning the lifeboat crew at Penhallow received a call. A fishing boat had lost its engine three miles out, with four men aboard. The coxswain, Mary Trevena, knew the sea was almost too rough to launch. She also knew there was no workable alternative. It took the crew twenty minutes simply to hoist the boat down the slipway. Beyond the harbor wall, waves broke over them with a force that made the hull groan. When they reached the fishing boat, they found the men alive but exhausted. One had tied himself to the mast. Another could only clasp the rail and stare. Getting them across took an hour. Trevena later said that what the fishermen had been through was unspeakable, and that she had seen grown men shudder at less. All four were brought home unharmed. On the quay, she allowed herself to exhale for what felt like the first time that night. It had been abundantly clear, she told a reporter, that luck had been on their side. Then she went home to feed her dog.","ja":"嵐の夜は、どう見ても危険に満ちていた。午前2時、ペンハローの救命艇の乗組員に連絡が入った。漁船が3マイル沖でエンジンを失い、4人が乗っているという。艇長のメアリー・トレベナは、海が出艇するにはほとんど荒すぎることを知っていた。ほかに実行可能な選択肢がないことも知っていた。乗組員が艇を斜路から降ろすだけで20分かかった。港の防波堤の外では、波が船体をきしませるほどの力で彼らに襲いかかった。漁船に着くと、男たちは生きていたが疲れ果てていた。一人は自分をマストに縛りつけていた。別の一人は手すりを握りしめて見つめることしかできなかった。彼らを移すのに1時間かかった。トレベナは後に、漁師たちが経験したことは言葉にできないもので、もっと軽いことで大の男が身震いするのを見たことがあると語った。4人全員が無事に連れ帰られた。岸壁で、彼女はその夜初めてと思えるほど、ようやく息を吐いた。運が自分たちに味方していたことは十分すぎるほど明らかだったと、彼女は記者に語った。それから犬に餌をやるために家へ帰った。","words":[["fraught","freq:fraught"],["workable","freq:workable"],["hoist","freq:hoist"],["groan","freq:groan"],["clasp","freq:clasp"],["unspeakable","freq:unspeakable"],["shudder","freq:shudder"],["unharmed","freq:unharmed"],["exhale","freq:exhale"],["abundantly","freq:abundantly"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did Trevena decide to launch the lifeboat?","o":["The sea had become calm.","There was no other practical way to help.","The fishermen had paid for it.","Another crew refused to go."],"a":1,"why":"there was no workable alternative（ほかに実行可能な選択肢がない）と知っていた。"},{"q":"What did she say about the rescue afterward?","o":["The crew had made mistakes.","The fishermen were to blame.","Luck had clearly been on their side.","The boat should be replaced."],"a":2,"why":"It had been abundantly clear ... that luck had been on their side とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0155","page":"freq","group":"2201〜2300","kind":"議会と大統領の対立","text":"The attempt to impeach President Lorenzo Vidal has thrown the republic of Asteria into confusion. The charge is that he accepted money from a mining lobbyist in exchange for a change in the law. Vidal calls the accusation baseless. In an emphatic speech on Monday, he said that his enemies were trying to derail a multilateral trade agreement that would free the country from the yoke of foreign debt. Parliament was not impressed. The speaker issued a formal rebuke, and opposition deputies accused the president of trying to obstruct the inquiry by refusing to release his diaries. Events moved quickly. When Vidal dismissed the chief prosecutor, protesters built a barricade outside the palace, and the court ordered the government to reinstate her within a day. The president's party then threatened to retaliate by blocking the budget. Its position is weak. Last month's local elections were a rout, and several of its deputies fear for their seats. Business leaders warn that the crisis could jeopardize the economy. They are calling for a resumption of talks between the two sides. So far, neither has shown any wish to speak first.","ja":"ロレンソ・ビダル大統領を弾劾しようとする動きは、アステリア共和国を混乱に陥れた。容疑は、法律の変更と引き換えに、鉱業界のロビイストから金を受け取ったというものだ。ビダルはこの告発を根拠がないと呼ぶ。月曜の断固とした演説で、彼は、国を対外債務のくびきから解放するはずの多国間貿易協定を、敵が頓挫させようとしているのだと述べた。議会は感銘を受けなかった。議長は正式な叱責を出し、野党議員たちは、大統領が日誌の公開を拒むことで調査を妨害しようとしていると非難した。事態は急速に動いた。ビダルが検事総長を解任すると、抗議者たちは宮殿の外にバリケードを築き、裁判所は政府に対し、1日以内に彼女を復職させるよう命じた。すると大統領の党は、予算を阻止することで報復すると脅した。その立場は弱い。先月の地方選挙は完敗で、同党の議員の何人かは自分の議席を心配している。経済界の指導者たちは、この危機が経済を危険にさらしかねないと警告する。彼らは双方の協議の再開を求めている。今のところ、どちらも先に話そうという気持ちを見せていない。","words":[["impeach","freq:impeach"],["lobbyist","freq:lobbyist"],["baseless","freq:baseless"],["emphatic","freq:emphatic"],["derail","freq:derail"],["multilateral","freq:multilateral"],["yoke","freq:yoke"],["rebuke","freq:rebuke"],["obstruct","freq:obstruct"],["barricade","freq:barricade"],["reinstate","freq:reinstate"],["retaliate","freq:retaliate"],["rout","freq:rout"],["jeopardize","freq:jeopardize"],["resumption","freq:resumption"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is the president accused of?","o":["Taking money in return for changing a law","Refusing to sign a trade agreement","Cancelling local elections","Borrowing from foreign banks"],"a":0,"why":"accepted money from a mining lobbyist in exchange for a change in the law とある。"},{"q":"What did the court order after the chief prosecutor was dismissed?","o":["That the protesters go home","That the president resign","That she be given her job back within a day","That parliament be closed"],"a":2,"why":"the court ordered the government to reinstate her within a day とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0156","page":"freq","group":"2201〜2300","kind":"銀行合併の後始末","text":"Three years ago, the amalgamation of two regional banks was presented as a rescue. One was ailing; the other was healthy; together they would be strong. A report published this week tells a different story. According to the regulator, the merged bank suffered from serious mismanagement from the first day. There was a mismatch between its computer systems, and for six months staff could not see which debtor owed what. Managers spent their time in unproductive meetings while rivals began to undercut the bank on loans. Then came the economic slowdown. The report gives little credence to the directors' defense. They had claimed that an outside assailant, a foreign fund, had set out to plunder the bank's assets. The regulator found no evidence for this and accused the board of trying to distort the record. The chairman's rebuttal, issued yesterday, was brief. He said that the regulator had exceeded its remit and that the bank's customers had not lost a penny. That is true, but only because taxpayers paid. Shareholders gave the report a lukewarm welcome. Most of them, one remarked, had already guessed the ending.","ja":"3年前、2つの地方銀行の合併は救済として発表された。一方は経営不振で、他方は健全だった。一緒になれば強くなるはずだった。今週公表された報告書は、異なる物語を語っている。規制当局によれば、合併した銀行は初日から深刻な管理の誤りに苦しんでいた。両行のコンピューターシステムが合わず、6か月の間、職員はどの債務者がいくら借りているのかを見ることができなかった。経営幹部が実りのない会議に時間を費やしている間に、競合他社は融資でこの銀行より安い条件を出し始めた。そこへ景気の減速がやって来た。報告書は取締役たちの弁明をほとんど信用していない。彼らは、外部の襲撃者である外国の基金が銀行の資産を略奪しようとしたと主張していた。規制当局はその証拠を見つけられず、取締役会が記録をゆがめようとしたと非難した。昨日出された会長の反論は短かった。規制当局は権限を逸脱しており、銀行の顧客は一銭も失っていないと彼は述べた。それは事実だが、納税者が払ったからにすぎない。株主たちはこの報告書を気乗りしない様子で迎えた。そのほとんどはすでに結末を見抜いていたと、一人が語った。","words":[["amalgamation","freq:amalgamation"],["ailing","freq:ailing"],["mismanagement","freq:mismanagement"],["mismatch","freq:mismatch"],["debtor","freq:debtor"],["unproductive","freq:unproductive"],["undercut","freq:undercut"],["slowdown","freq:slowdown"],["credence","freq:credence"],["assailant","freq:assailant"],["plunder","freq:plunder"],["distort","freq:distort"],["rebuttal","freq:rebuttal"],["remit","freq:remit"],["lukewarm","freq:lukewarm"]],"qs":[{"q":"What practical problem followed the merger?","o":["The two banks' computer systems did not work together.","Customers withdrew their savings.","The offices were too far apart.","The staff went on strike."],"a":0,"why":"There was a mismatch between its computer systems とある。"},{"q":"Why did customers lose no money?","o":["The foreign fund repaid them.","The directors used their own wealth.","The bank made a profit.","Taxpayers covered the losses."],"a":3,"why":"That is true, but only because taxpayers paid とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0157","page":"freq","group":"2201〜2300","kind":"洞窟の中の医学調査","text":"In a limestone cavern in the south of France, a team of doctors has spent two years studying how the body responds to life without daylight. Eight volunteers lived underground for forty days, in a kind of cocoon cut off from clocks and news. The findings have surprised specialists. Sleep patterns changed within a week, and several volunteers developed a slower gait, as if the body were saving energy. Blood tests suggested a greater susceptibility to infection. One man suffered a small hemorrhage in the eye, though doctors do not believe it was symptomatic of anything serious. A dispensary at the cave entrance provided medicines. The project had an anthropological side as well. Researchers recorded how the group organized itself, and a normative pattern soon appeared: meals at shared times, a leader chosen without discussion. There was no simple dichotomy between \"strong\" and \"weak\" members. Those who coped best valued rationality but also allowed themselves to feel afraid. Maintaining objectivity was hard for the observers, who grew fond of their subjects. Their report ends with a clear articulation of its limits. The air was damp, and evaporation from the walls may have affected the results. Applications to palliative care, where patients often lose track of time, will need further study.","ja":"フランス南部の石灰岩の大きな洞窟で、医師のチームが2年をかけて、日光のない生活に体がどう反応するかを研究してきた。8人の志願者が、時計や報道から切り離された一種のまゆの中で、40日間地下で暮らした。その結果は専門家を驚かせた。睡眠の型は1週間のうちに変わり、何人かの志願者は、体がエネルギーを節約しているかのように、歩き方が遅くなった。血液検査は、感染しやすくなっていることを示唆した。一人の男性は目に小さな出血を起こしたが、医師たちはそれが深刻な何かの症状だとは考えていない。洞窟の入口の薬局が薬を提供した。この計画には人類学的な側面もあった。研究者たちは集団がどう自らを組織するかを記録し、まもなく規範的な型が現れた。決まった時間の共同の食事、話し合いなしに選ばれた指導者である。「強い」成員と「弱い」成員という単純な二分はなかった。最もうまく対処した人々は合理性を重んじたが、恐れを感じることも自分に許していた。客観性を保つことは観察者たちにとって難しかった。彼らは対象者に愛着を抱くようになったからだ。報告書は、その限界を明確に述べて終わる。空気は湿っており、壁からの蒸発が結果に影響したかもしれない。患者がしばしば時間の感覚を失う緩和ケアへの応用には、さらなる研究が必要だ。","words":[["cavern","freq:cavern"],["cocoon","freq:cocoon"],["gait","freq:gait"],["susceptibility","freq:susceptibility"],["hemorrhage","freq:hemorrhage"],["symptomatic","freq:symptomatic"],["dispensary","freq:dispensary"],["anthropological","freq:anthropological"],["normative","freq:normative"],["dichotomy","freq:dichotomy"],["rationality","freq:rationality"],["objectivity","freq:objectivity"],["articulation","freq:articulation"],["evaporation","freq:evaporation"],["palliative","freq:palliative"]],"qs":[{"q":"What physical change appeared in several volunteers?","o":["They walked more slowly.","They lost their hearing.","They grew taller.","They stopped sleeping."],"a":0,"why":"several volunteers developed a slower gait とある。"},{"q":"Which volunteers coped best?","o":["Those who never felt fear","Those who valued reason but also accepted their fear","Those who refused to follow the leader","Those who slept the most"],"a":1,"why":"valued rationality but also allowed themselves to feel afraid とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0158","page":"freq","group":"2201〜2300","kind":"喜劇作家の業績","text":"The comic writer who published under the pseudonym \"J. Marlow\" was, in her heyday, the most widely read author in the country. A new study argues that she has been underrated. Her novels are usually described as light entertainment. Each is a spoof of some popular form, the detective story, the romance, the school tale, and each uses every familiar trope with obvious delight. But the author of the study shows how much subtlety lies beneath the jokes. Marlow had a gift for improvisation, and her plots take whimsical turns that no reader can predict. She also invented words freely, and several have entered the general lexicon. The best chapter concerns her penultimate novel, a parable about a village that forgets how to laugh. Its metaphorical meaning was missed by reviewers at the time, who saw only a comic interlude. Read today, it is clearly a book about fear. Every nuance of the dialogue repays attention. Marlow never sought stardom and gave only two interviews in forty years. Her work, the study concludes, was able to transcend its period because she took comedy seriously. Few writers have been funnier, and few have been wiser.","ja":"「J・マーロウ」という筆名で出版した喜劇作家は、全盛期にはこの国で最も広く読まれた著者だった。新しい研究は、彼女が過小評価されてきたと論じる。彼女の小説はたいてい軽い娯楽と評される。どの作品も、探偵小説、恋愛小説、学園物語といった何らかの人気の形式のもじりであり、おなじみの筋立てを明らかに楽しみながらすべて使っている。しかしこの研究の著者は、冗談の下にどれほどの繊細さがあるかを示している。マーロウには即興の才があり、彼女の筋はどの読者にも予測できない風変わりで愉快な展開をする。彼女はまた言葉を自由に作り出し、そのいくつかは一般の語彙に加わった。最良の章は、彼女の最後から2番目の小説、笑い方を忘れた村についての寓話を扱っている。その比喩的な意味は当時の書評家に見落とされ、彼らは喜劇的な幕間しか見なかった。今日読むと、それは明らかに恐怖についての本だ。会話の微妙な意味合いの一つひとつが、注意を払う価値がある。マーロウはスターの地位を求めたことがなく、40年間で取材に応じたのは2回だけだった。彼女の作品が時代を超えることができたのは、喜劇を真剣に受け止めたからだと、この研究は結論づけている。彼女ほど面白い作家はほとんどおらず、彼女ほど賢明な作家もほとんどいない。","words":[["pseudonym","freq:pseudonym"],["heyday","freq:heyday"],["spoof","freq:spoof"],["trope","freq:trope"],["subtlety","freq:subtlety"],["improvisation","freq:improvisation"],["whimsical","freq:whimsical"],["lexicon","freq:lexicon"],["penultimate","freq:penultimate"],["parable","freq:parable"],["metaphorical","freq:metaphorical"],["interlude","freq:interlude"],["nuance","freq:nuance"],["stardom","freq:stardom"],["transcend","freq:transcend"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does the study argue about Marlow?","o":["Her novels were badly written.","Her work is more subtle than it has been given credit for.","She copied other writers.","She wanted to be famous."],"a":1,"why":"she has been underrated とあり、how much subtlety lies beneath the jokes を示している。"},{"q":"What did reviewers miss about her penultimate novel?","o":["It was a spoof of a detective story.","It was written under her real name.","It was unfinished.","It was really about fear."],"a":3,"why":"Its metaphorical meaning was missed ... Read today, it is clearly a book about fear とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0159","page":"freq","group":"2201〜2300","kind":"修道院長の生涯","text":"The abbot Benedict of Hallow, who died in 1412, is still revered in the valley that bears his name. A new biography separates the man from the legend. Benedict was a diminutive figure, barely five feet tall, and by his own account an irritable one. He entered the monastic life at sixteen as an act of atonement, having, in his words, been a wayward and violent boy. The idealism of his youth never left him. He believed that a monastery should shelter anyone, and he took in the outcast and the sick without asking questions. This caused apprehension among the older monks, who thought him gullible. He was not. A watchful administrator, he doubled the abbey's lands and kept careful accounts. His greatest benefactor was a knight whose life he had saved, and the code of chivalry, with its duty to protect the weak, shaped his rule. He sought to impart learning as well as faith, and the school he founded lasted four centuries. His namesake, the present abbot, says that the old stories of miracles miss the point. What mattered was not that Benedict was a saint, but that he kept the door open.","ja":"1412年に亡くなったハロウの修道院長ベネディクトは、彼の名を冠した谷で今もあがめられている。新しい伝記は、その人物を伝説から切り離している。ベネディクトは小柄な人で、背丈は5フィートそこそこしかなく、本人の述べるところでは怒りっぽかった。彼は16歳で、罪の償いとして修道生活に入った。彼自身の言葉によれば、言うことを聞かない乱暴な少年だったからだ。若い頃の理想主義は決して彼を離れなかった。彼は、修道院は誰でもかくまうべきだと信じ、のけ者や病人を何も尋ねずに受け入れた。これは年長の修道士たちに不安を引き起こし、彼らは彼をだまされやすいと考えた。そうではなかった。用心深い管理者だった彼は、修道院の土地を倍にし、注意深く帳簿をつけた。彼の最大の後援者は、彼が命を救った騎士であり、弱者を守る義務を伴う騎士道の掟が彼の統治を形作った。彼は信仰だけでなく学問も授けようとし、彼が創設した学校は4世紀続いた。彼と同名の現在の修道院長は、奇跡についての古い物語は要点を外していると言う。大切なのは、ベネディクトが聖人だったことではなく、彼が扉を開けておいたことなのだ。","words":[["revered","freq:revere"],["diminutive","freq:diminutive"],["irritable","freq:irritable"],["monastic","freq:monastic"],["atonement","freq:atonement"],["wayward","freq:wayward"],["idealism","freq:idealism"],["outcast","freq:outcast"],["apprehension","freq:apprehension"],["gullible","freq:gullible"],["watchful","freq:watchful"],["benefactor","freq:benefactor"],["chivalry","freq:chivalry"],["impart","freq:impart"],["namesake","freq:namesake"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did Benedict become a monk?","o":["To make up for a violent youth","To escape poverty","To please a knight","To found a school"],"a":0,"why":"as an act of atonement, having ... been a wayward and violent boy とある。"},{"q":"Why did older monks think him gullible?","o":["He believed in miracles.","He kept poor accounts.","He accepted anyone into the monastery without questions.","He trusted the knight too much."],"a":2,"why":"he took in the outcast and the sick without asking questions とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0160","page":"freq","group":"2201〜2300","kind":"冬の山小屋","text":"We reached the hut an hour before dark, on a dreary afternoon in January. A gust of wind tore the door from my hand as I opened it. Inside, the place had only a semblance of order. Blankets lay in a tangle on the bunks, and the shelves held a motley collection of tins left by earlier visitors. Firewood was scant. Still, there was a stone hearth, and within twenty minutes we had a fire. The hut became snug surprisingly fast. Outside, the wind held a line of snow aloft from the ridge like a white flag. We ate, and then turned to the old pastime of every stormbound party: cards. A pesky draft kept blowing out the candle. Something seemed to lurk beyond the window, though it was only a branch moving. On the wall hung a patchwork quilt, stitched, according to a note, by the wife of the first keeper in 1931. I slept badly, and yet the night was oddly pleasurable. At six, the cold was enough to rouse us without any alarm. The storm had passed. We stepped out into a silence so complete that our own breathing sounded loud.","ja":"1月のわびしい午後、私たちは日没の1時間前に小屋に着いた。扉を開けると、突風がそれを私の手からもぎ取った。中は、かろうじて整っているように見えるだけだった。寝台の上には毛布がもつれて置かれ、棚には以前の訪問者が残した雑多な缶詰が並んでいた。まきはわずかだった。それでも石の暖炉があり、20分のうちに火がついた。小屋は驚くほど速く居心地がよくなった。外では、風が尾根から雪の筋を白い旗のように空中に高く掲げていた。私たちは食事をし、それから、嵐に閉じ込められたどの一行もする昔ながらの気晴らしに取りかかった。トランプである。厄介なすきま風が何度もろうそくを吹き消した。窓の向こうに何かが潜んでいるように思えたが、動いている枝にすぎなかった。壁にはつぎはぎ細工の掛け布団が掛かっており、書きつけによれば、1931年に最初の番人の妻が縫ったものだった。私はよく眠れなかったが、それでもその夜は妙に心地よかった。6時、寒さは目覚ましなしでも私たちを起こすのに十分だった。嵐は過ぎていた。私たちは、自分たちの息づかいが大きく聞こえるほど完全な静けさの中へ踏み出した。","words":[["dreary","freq:dreary"],["gust","freq:gust"],["semblance","freq:semblance"],["tangle","freq:tangle"],["motley","freq:motley"],["scant","freq:scant"],["hearth","freq:hearth"],["snug","freq:snug"],["aloft","freq:aloft"],["pastime","freq:pastime"],["pesky","freq:pesky"],["lurk","freq:lurk"],["patchwork","freq:patchwork"],["pleasurable","freq:pleasurable"],["rouse","freq:rouse"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was the condition of the hut when the group arrived?","o":["Clean and well stocked","Untidy, with little firewood","Locked and empty","Full of other climbers"],"a":1,"why":"Blankets lay in a tangle とあり、Firewood was scant とある。"},{"q":"What woke the group in the morning?","o":["An alarm","The storm","The candle going out","The cold"],"a":3,"why":"the cold was enough to rouse us without any alarm とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0161","page":"freq","group":"2201〜2300","kind":"処刑場の跡地","text":"On a hill above the old town stands a small park whose history most visitors do not know. Until 1820, it was the site of the gallows. Beset by debts and hunger, many of those who died here had stolen no more than a loaf of bread. The city council has now decided to redefine the place. A plan published last month proposes a memorial, with panels explaining whence the condemned came and what they had done. Not everyone approves. One councillor called the idea a retrograde step that would propagate gloom in a place where children play. His speech, according to those present, was angry and largely incoherent. Supporters reply that the foregoing century of silence has served nobody. A city, they say, should know its own past. The first public meeting was held on Tuesday, and the signs were auspicious: three hundred people came, and most stayed to the end. There will doubtless be more arguments before anything is built. But a retired teacher who spoke last seemed to express the general feeling. We walk over these people every day, she said. The least we can do is learn their names.","ja":"旧市街の上の丘に、ほとんどの訪問者がその歴史を知らない小さな公園がある。1820年まで、そこは絞首台のあった場所だった。借金と飢えに悩まされ、ここで死んだ人々の多くは、パン一つしか盗んでいなかった。市議会は今回、この場所を定義し直すことを決めた。先月公表された計画は、死刑を宣告された人々がどこから来て、何をしたのかを説明する掲示板を備えた記念の場を提案している。誰もが賛成しているわけではない。ある議員はこの考えを、子どもが遊ぶ場所に陰うつさを広める後退の一歩だと呼んだ。その場にいた人々によれば、彼の演説は怒りに満ち、ほとんど支離滅裂だった。支持者たちは、これまでの1世紀の沈黙は誰の役にも立たなかったと反論する。都市は自らの過去を知るべきだと彼らは言う。最初の公開集会は火曜に開かれ、幸先はよかった。300人が集まり、ほとんどが最後まで残った。何かが建てられるまでには、きっとさらに議論があるだろう。しかし、最後に発言した退職教師が、全体の気持ちを言い表したようだった。私たちは毎日この人たちの上を歩いている、と彼女は言った。せめて名前を知ることくらいはできるはずだ、と。","words":[["gallows","freq:gallows"],["Beset","freq:beset"],["redefine","freq:redefine"],["whence","freq:whence"],["retrograde","freq:retrograde"],["propagate","freq:propagate"],["incoherent","freq:incoherent"],["foregoing","freq:foregoing"],["auspicious","freq:auspicious"],["doubtless","freq:doubtless"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does the council's plan propose for the park?","o":["Closing it to children","Building houses on it","A memorial explaining who was executed there","Moving it to the old town"],"a":2,"why":"proposes a memorial, with panels explaining whence the condemned came and what they had done とある。"},{"q":"Why were the signs at the first meeting encouraging?","o":["Many people attended and stayed to the end.","The councillor withdrew his objection.","The money had already been raised.","The memorial was approved at once."],"a":0,"why":"three hundred people came, and most stayed to the end とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0162","page":"freq","group":"2301〜2400","kind":"徴兵をめぐる議会","text":"The debate on military service in the parliament of Ostland ended last night without a decision. The government had proposed to end the moratorium on conscription that has been in place since 1994. Supporters argued that a belligerent neighbor has left the country no choice. Opponents called the plan a return to the repressive years, when the state could banish young men to distant camps. The discussion was heated. A pacifist deputy, the grandson of a famous general, said unequivocally that he would go to prison before he voted for it. The prime minister was uncompromising. Defense, she said, is the prerogative of government, not of protest groups. There was indignation on the opposition benches when she described their objections as elitist. They replied that the existing system, in which the rich buy exemption, is hardly egalitarian. In the end, numbers decided the matter. Forty deputies chose to abstain, others left the chamber, and the session lacked a quorum. The government's opponents had found a way to thwart the bill without defeating it. The vote will be held again next month. Until then, neither side has more than a foothold.","ja":"オストランド議会での兵役をめぐる討論は昨夜、結論の出ないまま終わった。政府は、1994年から続いている徴兵の一時停止を終わらせることを提案していた。支持者たちは、好戦的な隣国のせいでこの国に選択の余地はなくなったと主張した。反対派はこの計画を、国が若い男性を遠くの収容所へ追いやることができた抑圧的な時代への逆戻りだと呼んだ。議論は白熱した。有名な将軍の孫である平和主義者の議員は、賛成票を投じるくらいなら刑務所に入ると、はっきり述べた。首相は妥協しなかった。国防は抗議団体ではなく政府の専権事項だと彼女は言った。彼女が反対派の異議をエリート主義的だと表現すると、野党席から憤りの声が上がった。彼らは、金持ちが免除を金で買える現行の制度はとても平等主義的とは言えないと応じた。結局、数が問題を決めた。40人の議員が棄権を選び、ほかの議員は議場を去り、会議は定足数を欠いた。政府の反対派は、法案を否決することなく阻止する方法を見つけたのだ。採決は来月もう一度行われる。それまでは、どちらの側も足がかり以上のものを持っていない。","words":[["moratorium","freq:moratorium"],["belligerent","freq:belligerent"],["repressive","freq:repressive"],["banish","freq:banish"],["pacifist","freq:pacifist"],["unequivocally","freq:unequivocally"],["uncompromising","freq:uncompromising"],["prerogative","freq:prerogative"],["indignation","freq:indignation"],["elitist","freq:elitist"],["egalitarian","freq:egalitarian"],["abstain","freq:abstain"],["quorum","freq:quorum"],["thwart","freq:thwart"],["foothold","freq:foothold"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the session end without a decision?","o":["The prime minister withdrew the bill.","Too few members were present for a valid vote.","The pacifist deputy was arrested.","The neighbor declared war."],"a":1,"why":"the session lacked a quorum（会議は定足数を欠いた）とある。"},{"q":"What did the opposition say about the present system?","o":["It is too expensive.","It has kept the peace.","It is unfair because the rich can buy their way out.","It should include women."],"a":2,"why":"the existing system, in which the rich buy exemption, is hardly egalitarian とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0163","page":"freq","group":"2301〜2400","kind":"家賃をめぐる不正","text":"An inquiry into the rental market in the capital has exposed practices that the minister for housing called a disgrace. The sector is largely unregulated. A tenancy can be ended with one month's notice, and unscrupulous landlords have learned to exploit the shortage of flats. Some demand a fee simply to place a family on a shortlist. Others solicit cash in return for \"exclusivity,\" a promise that the flat will not be shown to anyone else. The report quotes verbatim from recordings made by tenants. In one, an agent asks for a written testimonial from an employer, a bank statement, and three months' rent in advance. The inquiry found willful breaches of the law in a fifth of the cases it examined. It also blames the rules themselves. They are antiquated, dating from 1958, and so convoluted that honest landlords struggle to follow them. Rent controls introduced in 2015 proved counterproductive, because owners simply withdrew their properties. The report urges prudence. The main impediment to fair renting, it says, is not greed but supply. Until more homes are built, opportunistic behavior will continue, whatever the law says.","ja":"首都の賃貸市場についての調査が、住宅担当大臣が恥ずべきことと呼んだ慣行を明るみに出した。この分野はほとんど規制されていない。賃貸借は1か月前の通知で打ち切ることができ、悪徳な家主たちは住宅の不足につけ込むことを覚えた。一家を最終候補の名簿に載せるだけで手数料を要求する者もいる。ほかの人には部屋を見せないという約束、つまり「独占権」と引き換えに現金を求める者もいる。報告書は、入居希望者が録音した記録を一語一語そのまま引用している。その一つでは、仲介業者が雇用主からの書面の推薦状、銀行の明細書、3か月分の家賃の前払いを求めている。調査は、調べた事例の5分の1で故意の法律違反を見つけた。規則そのものも批判している。規則は1958年にさかのぼる時代遅れのもので、あまりに入り組んでいるため、誠実な家主は従うのに苦労する。2015年に導入された家賃統制は逆効果だった。所有者が単に物件を引き揚げたからだ。報告書は慎重さを促している。公正な賃貸の主な妨げは強欲ではなく供給だという。もっと多くの住宅が建てられるまで、法律が何と言おうと、機会に便乗する行動は続くだろう。","words":[["unregulated","freq:unregulated"],["tenancy","freq:tenancy"],["unscrupulous","freq:unscrupulous"],["shortlist","freq:shortlist"],["solicit","freq:solicit"],["exclusivity","freq:exclusivity"],["verbatim","freq:verbatim"],["testimonial","freq:testimonial"],["willful","freq:willful"],["antiquated","freq:antiquated"],["convoluted","freq:convoluted"],["counterproductive","freq:counterproductive"],["prudence","freq:prudence"],["impediment","freq:impediment"],["opportunistic","freq:opportunistic"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the rent controls of 2015 fail?","o":["Tenants refused to pay.","The courts rejected them.","Owners took their properties off the market.","Agents ignored the shortlists."],"a":2,"why":"owners simply withdrew their properties（所有者が物件を引き揚げた）とある。"},{"q":"What does the report say is the main obstacle to fair renting?","o":["The shortage of homes","The greed of tenants","The lack of recordings","The cost of testimonials"],"a":0,"why":"The main impediment to fair renting ... is not greed but supply とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0164","page":"freq","group":"2301〜2400","kind":"毒ヘビと新薬","text":"The Brazilian pit viper is one of the most venomous snakes in the world, and its bite can kill within hours. It is also, strangely, the source of a medicine taken by millions. In the 1960s, a young doctor noticed that farm workers who were bitten felt nauseous and then collapsed as their blood pressure fell. He wondered whether a dilution of the poison might help patients whose pressure was too high. The idea was nascent science at best, and colleagues dismissed it. The work required patience and rigor. Snake venom is a heterogeneous mixture of hundreds of chemicals, and separating them demanded great dexterity. One assistant, a neurotic perfectionist by his own description, spent three years on a single molecule. It worked. The drug that resulted is now used around the world. Researchers have since turned to other creatures. The taxonomy of poisonous animals has become a kind of map for drug hunters, and substances from spiders and sea snails are being tested for the treatment of gout, nerve pain, and the skin lesion caused by diabetes. Many early hopes have dissipated. But scientists refuse to succumb to pessimism. These poisons, they point out, are the product of primordial competition, refined over millions of years. A remedial use is often waiting to be found.","ja":"ブラジルのマムシは世界で最も毒の強いヘビの一つで、かまれると数時間で死ぬことがある。それは奇妙なことに、数百万人が服用する薬の源でもある。1960年代、ある若い医師が、かまれた農場労働者が吐き気を催し、それから血圧が下がって倒れることに気づいた。彼は、その毒を薄めたものが、血圧が高すぎる患者の助けになるのではないかと考えた。その考えはせいぜい生まれたばかりの科学であり、同僚たちは相手にしなかった。その仕事には忍耐と厳密さが必要だった。ヘビの毒は数百の化学物質の雑多な混合物であり、それらを分離するには大変な器用さが求められた。本人いわく神経質な完璧主義者だったある助手は、たった一つの分子に3年を費やした。それはうまくいった。その結果生まれた薬は、今では世界中で使われている。研究者たちはその後、ほかの生き物にも目を向けた。毒を持つ動物の分類は薬を探す人々にとって一種の地図になり、クモや海の巻き貝から取った物質が、痛風、神経痛、糖尿病が引き起こす皮膚の病変の治療のために試験されている。初期の期待の多くは消え去った。しかし科学者たちは悲観に屈することを拒む。これらの毒は太古からの競争の産物であり、何百万年もかけて磨かれてきたのだと彼らは指摘する。治療に役立つ使い道が、見つけられるのを待っていることが多いのだ。","words":[["venomous","freq:venomous"],["nauseous","freq:nauseous"],["dilution","freq:dilution"],["nascent","freq:nascent"],["rigor","freq:rigor"],["heterogeneous","freq:heterogeneous"],["dexterity","freq:dexterity"],["neurotic","freq:neurotic"],["taxonomy","freq:taxonomy"],["gout","freq:gout"],["lesion","freq:lesion"],["dissipated","freq:dissipated"],["succumb","freq:succumb"],["primordial","freq:primordial"],["remedial","freq:remedial"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the young doctor notice about people bitten by the snake?","o":["Their blood pressure fell sharply.","Their skin changed color.","They recovered within minutes.","They became immune to other poisons."],"a":0,"why":"collapsed as their blood pressure fell（血圧が下がって倒れた）とある。"},{"q":"Why do scientists remain hopeful about animal poisons?","o":["They are cheap to collect.","They have been refined by competition over millions of years.","They are found only in Brazil.","They never harm patients."],"a":1,"why":"the product of primordial competition, refined over millions of years とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0165","page":"freq","group":"2301〜2400","kind":"寓意画の解釈","text":"The great painting that hangs in the cathedral of Morven has puzzled visitors for five hundred years. At its center, a scribe sits writing while a city burns behind him. Around him are figures from a mythological world: a winged horse, a woman holding scales, a sacrificial lamb. Most scholars read the work as an allegory. For centuries the accepted view was religious. The burning city stood for sin, the lamb for deliverance. A new book offers a more palatable explanation for modern readers, and a more surprising one. The author argues that the painter was attacking the materialism of his own town. The merchants who paid for the picture, she suggests, had a penchant for display, and the artist mocked them with details that only a careful eye would catch. Some critics find this outlandish. It is almost inconceivable, they say, that a painter would insult his own patrons. The author replies that such criticism is parochial. Artists of the period often worked in emulation of Italian models, where hidden satire was common. Her book is strongest when it describes the picture itself, its somber colors and its strange mysticism. She has little time for a merely utilitarian view of art, and her enthusiasm is hard to resist.","ja":"モーベンの大聖堂に掛かる大きな絵は、500年にわたって訪問者を戸惑わせてきた。中央では、背後で都市が燃える中、一人の書記が座って書いている。彼の周りには神話の世界の姿がある。翼のある馬、天秤を持つ女性、いけにえの子羊。ほとんどの学者はこの作品を寓意として読む。何世紀もの間、受け入れられてきた見方は宗教的なものだった。燃える都市は罪を、子羊は救いを表すというのだ。新しい本は、現代の読者にとってより受け入れやすく、より意外な説明を提示している。著者は、画家が自分の町の物質主義を攻撃していたのだと論じる。絵の代金を払った商人たちは見せびらかすことを好んでおり、画家は注意深い目にしかとらえられない細部で彼らをあざけったのだと彼女は示唆する。これを奇抜だと感じる批評家もいる。画家が自分の後援者を侮辱するなどほとんどありえないと彼らは言う。著者は、そのような批判は視野が狭いと応じる。当時の芸術家はしばしば、隠れた風刺がありふれていたイタリアの手本を見習って制作していたのだ。彼女の本は、絵そのもの、そのくすんだ色彩と奇妙な神秘主義を描写するときに最も優れている。彼女は芸術を単に実用的に見る立場にはほとんど関心がなく、その熱意には抗いがたいものがある。","words":[["scribe","freq:scribe"],["mythological","freq:mythological"],["sacrificial","freq:sacrificial"],["allegory","freq:allegory"],["deliverance","freq:deliverance"],["palatable","freq:palatable"],["materialism","freq:materialism"],["penchant","freq:penchant"],["outlandish","freq:outlandish"],["inconceivable","freq:inconceivable"],["parochial","freq:parochial"],["emulation","freq:emulation"],["somber","freq:somber"],["mysticism","freq:mysticism"],["utilitarian","freq:utilitarian"]],"qs":[{"q":"What new interpretation does the book offer?","o":["The painting records a real fire.","The painter was criticizing the town's love of wealth.","The scribe is a portrait of a bishop.","The picture was painted in Italy."],"a":1,"why":"the painter was attacking the materialism of his own town とある。"},{"q":"How does the author answer the objection that painters would not insult their patrons?","o":["She says the patrons could not see well.","She says the painter was never paid.","She admits the objection is correct.","She points to Italian models where hidden satire was common."],"a":3,"why":"worked in emulation of Italian models, where hidden satire was common とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0166","page":"freq","group":"2301〜2400","kind":"老女の回想","text":"My grandmother Ilse was the most resourceful person I have known. She was a dainty woman, small and nimble, who could climb a ladder at eighty. People who met her briefly thought her gentle. They did not know her. She was feisty, and she was never averse to an argument. As a girl she had been unruly, the rascal of her village, and she carried something of that into old age. She had a hunch about people that was rarely wrong. A salesman who once tried to cheat her left the house without his samples. Her life had not been easy. Her first husband was unfaithful, and she spoke of him only once, without bitterness. She thought the men who had ruined her country depraved, and she could be callous about their fate. With everyone else, she showed tact. She belonged to a fraternal society that visited the sick, and she kept up the habit until the last year of her life. Her patience with us children was commendable. It must have been gratifying, in the end, to see all nine grandchildren at her table. She pretended to complain about the noise.","ja":"私の祖母イルゼは、私が知る中で最も機転の利く人だった。彼女はきゃしゃな女性で、小柄で機敏であり、80歳ではしごに登ることができた。短い間だけ会った人々は彼女を穏やかだと思った。彼らは彼女を知らなかったのだ。彼女は負けん気が強く、議論を嫌うことは決してなかった。少女の頃は手に負えない、村のいたずら者で、そのいくらかを老年まで持ち続けた。彼女には人についての直感があり、めったに外れなかった。かつて彼女をだまそうとした販売員は、見本を置いたまま家を出て行った。彼女の人生は楽ではなかった。最初の夫は不貞を働き、彼女は彼のことを一度だけ、恨みを交えずに話した。自分の国を破滅させた男たちを彼女は邪悪だと考え、彼らの運命については冷淡になることがあった。ほかの誰に対しても、彼女は如才なかった。病人を見舞う友愛団体に属し、人生の最後の年までその習慣を続けた。私たち子どもに対する彼女の忍耐は称賛に値した。最後に9人の孫全員が食卓にそろったのを見るのは、うれしいことだったに違いない。彼女は騒がしさに文句を言うふりをした。","words":[["resourceful","freq:resourceful"],["dainty","freq:dainty"],["nimble","freq:nimble"],["feisty","freq:feisty"],["averse","freq:averse"],["unruly","freq:unruly"],["rascal","freq:rascal"],["hunch","freq:hunch"],["unfaithful","freq:unfaithful"],["depraved","freq:depraved"],["callous","freq:callous"],["tact","freq:tact"],["fraternal","freq:fraternal"],["commendable","freq:commendable"],["gratifying","freq:gratifying"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did people who met Ilse only briefly misunderstand?","o":["They thought she was gentle, but she was spirited.","They thought she was wealthy.","They thought she was unfriendly.","They thought she was ill."],"a":0,"why":"thought her gentle. They did not know her. She was feisty とある。"},{"q":"What did she continue to do until the last year of her life?","o":["Climb ladders","Argue with salesmen","Visit sick people","Travel abroad"],"a":2,"why":"a fraternal society that visited the sick, and she kept up the habit until the last year of her life とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0167","page":"freq","group":"2301〜2400","kind":"国境の町にて","text":"We crossed into the outlying province at noon, under a scorching sun. A single sentry stood at the post, and he waved us through without looking up. The road beyond was strewn with stones. For an hour we saw nobody. Then, in a lull between two hills, we came upon a village. It was a ghastly sight. Half the houses had no roofs, and a group of children stood in a huddle beside a dry well. The people here are destitute. Before the war, this was farming country, with a large acreage of wheat. The industrialization promised by the government never arrived, and the fields are now an impenetrable mass of thorn. The villagers survive on a meager ration brought by truck twice a month. An old man filled a cup to the brim with water from his own jar and offered it to us. I could not refuse. The specter of hunger is everywhere in this region, yet nobody we met asked for anything. As we left, the light was beginning to ebb. I looked back once. The children had not moved.","ja":"私たちは正午、焼けつくような太陽の下で、辺境の州に入った。検問所には歩哨が一人立っているだけで、彼は顔も上げずに手を振って私たちを通した。その先の道には石が散らばっていた。1時間、誰にも会わなかった。それから、2つの丘の間の静かなくぼ地で、私たちは一つの村に出くわした。それは恐ろしい光景だった。家の半分には屋根がなく、子どもたちの一団が枯れた井戸のそばに身を寄せ合って立っていた。ここの人々は極貧だ。戦争の前、ここは広い面積の小麦畑を持つ農業地帯だった。政府が約束した工業化はついに実現せず、畑は今では通り抜けられないイバラの茂みになっている。村人たちは、月に2度トラックで運ばれてくるわずかな配給で生き延びている。一人の老人が、自分のかめの水をカップの縁までいっぱいに注ぎ、私たちに差し出した。私は断ることができなかった。この地域では飢えの影が至るところにあるが、私たちが出会った人は誰も何も求めなかった。私たちが去るとき、光は衰え始めていた。私は一度だけ振り返った。子どもたちは動いていなかった。","words":[["outlying","freq:outlying"],["scorching","freq:scorching"],["sentry","freq:sentry"],["strewn","freq:strewn"],["lull","freq:lull"],["ghastly","freq:ghastly"],["huddle","freq:huddle"],["destitute","freq:destitute"],["acreage","freq:acreage"],["industrialization","freq:industrialization"],["impenetrable","freq:impenetrable"],["meager","freq:meager"],["brim","freq:brim"],["specter","freq:specter"],["ebb","freq:ebb"]],"qs":[{"q":"What has happened to the village's fields?","o":["They are covered with thorn bushes.","They were sold to the government.","They are flooded.","They have been turned into factories."],"a":0,"why":"the fields are now an impenetrable mass of thorn とある。"},{"q":"What did the old man do?","o":["He asked the travelers for food.","He offered them a full cup of his own water.","He showed them the way out.","He told them about the war."],"a":1,"why":"filled a cup to the brim with water from his own jar and offered it to us とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0168","page":"freq","group":"2301〜2400","kind":"牛乳工場の一日","text":"At five each morning, the first trucks arrive at the Dunmere dairy, and the machines begin to churn. The dairy has made butter in the same way since 1921. Its manager, a quiet woman named Petra Voss, has worked there for thirty years, and the routine is ingrained in her. She walks the floor at six, checking each vat. A thin stream of cream may dribble from a loose pipe, and she will tighten it without breaking her stride. Voss has seen many changes. When a large company bought the dairy in 2005, the staff feared that it would be closed. Instead, the new owners chose to assimilate its methods into their other factories. One proposal she did find objectionable: to add flavoring to the butter. She refused, and the idea was dropped. A local paper once described the alliance between a small dairy and a food giant as unholy. Voss laughs at that. The company unwittingly saved a craft that it had meant to replace, she says, and henceforth nobody will touch the recipe. At noon she takes her lunch outside and sits on the wall to munch an apple. From the yard, the road runs down to the village and thence to the sea.","ja":"毎朝5時、最初のトラックがダンミアの乳製品工場に到着し、機械がかき回し始める。この工場は1921年以来、同じ方法でバターを作ってきた。工場長のペトラ・フォスという物静かな女性はそこで30年働いており、日課は彼女に深くしみ込んでいる。彼女は6時に現場を歩き、槽を一つずつ点検する。緩んだ管からクリームが細くしたたることがあれば、彼女は歩みを止めずにそれを締める。フォスは多くの変化を見てきた。2005年に大企業がこの工場を買収したとき、従業員は閉鎖されるのではないかと恐れた。そうはならず、新しい所有者は、この工場の方法を自社のほかの工場に取り入れることを選んだ。彼女が受け入れがたいと感じた提案が一つあった。バターに香料を加えるというものだ。彼女は拒み、その案は取り下げられた。かつて地元の新聞は、小さな乳製品工場と食品の巨大企業の結びつきをよこしまなものと書いた。フォスはそれを笑う。会社は、取って代わるつもりだった技を知らずに救ったのであり、今後は誰もこの製法に手を触れないだろうと彼女は言う。正午、彼女は昼食を外に持ち出し、塀に座ってリンゴをむしゃむしゃ食べる。構内から道は村へ下り、そこから海へと続いている。","words":[["churn","freq:churn"],["ingrained","freq:ingrained"],["dribble","freq:dribble"],["assimilate","freq:assimilate"],["objectionable","freq:objectionable"],["unholy","freq:unholy"],["unwittingly","freq:unwittingly"],["henceforth","freq:henceforth"],["munch","freq:munch"],["thence","freq:thence"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the new owners do after buying the dairy?","o":["They closed it.","They adopted its methods in their other factories.","They moved it to the coast.","They replaced the manager."],"a":1,"why":"chose to assimilate its methods into their other factories とある。"},{"q":"Which proposal did Voss refuse?","o":["To build a new yard","To hire more staff","To stop making butter","To add flavoring to the butter"],"a":3,"why":"to add flavoring to the butter. She refused とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0169","page":"freq","group":"2401〜2500","kind":"暴動への対応","text":"The riots that spread through three northern cities last month were the worst in a generation, and the government's response has been widely criticized. Police were sent to quell the unrest and to apprehend its leaders. More than a thousand people were arrested. At the behest of the interior minister, courts sat through the night, and sentences included the forfeiture of homes rented from the state. Community leaders say that such measures will only alienate young people further. They describe districts where families live in abject poverty and where the cost of travel to the nearest job center is prohibitive. A report by a group of bishops calls the conditions inexcusable. Ministers reject this. They blame a culture of individualism and a failure of socialization in schools, and they accuse critics of trying to disseminate excuses for crime. The claim that officers used live ammunition has been shown to be spurious. Yet the police themselves admit mistakes. Their tactics were too confrontational, one commander said, and the force had become insular, out of touch with the streets it patrols. He also warned against demanding uniformity. Each city, he said, had rioted for its own reasons.","ja":"先月、北部の3つの都市に広がった暴動は、この一世代で最悪のものであり、政府の対応は広く批判されている。警察は騒乱を鎮圧し、その首謀者を逮捕するために送り込まれた。1,000人を超える人々が逮捕された。内務大臣の命令で裁判所は夜通し開かれ、判決には国から借りた住宅の没収も含まれていた。地域の指導者たちは、そのような措置は若者をさらに遠ざけるだけだと言う。彼らは、家族が絶望的な貧困の中で暮らし、最寄りの職業紹介所までの交通費が法外に高い地区の様子を語る。司教たちの集まりによる報告書は、その状況を許しがたいと呼んでいる。閣僚たちはこれを退ける。彼らは個人主義の文化と学校における社会化の失敗のせいだと言い、批判者たちは犯罪の言い訳を広めようとしていると非難する。警官が実弾を使ったという主張は、偽りであることが示された。それでも警察自身が誤りを認めている。戦術が対決的すぎたと、ある指揮官は述べ、警察は内向きになり、巡回する街の実情から離れてしまっていたと語った。彼はまた、画一性を求めることを戒めた。どの都市も、それぞれの理由で暴動を起こしたのだと彼は言った。","words":[["quell","freq:quell"],["apprehend","freq:apprehend"],["behest","freq:behest"],["forfeiture","freq:forfeiture"],["alienate","freq:alienate"],["abject","freq:abject"],["prohibitive","freq:prohibitive"],["inexcusable","freq:inexcusable"],["individualism","freq:individualism"],["socialization","freq:socialization"],["disseminate","freq:disseminate"],["spurious","freq:spurious"],["confrontational","freq:confrontational"],["insular","freq:insular"],["uniformity","freq:uniformity"]],"qs":[{"q":"What do community leaders fear the government's measures will do?","o":["Push young people further away","Reduce the cost of travel","End poverty too quickly","Close the courts"],"a":0,"why":"such measures will only alienate young people further とある。"},{"q":"What did the police commander admit?","o":["Officers had used live ammunition.","The force had been too confrontational and out of touch.","Too few people had been arrested.","The riots had the same cause everywhere."],"a":1,"why":"Their tactics were too confrontational とあり、the force had become insular とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0170","page":"freq","group":"2401〜2500","kind":"実業家の評伝","text":"The shipping magnate Constantine Varga was not an easy man to like. He was austere in his habits, methodical in his work, and impatient with flattery. Yet a new biography makes a persuasive case that he was one of the more admirable figures of his age. Varga began with nothing. An enterprising boy from a fishing village, he bought his first boat at nineteen with borrowed money. What set him apart was an unwavering attention to detail and a perceptive eye for talent. He could coax the best work from difficult people, and he had a personal magnetism that surprised those who expected a cold accountant. In a crisis he showed remarkable poise. The author is frank about his failures. A lackluster attempt to enter the airline business cost him a fortune, and many early partners fell by the wayside. Critics called him materialistic. The record suggests otherwise. Varga gave away half his wealth during his lifetime, usually in secret, and refused every official commendation offered to him. Whether his motives were purely altruistic is impossible to know. He left no diary, and he once told a journalist that explanations were for people with nothing to do.","ja":"海運業界の大物コンスタンティン・バルガは、好きになりやすい男ではなかった。習慣は質素で、仕事は几帳面で、お世辞には我慢がならなかった。それでも新しい伝記は、彼がその時代の称賛すべき人物の一人だったことを説得力をもって論じている。バルガは何もないところから始めた。漁村出身の積極的な少年だった彼は、19歳のとき、借りた金で最初の船を買った。彼を際立たせたのは、細部への揺るぎない注意と、才能を見抜く鋭い目だった。彼は気難しい人々をうまく説得して最良の仕事を引き出すことができ、冷たい会計係を予想していた人々を驚かせる、人を引きつける力を持っていた。危機に際しては驚くべき落ち着きを見せた。著者は彼の失敗についても率直だ。航空事業に参入しようとした精彩を欠く試みは彼に巨額の損失をもたらし、初期の共同経営者の多くは途中で脱落した。批判する人々は彼を物質主義的だと呼んだ。記録はそうでないことを示唆している。バルガは生前に財産の半分を、たいていはひそかに寄付し、差し出された公式の表彰をすべて断った。彼の動機が純粋に利他的だったかどうかは知りようがない。彼は日記を残さず、かつて記者に、説明は暇な人のためのものだと語った。","words":[["magnate","freq:magnate"],["austere","freq:austere"],["methodical","freq:methodical"],["flattery","freq:flattery"],["enterprising","freq:enterprising"],["unwavering","freq:unwavering"],["perceptive","freq:perceptive"],["coax","freq:coax"],["magnetism","freq:magnetism"],["poise","freq:poise"],["lackluster","freq:lackluster"],["wayside","freq:wayside"],["materialistic","freq:materialistic"],["commendation","freq:commendation"],["altruistic","freq:altruistic"]],"qs":[{"q":"What qualities set Varga apart?","o":["His love of praise","His close attention to detail and eye for talent","His interest in airlines","His wealthy family"],"a":1,"why":"an unwavering attention to detail and a perceptive eye for talent とある。"},{"q":"What does the record show about his attitude to money?","o":["He hid his wealth abroad.","He spent it all on ships.","He left everything to his partners.","He gave away half of it, usually in secret."],"a":3,"why":"gave away half his wealth during his lifetime, usually in secret とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0171","page":"freq","group":"2401〜2500","kind":"都市の野生動物","text":"Foxes, rats, and gulls have lived in our cities for centuries, and most people regard them as vermin. A new study suggests that we understand them poorly. The fox is the classic urban scavenger. Researchers fitted two hundred animals with small transmitters and found that their behavior changes dynamically with the seasons. In winter, some enter a state close to hibernation, moving little for days. One deviant group had learned to cross a motorway by a footbridge. The study also used dissection of animals killed on the roads to examine what they eat. The results challenge an old axiom of pest control: that a cull reduces numbers. It does not. When foxes are removed, others move in within weeks, and the risk of contagion between groups rises. The authors are careful about their methods. They admit one methodological weakness, since animals near busy roads are easier to catch, which may skew the sample. No technique is infallible. Still, the pattern is consistent, and the one aberration, a district where numbers fell sharply, was explained by building work. The public image of urban wildlife, they conclude, is amorphous and largely wrong. A clear picture emerges only through a kind of distillation of many years' data.","ja":"キツネ、ネズミ、カモメは何世紀も私たちの都市にすんでおり、ほとんどの人はそれらを害獣と見なしている。新しい研究は、私たちがそれらをよく理解していないことを示唆する。キツネは都市の典型的な腐肉あさりの動物だ。研究者たちは200匹に小さな送信機を取り付け、その行動が季節とともに動的に変化することを見いだした。冬には冬眠に近い状態に入り、何日もほとんど動かないものもいる。ある変わり者の集団は、歩道橋を使って高速道路を渡ることを覚えていた。この研究は、道路で死んだ動物の解剖も用いて、彼らが何を食べているかを調べた。結果は、害獣駆除の古い自明の理、つまり間引けば数が減るという考えに疑問を投げかける。そうはならないのだ。キツネが取り除かれると、数週間のうちに別のキツネが入り込み、集団の間の感染の危険が高まる。著者たちは自分たちの方法について慎重だ。交通量の多い道路の近くにいる動物のほうが捕まえやすく、それが標本を偏らせるかもしれないという、方法論上の弱点を一つ認めている。絶対に誤らない手法はない。それでも傾向は一貫しており、唯一の異常、つまり数が急減したある地区は、建設工事で説明がついた。都市の野生動物についての世間の印象は形が定まらず、大部分が間違っていると彼らは結論づける。明確な像は、長年のデータを一種の蒸留のように凝縮して初めて浮かび上がるのだ。","words":[["vermin","freq:vermin"],["scavenger","freq:scavenger"],["dynamically","freq:dynamically"],["hibernation","freq:hibernation"],["deviant","freq:deviant"],["dissection","freq:dissection"],["axiom","freq:axiom"],["cull","freq:cull"],["contagion","freq:contagion"],["methodological","freq:methodological"],["skew","freq:skew"],["infallible","freq:infallible"],["aberration","freq:aberration"],["amorphous","freq:amorphous"],["distillation","freq:distillation"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does the study show about culling foxes?","o":["It reduces their numbers permanently.","It does not work, because other foxes soon move in.","It is the only way to stop disease.","It makes foxes hibernate."],"a":1,"why":"When foxes are removed, others move in within weeks とある。"},{"q":"What weakness in their methods do the authors admit?","o":["Animals near busy roads are easier to catch, which may bias the sample.","The transmitters were too heavy.","The study lasted only one winter.","They did not examine what the animals eat."],"a":0,"why":"animals near busy roads are easier to catch, which may skew the sample とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0172","page":"freq","group":"2401〜2500","kind":"劇団の記録集","text":"For sixty years, the Harbor Players were the best repertory company in the north. A handsome new book, produced to mark the reissue of their recorded performances, tells their story. The editor was asked to curate a selection from thousands of photographs, letters, and programs, and she has chosen well. There is a facsimile of the first poster, printed in 1958, and a riveting account of the night the theater roof collapsed. The company had a certain mystique. Its founder, a stern woman who was the antithesis of the glamorous star, believed that actors should be invisible. She became the archetype of a kind of director that hardly exists today. Not all the judgments here are kind. One scathing review from 1971 is printed in full, and it does not detract from the book's affection for its subject. Some of the detail is arcane, of interest only to specialists. And the editor is a little fanciful when she describes the company, figuratively, as a lighthouse. But theater is an ephemeral art. A performance is gone the moment it ends, and books like this are the only way to preserve it for posterity.","ja":"60年の間、ハーバー・プレイヤーズは北部で最良のレパートリー劇団だった。彼らの公演録音の再発売を記念して作られた立派な新しい本が、その物語を語っている。編者は数千点の写真、手紙、公演プログラムから選んで構成するよう依頼され、うまく選んでいる。1958年に印刷された最初のポスターの複製があり、劇場の屋根が崩れた夜についての、目が離せない記述がある。この劇団にはある種の神秘的な雰囲気があった。創設者は、華やかなスターとは正反対の厳格な女性で、俳優は目立たない存在であるべきだと信じていた。彼女は、今日ではほとんど存在しない種類の演出家の典型となった。ここにある評価のすべてが優しいわけではない。1971年の痛烈な劇評が全文掲載されているが、それはこの本が対象に寄せる愛情を損なってはいない。細部の一部は難解で、専門家にしか興味を持たれないだろう。そして編者が劇団を比喩的に灯台と表現するとき、少し空想的すぎる。しかし演劇ははかない芸術だ。公演は終わった瞬間に消えてしまうのであり、このような本こそが、それを後世のために保存する唯一の方法なのだ。","words":[["repertory","freq:repertory"],["reissue","freq:reissue"],["curate","freq:curate"],["facsimile","freq:facsimile"],["riveting","freq:riveting"],["mystique","freq:mystique"],["antithesis","freq:antithesis"],["archetype","freq:archetype"],["scathing","freq:scathing"],["detract","freq:detract"],["arcane","freq:arcane"],["fanciful","freq:fanciful"],["figuratively","freq:figuratively"],["ephemeral","freq:ephemeral"],["posterity","freq:posterity"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the company's founder believe about actors?","o":["They should become stars.","They should be invisible.","They should write their own plays.","They should avoid reviews."],"a":1,"why":"believed that actors should be invisible とある。"},{"q":"Why does the reviewer think books like this matter?","o":["They make money for theaters.","They replace live performance.","They train new directors.","Theater vanishes when it ends, and such books preserve it."],"a":3,"why":"A performance is gone the moment it ends, and books like this are the only way to preserve it for posterity とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0173","page":"freq","group":"2401〜2500","kind":"大聖堂の修復工事","text":"The minster at Aldbury has stood for nine hundred years, and for the last six it has been covered in scaffolding. The restoration is the largest in its history. Visitors who climb to the roof find a veritable village of workshops. In one, a mason shapes a block of limestone with a chisel, copying a carved face that the weather has worn away. In another, the carpentry team repairs oak beams joined by interlocking wooden pegs, without a single nail. The work is slow and, some would say, monotonous. The masons disagree. There is a tranquility in it, one told me, that he has found nowhere else. Below, the city has changed beyond recognition. Its suburbs sprawl for miles, and traffic fills the streets with an infernal noise. Inside the minster, the air is cool rather than stuffy, and light from the great window seems to shimmer on the placid stone floor. People come here for the sanctity of the place, whatever they believe. A site of human habitation since Roman times, the hill still has a restorative effect. The work will be finished in 2031, if the money lasts.","ja":"オルドベリーの大聖堂は900年間立ち続けており、この6年は足場に覆われている。修復はその歴史上最大のものだ。屋根まで登る訪問者は、まさしく作業場の村を見いだす。ある作業場では、石工が天候にすり減らされた彫刻の顔を写し取りながら、のみで石灰岩の塊を形作っている。別の作業場では、大工仕事の班が、くぎを一本も使わず、かみ合わせ式の木の栓でつながれたオークの梁を修理している。作業は遅く、単調だと言う人もいるだろう。石工たちはそうは思わない。ほかのどこにも見いだせなかった静けさがそこにはあると、一人が私に語った。下では、都市は見違えるほど変わった。郊外は何マイルも無秩序に広がり、交通が通りをひどい騒音で満たしている。大聖堂の中では、空気は息苦しいどころか涼しく、大きな窓からの光が穏やかな石の床の上でちらちら光るように見える。人々は、何を信じていようと、この場所の神聖さを求めてここへ来る。ローマ時代から人が住んできたこの丘には、今も元気を回復させる効果がある。資金が続けば、工事は2031年に終わる。","words":[["minster","freq:minster"],["veritable","freq:veritable"],["chisel","freq:chisel"],["carpentry","freq:carpentry"],["interlocking","freq:interlocking"],["monotonous","freq:monotonous"],["tranquility","freq:tranquility"],["sprawl","freq:sprawl"],["infernal","freq:infernal"],["stuffy","freq:stuffy"],["shimmer","freq:shimmer"],["placid","freq:placid"],["sanctity","freq:sanctity"],["habitation","freq:habitation"],["restorative","freq:restorative"]],"qs":[{"q":"How are the oak beams joined?","o":["With iron nails","With modern glue","With interlocking wooden pegs","With rope"],"a":2,"why":"oak beams joined by interlocking wooden pegs, without a single nail とある。"},{"q":"How do the masons feel about their slow work?","o":["They find a calm in it that they find nowhere else.","They find it boring.","They want to finish quickly.","They think it is badly paid."],"a":0,"why":"There is a tranquility in it ... that he has found nowhere else とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0174","page":"freq","group":"2401〜2500","kind":"寄宿学校の思い出","text":"I was sent to boarding school at eleven, and I remember it chiefly as a place of noise. Mornings began with the honk of the caretaker's old van outside the dormitory. We dressed in gray overalls for workshop, and anyone who tried to snooze was pulled out of bed by his feet. I was not an athletic child. The school valued athleticism above everything, and my attempts at running gave me nothing but a blister on each heel. I would daydream through the afternoons and wait for the extracurricular clubs, where I could draw. My best friend, Tomas, was my opposite: exuberant, loud, and good at everything. He ate with gusto, and he would pounce on any crumb left on my plate. On Sundays we were given a succulent roast, the one good meal of the week. I was elated when term ended, and miserable when it began. Tomas had a sister who visited once, with a manicure and a suitor in a sports car, and for a month the whole dormitory talked of nothing else. I have not seen him for forty years. I still think of him when I hear a van.","ja":"私は11歳で寄宿学校に送られ、そこを主に騒音の場所として覚えている。朝は、寄宿舎の外で鳴る管理人の古いバンの警笛で始まった。私たちは工作の時間のために灰色の作業服を着た。うたた寝しようとする者は誰でも、足をつかまれてベッドから引きずり出された。私は運動の得意な子ではなかった。学校は何よりも運動能力を重んじており、私が走ってみても、両方のかかとに水ぶくれができただけだった。私は午後の間じゅう空想にふけり、絵を描くことのできる課外のクラブを待った。親友のトマスは私と正反対だった。元気いっぱいで、声が大きく、何でも得意だった。彼は心から楽しんで食べ、私の皿に残ったどんなパンくずにも飛びついた。日曜には汁気たっぷりの焼いた肉が出た。1週間でただ一度のまともな食事だった。学期が終わると私は大喜びし、始まるとみじめになった。トマスには姉がいて、一度だけ訪ねてきた。爪を手入れし、スポーツカーに乗った求婚者を連れていて、1か月の間、寄宿舎中がその話しかしなかった。彼とは40年会っていない。今でもバンの音を聞くと、彼のことを思い出す。","words":[["honk","freq:honk"],["overalls","freq:overalls"],["snooze","freq:snooze"],["athleticism","freq:athleticism"],["blister","freq:blister"],["daydream","freq:daydream"],["extracurricular","freq:extracurricular"],["exuberant","freq:exuberant"],["gusto","freq:gusto"],["pounce","freq:pounce"],["crumb","freq:crumb"],["succulent","freq:succulent"],["elated","freq:elated"],["manicure","freq:manicure"],["suitor","freq:suitor"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the writer look forward to at school?","o":["Running races","The clubs where he could draw","Workshop lessons","Mornings in the dormitory"],"a":1,"why":"wait for the extracurricular clubs, where I could draw とある。"},{"q":"How was Tomas different from the writer?","o":["He was quiet and shy.","He disliked food.","He was lively and good at everything.","He hated sports."],"a":2,"why":"my opposite: exuberant, loud, and good at everything とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0175","page":"freq","group":"2401〜2500","kind":"老船長の話","text":"The old mariner who keeps the lighthouse at Cape Farran has been at sea, one way or another, for sixty years. I asked him what he had learned. He was silent for a while, and then began to swivel his chair toward the window, as if the answer might materialize out there. Most of what matters, he said at last, is instinctive. You feel a change in the wind before the instruments show it. Too much equipment is a hindrance; half the screens on a modern bridge are superfluous. He told me about a night in 1974 when his ship broke cleanly in two on a reef. The sound, he said, was jarring, like a door slammed in an empty house. Eleven men got into the boats. Inexplicably, the sea went calm within the hour, and all of them lived. He does not call it a miracle. He finds the idea that the sea cares about anyone abhorrent, almost an insult to the dead. \"It was weather,\" he said. \"Only weather.\" Then he poured the tea, and we watched a tanker cross the horizon without another word.","ja":"ファラン岬の灯台を守る老いた船乗りは、何らかの形で60年間、海に関わってきた。私は彼に、何を学んだかを尋ねた。彼はしばらく黙り、それから、答えがそこに現れるかもしれないとでもいうように、椅子を窓のほうへ回し始めた。大切なことの大半は本能的なものだと、彼はついに言った。計器が示す前に風の変化を感じるのだ。機器が多すぎるのは妨げになる。現代の船橋にある画面の半分は余分だ。彼は、1974年に自分の船が岩礁の上できれいに真っ二つに折れた夜のことを話してくれた。その音は、誰もいない家でドアがばたんと閉まるような、耳障りなものだったと彼は言った。11人が救命艇に乗り込んだ。不可解なことに、1時間のうちに海は穏やかになり、全員が生き延びた。彼はそれを奇跡とは呼ばない。海が誰かのことを気にかけるという考えを、彼は忌まわしく、死者への侮辱に近いと感じている。「あれは天気だった」と彼は言った。「ただの天気だ」。それから彼はお茶を注ぎ、私たちはそれ以上一言も交わさずに、タンカーが水平線を横切るのを眺めた。","words":[["mariner","freq:mariner"],["swivel","freq:swivel"],["materialize","freq:materialize"],["instinctive","freq:instinctive"],["hindrance","freq:hindrance"],["superfluous","freq:superfluous"],["cleanly","freq:cleanly"],["jarring","freq:jarring"],["Inexplicably","freq:inexplicably"],["abhorrent","freq:abhorrent"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does the old man think about modern equipment?","o":["It is essential for safety.","Much of it is unnecessary and gets in the way.","It should replace lighthouses.","It never fails."],"a":1,"why":"Too much equipment is a hindrance; half the screens on a modern bridge are superfluous とある。"},{"q":"Why does he refuse to call the calm sea a miracle?","o":["He was not on the ship.","Several men died.","He does not remember the night.","He dislikes the idea that the sea cares about anyone."],"a":3,"why":"He finds the idea that the sea cares about anyone abhorrent とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0176","page":"freq","group":"2501〜2600","kind":"軍法会議の再審査","text":"A scandal that threatened to topple the defense minister has ended, unexpectedly, in his favor. The affair began with the trial of a young lieutenant for desertion. He was convicted and given five years. His family claimed that military police had tried to coerce a confession, and that they had gone so far as to fabricate a statement in his name. An inquiry was ordered. Investigators were allowed to interrogate every officer involved, and two sergeants agreed to corroborate the family's account. Their evidence seemed to implicate the minister himself, who had signed an order with an unusual stipulation: that the trial be held in secret. Newspapers said this was tantamount to an admission of guilt. The inquiry's report, published yesterday, takes a different view. It finds that the minister acted on false advice and that he moved to rescind the order as soon as he learned the truth. A general has received a formal reprimand. The lieutenant has been freed, and his family calls the result a vindication. Opposition deputies remain indignant. Delay, they argue, served only to exacerbate the injustice, and the affair will tarnish the army's name for years.","ja":"国防大臣を失脚させかねなかった不祥事は、予想外にも、彼に有利な形で終わった。事の始まりは、ある若い中尉が脱走の罪で裁かれたことだった。彼は有罪となり、5年の刑を受けた。彼の家族は、憲兵が自白を強要しようとし、彼の名前で供述書をでっち上げることまでしたと主張した。調査が命じられた。調査官は関わったすべての将校を尋問することを許され、2人の軍曹が家族の説明を裏づけることに同意した。彼らの証言は、大臣本人の関与を示すように見えた。大臣は、裁判を非公開で行うという異例の条件の付いた命令に署名していたのだ。新聞は、これは罪を認めたに等しいと書いた。昨日公表された調査報告書は、異なる見方をしている。大臣は誤った助言に基づいて行動したのであり、真実を知るとすぐに命令を取り消す措置を取ったと認定している。ある将軍が正式な叱責を受けた。中尉は釈放され、家族はこの結果を正しさが証明されたものと呼んでいる。野党議員たちは憤慨したままだ。遅れは不正を悪化させただけであり、この事件は何年も軍の名を傷つけるだろうと彼らは主張している。","words":[["topple","freq:topple"],["desertion","freq:desertion"],["coerce","freq:coerce"],["fabricate","freq:fabricate"],["interrogate","freq:interrogate"],["corroborate","freq:corroborate"],["implicate","freq:implicate"],["stipulation","freq:stipulation"],["tantamount","freq:tantamount"],["rescind","freq:rescind"],["reprimand","freq:reprimand"],["vindication","freq:vindication"],["indignant","freq:indignant"],["exacerbate","freq:exacerbate"],["tarnish","freq:tarnish"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the minister seem to be involved?","o":["He had ordered that the trial be held in secret.","He had written the false statement.","He had refused to answer questions.","He was related to the lieutenant."],"a":0,"why":"signed an order with an unusual stipulation: that the trial be held in secret とある。"},{"q":"What did the inquiry conclude about the minister?","o":["He should resign at once.","He had invented the evidence.","He was misled and withdrew the order when he learned the truth.","He had bribed the sergeants."],"a":2,"why":"the minister acted on false advice and that he moved to rescind the order as soon as he learned the truth とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0177","page":"freq","group":"2501〜2600","kind":"空き家と人口","text":"In the hill towns of the interior, one house in four is unoccupied. The reason lies in demography. Young people gravitate toward the coast, where the jobs are, and the old remain. Property prices in the hills began to plummet twenty years ago and have never recovered. A program launched in 2019 offers empty houses for a single euro to anyone who will repair them. Its results have been mixed. Buyers discovered that the paperwork was a minefield, and that costs accrue quickly once work begins. Each town appointed an overseer to guide them, but the process still felt impersonal. Some newcomers left within a year. Others stayed, and their experience is instructive. A retired couple from the north turned a crumbling edifice into an opulent guesthouse. A group of artists created a new cultural milieu in a village that had lost its school. Officials say that the facilitation of such projects is now their main task. They are aware of the risks. An influx of outsiders may heighten tensions, and the fluctuation of tourist numbers makes planning difficult. Still, a town with visitors is in a more enviable position than one with none.","ja":"内陸の丘の町では、4軒に1軒が空き家だ。その理由は人口構成にある。若者は仕事のある沿岸部へと引き寄せられ、高齢者が残る。丘の町の不動産価格は20年前に急落し始め、一度も回復していない。2019年に始まったある事業は、修繕する意思のある人なら誰にでも、空き家をたった1ユーロで提供している。その結果は良い面と悪い面が入り混じっている。買い手たちは、書類の手続きが危険だらけで、工事が始まると費用がすぐにかさんでいくことを知った。各町は買い手を案内する監督者を任命したが、それでも手続きは人間味がないように感じられた。1年以内に去った新参者もいる。とどまった人もおり、その経験は参考になる。北部から来た退職した夫婦は、崩れかけた大きな建物を豪華な宿に変えた。芸術家の一団は、学校を失った村に新しい文化的な環境を作り出した。役人たちは、そうした事業を促進することが今や自分たちの主な仕事だと言う。彼らは危険も承知している。よそ者の流入は緊張を高めるかもしれず、観光客数の変動は計画を難しくする。それでも、訪問者のいる町は、誰も来ない町よりうらやましい立場にある。","words":[["unoccupied","freq:unoccupied"],["demography","freq:demography"],["gravitate","freq:gravitate"],["plummet","freq:plummet"],["minefield","freq:minefield"],["accrue","freq:accrue"],["overseer","freq:overseer"],["impersonal","freq:impersonal"],["edifice","freq:edifice"],["opulent","freq:opulent"],["milieu","freq:milieu"],["facilitation","freq:facilitation"],["heighten","freq:heighten"],["fluctuation","freq:fluctuation"],["enviable","freq:enviable"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why are so many houses empty in the hill towns?","o":["They were damaged by earthquakes.","The government bought them.","Tourists refuse to stay there.","Young people have moved to the coast for work."],"a":3,"why":"Young people gravitate toward the coast, where the jobs are, and the old remain とある。"},{"q":"What difficulties did buyers of one-euro houses face?","o":["Complicated paperwork and fast-rising costs","Hostile neighbors","A lack of building materials","High taxes on tourists"],"a":0,"why":"the paperwork was a minefield, and that costs accrue quickly once work begins とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0178","page":"freq","group":"2501〜2600","kind":"科学ニュースの短信","text":"This week's science news ranges from the sea floor to the edge of space. Off the coast of Norway, biologists filming a shoal of herring have shown that the fish are closely attuned to one another, turning within a fiftieth of a second. Their supple bodies, the team says, are almost impervious to the pressure waves that would injure other species. In Germany, engineers at a particle collider report a result that may require physicists to dissect one of their favorite theories. The evidence is still patchy, and a second run is planned. A study of the magpie has found that the black-and-white plumage of some birds is controlled by a recessive gene, which explains why two normal parents can produce a pale chick. Researchers in Japan have built a robot whose method of locomotion is copied from the earthworm. From Kenya comes a report on midwifery: a simple training course in the resuscitation of newborn babies has halved deaths in forty rural clinics. Finally, a weather balloon launched during torrential rain in Brazil reached the stratosphere and returned with samples of dust from the Sahara.","ja":"今週の科学ニュースは、海底から宇宙の縁まで及ぶ。ノルウェー沖でニシンの群れを撮影した生物学者たちは、魚たちが互いにぴったり波長を合わせ、50分の1秒以内に向きを変えることを示した。チームによれば、そのしなやかな体は、ほかの種なら傷つく圧力波をほとんど受けつけない。ドイツでは、粒子衝突型加速器の技師たちが、物理学者にお気に入りの理論の一つを詳しく分析し直すことを求めるかもしれない結果を報告している。証拠はまだ不完全で、2回目の実験が計画されている。カササギについての研究は、一部の鳥の白黒の羽毛が劣性遺伝子に支配されていることを見いだした。それは、普通の両親から淡い色のひなが生まれうる理由を説明する。日本の研究者たちは、移動の方法をミミズからまねたロボットを作った。ケニアからは助産についての報告が届いた。新生児の蘇生についての簡単な研修課程が、40の農村の診療所で死亡を半減させたという。最後に、ブラジルで激しい雨の中に放たれた気象観測気球が成層圏に達し、サハラの砂ぼこりの試料を持ち帰った。","words":[["shoal","freq:shoal"],["attuned","freq:attuned"],["supple","freq:supple"],["impervious","freq:impervious"],["collider","freq:collider"],["dissect","freq:dissect"],["patchy","freq:patchy"],["magpie","freq:magpie"],["plumage","freq:plumage"],["recessive","freq:recessive"],["locomotion","freq:locomotion"],["midwifery","freq:midwifery"],["resuscitation","freq:resuscitation"],["torrential","freq:torrential"],["stratosphere","freq:stratosphere"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the study of magpies explain?","o":["Why they steal shiny objects","Why two normal parents can have a pale chick","Why they migrate in winter","Why their numbers are falling"],"a":1,"why":"controlled by a recessive gene, which explains why two normal parents can produce a pale chick とある。"},{"q":"What was the result of the training course in Kenya?","o":["More clinics were built.","Robots were introduced.","Deaths of newborn babies were cut by half.","Midwives moved to the cities."],"a":2,"why":"has halved deaths in forty rural clinics とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0179","page":"freq","group":"2501〜2600","kind":"朗読会の夜","text":"The poet Hana Sorel gave a recitation at the Guildhall last night, and those who were there will not forget it. Sorel is a virtuoso of the spoken word. She used no microphone and no reverb, only her voice, and the hall was silent from the first line. Her poems are short. She values brevity above everything, and a single allusion may carry the weight of a whole stanza. The language is colloquial, almost plain, yet every word has a second connotation. In one poem, the phrase \"gone to sleep,\" an old euphemism for death, is turned slowly into something terrible. The evening was artistically daring. Behind the poet, a cinematographer had arranged a series of silent images: a road, a hand, an archetypal mother and child. Some listeners found these distracting. I thought them perfectly judged. The climactic moment came near the end, in a long allegorical poem about a river. Sorel read it with such verve that the audience rose before she had finished. Afterward, a famous critic, a connoisseur of modern verse, was heard to say that he had waited thirty years for such a night.","ja":"詩人ハナ・ソレルが昨夜、ギルドホールで朗読を行った。その場にいた人々はそれを忘れないだろう。ソレルは語られる言葉の名手だ。彼女はマイクも残響の効果も使わず、ただ自分の声だけを使い、会場は最初の一行から静まり返った。彼女の詩は短い。彼女は何よりも簡潔さを重んじ、たった一つのほのめかしが一連全体の重みを担うこともある。言葉は口語的で、ほとんど平易だが、どの語にも第二の含みがある。ある詩では、「眠りについた」という死の古い婉曲表現が、ゆっくりと何か恐ろしいものへと変えられていく。この晩は芸術的に大胆だった。詩人の背後に、ある撮影監督が一連の無声の映像を配置していた。道、手、典型的な母と子である。これを気が散ると感じた聞き手もいた。私は完璧な判断だと思った。山場は終わり近く、川についての長い寓意的な詩の中でやって来た。ソレルがあまりに生き生きとそれを読んだので、聴衆は彼女が読み終える前に立ち上がった。その後、現代詩の目利きである有名な批評家が、このような晩を30年待っていたと言うのが聞こえた。","words":[["recitation","freq:recitation"],["virtuoso","freq:virtuoso"],["reverb","freq:reverb"],["brevity","freq:brevity"],["allusion","freq:allusion"],["colloquial","freq:colloquial"],["connotation","freq:connotation"],["euphemism","freq:euphemism"],["artistically","freq:artistically"],["cinematographer","freq:cinematographer"],["archetypal","freq:archetypal"],["climactic","freq:climactic"],["allegorical","freq:allegorical"],["verve","freq:verve"],["connoisseur","freq:connoisseur"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did Sorel perform her poems?","o":["With music and a microphone","With her voice alone","With the help of actors","By reading from a screen"],"a":1,"why":"She used no microphone and no reverb, only her voice とある。"},{"q":"What happened during the long poem near the end?","o":["The images failed.","Some listeners left.","The poet forgot her lines.","The audience stood up before she had finished."],"a":3,"why":"the audience rose before she had finished とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0180","page":"freq","group":"2501〜2600","kind":"数学者の横顔","text":"The mathematician Jonas Ek, who died last month at ninety, was famous for two things: his proofs and his eccentricity. He was a precocious child who taught himself algebra at six. As an adult he became a recluse, living alone in a wooden house with eleven clocks, none of which showed the right time. His habits were idiosyncratic. He wrote only in pencil, would detest any form of ceremony, and once walked out of a prize dinner held in his honor. Yet he was not unkind. Students who visited found him docile and attentive, with a wry sense of humor. He would confide small worries about his garden, and never talked about his own fame. His preoccupation was a single problem in number theory, to which he gave forty years of painstaking work. When he finally solved it, friends expected him to be euphoric. He merely said that he would miss it. Ek did not profess any philosophy of life. But there was real altruism in the way he gave his time to young researchers, and his rare moments of spontaneity are remembered with affection. One devotee has kept every letter he wrote.","ja":"先月90歳で亡くなった数学者ヨナス・エクは、2つのことで有名だった。証明と、風変わりさである。彼は6歳で独学で代数を身につけた早熟な子どもだった。大人になると世捨て人になり、11個の時計のある木造の家に一人で住んだ。どの時計も正しい時刻を示していなかった。彼の習慣は独特だった。鉛筆でしか書かず、どんな形の儀式もひどく嫌い、自分をたたえて開かれた受賞記念の晩餐会から歩いて出て行ったこともある。それでも彼は不親切ではなかった。訪ねてきた学生たちは、彼がおとなしく、よく話を聞き、皮肉っぽいユーモアの感覚を持っていることを知った。彼は庭についての小さな心配事を打ち明け、自分の名声については決して語らなかった。彼が没頭していたのは数論のただ一つの問題で、それに40年の入念な研究をささげた。ついにそれを解いたとき、友人たちは彼が有頂天になると予想した。彼はただ、あの問題がなくなって寂しくなると言っただけだった。エクは人生哲学といったものを公言しなかった。しかし、若い研究者たちに自分の時間を与える彼のやり方には本物の思いやりがあり、めったにない自然な振る舞いの瞬間は愛情をもって記憶されている。ある熱心な信奉者は、彼が書いた手紙をすべて取ってある。","words":[["eccentricity","freq:eccentricity"],["precocious","freq:precocious"],["recluse","freq:recluse"],["idiosyncratic","freq:idiosyncratic"],["detest","freq:detest"],["docile","freq:docile"],["wry","freq:wry"],["confide","freq:confide"],["preoccupation","freq:preoccupation"],["painstaking","freq:painstaking"],["euphoric","freq:euphoric"],["profess","freq:profess"],["altruism","freq:altruism"],["spontaneity","freq:spontaneity"],["devotee","freq:devotee"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did Ek react when he solved his great problem?","o":["He celebrated with his students.","He simply said he would miss it.","He refused to publish the proof.","He walked out of a dinner."],"a":1,"why":"He merely said that he would miss it とある。"},{"q":"What did visiting students find?","o":["A gentle, attentive man with a dry sense of humor","A proud man who spoke of his fame","A man who refused to see them","A man who disliked gardens"],"a":0,"why":"found him docile and attentive, with a wry sense of humor とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0181","page":"freq","group":"2501〜2600","kind":"幽霊屋敷の調査","text":"The old inn at Marrow Cross has a macabre reputation. In 1887 a traveler was found dead in the lavatory, and ever since, guests have reported cold drafts and a claustrophobic feeling on the stairs. The present owners, a homely couple with no interest in the supernatural, finally asked for help. Three experts came. The first was a priest, a retired exorcist, who said that an exorcism was unnecessary and that the house needed only prayer. The second practiced divination with cards and spoke darkly of ancient depravity. The third was an engineer. He was a strapping young man with a toolbox, and he spent a day in the cellar. His report was short. The telltale drafts came from a blocked chimney. The feeling on the stairs was caused by a low sound, too deep to hear, produced by a loose pipe. As for the hapless traveler, parish records showed that he had died of a heart attack. Locals were disappointed. The inn had been treated with a kind of veneration, and people used to congregate there on winter nights to tell stories. One newspaper replied with innuendo about the engineer's motives. He answered with some ferocity that facts need no motive.","ja":"マロー・クロスの古い宿には、気味の悪い評判がある。1887年に一人の旅人が便所で死んでいるのが見つかり、それ以来、宿泊客は冷たいすきま風と、階段での息が詰まるような感じを訴えてきた。現在の所有者は超自然的なものに関心のない素朴な夫婦で、ついに助けを求めた。3人の専門家がやって来た。一人目は司祭で、引退した悪魔払いの祈祷師だった。彼は、悪魔払いは必要なく、この家に必要なのは祈りだけだと言った。二人目はカードで占いを行い、太古の邪悪さについて陰うつに語った。三人目は技師だった。道具箱を持った体格のよい若者で、彼は地下室で一日を過ごした。彼の報告は短かった。正体を明かすすきま風は、詰まった煙突から来ていた。階段での感覚は、緩んだ管が出す、低すぎて聞こえない音が原因だった。不運な旅人については、教区の記録が、彼が心臓発作で死んだことを示していた。地元の人々はがっかりした。この宿は一種の崇拝をもって扱われており、人々は冬の夜にそこに集まって物語を語り合ったものだった。ある新聞は、技師の動機について当てこすりで応じた。彼は、事実に動機は要らないと、かなり激しく言い返した。","words":[["macabre","freq:macabre"],["lavatory","freq:lavatory"],["claustrophobic","freq:claustrophobic"],["homely","freq:homely"],["exorcist","freq:exorcist"],["exorcism","freq:exorcism"],["divination","freq:divination"],["depravity","freq:depravity"],["strapping","freq:strapping"],["telltale","freq:telltale"],["hapless","freq:hapless"],["veneration","freq:veneration"],["congregate","freq:congregate"],["innuendo","freq:innuendo"],["ferocity","freq:ferocity"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the engineer find was causing the cold drafts?","o":["A broken window","A blocked chimney","A hidden room","An underground stream"],"a":1,"why":"The telltale drafts came from a blocked chimney とある。"},{"q":"Why were local people disappointed?","o":["The inn was going to close.","The priest refused to pray.","The engineer charged too much.","They had enjoyed the inn's ghostly reputation."],"a":3,"why":"The inn had been treated with a kind of veneration, and people used to congregate there ... to tell stories とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0182","page":"freq","group":"2501〜2600","kind":"紳士クラブの一夜","text":"The Carlton Club has stood in the same street since 1840, sandwiched between a bank and a tailor. Its rules are few. Members may play billiards until midnight, and by tacit agreement nobody discusses business. On most evenings the place is uneventful. Last Thursday was an exception. The club's records, arranged chronologically on a shelf in the library, show nothing like it. At nine, a stranger arrived and gave the name of a member who had died in March. His impersonation was good, down to the walk. The porter, his nerves already taut after a long day, let him in. It was a small matter, a mere trifle, that gave him away. He asked for ice in his whisky. The real member had detested ice. What followed was closer to a romp than a crime. The man ran through the dining room, upset a tray, and escaped by the kitchen. Nothing was stolen. It is paradoxical, the secretary remarked afterward, that a club founded to keep the world out should be so pleased to have had a visitor. The members have talked of little else since.","ja":"カールトン・クラブは1840年以来、銀行と仕立て屋に挟まれて同じ通りに建っている。その規則は少ない。会員は真夜中までビリヤードをすることができ、暗黙の了解で、誰も仕事の話をしない。たいていの晩、ここでは何事も起こらない。先週の木曜は例外だった。図書室の棚に年代順に並べられたクラブの記録には、それに似たものは何もない。9時に見知らぬ男がやって来て、3月に亡くなった会員の名を名乗った。彼のなりすましは、歩き方に至るまで見事だった。長い一日の後ですでに神経が張り詰めていた門番は、彼を中に入れた。彼の正体を暴いたのは小さなこと、ほんのささいなことだった。彼はウイスキーに氷を頼んだのだ。本物の会員は氷をひどく嫌っていた。その後に続いたのは、犯罪というより大はしゃぎに近かった。男は食堂を駆け抜け、盆をひっくり返し、台所から逃げた。盗まれたものは何もなかった。世間を締め出すために作られたクラブが、訪問者があったことをこれほど喜ぶとは逆説的だと、後で事務長は語った。それ以来、会員たちはほとんどその話しかしていない。","words":[["sandwiched","freq:sandwiched"],["billiards","freq:billiards"],["tacit","freq:tacit"],["uneventful","freq:uneventful"],["chronologically","freq:chronologically"],["impersonation","freq:impersonation"],["taut","freq:taut"],["trifle","freq:trifle"],["romp","freq:romp"],["paradoxical","freq:paradoxical"]],"qs":[{"q":"What gave the stranger away?","o":["His way of walking","His clothes","His request for ice in his whisky","His knowledge of billiards"],"a":2,"why":"He asked for ice in his whisky. The real member had detested ice とある。"},{"q":"What did the secretary find paradoxical?","o":["That a club meant to keep the world out enjoyed having a visitor","That nothing was stolen","That the porter was tired","That the records were in order"],"a":0,"why":"a club founded to keep the world out should be so pleased to have had a visitor とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0183","page":"freq","group":"2601〜2700","kind":"少数民族と和解委員会","text":"For fifty years, successive governments of Halden chose to persecute the Marsh people. Their villages were burned, their language was forbidden, and thousands were sent into banishment in the eastern desert. Some communities were all but obliterated. Last year, the new government took a different course. It set up a commission whose aim is conciliation rather than punishment. The law creating it is careful. It does stipulate that no one can be summarily dismissed from public office on the basis of an accusation alone; every claim must be substantiated by documents or witnesses. Commissioners scrutinize each case in public. Critics on both sides are unhappy. Some survivors feel that the government is trying to placate them with words, and they note that the vandal who destroyed a Marsh cemetery last spring has still not been charged. Former officials complain that their reputations have been irrevocably damaged. The chairwoman, a judge whose credential as a neutral figure nobody disputes, strikes a conciliatory tone. Certain losses, she admits, are irrevocable. The dead cannot be returned. But she hopes that the hearings will culminate in a shared account of what happened, written down for the first time.","ja":"50年にわたり、ハルデンの歴代政府はマーシュの人々を迫害することを選んできた。彼らの村は焼かれ、言語は禁じられ、数千人が東部の砂漠へ追放された。ほとんど消し去られた共同体もある。昨年、新政府は別の道を取った。処罰ではなく和解を目的とする委員会を設置したのだ。それを創設した法律は慎重だ。告発だけを根拠に誰かを即座に公職から解任することはできず、どの主張も書類か証人によって裏づけられなければならないと明記している。委員たちは一つひとつの事例を公開の場で綿密に調べる。双方の批判者が不満を抱いている。生存者の一部は、政府が言葉で自分たちをなだめようとしていると感じており、昨年の春にマーシュの墓地を破壊した者がいまだに起訴されていないことを指摘する。元役人たちは、自分たちの評判が取り返しがつかないほど傷つけられたと不満を述べる。中立的な人物としての資格を誰も疑わない裁判官である委員長は、融和的な調子で語る。取り返しのつかない損失があることを彼女は認める。死者は戻らない。しかし彼女は、聴聞会が最終的に、何が起きたかについての共有された記録に行き着き、それが初めて書き留められることを望んでいる。","words":[["persecute","freq:persecute"],["banishment","freq:banishment"],["obliterated","freq:obliterate"],["conciliation","freq:conciliation"],["stipulate","freq:stipulate"],["summarily","freq:summarily"],["substantiated","freq:substantiate"],["scrutinize","freq:scrutinize"],["placate","freq:placate"],["vandal","freq:vandal"],["irrevocably","freq:irrevocably"],["credential","freq:credential"],["conciliatory","freq:conciliatory"],["irrevocable","freq:irrevocable"],["culminate","freq:culminate"]],"qs":[{"q":"What is the aim of the new commission?","o":["To reach reconciliation rather than to punish","To send officials into exile","To rebuild the villages","To translate the Marsh language"],"a":0,"why":"a commission whose aim is conciliation rather than punishment とある。"},{"q":"What safeguard does the law contain?","o":["Hearings must be secret.","No one can be removed from office on an accusation alone.","Survivors may not speak.","Only judges may be accused."],"a":1,"why":"no one can be summarily dismissed from public office on the basis of an accusation alone とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0184","page":"freq","group":"2601〜2700","kind":"家計と借金","text":"A report on household debt published this week makes uncomfortable reading. One family in three now relies on an overdraft to reach the end of the month. The common picture of the debtor as someone who chose to squander money on extravagance is, the authors say, simply wrong. Most borrowers hold menial jobs with wages that are not commensurate with the cost of living. Many live in suburbia, far from work, and must allot a fifth of their income to transport. The report is critical of banks. It describes the opacity of their charges and calls easy credit an enabler of long-term hardship. Lending rules, it says, are applied in a haphazard way. But it is equally hard on government advice, which it finds too prescriptive: leaflets tell people to save without explaining how. Among the young, the authors detected a growing disillusionment. Confidence in ever owning a home has begun to wane, and attempts to revive the flagging housing market have had little effect. Nothing, the report concludes, will dampen demand for credit while pay stands still. Its main proposal is modest: a legal limit on fees for small loans.","ja":"今週公表された家計の負債についての報告書は、読んで心地のよいものではない。今では3世帯に1世帯が、月末まで持ちこたえるために当座貸越に頼っている。ぜいたくに金を浪費することを選んだ人という、債務者についてのありふれた印象は、まったくの誤りだと著者たちは言う。借り手のほとんどは、生活費に見合わない賃金の単純な仕事に就いている。多くは職場から遠い郊外に住み、収入の5分の1を交通費に割り当てなければならない。報告書は銀行に批判的だ。手数料のわかりにくさを指摘し、安易な融資を長期的な困窮を助長するものと呼んでいる。貸し付けの規則は行き当たりばったりに適用されているという。しかし政府の助言にも同じくらい厳しく、細かく指図しすぎていると見ている。小冊子は方法を説明せずに貯蓄せよと言うのだ。若者の間に、著者たちは幻滅が広がっているのを見いだした。いつか家を持てるという自信は衰え始めており、弱ってきた住宅市場を立て直そうとする試みはほとんど効果がなかった。賃金が据え置かれている限り、融資への需要をそぐものは何もないと報告書は結論づける。その主な提案は控えめだ。小口融資の手数料に法的な上限を設けることである。","words":[["overdraft","freq:overdraft"],["squander","freq:squander"],["extravagance","freq:extravagance"],["menial","freq:menial"],["commensurate","freq:commensurate"],["suburbia","freq:suburbia"],["allot","freq:allot"],["opacity","freq:opacity"],["enabler","freq:enabler"],["haphazard","freq:haphazard"],["prescriptive","freq:prescriptive"],["disillusionment","freq:disillusionment"],["wane","freq:wane"],["flagging","freq:flagging"],["dampen","freq:dampen"]],"qs":[{"q":"What does the report say about most people in debt?","o":["They wasted their money on luxuries.","They have low-paid jobs and high living costs.","They refuse to save.","They live in city centers."],"a":1,"why":"hold menial jobs with wages that are not commensurate with the cost of living とある。"},{"q":"What is the report's main proposal?","o":["A ban on overdrafts","Free transport for workers","A legal limit on fees for small loans","Higher interest rates"],"a":2,"why":"a legal limit on fees for small loans とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0185","page":"freq","group":"2601〜2700","kind":"高地の牧羊と自然","text":"High on the Corrie hills, a shepherd named Alan Reid has farmed the same land for forty years. His flock is small, and he knows each ewe by sight. The history of the place is the history of domestication. Sheep were brought here three thousand years ago, and the mottled local breed has changed little since. Reid's work follows the seasons. In spring he checks the lambs for the maggot that can kill within days. In summer he plants each seedling of a new hedge by hand. Foxes prowl the lower fields, and a buzzard circles above the ridge, its cold, reptilian eye fixed on the grass. Scientists from the university come to study the hills. One team uses a topographical survey to map a fissure that opened after last year's rains. Another, with telescopic cameras, records which plants the sheep eat. They posit that the animals' selectivity shapes the whole landscape. Reid listens politely. The land, he says, is more malleable than people think, and less. A mountaineer who passed last week asked whether the life was lonely. Reid told him that he had never had time to find out.","ja":"コリー丘陵の高所で、アラン・リードという羊飼いが40年間、同じ土地で牧畜を続けてきた。彼の群れは小さく、彼は雌羊を一頭ずつ見分けられる。この場所の歴史は家畜化の歴史だ。羊は3,000年前にここへ連れて来られ、まだら模様の地元の品種はそれ以来ほとんど変わっていない。リードの仕事は季節に従う。春には、数日で命を奪うことのあるウジ虫がいないか子羊を調べる。夏には、新しい生け垣の苗を一本ずつ手で植える。キツネが下のほうの畑をうろつき、ノスリが尾根の上を旋回して、その冷たい、は虫類のような目を草地に据えている。大学の科学者たちが丘陵を研究しにやって来る。あるチームは地形測量を使って、昨年の雨の後に開いた裂け目の地図を作っている。別のチームは望遠カメラで、羊がどの植物を食べるかを記録している。彼らは、動物のえり好みが風景全体を形作っていると仮定している。リードは礼儀正しく耳を傾ける。土地は人が思うより変えやすく、また変えにくいものだと彼は言う。先週通りかかった登山家が、この暮らしは寂しくないかと尋ねた。リードは、それを確かめる暇が一度もなかったと答えた。","words":[["ewe","freq:ewe"],["domestication","freq:domestication"],["mottled","freq:mottled"],["maggot","freq:maggot"],["seedling","freq:seedling"],["prowl","freq:prowl"],["buzzard","freq:buzzard"],["reptilian","freq:reptilian"],["topographical","freq:topographical"],["fissure","freq:fissure"],["telescopic","freq:telescopic"],["posit","freq:posit"],["selectivity","freq:selectivity"],["malleable","freq:malleable"],["mountaineer","freq:mountaineer"]],"qs":[{"q":"What do the scientists with cameras suggest?","o":["The sheep's choice of plants shapes the landscape.","Foxes are killing the lambs.","The local breed is dying out.","The hedge should be removed."],"a":0,"why":"They posit that the animals' selectivity shapes the whole landscape とある。"},{"q":"How did Reid answer the question about loneliness?","o":["He said the life was very lonely.","He said he preferred the city.","He refused to answer.","He said he had never had time to find out."],"a":3,"why":"he had never had time to find out とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0186","page":"freq","group":"2601〜2700","kind":"画家と劇作家の友情","text":"The friendship between the painter Elena Varro and the dramatist Hugo Stein lasted forty years and shaped the work of both. A new exhibition tells the story through letters, drawings, and a remarkable sketchbook. They met in Paris in 1928, at the height of surrealism. Varro was already known for her portraiture. She would elongate her figures until they seemed to float, and she liked to etch fine lines into wet paint with a needle. Stein, a preeminent writer of comedies, found in her pictures a pathos that his own plays lacked. He asked her to design his next production, a raunchy farce about a fiddler and a duchess. The result was a scandal and a triumph. Critics praised the physicality of the staging, and one called the painted sets the finest surrealist work ever seen in a theater. The exhibition shows the elaboration of each design, from first idea to finished cloth. There is charm in the smaller items too. Stein was also a lyricist, and a song he wrote for Varro's birthday hangs beside her reply, a drawing of the two of them as owls. The blurb on the catalog calls it a love story. It was something rarer, a friendship between equals.","ja":"画家エレナ・バロと劇作家フーゴ・シュタインの友情は40年続き、双方の仕事を形作った。新しい展覧会が、手紙、素描、そして一冊の注目すべき写生帳を通じてその物語を語っている。2人は1928年、超現実主義の最盛期のパリで出会った。バロはすでに肖像画で知られていた。彼女は人物を、浮かんでいるように見えるまで細長く引き伸ばし、ぬれた絵の具に針で細い線を刻むのを好んだ。喜劇の卓越した書き手だったシュタインは、彼女の絵の中に、自分の劇に欠けていた哀感を見いだした。彼は、バイオリン弾きと公爵夫人についてのきわどい笑劇である次回作の美術を彼女に依頼した。結果は醜聞であり、大成功でもあった。批評家たちは演出の肉体的な力強さを称賛し、ある批評家は、その描かれた舞台装置を、劇場でこれまで見られた中で最も見事な超現実主義の作品と呼んだ。展覧会は、最初の着想から仕上がった幕まで、それぞれの美術案が練り上げられていく過程を見せる。小さな品々にも魅力がある。シュタインは作詞家でもあり、彼がバロの誕生日のために書いた歌が、彼女の返事、つまり2人をフクロウとして描いた絵の隣に掛かっている。図録の宣伝文はこれを恋物語と呼んでいる。それはもっとまれなもの、対等な者同士の友情だった。","words":[["dramatist","freq:dramatist"],["sketchbook","freq:sketchbook"],["surrealism","freq:surrealism"],["portraiture","freq:portraiture"],["elongate","freq:elongate"],["etch","freq:etch"],["preeminent","freq:preeminent"],["pathos","freq:pathos"],["raunchy","freq:raunchy"],["fiddler","freq:fiddler"],["physicality","freq:physicality"],["surrealist","freq:surrealist"],["elaboration","freq:elaboration"],["lyricist","freq:lyricist"],["blurb","freq:blurb"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did Stein find in Varro's pictures?","o":["Humor that he wanted to copy","An emotional depth his own plays lacked","A style he disliked","Portraits of himself"],"a":1,"why":"found in her pictures a pathos that his own plays lacked とある。"},{"q":"How does the reviewer describe their relationship?","o":["As a love story","As a rivalry","As a friendship between equals","As a business partnership"],"a":2,"why":"It was something rarer, a friendship between equals とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0187","page":"freq","group":"2601〜2700","kind":"編集長の肖像","text":"Everyone who worked at the Morning Courier in the 1980s has a story about its editor, Douglas Farr. He was, to put it mildly, an oddity. A large, untidy man whom strangers took for a slob, he wore the same brown jacket for ten years. He was temperamental, often irate before the first edition, and lethargic after lunch. Rivals called him a buffoon. They were mistaken. Farr was a voracious reader with a memory that frightened his staff, and beneath the noise he was a cynic only about the powerful. With junior reporters he was patient. One recalls arriving on her first day, forlorn and certain that she would fail. Farr handed her a cup of tea and a murder trial. He disliked showy writing and crossed out adjectives with visible pleasure. In his last years he grew reclusive, and the paper, bereft of his energy, became sedate and dull. Those who visited him found the old exuberance intact. He remained nonchalant about his own reputation. Asked how he wished to be remembered, he said that he would settle for an accurate obituary, and that he did not expect one.","ja":"1980年代にモーニング・クーリエ紙で働いた人は誰でも、編集長ダグラス・ファーについての逸話を持っている。彼は、控えめに言っても変わり者だった。大柄でだらしなく、見知らぬ人には無精者と思われた彼は、同じ茶色の上着を10年着ていた。彼は気性が激しく、第一版の前にはしばしば激怒し、昼食の後はだるそうにしていた。競争相手は彼を道化者と呼んだ。それは間違いだった。ファーはむさぼるように本を読む人で、部下を怖がらせるほどの記憶力を持ち、騒がしさの下では、権力者についてだけ皮肉屋だった。若い記者に対しては辛抱強かった。ある記者は、初日に、心細く、きっと失敗すると思い込んで出社したときのことを覚えている。ファーは彼女に一杯のお茶と殺人事件の裁判を手渡した。彼は派手な文章を嫌い、形容詞を目に見えて楽しそうに線で消した。晩年には引きこもりがちになり、彼の活力を失った新聞は落ち着いた退屈なものになった。彼を訪ねた人々は、昔のあふれる元気が損なわれていないことを知った。彼は自分の評判については平然としたままだった。どう記憶されたいかと尋ねられ、正確な死亡記事があればそれで十分だが、それは期待していないと彼は答えた。","words":[["oddity","freq:oddity"],["slob","freq:slob"],["temperamental","freq:temperamental"],["irate","freq:irate"],["lethargic","freq:lethargic"],["buffoon","freq:buffoon"],["voracious","freq:voracious"],["cynic","freq:cynic"],["forlorn","freq:forlorn"],["showy","freq:showy"],["reclusive","freq:reclusive"],["bereft","freq:bereft"],["sedate","freq:sedate"],["exuberance","freq:exuberance"],["nonchalant","freq:nonchalant"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why were Farr's rivals wrong to call him a buffoon?","o":["He was widely read and had a formidable memory.","He never lost his temper.","He dressed with great care.","He disliked young reporters."],"a":0,"why":"a voracious reader with a memory that frightened his staff とある。"},{"q":"What happened to the newspaper in his last years?","o":["It won many prizes.","It became calm and dull without his energy.","It was closed down.","It hired more editors."],"a":1,"why":"the paper, bereft of his energy, became sedate and dull とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0188","page":"freq","group":"2601〜2700","kind":"同窓会の夜","text":"Our class met again last month, thirty years after we left school, in the old cloister where we used to eat lunch. I had expected the evening to be awkward. It was, at first. We stood in small groups and tried not to squint at each other's name badges. Then someone began to reminisce about the chemistry teacher, and the room came alive. People who had not spoken for decades started to babble like children. One woman would gush about her grandchildren to anyone who stood still. A man I remembered as shy made the whole table squirm with a story about the headmaster's car. There were sadder moments. A friend told me, her voice beginning to quiver, that her husband had died in the spring. I had no salve to offer except to listen. Some people like to scoff at reunions, and to grumble that nostalgia is a way to wallow in the past. I understand the derision. But there was real consternation when the caretaker announced that the school would close next year. After the monotony of ordinary weeks, the evening felt like a gift. I drove home late, and found a kind of repose in the empty road.","ja":"私たちの学年は先月、卒業から30年ぶりに、かつて昼食を食べていた古い回廊で再会した。私はその晩が気まずいものになると予想していた。最初はそうだった。私たちは小さな集まりに分かれて立ち、互いの名札を目を細めて見ないように努めた。それから誰かが化学の先生の思い出を語り始め、部屋は活気づいた。何十年も話していなかった人々が、子どものようにぺちゃくちゃしゃべり出した。ある女性は、立ち止まっている人なら誰にでも孫のことを大げさに褒めて聞かせた。私が内気だと記憶していた男性は、校長の車についての話でテーブル中をもじもじさせた。もっと悲しい瞬間もあった。ある友人は、声を震わせ始めながら、夫が春に亡くなったと私に話した。私には、耳を傾けることのほかに差し出せる慰めがなかった。同窓会をあざ笑い、郷愁は過去に浸る方法にすぎないと不平を言うのが好きな人もいる。そのあざけりは理解できる。しかし、管理人が学校は来年閉鎖されると告げたとき、皆が本当にろうばいした。普段の週の単調さの後で、その晩は贈り物のように感じられた。私は遅くに車で家へ向かい、誰もいない道に一種の安らぎを見いだした。","words":[["cloister","freq:cloister"],["squint","freq:squint"],["reminisce","freq:reminisce"],["babble","freq:babble"],["gush","freq:gush"],["squirm","freq:squirm"],["quiver","freq:quiver"],["salve","freq:salve"],["scoff","freq:scoff"],["grumble","freq:grumble"],["wallow","freq:wallow"],["derision","freq:derision"],["consternation","freq:consternation"],["monotony","freq:monotony"],["repose","freq:repose"]],"qs":[{"q":"What made the room come alive?","o":["The arrival of the headmaster","Someone sharing memories of a teacher","The announcement about the school","A speech by the caretaker"],"a":1,"why":"someone began to reminisce about the chemistry teacher, and the room came alive とある。"},{"q":"What news caused dismay at the reunion?","o":["The teacher had retired.","The lunch was cancelled.","A classmate had moved abroad.","The school would close the following year."],"a":3,"why":"the caretaker announced that the school would close next year とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0189","page":"freq","group":"2601〜2700","kind":"見世物小屋の復活","text":"The traveling fair that visits our town each September is justly famous for one attraction: a wooden contraption built in 1902, in which a carved devil rises from a painted pit of fire and brimstone. For years it stood broken in a seedy corner of the fairground. Children walked past without a glance. Then a retired engineer decided to familiarize himself with its workings. The mechanism had managed to elude three earlier repairers. Verily, he told me with a smile, it was the hardest puzzle of his life. Nothing matched the drawings; at every joint, the real machine would diverge from the plan. He worked through two winters. Last week it ran again. The devil rose, the bellows roared, and a small acrobatic figure turned a somersault on the rim. The crowd was larger than any the fair has seen in decades. Parents who had come as children stood with their own sons and daughters. It was moving to watch a town rediscover something it had forgotten it owned. The engineer refused all payment. He asked only that someone learn how to oil it.","ja":"毎年9月に私たちの町を訪れる移動遊園地は、一つの出し物で有名だが、それは当然のことだ。1902年に作られた木製の奇妙な仕掛けで、火と硫黄の描かれた穴から、彫刻の悪魔がせり上がる。何年もの間、それは遊園地のみすぼらしい一角に壊れたまま置かれていた。子どもたちは目もくれずに通り過ぎた。その後、ある引退した技師が、その仕組みに習熟しようと決めた。その機構は、それまでの3人の修理人の手を巧みに逃れていた。まことに、それは人生で最も難しいパズルだったと、彼は笑みを浮かべて私に語った。図面と合うものは何もなく、どの継ぎ目でも、実際の機械は設計図と食い違っていた。彼は2度の冬を通して作業した。先週、それは再び動いた。悪魔がせり上がり、ふいごがうなり、小さな軽業の人形が縁で宙返りをした。観衆は、この遊園地がここ数十年で見たどの観衆よりも多かった。子どもの頃に来たことのある親たちが、自分の息子や娘と並んで立っていた。持っていることを忘れていたものを町が再発見するのを見るのは、心を動かされることだった。技師は一切の報酬を断った。彼が求めたのは、誰かが油の差し方を覚えることだけだった。","words":[["justly","freq:justly"],["contraption","freq:contraption"],["brimstone","freq:brimstone"],["seedy","freq:seedy"],["familiarize","freq:familiarize"],["elude","freq:elude"],["Verily","freq:verily"],["diverge","freq:diverge"],["acrobatic","freq:acrobatic"],["rediscover","freq:rediscover"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why had earlier repairers failed?","o":["They lacked the money.","The fair refused to let them try.","The machine did not match its drawings.","The wood had rotted completely."],"a":2,"why":"Nothing matched the drawings; at every joint, the real machine would diverge from the plan とある。"},{"q":"What did the engineer ask for in return?","o":["That someone learn to oil the machine","A share of the ticket sales","His name on the machine","A new workshop"],"a":0,"why":"He asked only that someone learn how to oil it とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0190","page":"freq","group":"2701〜2800","kind":"王位をめぐる危機","text":"The kingdom of Marenta has been drawn into a political maelstrom. Last month the king, who is eighty-six and ill, announced that he would abdicate in favor of his daughter. Within days, his younger brother tried to usurp the throne. The prince's supporters are mostly officers and one old crony from his business days. They argue that an ancient law, never repealed, must supersede the king's wishes. The attempt has not gone well. When the prince ordered the requisition of the royal guard's barracks, the commander refused, and was promptly charged with insubordination. That charge served only to engender sympathy for the princess. A strike by civil servants threatened to paralyze the capital. The archbishop, in a rare denunciation, accused the prince of trying to brainwash the young with promises of glory. The prince insists that an inquiry will exonerate him of any wrongdoing. Few believe it. His idea of monarchy, in which the ruler stands above the law, is antithetical to the constitution of 1952. Lawyers expect a long wrangle in the courts. Public opinion, meanwhile, is moving inexorably toward the princess, who has so far said almost nothing at all.","ja":"マレンタ王国は政治の大混乱に引き込まれた。先月、86歳で病身の王が、娘に位を譲って退位すると発表した。数日のうちに、王の弟が王位を奪おうとした。王子の支持者は主に将校たちと、実業家時代からの古い取り巻き一人だ。彼らは、一度も廃止されていない古い法律が王の意向に優先しなければならないと主張する。その試みはうまくいっていない。王子が近衛隊の兵舎の徴発を命じると、司令官は拒否し、ただちに不服従の罪に問われた。その告発は王女への同情を生んだだけだった。公務員のストライキが首都をまひさせるおそれがあった。大司教は異例の公然たる非難の中で、王子が栄光の約束で若者を洗脳しようとしていると責めた。王子は、調査によって自分のいかなる不正行為の疑いも晴れると主張している。それを信じる人はほとんどいない。支配者が法の上に立つという彼の君主制の考えは、1952年の憲法と相いれない。法律家たちは、法廷での長い争いを予想している。その間にも世論は、これまでほとんど何も語っていない王女のほうへ、止めようもなく動いている。","words":[["maelstrom","freq:maelstrom"],["abdicate","freq:abdicate"],["usurp","freq:usurp"],["crony","freq:crony"],["supersede","freq:supersede"],["requisition","freq:requisition"],["insubordination","freq:insubordination"],["engender","freq:engender"],["paralyze","freq:paralyze"],["denunciation","freq:denunciation"],["brainwash","freq:brainwash"],["exonerate","freq:exonerate"],["antithetical","freq:antithetical"],["wrangle","freq:wrangle"],["inexorably","freq:inexorably"]],"qs":[{"q":"On what do the prince's supporters base their claim?","o":["The king's illness","An old law that was never repealed","A vote by the civil servants","The archbishop's support"],"a":1,"why":"an ancient law, never repealed, must supersede the king's wishes とある。"},{"q":"What effect did the charge against the commander have?","o":["It ended the strike.","It frightened the princess.","It strengthened the prince.","It created sympathy for the princess."],"a":3,"why":"That charge served only to engender sympathy for the princess とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0191","page":"freq","group":"2701〜2800","kind":"出稼ぎと送金","text":"In the fishing villages of the southern coast, the main source of income is no longer fish. It is the remittance sent home each month by a son or daughter working abroad. A study of forty villages shows how deeply this has changed daily life. The typical breadwinner is now a woman of thirty, employed as a nurse or cleaner in a foreign city. The money she sends has replaced the drudgery of mending nets, and families who once had to haggle over the price of every fish can plan ahead. The authors are careful not to extrapolate too far. Their figures cover only five years. Still, they make a cogent case that the system cannot continue sustainably. Shops selling phones and motorbikes proliferate while boats lie unused. If foreign governments were to open the floodgates to cheaper workers from elsewhere, village incomes could plausibly halve. The report does not dwell on minutiae. It proposes three steps, each amply supported by evidence: training for local jobs, small loans, and a savings scheme that banks could personalize for each family. The authors envisage a slow change. Without it, they warn, the villages' dependence will become unmanageable.","ja":"南海岸の漁村では、主な収入源はもう魚ではない。海外で働く息子や娘が毎月故郷に送る送金だ。40の村についての研究は、これが日常生活をどれほど深く変えたかを示している。今では典型的な一家の稼ぎ手は、外国の都市で看護師か清掃員として雇われている30歳の女性だ。彼女が送る金は、網を繕うという骨の折れる単調な仕事に取って代わり、かつては魚一匹ごとの値段で押し問答しなければならなかった家族が、先の計画を立てられるようになった。著者たちは、推定を広げすぎないよう注意している。彼らの数字は5年分しかない。それでも、この仕組みは持続可能な形では続けられないという説得力のある主張を展開している。電話やバイクを売る店が急増する一方で、船は使われないまま置かれている。外国政府がほかの地域からのより安い労働者にせきを切ったように門戸を開けば、村の収入が半減することも十分ありうる。報告書は細かい点にこだわらない。それぞれ証拠に十分支えられた3つの措置を提案している。地元の仕事のための訓練、小口融資、そして銀行が家族ごとに合わせられる貯蓄制度である。著者たちはゆっくりした変化を思い描いている。それがなければ、村の依存は手に負えなくなると彼らは警告する。","words":[["remittance","freq:remittance"],["breadwinner","freq:breadwinner"],["drudgery","freq:drudgery"],["haggle","freq:haggle"],["extrapolate","freq:extrapolate"],["cogent","freq:cogent"],["sustainably","freq:sustainably"],["proliferate","freq:proliferate"],["floodgates","freq:floodgates"],["plausibly","freq:plausibly"],["minutiae","freq:minutiae"],["amply","freq:amply"],["personalize","freq:personalize"],["envisage","freq:envisage"],["unmanageable","freq:unmanageable"]],"qs":[{"q":"Who is now the typical earner for these villages?","o":["A fisherman in his fifties","A shopkeeper selling phones","A woman working abroad as a nurse or cleaner","A government official"],"a":2,"why":"a woman of thirty, employed as a nurse or cleaner in a foreign city とある。"},{"q":"What risk do the authors identify?","o":["Foreign countries might admit cheaper workers from elsewhere, cutting village incomes.","Fish stocks might recover too quickly.","Banks might refuse all loans.","The villages might grow too large."],"a":0,"why":"If foreign governments were to open the floodgates to cheaper workers from elsewhere, village incomes could plausibly halve とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0192","page":"freq","group":"2701〜2800","kind":"島の冬の自然","text":"The cay lies three miles off the coast, a strip of sand and rock no longer than a football field. I spent a week there in February with a team of biologists. Winter is the quiet season. The island's tortoises hibernate in burrows beneath a thicket of thorn, and the only sound at night is the rustle of dry leaves. The team studies recovery. Ten years ago, a storm covered the cay with detritus from a wrecked ship, and much of the wildlife died. Its recuperation has been slow but steady. Each morning we checked the tortoises for signs of lethargy that might mean illness. One young animal had stopped breathing, and a student managed to resuscitate it by warming it in her hands. Samples from its salivary glands were sent to the mainland. There were small wonders everywhere. On a branch I found the chrysalis of a moth, and on the rocks, the slow crystallization of salt left by the tide. The landscape feels primeval. By a strange arrangement, a farmer keeps a single heifer on the island; we watched her frolic on the beach at dawn. I left reluctantly, feeling that I too had been allowed to recuperate.","ja":"その小島は海岸から3マイル沖にあり、サッカー場ほどの長さもない砂と岩の帯だ。私は2月に、生物学者のチームとそこで1週間過ごした。冬は静かな季節だ。島のリクガメはイバラの茂みの下の巣穴で冬眠し、夜に聞こえるのは枯れ葉のさらさらいう音だけだ。チームは回復を研究している。10年前、嵐が難破船の残骸でこの小島を覆い、野生生物の多くが死んだ。その回復は遅いが着実だ。毎朝、私たちは病気を意味するかもしれないけだるさの兆候がないか、リクガメを調べた。若い一匹が呼吸を止めており、ある学生が両手で温めてそれを蘇生させることができた。その唾液腺から採った試料は本土に送られた。小さな驚きが至るところにあった。枝の上で私はガのさなぎを見つけ、岩の上では、潮が残した塩がゆっくり結晶化していくのを見た。風景は太古のもののように感じられる。奇妙な取り決めで、ある農家が島に若い雌牛を一頭だけ飼っている。私たちは夜明けに、彼女が浜ではしゃぎ回るのを眺めた。私は去りがたい思いで島を後にした。自分もまた、回復することを許されたような気がしていた。","words":[["cay","freq:cay"],["hibernate","freq:hibernate"],["thicket","freq:thicket"],["rustle","freq:rustle"],["detritus","freq:detritus"],["recuperation","freq:recuperation"],["lethargy","freq:lethargy"],["resuscitate","freq:resuscitate"],["salivary","freq:salivary"],["chrysalis","freq:chrysalis"],["crystallization","freq:crystallization"],["primeval","freq:primeval"],["heifer","freq:heifer"],["frolic","freq:frolic"],["recuperate","freq:recuperate"]],"qs":[{"q":"What damaged the island's wildlife ten years ago?","o":["A fire","Debris from a wrecked ship after a storm","Hunters","A new disease"],"a":1,"why":"a storm covered the cay with detritus from a wrecked ship, and much of the wildlife died とある。"},{"q":"How was the young tortoise saved?","o":["A student warmed it in her hands.","It was taken to the mainland.","It was given medicine.","It was returned to its burrow."],"a":0,"why":"a student managed to resuscitate it by warming it in her hands とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0193","page":"freq","group":"2701〜2800","kind":"演芸場の最後の公演","text":"The Palace Theatre closed its doors on Saturday after a hundred and ten years, and it did so in style. The final show was a zany mixture of everything the old hall had been known for. There was an impersonator who did six prime ministers in four minutes, and a juggler of unsurpassed skill. A comedian opened with a quip about the leaking roof. The middle of the evening was farcical in the best sense: a sketch in which a waiter, a duchess, and a goat exchange hats. The audience laughed until it hurt. For all the glitz, there was feeling too. The opulence of the gold ceiling, restored for the occasion, drew gasps. Old posters with their lurid colors lined the stairs, and a small exhibition in the former darkroom showed photographs of forgotten stars. The owners held a fete in the street outside. Critics have been quick to laud the evening, and they are right. The cleverness of music hall lay in hiding its artifice; everything looked easy. Saturday's violinist played with a virtuosity that would grace any concert hall. It was a fabulously happy night, and a sad one.","ja":"パレス劇場は110年の歴史の末、土曜に幕を下ろした。それも見事なやり方で。最後の公演は、この古い劇場が知られてきたあらゆるものの、ばかばかしく愉快な取り合わせだった。4分で6人の首相をまねる物まね芸人がいて、並ぶもののない腕前の曲芸師がいた。喜劇役者は、雨漏りする屋根についての気の利いた冗談で幕を開けた。晩の中盤は、最良の意味で茶番だった。給仕と公爵夫人とヤギが帽子を取り替える寸劇である。観客は痛くなるまで笑った。派手さの一方で、情感もあった。この日のために修復された金色の天井の豪華さは、息をのむ声を誘った。どぎつい色の古いポスターが階段に並び、かつての暗室での小さな展示が、忘れられたスターたちの写真を見せていた。経営者たちは外の通りで祝いの催しを開いた。批評家たちはいち早くこの晩を称賛したが、それは正しい。演芸場の賢さは、その巧妙な工夫を隠すところにあった。すべてが簡単そうに見えたのだ。土曜のバイオリン奏者は、どんな演奏会場をも飾るであろう名人芸で弾いた。途方もなく幸せな夜であり、悲しい夜でもあった。","words":[["zany","freq:zany"],["impersonator","freq:impersonator"],["unsurpassed","freq:unsurpassed"],["quip","freq:quip"],["farcical","freq:farcical"],["glitz","freq:glitz"],["opulence","freq:opulence"],["lurid","freq:lurid"],["darkroom","freq:darkroom"],["fete","freq:fete"],["laud","freq:laud"],["cleverness","freq:cleverness"],["artifice","freq:artifice"],["virtuosity","freq:virtuosity"],["fabulously","freq:fabulously"]],"qs":[{"q":"What kind of show marked the theater's closing?","o":["A serious play","A film","A lively variety show of the kind the hall was known for","A political meeting"],"a":2,"why":"a zany mixture of everything the old hall had been known for とある。"},{"q":"What does the reviewer say was the skill of music hall?","o":["Making difficult things look easy","Using expensive scenery","Avoiding comedy","Copying concert halls"],"a":0,"why":"The cleverness of music hall lay in hiding its artifice; everything looked easy とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0194","page":"freq","group":"2701〜2800","kind":"ある外交官の評価","text":"Lady Honor Blake, who died this week at ninety-four, was the most admired diplomat of her generation, and one of the least conventional. She was gutsy from the start. As a headstrong young woman, she ignored her father's wishes and joined the foreign service at a time when it employed almost no women. Senior colleagues tried to patronize her. One haughty ambassador liked to disparage her reports as \"letters from a schoolgirl.\" She remained undaunted. Her repute rested on three qualities. She was tactful without being weak, magnanimous toward opponents, and dutiful to the point of exhaustion. During the crisis of 1962, she worked for nine days without leaving the embassy, and showed a stoicism that became famous. She was never contemptuous of the people she negotiated with, even those she distrusted. In old age she spoke with a wistful humor about the past. She was remorseful, she said, about only one decision, and she never revealed which. Friends recall her elation on the day the treaty she had worked on for six years was finally signed. She celebrated, characteristically, by going home to bed.","ja":"今週94歳で亡くなったオナー・ブレイク女史は、その世代で最も敬愛された外交官であり、最も型破りな一人でもあった。彼女は最初から度胸があった。強情な若い女性だった彼女は、父親の意向を無視し、女性をほとんど雇っていなかった時代の外務省に入った。上司たちは彼女を見下した態度で扱おうとした。ある高慢な大使は、彼女の報告書を「女学生からの手紙」とけなすのを好んだ。彼女はひるまなかった。彼女の評判は3つの資質に支えられていた。弱さを伴わない如才なさ、相手に対する寛大さ、そして疲れ果てるまで義務を果たす姿勢である。1962年の危機の間、彼女は9日間大使館を離れずに働き、有名になった冷静さを見せた。信用していない相手に対してさえ、交渉相手をさげすむことは決してなかった。老年には、物思いを帯びたユーモアで過去を語った。深く後悔している決定は一つだけだと彼女は言い、それがどれなのかは決して明かさなかった。友人たちは、彼女が6年間取り組んだ条約がついに署名された日の彼女の大喜びを覚えている。彼女は、いかにも彼女らしく、家に帰って寝ることで祝った。","words":[["gutsy","freq:gutsy"],["headstrong","freq:headstrong"],["patronize","freq:patronize"],["haughty","freq:haughty"],["disparage","freq:disparage"],["undaunted","freq:undaunted"],["repute","freq:repute"],["tactful","freq:tactful"],["magnanimous","freq:magnanimous"],["dutiful","freq:dutiful"],["stoicism","freq:stoicism"],["contemptuous","freq:contemptuous"],["wistful","freq:wistful"],["remorseful","freq:remorseful"],["elation","freq:elation"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did some senior colleagues treat Blake early in her career?","o":["They promoted her quickly.","They looked down on her.","They ignored her completely.","They copied her reports."],"a":1,"why":"Senior colleagues tried to patronize her とあり、報告書を disparage した大使もいた。"},{"q":"What did she say about her regrets?","o":["She regretted nothing.","She regretted joining the service.","She regretted the treaty.","She regretted one decision but never said which."],"a":3,"why":"She was remorseful ... about only one decision, and she never revealed which とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0195","page":"freq","group":"2701〜2800","kind":"学校の怪談","text":"The story is still told at St. Agnes School, though nobody knows how much of it is true. In 1911 the headmistress, a stern woman named Miss Crale, lived in a nondescript cottage at the edge of the grounds. One winter evening a pupil crossed the rickety bridge to deliver a sheaf of papers and found the door open. Miss Crale lay prostrate on the verandah. The girl was aghast. She ran back, too frightened to retrace her steps alone, and returned with the porter. The house was empty. Miss Crale was found the next morning at her desk, apparently well, and she chose to feign ignorance of the whole affair. Her explanation, that she had fainted and recovered, was unconvincing. The gardener later claimed that he had seen a fire like a funeral pyre behind the cottage that night. Some said that the headmistress had burned letters connected with an old indiscretion. The girl who found her could never speak of it without having to clench her hands. She remembered a feeling of revulsion that she could not explain. An unobtrusive plaque marks the place where the cottage stood. The bridge was pulled down in 1950.","ja":"この話は聖アグネス校で今も語られているが、どこまでが本当なのかは誰も知らない。1911年、クレイル先生という厳格な女性校長が、敷地のはずれの特徴のない小さな家に住んでいた。ある冬の晩、一人の生徒が紙の束を届けるためにぐらぐらする橋を渡ると、扉が開いていた。クレイル先生はベランダにうつぶせに倒れていた。少女は仰天した。彼女は走って戻り、怖くて一人では来た道を引き返せなかったので、門番を連れて戻った。家は空だった。クレイル先生は翌朝、一見元気な様子で机に向かっているのが見つかり、この件については一切知らないふりをすることを選んだ。気を失って回復したという彼女の説明は、説得力がなかった。庭師は後に、その夜、家の裏で火葬のまきの山のような火を見たと主張した。校長は昔の軽率な行いに関わる手紙を燃やしたのだと言う人もいた。彼女を見つけた少女は、両手を固く握りしめずにはその話をすることができなかった。彼女は、説明のつかない嫌悪感を覚えていた。目立たない銘板が、その家が建っていた場所を示している。橋は1950年に取り壊された。","words":[["headmistress","freq:headmistress"],["nondescript","freq:nondescript"],["rickety","freq:rickety"],["sheaf","freq:sheaf"],["prostrate","freq:prostrate"],["verandah","freq:verandah"],["aghast","freq:aghast"],["retrace","freq:retrace"],["feign","freq:feign"],["unconvincing","freq:unconvincing"],["pyre","freq:pyre"],["indiscretion","freq:indiscretion"],["clench","freq:clench"],["revulsion","freq:revulsion"],["unobtrusive","freq:unobtrusive"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the pupil find when she reached the cottage?","o":["The headmistress lying on the verandah","The cottage on fire","The porter waiting","A pile of letters"],"a":0,"why":"Miss Crale lay prostrate on the verandah とある。"},{"q":"How did the headmistress respond the next day?","o":["She resigned.","She punished the girl.","She pretended to know nothing about it.","She explained everything to the school."],"a":2,"why":"she chose to feign ignorance of the whole affair とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0196","page":"freq","group":"2701〜2800","kind":"温泉宿の客","text":"The hotel at Bad Linden has welcomed the same guests each October for decades, and I went there to glean what I could about its appeal. It is an incongruous place. The building is a concrete block from the 1970s, yet the service is impeccably old-fashioned. Waiters bow. Nobody hurries. A notice in the lobby offers a pedicure, a mud bath, and a lecture on birds. I had only a smattering of German, but the guests were patient. Most were over seventy. One retired judge told me that he came for the pleasure of an aimless week. At home, he said, every hour had a purpose; here he walked without a destination, unencumbered by plans. Another guest, a former actress, would allude to famous friends and then change the subject. I had expected to abhor the place. Instead I found something close to unadulterated calm. On my last evening I sat on the terrace and watched the mist rise from the valley. The judge raised his glass. \"You will come back,\" he said. It was not a question, and he was probably right.","ja":"バート・リンデンのホテルは何十年も、毎年10月に同じ客を迎えてきた。私はその魅力について集められるだけのことを集めようと、そこへ出かけた。そこは不釣り合いな場所だ。建物は1970年代のコンクリートの塊なのに、サービスは申し分なく昔風である。給仕はおじぎをする。誰も急がない。ロビーの掲示は、足の爪の手入れ、泥風呂、鳥についての講演を案内している。私には生かじりのドイツ語しかなかったが、客たちは辛抱強かった。ほとんどが70歳を超えていた。ある引退した裁判官は、当てのない1週間の楽しみのために来るのだと私に語った。家ではどの1時間にも目的があるが、ここでは計画に煩わされず、行き先もなく歩くのだと彼は言った。別の客である元女優は、有名な友人たちのことをほのめかしては話題を変えた。私はこの場所をひどく嫌うだろうと予想していた。ところが、混じりけのない穏やかさに近いものを見いだした。最後の晩、私はテラスに座り、谷から霧が立ち上るのを眺めた。裁判官がグラスを上げた。「あなたはまた来ますよ」と彼は言った。それは質問ではなく、おそらく彼は正しかった。","words":[["glean","freq:glean"],["incongruous","freq:incongruous"],["impeccably","freq:impeccably"],["pedicure","freq:pedicure"],["smattering","freq:smattering"],["aimless","freq:aimless"],["unencumbered","freq:unencumbered"],["allude","freq:allude"],["abhor","freq:abhor"],["unadulterated","freq:unadulterated"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why does the retired judge come to the hotel?","o":["To attend lectures on birds","To enjoy a week without any purpose","To meet famous people","To practice his German"],"a":1,"why":"he came for the pleasure of an aimless week とある。"},{"q":"How did the writer's feelings about the place change?","o":["He expected to like it and was disappointed.","He left early.","He complained to the staff.","He expected to hate it but found it calming."],"a":3,"why":"I had expected to abhor the place. Instead I found something close to unadulterated calm とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0197","page":"freq","group":"2801〜2900","kind":"労働争議の決着","text":"The longest strike in the history of the Valdran coal fields ended this week, when the miners' union agreed to return to work. Its leaders deny that they chose to capitulate. The dispute lasted fourteen months. The ostensible issue was pay, but the real question was whether the moribund pits of the north should close at all. The company called their decline inexorable. The union described the closures as calamitous for whole towns. Both sides were accused of ruthlessness. Managers offered a cash inducement to any miner who crossed the picket line, and there was violence at several gates. Ministers said they would deplore any attempt to starve families into surrender, yet they did little to prevent it. In the end, dissension within the union decided the matter. Younger members, tired of what they saw as a paternalistic leadership, demanded a vote. The meetings that followed were anarchic. The final agreement required the concurrence of three regions, and it passed by a narrow margin. Union leaders now repudiate the suggestion that the strike achieved nothing. They can credibly point to better pensions. Whether history will vindicate their strategy is a question that the empty villages will answer.","ja":"バルドラン炭田の歴史で最も長いストライキが今週、鉱山労働者の組合が職場復帰に同意して終わった。組合の指導者たちは、屈服することを選んだのではないと言っている。争議は14か月続いた。表向きの争点は賃金だったが、本当の問題は、北部の瀕死の炭鉱をそもそも閉鎖すべきかどうかだった。会社はその衰退を止められないものと呼んだ。組合は、閉鎖は町全体にとって悲惨だと述べた。双方が冷酷さを非難された。経営側はピケを越えた鉱山労働者に現金の報奨を申し出、いくつかの門では暴力があった。閣僚たちは、家族を飢えさせて降伏に追い込もうとするいかなる試みも強く非難すると述べたが、それを防ぐためにはほとんど何もしなかった。結局、組合内部の意見の対立が事を決めた。温情主義的と見える指導部にうんざりした若い組合員たちが、投票を要求したのだ。その後の集会は無秩序だった。最終合意には3つの地域の同意が必要で、僅差で可決された。組合の指導者たちは今、ストライキが何も達成しなかったという見方を否認している。彼らは、年金の改善を信頼できる形で挙げることができる。歴史が彼らの戦略の正しさを立証するかどうかは、人のいなくなった村々が答える問いだ。","words":[["capitulate","freq:capitulate"],["ostensible","freq:ostensible"],["moribund","freq:moribund"],["inexorable","freq:inexorable"],["calamitous","freq:calamitous"],["ruthlessness","freq:ruthlessness"],["inducement","freq:inducement"],["deplore","freq:deplore"],["dissension","freq:dissension"],["paternalistic","freq:paternalistic"],["anarchic","freq:anarchic"],["concurrence","freq:concurrence"],["repudiate","freq:repudiate"],["credibly","freq:credibly"],["vindicate","freq:vindicate"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was the real issue behind the strike?","o":["The length of the working day","Whether the northern pits should be closed","The safety of the mines","The price of coal"],"a":1,"why":"the real question was whether the moribund pits of the north should close at all とある。"},{"q":"What finally brought the strike to an end?","o":["A government order","A fall in coal prices","Disagreement inside the union leading to a vote","The arrest of the leaders"],"a":2,"why":"dissension within the union decided the matter とあり、若い組合員が投票を要求したとある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0198","page":"freq","group":"2801〜2900","kind":"鯨の骨の謎","text":"Deep inside the body of every whale lies a pair of small bones that have puzzled biologists for two centuries. They are the remains of hind legs. In the textbooks they are described as vestigial, left over from ancestors that walked on land, and it was long thought axiomatic that they served no purpose. A zoologist at the University of Bergen has challenged that view. Dr. Ingrid Sande examined the skeletons of sixty whales, removing the bones from beneath a thick layer of blubber. She found that the bones do not simply float unassisted in the tissue. Muscles are attached to them, and a small protrusion on each bone varies between species in a regular way. She was able to hypothesize that the bones help to control movement during mating. The differences are almost imperceptible to the eye, but they appear clearly in measurements. Sande is cautious about the plausibility of wider claims. Her study cannot show whether damage to the bones causes sterility, and the postural effects remain unknown. But she has managed to distill a simple lesson. Evolution rarely leaves anything idle. The idea was incipient in earlier work; a bone that seems to protrude uselessly, like the legs of a snake that can only slither, may still have a task.","ja":"どのクジラの体の奥深くにも、2世紀にわたって生物学者を戸惑わせてきた一対の小さな骨がある。それは後ろ足の名残だ。教科書では、陸を歩いていた祖先から残った痕跡的なものと説明され、何の役にも立っていないことは長く自明だと考えられてきた。ベルゲン大学のある動物学者がその見方に異議を唱えた。イングリッド・サンデ博士は、厚い脂肪の層の下から骨を取り出しながら、60頭のクジラの骨格を調べた。彼女は、その骨が組織の中で支えなしにただ浮いているのではないことを見いだした。筋肉がそれに付いており、それぞれの骨にある小さな突起は、種によって規則的に異なっている。彼女は、この骨が交尾の際の動きを制御する助けになっているという仮説を立てることができた。その違いは目にはほとんど気づかないほどだが、測定でははっきり現れる。サンデは、より広い主張の妥当性については慎重だ。彼女の研究は、骨の損傷が不妊を引き起こすかどうかを示すことはできず、姿勢への影響もわかっていない。しかし彼女は、単純な教訓を抽出することができた。進化はめったに何かを遊ばせておかないのだ。その考えは以前の研究に芽生えていた。地をはうことしかできないヘビの足のように、無用に突き出ているように見える骨にも、なお役目があるのかもしれない。","words":[["vestigial","freq:vestigial"],["axiomatic","freq:axiomatic"],["zoologist","freq:zoologist"],["blubber","freq:blubber"],["unassisted","freq:unassisted"],["protrusion","freq:protrusion"],["hypothesize","freq:hypothesize"],["imperceptible","freq:imperceptible"],["plausibility","freq:plausibility"],["sterility","freq:sterility"],["postural","freq:postural"],["distill","freq:distill"],["incipient","freq:incipient"],["protrude","freq:protrude"],["slither","freq:slither"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did Sande discover about the small bones?","o":["They are larger than textbooks say.","Muscles are attached to them and they vary between species.","They are made of cartilage.","They are missing in most whales."],"a":1,"why":"Muscles are attached to them, and a small protrusion on each bone varies between species in a regular way とある。"},{"q":"What lesson does she draw?","o":["Whales once had wings.","Textbooks are always wrong.","Measurements are unreliable.","Evolution seldom keeps useless parts."],"a":3,"why":"Evolution rarely leaves anything idle とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0199","page":"freq","group":"2801〜2900","kind":"音楽祭の批評","text":"The Linden Festival, now in its fortieth year, has always been the creation of one man. Its founder, the impresario Carl Weiss, is said to regard anyone who dislikes modern music as a philistine. This year's program showed both his strengths and his limits. The opening concert was beguiling. A young quartet played a melodious suite built on folk songs, and followed it with an improvisational piece full of sharp, staccato rhythms. The audience was delighted. The second evening was less successful. A new symphony, described in the program as \"impressionistic,\" proved ponderous, and its conductor seemed to find it as uninspiring as we did. The festival's academic side has grown. A colloquium on the idiomatic use of instruments drew two hundred listeners, and an exhibition of concert ephemera, tickets, posters, and letters, was unexpectedly moving. Some of Weiss's ideas now look outmoded. He still insists on concerts without interval or adornment, in a hall with hard seats. Yet the closing night was revelatory. An unknown pianist played Schubert as if nobody had played him before. Festivals exist for such evenings, and this one, for all its faults, still provides them.","ja":"40年目を迎えたリンデン音楽祭は、ずっと一人の男の創造物だった。創設者である興行主カール・バイスは、現代音楽を嫌う人を誰でも教養のない俗物と見なすと言われている。今年の演目は、彼の強みと限界の両方を示した。開幕の演奏会は魅惑的だった。若い四重奏団が民謡に基づく旋律の美しい組曲を演奏し、続いて鋭い断続的なリズムに満ちた即興的な曲を奏でた。聴衆は大喜びだった。2日目の晩はそれほど成功しなかった。プログラムで「印象主義的」と紹介された新作の交響曲は重苦しく、指揮者も私たちと同じくらい面白みを感じていないように見えた。音楽祭の学術的な面は充実してきた。楽器のその楽器らしい使い方についての討論会は200人の聴衆を集め、切符、ポスター、手紙といった演奏会の一時的な印刷物の展示は、思いがけず心を打つものだった。バイスの考えのいくつかは、今では時代遅れに見える。彼は今も、休憩も飾りもなしに、硬い座席のホールで演奏会を行うことにこだわっている。それでも最終日の夜は、目を開かされるものだった。無名のピアニストが、これまで誰も弾いたことがないかのようにシューベルトを弾いた。音楽祭はそうした晩のために存在するのであり、この音楽祭は、欠点はあっても、今もそれを与えてくれる。","words":[["impresario","freq:impresario"],["philistine","freq:philistine"],["beguiling","freq:beguiling"],["melodious","freq:melodious"],["improvisational","freq:improvisational"],["staccato","freq:staccato"],["impressionistic","freq:impressionistic"],["ponderous","freq:ponderous"],["uninspiring","freq:uninspiring"],["colloquium","freq:colloquium"],["idiomatic","freq:idiomatic"],["ephemera","freq:ephemera"],["outmoded","freq:outmoded"],["adornment","freq:adornment"],["revelatory","freq:revelatory"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was wrong with the new symphony?","o":["It was too short.","It was heavy and dull.","It was played out of tune.","It was cancelled."],"a":1,"why":"proved ponderous とあり、指揮者も uninspiring と感じているように見えたとある。"},{"q":"What made the closing night special?","o":["An unknown pianist's performance of Schubert","A speech by the founder","A display of posters","A new concert hall"],"a":0,"why":"An unknown pianist played Schubert as if nobody had played him before とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0200","page":"freq","group":"2801〜2900","kind":"小説の主人公たち","text":"The heroine of this novel, Clara Finch, is twenty-two and, by her own admission, angsty. She has just arrived in London from a small town, and her naivety is the engine of the plot. She cannot intuit what people mean when they say the opposite. At her first dinner party she is gauche, uses the wrong fork, and is treated with open snobbery by her hostess. The author has a predilection for comic disaster, and she writes these scenes with an astringent wit. The other characters are equally well drawn. Clara's aunt is a puritanical widow who believes in her own infallibility. Her uncle is glum and neglectful, forever losing his spectacles. The landlord, an inscrutable man who never explains himself, turns out to be the kindest person in the book. Only the villain disappoints. He is merely detestable, and he seems to exude menace without any reason for it. The plot moves blithely from one misunderstanding to the next, and the ending is too tidy. Still, few recent novels have given me as much pleasure. Clara learns very little, which is exactly why we believe in her.","ja":"この小説の主人公クララ・フィンチは22歳で、本人も認めるとおり、悩み多き若者だ。彼女は小さな町からロンドンに着いたばかりで、その世間知らずぶりが筋を動かす原動力になっている。人が反対のことを言うとき、何を意味しているのかを直感でつかむことができないのだ。最初の晩餐会で彼女は不器用で、間違ったフォークを使い、女主人からあからさまな上流気取りの扱いを受ける。作者は喜劇的な災難を好み、こうした場面を辛辣な機知で書いている。ほかの登場人物も同じようによく描かれている。クララのおばは、自分は絶対に誤らないと信じている厳格で禁欲的な未亡人だ。おじはむっつりしていて、何事もなおざりにし、いつも眼鏡をなくしている。決して自分のことを説明しない不可解な男である家主は、この本で最も親切な人物だとわかる。悪役だけが期待外れだ。彼は単に憎むべき人物で、何の理由もなく威圧感を漂わせているように見える。筋は一つの誤解から次の誤解へとのんきに進み、結末は整いすぎている。それでも、最近の小説でこれほど私を楽しませてくれたものはほとんどない。クララはほとんど何も学ばない。それこそが、私たちが彼女を信じる理由なのだ。","words":[["angsty","freq:angsty"],["naivety","freq:naivety"],["intuit","freq:intuit"],["gauche","freq:gauche"],["snobbery","freq:snobbery"],["predilection","freq:predilection"],["astringent","freq:astringent"],["puritanical","freq:puritanical"],["infallibility","freq:infallibility"],["glum","freq:glum"],["neglectful","freq:neglectful"],["inscrutable","freq:inscrutable"],["detestable","freq:detestable"],["exude","freq:exude"],["blithely","freq:blithely"]],"qs":[{"q":"What drives the plot of the novel?","o":["The heroine's inexperience","The villain's cleverness","The aunt's wealth","The landlord's secret"],"a":0,"why":"her naivety is the engine of the plot とある。"},{"q":"Which character does the reviewer find weak?","o":["The uncle","The landlord","The villain","The aunt"],"a":2,"why":"Only the villain disappoints とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0201","page":"freq","group":"2801〜2900","kind":"洞窟観光の是非","text":"The caves of Mount Serrat were closed to the public for sixty years. Last spring the regional government decided to refurbish the entrance and open them for ecotourism. I joined one of the first groups. The descent is not for everyone. A narrow passage leads down for two hundred meters, and anyone prone to claustrophobia should stay at the surface. The air is clammy, and the ceaseless drip of water is the only sound. After forty minutes of gruelling progress, some of it on hands and knees, the passage opens into a chamber the size of a cathedral. The exhilaration is hard to describe. The floor is covered with virginal white crystal, untouched since it formed. Guides explain that the cave is an almost hermetic world. Blind insects subsist here on whatever the water brings. The return is torturous, and the tedium of the queue at the exit tests the patience. Critics argue that the visits are a mistake. The cave's wonders are not inexhaustible, they say, and breath alone will damage them. The government hopes to ameliorate the harm by limiting numbers. Closing the caves again, it insists, would be unfeasible now that the villages depend on the income.","ja":"セラット山の洞窟は60年間、一般には閉ざされていた。昨年の春、地方政府は入口を改装し、エコツーリズムのために開放することを決めた。私は最初の団体の一つに加わった。その下りは誰にでも向いているわけではない。狭い通路が200メートル下へ続いており、閉所恐怖症になりやすい人は地上にとどまるべきだ。空気は冷たく湿っており、絶え間ない水のしたたりだけが唯一の音だ。一部は四つんばいで進む40分の過酷な前進の後、通路は大聖堂ほどの大きさの空間に開ける。その爽快感は言い表しがたい。床は、形成されて以来誰にも触れられていない、手つかずの白い結晶に覆われている。案内人は、この洞窟がほとんど外界から隔絶した世界だと説明する。目の見えない昆虫が、水が運んでくるわずかなもので命をつないでいる。帰り道はひどく苦しく、出口での行列の退屈さは忍耐を試す。批判する人々は、見学は間違いだと主張する。洞窟の驚異は尽きることがないわけではなく、人の息だけでもそれを損なうというのだ。政府は人数を制限することで害を和らげたいと考えている。村々がその収入に頼るようになった今、洞窟を再び閉鎖するのは実行不可能だと政府は主張している。","words":[["refurbish","freq:refurbish"],["ecotourism","freq:ecotourism"],["claustrophobia","freq:claustrophobia"],["clammy","freq:clammy"],["ceaseless","freq:ceaseless"],["gruelling","freq:gruelling"],["exhilaration","freq:exhilaration"],["virginal","freq:virginal"],["hermetic","freq:hermetic"],["subsist","freq:subsist"],["torturous","freq:torturous"],["tedium","freq:tedium"],["inexhaustible","freq:inexhaustible"],["ameliorate","freq:ameliorate"],["unfeasible","freq:unfeasible"]],"qs":[{"q":"Who is advised not to go down into the caves?","o":["Children under ten","People who fear enclosed spaces","Anyone without a guide","People who cannot swim"],"a":1,"why":"anyone prone to claustrophobia should stay at the surface とある。"},{"q":"Why does the government say the caves cannot be closed again?","o":["The entrance cannot be sealed.","The crystals have already been removed.","The guides would protest.","Local villages now depend on the income."],"a":3,"why":"would be unfeasible now that the villages depend on the income とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0202","page":"freq","group":"2801〜2900","kind":"日曜の午後","text":"Sunday afternoons at my grandmother's house followed a pattern that never changed. My grandfather lay recumbent on the settee with a newspaper over his face. In the kitchen, my grandmother stood at the sink, her arms deep in suds, washing every utensil she owned. The baby, in a yellow romper, sat under the table and tried to gnaw the leg of a chair. I was eight, and my task was to open the tin of biscuits, a fiddly job that required a spoon to prise up the lid. Inside lay a pink confection from the delicatessen on the corner, saved for visitors. I was not allowed to touch it. My cousin would turn a somersault on the rug, and the cat would flit from the windowsill to the top of the cupboard. Nothing happened, and we were perfectly happy. Once, my grandfather fell asleep too near the fire, and the heat began to singe the edge of his newspaper. We could only gape. My grandmother seemed to stiffen for a moment, then crossed the room, removed the paper, and went back to the sink without a word.","ja":"祖母の家での日曜の午後は、決して変わらない型に従っていた。祖父は顔に新聞を載せて長いすに横になっていた。台所では祖母が流しに立ち、両腕をせっけんの泡に深く入れて、持っている台所用具を残らず洗っていた。黄色いつなぎ服を着た赤ん坊はテーブルの下に座り、椅子の脚をかじろうとしていた。私は8歳で、私の役目はビスケットの缶を開けることだった。ふたをこじ開けるのにスプーンが要る、細かくて面倒な仕事だ。中には、角の総菜店で買った、来客用に取ってあるピンク色の菓子が入っていた。私はそれに触ることを許されなかった。いとこは敷物の上ででんぐり返しをし、猫は窓枠から戸棚の上へとひらりと飛び移った。何も起こらず、私たちはまったく幸せだった。一度、祖父が火の近くで眠り込みすぎて、熱が新聞の端を焦がし始めたことがある。私たちはぽかんと口を開けて見ていることしかできなかった。祖母は一瞬身をこわばらせたように見えたが、それから部屋を横切り、新聞を取り除き、一言も言わずに流しへ戻った。","words":[["recumbent","freq:recumbent"],["settee","freq:settee"],["suds","freq:suds"],["utensil","freq:utensil"],["romper","freq:romper"],["gnaw","freq:gnaw"],["fiddly","freq:fiddly"],["prise","freq:prise"],["confection","freq:confection"],["delicatessen","freq:delicatessen"],["somersault","freq:somersault"],["flit","freq:flit"],["singe","freq:singe"],["gape","freq:gape"],["stiffen","freq:stiffen"]],"qs":[{"q":"What was the writer's job on Sunday afternoons?","o":["Opening the biscuit tin","Washing the dishes","Looking after the baby","Reading the newspaper aloud"],"a":0,"why":"my task was to open the tin of biscuits とある。"},{"q":"What did the grandmother do when the newspaper began to burn?","o":["She shouted for help.","She woke the grandfather.","She calmly removed it and returned to the sink.","She threw water on the fire."],"a":2,"why":"crossed the room, removed the paper, and went back to the sink without a word とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0203","page":"freq","group":"2801〜2900","kind":"遺言と図書館","text":"When the collector Edmund Vane died in 1961, his will caused a sensation. He was the possessor of forty thousand rare books, and he chose to bequeath them not to a university but to the public library of the small town where he was born. It was no happenstance. Vane had been refused a reader's ticket there as a boy, and he had never forgotten it. The will contained a fiendish condition. The library must keep every volume on open shelves, or lose them all. The trustees were appointed to liaise with the town council, and for years the arrangement worked. Then readers began to steal. Losses grew explosively in the 1980s, and one summer went catastrophically wrong when a flood reached the lower floor. Some councillors wanted to consign the collection to a locked store. The trustees refused. No committee, they said, could ordain a change that the donor had forbidden. Today a new system offers a compromise. Visitors may handle any book, but a camera records each one, and the personalization of every reader's card allows losses to be traced. Vane, one suspects, would have approved of the cunning.","ja":"収集家エドマンド・ベインが1961年に亡くなったとき、彼の遺言は大評判を巻き起こした。彼は4万冊の希少本の所有者であり、それらを大学にではなく、自分が生まれた小さな町の公立図書館に遺贈することを選んだのだ。それは偶然の出来事ではなかった。ベインは少年の頃、そこで閲覧証の発行を断られ、それを決して忘れなかった。遺言には極めて厄介な条件が含まれていた。図書館はすべての本を開架の棚に置かなければならず、さもなければ全部を失うというものだ。管財人たちが町議会と連絡を取り合うよう任命され、何年もの間、この取り決めはうまくいった。それから読者が盗み始めた。紛失は1980年代に爆発的に増え、ある夏には洪水が下の階に達して、壊滅的な事態になった。何人かの議員は、蔵書を鍵のかかった書庫に追いやりたがった。管財人たちは拒んだ。寄贈者が禁じた変更を、どんな委員会も定めることはできないと彼らは述べた。今日、新しい仕組みが妥協案を提供している。来館者はどの本でも手に取れるが、カメラが一冊ずつ記録し、閲覧証を一人ひとり個人向けに作り込むことで紛失を追跡できる。ベインはそのずる賢さを気に入ったのではないかと思われる。","words":[["possessor","freq:possessor"],["bequeath","freq:bequeath"],["happenstance","freq:happenstance"],["fiendish","freq:fiendish"],["liaise","freq:liaise"],["explosively","freq:explosively"],["catastrophically","freq:catastrophically"],["consign","freq:consign"],["ordain","freq:ordain"],["personalization","freq:personalization"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did Vane leave his books to the town library?","o":["The university had refused them.","He had been denied a reader's ticket there as a boy.","He owed the town money.","The library had bought them."],"a":1,"why":"Vane had been refused a reader's ticket there as a boy, and he had never forgotten it とある。"},{"q":"What condition did the will impose?","o":["The books must never leave the town.","Only scholars could read them.","The library must be renamed.","Every book must be kept on open shelves."],"a":3,"why":"The library must keep every volume on open shelves, or lose them all とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0204","page":"freq","group":"2901〜3000","kind":"世界一周の船と反乱","text":"In 1764, the merchant ship Halcyon left Bristol in an attempt to circumnavigate the globe. Her captain, Elias Morrow, was a taciturn man who rarely explained his orders. Problems began in the Pacific. For six weeks there was no wind, and a strange lassitude settled over the ship. Morrow believed that an indolent crew was a dangerous one, so he invented endless tasks. The men grew fractious. One sailor, a former buccaneer named Pike, became openly insubordinate and refused to climb the mast. Morrow decided to incarcerate him below deck. That night, a mutinous group gathered at the stern. According to the ship's log, several of them began to brandish knives. The first officer was in an unenviable position, standing alone between the crew and the captain's door. He did not let the knives daunt him. He simply reminded the men that none of them could navigate. Without the captain, the remorseless ocean would kill them all within a month. The men went back to their hammocks. Pike was released the next morning, and the wind returned two days later. The Halcyon reached Bristol in 1767. It was hardly a triumph. The ship was so damaged that she seemed to totter into the harbour, and her owners, facing an ignominious loss, never sent her to sea again.","ja":"1764年、商船ハルシオン号は世界を一周しようとブリストルを出港した。船長のイライアス・モローは無口な男で、命令の理由をめったに説明しなかった。問題は太平洋で始まった。6週間風がなく、奇妙な倦怠感が船を覆った。モローは、怠惰な乗組員は危険だと考え、際限なく仕事をこしらえた。男たちは怒りっぽくなった。パイクという元海賊の水夫が公然と反抗的になり、マストに登ることを拒んだ。モローは彼を甲板の下に監禁することにした。その夜、反乱を起こそうとする一団が船尾に集まった。航海日誌によれば、そのうちの数人がナイフを振りかざし始めた。一等航海士は、乗組員と船長室の扉の間に一人で立つという、ありがたくない立場にあった。彼はナイフにひるまなかった。ただ、誰も航海術を知らないことを男たちに思い出させた。船長がいなければ、容赦のない海が1か月以内に全員を殺すだろう、と。男たちはハンモックに戻った。パイクは翌朝釈放され、2日後に風が戻った。ハルシオン号は1767年にブリストルに着いた。とても凱旋とは言えなかった。船はひどく傷んでいて、よろめきながら港に入るように見え、不名誉な損失に直面した船主たちは二度とこの船を海に出さなかった。","words":[["circumnavigate","freq:circumnavigate"],["taciturn","freq:taciturn"],["lassitude","freq:lassitude"],["indolent","freq:indolent"],["fractious","freq:fractious"],["buccaneer","freq:buccaneer"],["insubordinate","freq:insubordinate"],["incarcerate","freq:incarcerate"],["mutinous","freq:mutinous"],["brandish","freq:brandish"],["unenviable","freq:unenviable"],["daunt","freq:daunt"],["remorseless","freq:remorseless"],["totter","freq:totter"],["ignominious","freq:ignominious"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the captain keep inventing tasks for the crew?","o":["He wanted to finish the voyage faster","The ship needed urgent repairs","The owners had ordered him to do so","He thought an idle crew was dangerous"],"a":3,"why":"Morrow believed that an indolent crew was a dangerous one とある。"},{"q":"How did the first officer stop the mutiny?","o":["He promised the men more pay","He threatened them with a pistol","He pointed out that none of them could navigate","He released Pike at once"],"a":2,"why":"He simply reminded the men that none of them could navigate とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0205","page":"freq","group":"2901〜3000","kind":"洪水を再現する野外劇","text":"Every summer, the town of Wexley stages an outdoor play in which local people reenact the flood of 1852. For decades, it was a modest affair. This year, the committee hired a professional director, Carla Voss, and the result has divided the town. Voss wanted verisimilitude. She pumped real water across the stage and trained forty volunteers for three months. The opening scene is stunning. The roar of the river seems to resound across the whole valley, and on the first night the sight of the water appeared to transfix the audience. The applause at the interval was rapturous. The second half is less successful. The lead actor, a television star, gives a histrionic performance, full of shouting and tears. One local critic called him an exhibitionist whose accent was pure affectation. He also managed to mangle several of the most famous lines. Worse, the ending is anticlimactic: the flood simply stops, and a narrator steps forward to interject a short history lesson. Some reviews have been laudatory. A national newspaper chose to extol the production as a model of community theatre. Others were derisive. Still, nobody denies that the first hour can engross even small children, and tickets for the remaining nights have sold out.","ja":"ウェクスリーの町では毎年夏、地元の人々が1852年の洪水を再現する野外劇が上演される。何十年もの間、それはささやかな催しだった。今年、実行委員会はプロの演出家カーラ・ヴォスを雇い、その結果が町を二分している。ヴォスは迫真性を求めた。彼女は本物の水を舞台に流し、40人のボランティアを3か月間訓練した。冒頭の場面は見事だ。川のごう音は谷全体に鳴り響くようで、初日の夜、水の光景は観客をくぎ付けにしたようだった。休憩時の拍手は熱狂的だった。後半はそれほどうまくいっていない。主演のテレビスターは、叫びと涙に満ちた芝居がかった演技をする。ある地元の批評家は彼を、なまりがまったくの見せかけにすぎない目立ちたがり屋と呼んだ。彼はまた、最も有名なせりふのいくつかを台無しにした。さらに悪いことに、結末は拍子抜けだ。洪水はただ止まり、語り手が進み出て短い歴史の講義を差し挟むのである。称賛の批評もある。ある全国紙はこの上演を地域演劇の手本として激賞した。あざけるような批評もあった。それでも、最初の1時間が小さな子どもでさえ夢中にさせることは誰も否定せず、残りの夜のチケットは売り切れている。","words":[["reenact","freq:reenact"],["verisimilitude","freq:verisimilitude"],["resound","freq:resound"],["transfix","freq:transfix"],["rapturous","freq:rapturous"],["histrionic","freq:histrionic"],["exhibitionist","freq:exhibitionist"],["affectation","freq:affectation"],["mangle","freq:mangle"],["anticlimactic","freq:anticlimactic"],["interject","freq:interject"],["laudatory","freq:laudatory"],["extol","freq:extol"],["derisive","freq:derisive"],["engross","freq:engross"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the new director do to make the play realistic?","o":["She hired only professional actors","She moved the play indoors","She rewrote the ending","She used real water on the stage"],"a":3,"why":"Voss wanted verisimilitude. She pumped real water across the stage とある。"},{"q":"What is one criticism of the second half?","o":["The water stopped working","The lead actor's performance was exaggerated","The volunteers forgot their lines","The audience left early"],"a":1,"why":"The lead actor ... gives a histrionic performance とあり、批評家に酷評されている。"}]},
{"id":"f-0206","page":"freq","group":"2901〜3000","kind":"新人党首の半年","text":"When Mara Teles was chosen to lead the Reform Party last spring, few people expected much. She was a neophyte who had entered parliament only three years earlier, and her rivals were openly scornful. Six months later, she has managed to galvanize a party that had seemed close to collapse. Her first test came quickly. A leaked report on party finances caused a brouhaha, and its effects continued to reverberate for weeks. Her predecessor would have tried to dither until the story faded. Teles instead chose to ventilate the whole matter at a public meeting, answering questions for four hours. Critics had accused the old leadership of timidity. Nobody says that now. Her ideas have also begun to crystallize. She wants to decentralize power, moving tax decisions from the capital to the regions. Older members dislike the plan, and she has made little effort to mollify them. Nor does she try to ingratiate herself with journalists. Some colleagues worry about her belligerence in debates. Yet the ardor of her younger supporters is obvious at every rally. Next month, the party will formally induct her as leader at its annual conference. Polls suggest that, for the first time in a decade, it could win the next election.","ja":"昨年の春、マーラ・テレスが改革党の党首に選ばれたとき、多くを期待する人はほとんどいなかった。彼女はわずか3年前に議会に入ったばかりの新参者で、ライバルたちは公然と軽蔑していた。6か月後、彼女は崩壊寸前に見えた党を奮い立たせることに成功している。最初の試練はすぐに来た。党の財政に関する報告書が流出して大騒ぎになり、その影響は何週間も波紋を広げ続けた。前任者なら、話が消えるまでぐずぐず迷おうとしただろう。テレスはそうせず、公開の集会で問題全体を公に議論することを選び、4時間にわたって質問に答えた。批判する人々は、かつての指導部を臆病だと非難していた。今は誰もそう言わない。彼女の考えも明確になり始めている。彼女は権力を分散させ、税に関する決定を首都から地方に移したいと考えている。古参の党員はこの計画を嫌っているが、彼女は彼らをなだめる努力をほとんどしていない。記者の機嫌を取ろうともしない。同僚の中には、討論での彼女のけんか腰を心配する者もいる。それでも、若い支持者たちの熱意はどの集会でも明らかだ。来月、党は年次大会で彼女を正式に党首に就任させる。世論調査によれば、党は10年ぶりに次の選挙で勝つ可能性がある。","words":[["neophyte","freq:neophyte"],["scornful","freq:scornful"],["galvanize","freq:galvanize"],["brouhaha","freq:brouhaha"],["reverberate","freq:reverberate"],["dither","freq:dither"],["ventilate","freq:ventilate"],["timidity","freq:timidity"],["crystallize","freq:crystallize"],["decentralize","freq:decentralize"],["mollify","freq:mollify"],["ingratiate","freq:ingratiate"],["belligerence","freq:belligerence"],["ardor","freq:ardor"],["induct","freq:induct"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did Teles respond to the leaked finance report?","o":["She waited for the story to fade","She blamed her predecessor","She discussed it openly at a public meeting","She resigned from her post"],"a":2,"why":"Teles instead chose to ventilate the whole matter at a public meeting とある。"},{"q":"What does Teles want to change?","o":["She wants tax decisions moved to the regions","She wants to raise taxes in the capital","She wants fewer party conferences","She wants journalists kept out of rallies"],"a":0,"why":"She wants to decentralize power, moving tax decisions from the capital to the regions とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0207","page":"freq","group":"2901〜3000","kind":"子ども基金の横領事件","text":"For thirty years, the Harlow Children's Fund was known for its munificence. It paid for schools, clinics and playgrounds across the region. Last month, auditors found that its finance director had managed to embezzle almost two million dollars. The details are disquieting. The director, Paul Renn, moved the money in small, surreptitious transfers over eight years. He contrived to hide them by recording each one as a consulting fee. When a junior accountant began to remonstrate with him in 2019, Renn told her that the fund simply needed to economize, and she was moved to another office. He continued to dissemble until the day of his arrest. The news has done more than unsettle the staff. It will appall the thousands of small donors who trusted the fund. Reporters now besiege its headquarters every morning. The board's first statement was euphemistic, referring only to accounting irregularities, and newspapers were quick to revile its members for it. The founder, Edith Harlow, who is ninety-one, has responded with surprising magnanimity. She says she feels sorrow rather than anger. She has no qualm, however, about what must happen next: every member of the board has been asked to resign, and the police have been given all the records.","ja":"30年にわたり、ハーロウ子ども基金は気前のよさで知られていた。地域全体の学校や診療所、遊び場の費用を出してきた。先月、監査人は、財務責任者が200万ドル近くを横領していたことを突き止めた。詳細は人を不安にさせるものだ。責任者のポール・レンは、8年にわたり少額のこそこそした送金で金を動かした。その一つ一つをコンサルタント料として記録することで、どうにか隠していた。2019年に若手の経理担当者が彼に抗議し始めると、レンは基金はただ節約する必要があるのだと言い、彼女は別の部署に移された。彼は逮捕の日までしらばくれ続けた。この知らせは職員を動揺させただけではない。基金を信頼していた何千人もの小口の寄付者をがく然とさせるだろう。記者たちは今、毎朝本部に殺到している。理事会の最初の声明は婉曲的で、「会計上の不備」に触れただけだったため、新聞はすぐに理事たちを激しく非難した。91歳の創設者イーディス・ハーロウは、驚くほどの寛大さで応じた。怒りよりも悲しみを感じると言う。しかし、次に行うべきことについてはためらいがない。理事全員が辞任を求められ、警察にはすべての記録が渡された。","words":[["munificence","freq:munificence"],["embezzle","freq:embezzle"],["disquieting","freq:disquieting"],["surreptitious","freq:surreptitious"],["contrived","freq:contrive"],["remonstrate","freq:remonstrate"],["economize","freq:economize"],["dissemble","freq:dissemble"],["unsettle","freq:unsettle"],["appall","freq:appall"],["besiege","freq:besiege"],["euphemistic","freq:euphemistic"],["revile","freq:revile"],["magnanimity","freq:magnanimity"],["qualm","freq:qualm"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did Renn hide the transfers?","o":["He used a foreign bank","He destroyed the records","He paid the auditors","He recorded them as consulting fees"],"a":3,"why":"He contrived to hide them by recording each one as a consulting fee とある。"},{"q":"What has the founder done in response?","o":["She has defended the board","She has asked the whole board to resign","She has closed the fund","She has refused to talk to the police"],"a":1,"why":"every member of the board has been asked to resign とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0208","page":"freq","group":"2901〜3000","kind":"言葉の意味をめぐる本","text":"It is a truism that words mean more than dictionaries say. A dictionary tells us what a word denotes. It rarely tells us everything the word may connote. Consider the words thrifty and stingy. Juxtapose them, and the incongruity is clear: they describe the same behaviour, yet one praises and the other blames. Every speaker brings some preconception to such choices. The linguist Dana Okoro has spent her career trying to demystify this process. Her new book is meant to edify general readers rather than specialists, and it largely succeeds. She does not deprecate everyday speech, as older scholars often did. She argues instead that rigid rules stultify a language. Her central claim is simple. Communication must presuppose shared experience. If meaning existed only inside one mind, we would be trapped in a kind of solipsism, each of us talking to ourselves. Okoro admits that her theory is still inchoate in places. She tends to ruminate on a single example for many pages, an idiosyncrasy that some readers will enjoy and others will not. There is also a certain perversity in a book about clear language that uses so many technical terms. Even so, it is the most stimulating work on meaning published this year.","ja":"言葉が辞書に書かれている以上のことを意味するのは、自明の理だ。辞書は、ある語が何を指すかを教えてくれる。その語が言外に意味しうることのすべては、めったに教えてくれない。thrifty（倹約家の）と stingy（けちな）という語を考えてみよう。並べてみると、不釣り合いは明らかだ。同じ行動を表しているのに、一方はほめ、他方は責める。話し手は誰でも、こうした選択に何らかの先入観を持ち込む。言語学者のダナ・オコロは、この過程をわかりやすく解き明かそうと研究生活を送ってきた。彼女の新著は専門家ではなく一般の読者を啓発するためのもので、おおむね成功している。彼女は、昔の学者がよくしたように日常の話し言葉を軽んじたりしない。むしろ、硬直した規則は言語を鈍らせると主張する。中心となる主張は単純だ。意思の伝達は、共有された経験を前提としなければならない。もし意味が一つの心の中にしか存在しないなら、私たちは一種の独我論に閉じ込められ、めいめいが独り言を言っていることになる。オコロは、自分の理論がところどころまだ未完成であることを認めている。彼女は一つの例について何ページも思いめぐらす傾向があり、この特異な癖を楽しむ読者もいれば、そうでない読者もいるだろう。明快な言葉についての本がこれほど多くの専門用語を使うことにも、ある種のひねくれがある。それでも、これは今年出版された意味に関する著作の中で最も刺激的なものだ。","words":[["truism","freq:truism"],["connote","freq:connote"],["Juxtapose","freq:juxtapose"],["incongruity","freq:incongruity"],["preconception","freq:preconception"],["demystify","freq:demystify"],["edify","freq:edify"],["deprecate","freq:deprecate"],["stultify","freq:stultify"],["presuppose","freq:presuppose"],["solipsism","freq:solipsism"],["inchoate","freq:inchoate"],["ruminate","freq:ruminate"],["idiosyncrasy","freq:idiosyncrasy"],["perversity","freq:perversity"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why does the speaker mention the words thrifty and stingy?","o":["To show that dictionaries are often wrong","To explain where the words came from","To show that words for the same behaviour can carry different judgments","To prove that rules are necessary"],"a":2,"why":"they describe the same behaviour, yet one praises and the other blames とある。"},{"q":"What is one weakness of Okoro's book?","o":["It uses many technical terms","It ignores everyday speech","It is written only for specialists","It is too short"],"a":0,"why":"a book about clear language that uses so many technical terms とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0209","page":"freq","group":"2901〜3000","kind":"山の村の蜂蜜","text":"High in the Karven mountains, the village of Sollin has kept bees for a thousand years. Nobody knows who first managed to domesticate the local black bee, but villagers venerate a saint who is said to have done so. The best-known beekeeper today is Tomas Reil, a wizened man of eighty-four. He climbs to his hives each morning, though the steep path makes him wheeze. In June the meadows teem with wildflowers, and the scent seems to suffuse the whole valley. Reil says that the first warm day of the season can still exhilarate him. His methods look antediluvian. He uses clay hives and no machines. Yet buyers travel for days to reach him. Sollin's reputation was nearly destroyed in the 1990s, when several traders began to adulterate the honey with sugar syrup. There was loud lamentation in the village, and the council voted to proscribe the practice. Offenders now lose their licence for life. Reil has no patience with indolence. Young men who loiter in the square are quickly handed a bucket. He likes to intersperse his lessons with old songs. A poet once tried to immortalize him in verse; he says he would rather be remembered for the honey.","ja":"カルヴェン山脈の高地にあるソリン村は、千年にわたってミツバチを飼ってきた。地元の黒いミツバチを最初に飼いならしたのが誰なのかは誰も知らないが、村人たちは、それを成し遂げたとされる聖人を崇敬している。今日最もよく知られた養蜂家はトマス・ライルで、しわだらけの84歳の男だ。彼は毎朝巣箱まで登るが、急な道のせいでぜいぜい息をする。6月には牧草地が野の花で満ちあふれ、その香りが谷全体を満たすようだ。ライルは、季節の最初の暖かい日には今でも心が浮き立つと言う。彼のやり方はひどく時代遅れに見える。粘土の巣箱を使い、機械は使わない。それでも、買い手は何日もかけて彼のもとへやって来る。ソリンの評判は1990年代に、数人の商人が蜂蜜に砂糖のシロップを混ぜ始めたとき、ほとんど台無しになった。村には大きな嘆きの声が上がり、村議会はこの行為を禁止することを決議した。違反者は今では免許を一生失う。ライルは怠惰に我慢がならない。広場でぶらついている若者は、すぐにバケツを渡される。彼は教えの合間に古い歌を所々に入れるのが好きだ。ある詩人がかつて彼を詩にして不滅にしようとしたが、彼は蜂蜜で覚えられるほうがいいと言う。","words":[["domesticate","freq:domesticate"],["venerate","freq:venerate"],["wizened","freq:wizened"],["wheeze","freq:wheeze"],["teem","freq:teem"],["suffuse","freq:suffuse"],["exhilarate","freq:exhilarate"],["antediluvian","freq:antediluvian"],["adulterate","freq:adulterate"],["lamentation","freq:lamentation"],["proscribe","freq:proscribe"],["indolence","freq:indolence"],["loiter","freq:loiter"],["intersperse","freq:intersperse"],["immortalize","freq:immortalize"]],"qs":[{"q":"What nearly destroyed the village's reputation in the 1990s?","o":["A disease among the bees","A series of cold summers","The death of a famous beekeeper","Traders mixing sugar syrup into the honey"],"a":3,"why":"several traders began to adulterate the honey with sugar syrup とある。"},{"q":"What is true of Tomas Reil's methods?","o":["He uses the newest machines","He uses clay hives and no machines","He buys bees from other villages","He sells only to local people"],"a":1,"why":"He uses clay hives and no machines とある。"}]},
{"id":"f-0210","page":"freq","group":"2901〜3000","kind":"裁判報道のあり方","text":"The trial of the actor Julian Vane ended last week, but the debate about how it was reported has only begun. For two months, newspapers fed a prurient interest in the case. Editors seemed to salivate over every detail of the actor's private life. Headlines were written to tantalize readers, promising secrets that the articles rarely delivered. The judge, Helen Marsh, was openly disdainful of this coverage. She warned reporters three times, and their behaviour continued to exasperate her. The worst moment involved a nineteen-year-old witness. She was an ingenuous young woman who had never spoken in public. Photographers followed her home, hoping to unnerve her before she gave evidence. She told the court that she had begun to agonize over every word, afraid of how it would appear in print. Her testimony, which was important, became hesitant and confused. Lawyers are now asking whether new rules are needed. Some propose a ban on naming witnesses until a trial is over. Editors reply that such rules would encumber honest reporting and hide the courts from the public. Both arguments have force. What is certain is that the reverberation of this case will be felt in courtrooms and newsrooms for many years.","ja":"俳優ジュリアン・ヴェインの裁判は先週終わったが、それがどう報じられたかをめぐる議論は始まったばかりだ。2か月の間、新聞はこの事件へのわいせつな興味をあおった。編集者たちは、俳優の私生活のあらゆる細部によだれを流しているようだった。見出しは読者をじらすように書かれ、記事がめったに明かさない秘密を約束していた。裁判官のヘレン・マーシュは、こうした報道を公然と軽蔑していた。彼女は記者たちに3度警告したが、彼らの振る舞いは彼女をいら立たせ続けた。最悪の場面は、19歳の証人に関するものだった。彼女は人前で話したことのない、無邪気な若い女性だった。カメラマンたちは、証言の前に彼女をおじけづかせようと、家までつけ回した。彼女は法廷で、活字でどう見えるかが怖くて、一言一言にひどく悩むようになったと語った。重要だった彼女の証言は、ためらいがちで混乱したものになった。法律家たちは今、新しい規則が必要かどうかを問うている。裁判が終わるまで証人の名前を出すことを禁じるよう提案する者もいる。編集者たちは、そうした規則は誠実な報道を妨げ、裁判所を国民の目から隠すことになると答える。どちらの主張にも一理ある。確かなのは、この事件の余波が法廷でも報道の現場でも長年にわたって感じられるだろうということだ。","words":[["prurient","freq:prurient"],["salivate","freq:salivate"],["tantalize","freq:tantalize"],["disdainful","freq:disdainful"],["exasperate","freq:exasperate"],["ingenuous","freq:ingenuous"],["unnerve","freq:unnerve"],["agonize","freq:agonize"],["encumber","freq:encumber"],["reverberation","freq:reverberation"]],"qs":[{"q":"Why did the young witness's testimony become confused?","o":["She worried about how her words would be reported","She did not understand the questions","The judge interrupted her repeatedly","She had been paid by a newspaper"],"a":0,"why":"she had begun to agonize over every word, afraid of how it would appear in print とある。"},{"q":"What do some lawyers propose?","o":["Closing all trials to the public","Fining editors after every trial","Banning the naming of witnesses until a trial ends","Letting judges write the headlines"],"a":2,"why":"Some propose a ban on naming witnesses until a trial is over とある。"}]},
{"id":"i-0001","page":"idioms","group":"その他 ① 動詞＋副詞（自動詞）","kind":"製鉄所の交渉決裂","text":"Talks over the future of the Brenton steelworks ended on Friday with no agreement. The negotiations had begun to drag on long before that. They started in March, and by June both sides were exhausted. Tempers would flare up whenever jobs were mentioned. At one meeting, the union's chief negotiator began to lash out at the managers, calling them liars, and the chairman had to stop the session until everyone could simmer down. Smaller problems made things worse. Lawyers for the company would butt in whenever a worker tried to speak. Local politicians were invited to chime in with their own ideas, which helped nobody. When the union asked for detailed figures on the company's debts, the managers would simply clam up. By autumn, the company expected the union to cave in. Its members had gone without full pay for months. Instead, they voted by a large majority to continue. The managers then announced that they would press ahead with the closure of the older furnace, with or without an agreement. That announcement ended the talks. Observers had long feared that the process would fizzle out in this way. A government mediator has now offered to help, but neither side has yet accepted the offer.","ja":"ブレントン製鉄所の将来をめぐる協議は金曜日、合意のないまま終わった。交渉はそれよりずっと前からだらだらと長引き始めていた。3月に始まり、6月には双方とも疲れ切っていた。雇用の話が出るたびに怒りが再燃した。ある会合では、組合の交渉責任者が経営陣をうそつきと呼んで激しく非難し始め、議長は皆が気を静めるまで会議を中断しなければならなかった。細かな問題が事態を悪化させた。労働者が話そうとするたびに、会社側の弁護士が口を挟んだ。地元の政治家たちが招かれて自分の考えを述べて話に加わったが、誰の助けにもならなかった。組合が会社の負債の詳しい数字を求めると、経営陣はただ口を閉ざした。秋までに、会社は組合が屈するものと見込んでいた。組合員は何か月も満額の賃金なしで過ごしていたからだ。ところが彼らは、大差で継続を決めた。すると経営陣は、合意があろうとなかろうと古いほうの溶鉱炉の閉鎖を推し進めると発表した。この発表が協議を終わらせた。事情を見守ってきた人々は、交渉がこのように立ち消えになることを長く恐れていた。政府の調停人が今、協力を申し出ているが、どちらの側もまだその申し出を受け入れていない。","words":[["drag on","idioms:1:drag on"],["flare up","idioms:1:flare up"],["lash out","idioms:1:lash out"],["simmer down","idioms:1:simmer down"],["butt in","idioms:1:butt in"],["chime in","idioms:1:chime in"],["clam up","idioms:1:clam up"],["cave in","idioms:1:cave in"],["press ahead","idioms:1:press ahead"],["fizzle out","idioms:1:fizzle out"]],"qs":[{"q":"How did the managers react when asked for figures on the company's debts?","o":["They published a full report","They blamed the politicians","They asked for more time","They refused to say anything"],"a":3,"why":"the managers would simply clam up とある。"},{"q":"What finally ended the talks?","o":["The union voted to stop the strike","The mediator left the meeting","The managers said they would close the older furnace regardless","The politicians withdrew their support"],"a":2,"why":"they would press ahead with the closure of the older furnace ... That announcement ended the talks とある。"}]},
{"id":"i-0002","page":"idioms","group":"その他 ① 動詞＋副詞（自動詞）","kind":"受験までの3週間","text":"Kenji had three weeks before his university entrance exam, and his teacher's advice was blunt: it was time to buckle down. Until then, he had not taken his studies seriously. He would nod off over his history notes after dinner. In class, he tended to zone out whenever the teacher explained grammar. His mother warned him that he would have to shape up or repeat the year. So he made a plan. He got up at five every morning and tried to knuckle down for two hours before school. It was not easy. His younger brothers played video games in the next room, and he had to shout at them to pipe down. He also worried too much. Every time he happened to slip up on a practice test, he lost confidence for days. A week before the exam, he almost decided to chicken out and apply to an easier college instead. His grandfather told him to lighten up. Nobody is ever fully prepared, the old man said; most people simply muddle through and learn as they go. Kenji laughed for the first time in weeks. He took the exam, made a few mistakes, and passed. He still gets up at five, though nobody knows why.","ja":"ケンジの大学入試まで3週間。先生の助言は率直だった。本腰を入れる時だ、と。それまで彼は勉強を真剣にやってこなかった。夕食の後、歴史のノートを前にうとうとしていた。授業では、先生が文法を説明するたびにぼんやりしがちだった。母親は、行いを改めなければ留年することになると警告した。そこで彼は計画を立てた。毎朝5時に起き、学校の前に2時間、真剣に取り組もうとした。簡単ではなかった。弟たちが隣の部屋でテレビゲームをしていて、静かにしろと怒鳴らなければならなかった。彼は心配もしすぎた。模擬試験でうっかり間違えるたびに、何日も自信を失った。試験の1週間前には、おじけづいてやめ、もっと易しい大学に出願しようと決めかけた。祖父は、気楽に構えろと言った。完全に準備ができている人などいない、たいていの人は何とか切り抜けながら学んでいくものだ、と老人は言った。ケンジは数週間ぶりに笑った。彼は試験を受け、いくつか間違え、合格した。今でも5時に起きているが、なぜなのかは誰も知らない。","words":[["buckle down","idioms:1:buckle down"],["nod off","idioms:1:nod off"],["zone out","idioms:1:zone out"],["shape up","idioms:1:shape up"],["knuckle down","idioms:1:knuckle down"],["pipe down","idioms:1:pipe down"],["slip up","idioms:1:slip up"],["chicken out","idioms:1:chicken out"],["lighten up","idioms:1:lighten up"],["muddle through","idioms:1:muddle through"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did Kenji almost decide to do a week before the exam?","o":["Give up and apply to an easier college","Study all night","Ask his teacher for help","Move out of his home"],"a":0,"why":"he almost decided to chicken out and apply to an easier college instead とある。"},{"q":"What was his grandfather's advice?","o":["To get up even earlier","To take more practice tests","To stop studying history","To relax, because nobody is ever fully prepared"],"a":3,"why":"His grandfather told him to lighten up. Nobody is ever fully prepared とある。"}]},
{"id":"i-0003","page":"idioms","group":"その他 ① 動詞＋副詞（自動詞）","kind":"自転車選手の引退","text":"After twenty-two years as a professional cyclist, Lena Brandt has decided to bow out. She announced her retirement yesterday at a press conference in Hamburg. Her final season was difficult. Injuries seemed to crop up every few weeks, and her results began to slide. She says her form appeared to bottom out in July, when she finished last in a mountain stage. Many riders would have stopped then. Brandt chose to soldier on until the end of the season. Her last race was the hardest. On a climb in heavy rain, her bicycle's gears began to conk out, and she rode the final ten kilometres in a single gear. At the finish, she was so exhausted that she seemed about to keel over. Yesterday, she thanked her teammates and her mechanics. When she mentioned her late father, who taught her to ride, she began to choke up. Her voice started to trail off, and for a moment she could not continue. Then she smiled. She admitted that, as a teenager, she used to duck out of school early to train. She also announced a new project: a cycling school for girls. Several sponsors have already agreed to chip in, and the first classes will begin next spring.","ja":"プロの自転車選手として22年を過ごしたレナ・ブラントが、身を引くことを決めた。昨日、ハンブルクの記者会見で引退を発表した。最後のシーズンは苦しかった。けがが数週間おきに不意に持ち上がり、成績は下がり始めた。本人によれば、調子は7月に底を打ったようで、山岳ステージで最下位に終わった。多くの選手ならそこでやめただろう。ブラントはシーズンの終わりまでくじけず続けることを選んだ。最後のレースが最も厳しかった。激しい雨の中の登りで自転車の変速機が故障し始め、最後の10キロを1つのギアだけで走った。ゴールでは疲れ切っていて、今にも倒れそうに見えた。昨日、彼女はチームメートと整備士たちに感謝を述べた。自転車の乗り方を教えてくれた亡き父に触れたとき、彼女は言葉に詰まり始めた。声が次第に小さくなり、しばらく続けることができなかった。それから彼女はほほえんだ。10代のころ、練習のために学校を早くこっそり抜け出していたことを認めた。また、新しい計画も発表した。女の子のための自転車学校だ。すでにいくつかのスポンサーが金を出し合うことに同意しており、最初の授業は来年の春に始まる。","words":[["bow out","idioms:1:bow out"],["crop up","idioms:1:crop up"],["bottom out","idioms:1:bottom out"],["soldier on","idioms:1:soldier on"],["conk out","idioms:1:conk out"],["keel over","idioms:1:keel over"],["choke up","idioms:1:choke up"],["trail off","idioms:1:trail off"],["duck out","idioms:1:duck out"],["chip in","idioms:1:chip in"]],"qs":[{"q":"What happened during Brandt's last race?","o":["She won the mountain stage","Her gears stopped working properly","She crashed in the rain","She left before the finish"],"a":1,"why":"her bicycle's gears began to conk out, and she rode the final ten kilometres in a single gear とある。"},{"q":"What new project did she announce?","o":["A book about her father","A professional team in Hamburg","A cycling school for girls","A race for retired riders"],"a":2,"why":"a new project: a cycling school for girls とある。"}]},
{"id":"i-0004","page":"idioms","group":["scrape","その他 ④ 動詞＋副詞＋前置詞"],"kind":"港町の小さな食堂と税金","text":"For years, small restaurants in the port city of Marlow could barely scrape by. Rents were high, and tourists came only in summer. Then the city council decided to clamp down on businesses that failed to pay local taxes. Inspectors began to home in on the old harbour district, where records were often poor. Owners complained that the council had chosen to gang up on the weakest businesses while ignoring large hotels. Some hotel managers, they said, knew how to cozy up to officials. One restaurant owner, Rosa Delgado, had more trouble than most. It had been a mistake to fall out with her accountant the year before, and her books were a mess. When a deadline began to bear down on her, friends advised her to find a clever lawyer and wriggle out of the fine. Others told her to suck up to the chief inspector. She refused. She said she would not weasel out of a debt she really owed. Instead, she asked for more time, sold her car, and paid in full. Her restaurant managed to scrape through the winter. The following spring, the council introduced lower taxes for small businesses, and Delgado was invited to explain to officials how the old system had failed.","ja":"何年もの間、港町マーロウの小さな食堂は、どうにか暮らしていくのがやっとだった。家賃は高く、観光客が来るのは夏だけだった。そんなとき市議会は、地方税を払わない事業者を厳しく取り締まることを決めた。検査官たちは、帳簿が不十分なことの多い古い港の地区に的を絞り始めた。店主たちは、市議会が大きなホテルを見逃しながら、最も弱い事業者を集団で攻撃することを選んだと不満を言った。ホテルの支配人の中には役人に取り入るすべを知っている者がいる、とも言った。食堂の店主ローザ・デルガドは、たいていの人より苦労した。前の年に会計士と仲たがいしたのが間違いで、帳簿はめちゃくちゃだった。期限が迫ってくると、友人たちは、腕のいい弁護士を見つけて罰金をうまく逃れるよう勧めた。主任検査官にごまをすれと言う人もいた。彼女は断った。本当に負っている借りをずるく逃れることはしない、と言った。代わりに、彼女は猶予を求め、車を売り、全額を払った。彼女の食堂はその冬をかろうじて切り抜けた。翌年の春、市議会は小さな事業者のために低い税率を導入し、デルガドは、古い制度がどう失敗したかを役人に説明するよう招かれた。","words":[["scrape by","idioms:1:scrape by"],["clamp down on","idioms:4:clamp down on"],["home in on","idioms:4:home in on"],["gang up on","idioms:4:gang up on"],["cozy up to","idioms:4:cozy up to"],["fall out with","idioms:4:fall out with"],["bear down on","idioms:4:bear down on"],["wriggle out of","idioms:4:wriggle out of"],["suck up to","idioms:4:suck up to"],["weasel out of","idioms:4:weasel out of"],["scrape through","idioms:1:scrape through"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did restaurant owners complain about?","o":["The inspectors asked for bribes","The harbour district was being rebuilt","Tourists had stopped coming","The council targeted weak businesses and ignored big hotels"],"a":3,"why":"the council had chosen to gang up on the weakest businesses while ignoring large hotels とある。"},{"q":"How did Delgado deal with the fine?","o":["She hired a clever lawyer","She asked for time, sold her car and paid it all","She closed her restaurant","She flattered the chief inspector"],"a":1,"why":"she asked for more time, sold her car, and paid in full とある。"}]},
{"id":"i-0005","page":"idioms","group":"その他 ② 動詞＋副詞（他動詞）","kind":"おもちゃ会社の破綻","text":"The collapse of the toy company Brightway has become a lesson in how not to run a business. Five years ago, its factories would churn out two million plastic robots a year, and the firm seemed to rake in money without effort. Its founder, Dale Kirby, made a series of bad decisions. To cut costs, he chose to farm out production to cheaper suppliers. Quality fell at once. He then decided to jack up prices by thirty percent. Parents felt that the company was trying to rip off its most loyal customers, and many refused to fork over the extra money. Meanwhile, rivals began to crank out cheaper copies. Kirby did not save for hard times. A careful owner would squirrel away some profit each year. He preferred to fritter away millions on a private jet and a football team. He continued to dole out large bonuses to senior managers even as sales fell. When workers complained, he tried to buy off their leaders with promotions. Last month, the company ran out of cash. A rival firm moved quickly to scoop up its brand name and its best designers for a fraction of their former value. Kirby says he was simply unlucky. Few of his former employees agree.","ja":"おもちゃ会社ブライトウェイの破綻は、事業をどう経営してはいけないかの教訓になった。5年前、同社の工場は年に200万個のプラスチックのロボットを大量に作り出し、会社は苦もなく大金を稼いでいるように見えた。創業者のデール・カービーは、まずい決定を重ねた。費用を削るため、生産をもっと安い業者に外注することにした。品質はたちまち落ちた。次に彼は、価格を30パーセントつり上げることにした。親たちは、会社が最も忠実な客からぼったくろうとしていると感じ、多くは余分な金をしぶしぶ払うことを拒んだ。その間に、競合他社はもっと安い模造品を次々に作り出し始めた。カービーは苦しい時に備えて蓄えなかった。慎重な経営者なら毎年いくらかの利益をため込むものだ。彼は、自家用ジェット機とサッカーチームに何百万ドルも浪費するほうを好んだ。売上が落ちる中でも、上級管理職に多額の賞与を分け与え続けた。従業員が不満を言うと、昇進で彼らのリーダーを買収しようとした。先月、会社は資金が尽きた。競合の一社がすばやく動き、ブランド名と最も優れたデザイナーたちを、かつての価値のごく一部でかっさらった。カービーは、ただ運が悪かっただけだと言う。元従業員でそれに同意する人はほとんどいない。","words":[["churn out","idioms:2:churn out"],["rake in","idioms:2:rake in"],["farm out","idioms:2:farm out"],["jack up","idioms:2:jack up"],["rip off","idioms:2:rip off"],["fork over","idioms:2:fork over"],["crank out","idioms:2:crank out"],["squirrel away","idioms:2:squirrel away"],["fritter away","idioms:2:fritter away"],["dole out","idioms:2:dole out"],["buy off","idioms:2:buy off"],["scoop up","idioms:2:scoop up"]],"qs":[{"q":"What happened when production was moved to cheaper suppliers?","o":["Sales rose sharply","The workers went on strike","The quality of the toys fell","The founder resigned"],"a":2,"why":"he chose to farm out production to cheaper suppliers. Quality fell at once とある。"},{"q":"How did Kirby use the company's profits?","o":["He spent millions on a jet and a football team","He saved them for hard times","He invested in new designs","He lowered prices for parents"],"a":0,"why":"He preferred to fritter away millions on a private jet and a football team とある。"}]},
{"id":"i-0006","page":"idioms","group":"その他 ② 動詞＋副詞（他動詞）","kind":"泥仕合の市長選","text":"The election for mayor of Castell has turned ugly. Last week, a newspaper reported that the current mayor, Ivo Sarno, once helped to hush up a safety problem at a city bridge. Sarno denies it. He says his opponent's team decided to cook up the story in order to stir up anger before the vote. He has even accused them of trying to trump up charges against his former assistant. His opponent, Greta Lind, rejects this. She says she did not need to dredge up anything; the documents were sent to reporters by a city engineer. She has continued to play up the issue at every rally, and her speeches whip up loud applause. Her advisers have reportedly asked her to tone down her language, fearing that voters are growing tired of the fight. Sarno's own party is uneasy. Senior members tried to smooth over the dispute by proposing an independent inquiry. The mayor refused, and chose to trot out a familiar excuse: that the timing was political. Voters seem unimpressed. In a poll published yesterday, most said that both candidates had behaved badly. Elections, however, tend to mete out punishment unevenly, and it is Sarno who has fallen ten points behind with a week to go.","ja":"カステル市長選は醜い争いになった。先週ある新聞が、現職のイヴォ・サルノ市長がかつて市の橋の安全上の問題をもみ消すのに手を貸したと報じた。サルノは否定している。投票の前に怒りを引き起こすために、対立候補の陣営がこの話をでっち上げることにしたのだ、と言う。彼は、相手陣営が自分の元秘書に対する罪をでっち上げようとしているとまで非難した。対立候補のグレタ・リンドはこれを退ける。何も蒸し返す必要はなかった、文書は市の技師が記者たちに送ったものだ、と言う。彼女はどの集会でもこの問題を強調し続け、その演説は大きな拍手をかき立てている。伝えられるところでは、顧問たちは、有権者が争いにうんざりしてきているのを恐れ、言葉づかいを和らげるよう彼女に求めた。サルノ自身の党も落ち着かない。幹部たちは、独立した調査を提案することで対立を取り繕おうとした。市長はこれを拒み、時期が政治的だ、というおなじみの言い訳を持ち出すことを選んだ。有権者は感心していないようだ。昨日発表された世論調査では、大半が両候補とも振る舞いが悪かったと答えた。しかし、選挙は罰を不均等に与えるもので、あと1週間というところで10ポイント後れを取ったのはサルノのほうだ。","words":[["hush up","idioms:2:hush up"],["cook up","idioms:2:cook up"],["stir up","idioms:2:stir up"],["trump up","idioms:2:trump up"],["dredge up","idioms:2:dredge up"],["play up","idioms:2:play up"],["whip up","idioms:2:whip up"],["tone down","idioms:2:tone down"],["smooth over","idioms:2:smooth over"],["trot out","idioms:2:trot out"],["mete out","idioms:2:mete out"]],"qs":[{"q":"According to Lind, where did the documents come from?","o":["Her own campaign team wrote them","A city engineer sent them to reporters","The mayor's former assistant leaked them","They were found at the bridge"],"a":1,"why":"the documents were sent to reporters by a city engineer とある。"},{"q":"How did the mayor respond to the proposal for an independent inquiry?","o":["He welcomed it","He asked for a delay","He resigned from his party","He refused and said the timing was political"],"a":3,"why":"The mayor refused, and chose to trot out a familiar excuse: that the timing was political とある。"}]},
{"id":"i-0007","page":"idioms","group":"その他 ② 動詞＋副詞（他動詞）","kind":"警察署長の別のやり方","text":"When Chief Ana Ferreira took charge of the police in Porto Novo, the city had the highest murder rate in the country. Gangs would gun down rivals in daylight, and witnesses were too frightened to speak. Her predecessors had promised to stamp out the gangs by force. Ferreira tried something different. She spent her first month trying to sound out priests, teachers and shopkeepers about what they needed. She managed to rope in several former gang members as advisers. Together, they sat down to thrash out a plan based on jobs and schools as well as arrests. Not everyone approved. The city council voted to shoot down her first budget. Some politicians accused her of trying to butter up criminals. A curfew for teenagers, which she had opposed, was introduced anyway, until a court chose to strike down the rule. Slowly, the plan worked. Older gang leaders were arrested, and special units were sent to mop up the smaller groups that remained. Teenagers who used to egg on the fighters found work in a new training centre. Murders have fallen by half in three years. Ferreira is careful not to claim victory. She says only that she hopes these years will usher in a calmer period for the city.","ja":"アナ・フェレイラ署長がポルト・ノヴォの警察を引き継いだとき、この市の殺人発生率は国内で最も高かった。ギャングは白昼に敵を銃で撃ち倒し、目撃者はおびえて口を開けなかった。前任者たちは、力でギャングを根絶すると約束していた。フェレイラは別のやり方を試した。最初の1か月を、司祭や教師、商店主たちに何が必要かの意向を探ることに費やした。元ギャングの数人を助言役として引き込むことにも成功した。彼らは一緒に腰を据え、逮捕だけでなく仕事と学校に基づく計画を徹底的に議論してまとめた。誰もが賛成したわけではない。市議会は彼女の最初の予算を退けることを決議した。犯罪者におべっかを使おうとしていると彼女を非難する政治家もいた。彼女が反対していた10代の夜間外出禁止令はそれでも導入され、裁判所がこの規則を無効とするまで続いた。ゆっくりと、計画は効果を上げた。古参のギャングのリーダーたちは逮捕され、残った小さな集団を掃討するために特別部隊が送られた。かつて戦う者たちをけしかけていた10代の若者は、新しい訓練センターで仕事を見つけた。殺人は3年で半分に減った。フェレイラは慎重で、勝利を宣言しない。この数年が市にもっと穏やかな時代の到来を告げることを願っている、と言うだけだ。","words":[["gun down","idioms:2:gun down"],["stamp out","idioms:2:stamp out"],["sound out","idioms:2:sound out"],["rope in","idioms:2:rope in"],["thrash out","idioms:2:thrash out"],["shoot down","idioms:2:shoot down"],["butter up","idioms:2:butter up"],["strike down","idioms:2:strike down"],["mop up","idioms:2:mop up"],["egg on","idioms:2:egg on"],["usher in","idioms:2:usher in"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did Ferreira do in her first month?","o":["She arrested the gang leaders","She introduced a curfew","She asked community members what they needed","She asked the council for more officers"],"a":2,"why":"She spent her first month trying to sound out priests, teachers and shopkeepers about what they needed とある。"},{"q":"What happened to the curfew for teenagers?","o":["A court declared it invalid","Ferreira extended it","The council voted against it","It reduced murders by half"],"a":0,"why":"until a court chose to strike down the rule とある。"}]},
{"id":"i-0008","page":"idioms","group":"その他 ② 動詞＋副詞（他動詞）","kind":"おじの70歳の誕生会","text":"My uncle Marco's seventieth birthday party was held in the garden of his restaurant, and nobody who was there will forget it. Forty relatives arrived at noon to soak up the sunshine. The smell of grilled fish was enough to conjure up every summer of my childhood. The children were the first to eat. I watched my nephews wolf down three plates of pasta each and then polish off an entire chocolate cake. My aunt can still rattle off the names and birthdays of all forty relatives, and she did so twice. My cousin, who is a waiter, likes to reel off the whole menu from memory. After lunch, a band arrived. Marco, who once sang in a choir, began to belt out old love songs. He was so loud that he managed to drown out the trumpet. Then came the surprise. His daughter Sofia tends to bottle up her feelings, and she had kept a secret for months. In the middle of her speech, she happened to blurt out the news that she was expecting a baby. The announcement seemed to bowl over the whole family. Marco sat down, speechless for once. Then he stood up, hugged her, and asked the band to play something faster.","ja":"おじのマルコの70歳の誕生会は彼のレストランの庭で開かれ、その場にいた人は誰も忘れないだろう。40人の親戚が正午に集まり、日差しを満喫した。焼き魚のにおいは、私の子ども時代のすべての夏を思い起こさせるのに十分だった。最初に食べたのは子どもたちだった。おいたちがそれぞれパスタを3皿がつがつ食べ、それからチョコレートケーキを丸ごと平らげるのを私は見ていた。おばは今でも40人の親戚全員の名前と誕生日をすらすら言うことができ、実際に2回やってみせた。ウェイターをしているいとこは、メニューを全部そらですらすら並べ立てるのが好きだ。昼食の後、楽団がやって来た。かつて合唱団で歌っていたマルコは、古い恋の歌を大声で歌い始めた。あまりに声が大きく、トランペットの音をかき消してしまった。それから驚きがやって来た。彼の娘のソフィアは感情を抑え込みがちで、何か月も秘密を守っていた。スピーチの途中で、彼女は赤ちゃんができたという知らせをうっかり口走った。この発表は家族全員をひどく驚かせたようだった。マルコは、このときばかりは言葉を失って座り込んだ。それから立ち上がり、彼女を抱きしめ、もっと速い曲を演奏するよう楽団に頼んだ。","words":[["soak up","idioms:2:soak up"],["conjure up","idioms:2:conjure up"],["wolf down","idioms:2:wolf down"],["polish off","idioms:2:polish off"],["rattle off","idioms:2:rattle off"],["reel off","idioms:2:reel off"],["belt out","idioms:2:belt out"],["drown out","idioms:2:drown out"],["bottle up","idioms:2:bottle up"],["blurt out","idioms:2:blurt out"],["bowl over","idioms:2:bowl over"]],"qs":[{"q":"What did the speaker's aunt do at the party?","o":["She sang old love songs","She recited the names and birthdays of all the relatives","She baked the chocolate cake","She played the trumpet"],"a":1,"why":"My aunt can still rattle off the names and birthdays of all forty relatives, and she did so twice とある。"},{"q":"What was the surprise at the party?","o":["Marco announced his retirement","The band failed to arrive","The restaurant was being sold","Sofia revealed that she was expecting a baby"],"a":3,"why":"she happened to blurt out the news that she was expecting a baby とある。"}]},
{"id":"i-0009","page":"idioms","group":"その他 ③ 動詞＋前置詞","kind":"辛口コラムニスト","text":"For forty years, the columnist Walter Epps has made a career of saying what politicians would prefer not to hear. His weekly column in the Northern Courier is read by two million people. Epps does not defer to anyone. He was among the first to rail against the banking rules of the 1990s, and he continues to tear into ministers of every party. He refuses to pander to his readers, too. When they wanted lower taxes, he told them that the country could not afford them. His critics say that he tends to harp on the same few subjects: debt, pensions and the cost of housing. They add that his attacks sometimes border on cruelty. His editors frown on personal insults, and they have removed several from his drafts. Even his admirers admit that his confidence can verge on arrogance. Yet governments have learned to reckon with him. A minister once delayed a budget speech by a day in order to answer one of his columns. Epps, now seventy-eight, says the job of a journalist is to guard against comfortable lies. He has no plans to retire. Asked what he would do without his column, he replied that he would probably write angry letters to whoever replaced him.","ja":"40年にわたり、コラムニストのウォルター・エップスは、政治家が聞きたがらないことを言うのを仕事にしてきた。ノーザン・クーリエ紙の彼の週1回のコラムは200万人に読まれている。エップスは誰の意見にも従わない。1990年代の銀行規制を最初に激しく非難した一人であり、今もあらゆる党の閣僚を激しく非難し続けている。読者に迎合することも拒む。読者が減税を望んだとき、彼は、国にはその余裕がないと告げた。批判する人々は、彼が負債、年金、住宅費という同じいくつかの話題をくどくど言いがちだと言う。彼の攻撃はときに残酷に近い、とも付け加える。編集者たちは個人への侮辱に眉をひそめ、原稿からいくつかを削ってきた。彼を称賛する人々でさえ、その自信がほとんど傲慢に近くなりうることを認める。それでも、歴代の政府は彼を考慮に入れることを学んできた。ある大臣は、彼のコラムの一つに答えるために、予算演説を1日遅らせたことがある。現在78歳のエップスは、ジャーナリストの仕事は心地よいうそを防ぐことだと言う。引退の予定はない。コラムがなくなったら何をするかと尋ねられ、たぶん後任の誰かに怒りの手紙を書くだろうと答えた。","words":[["defer to","idioms:3:defer to"],["rail against","idioms:3:rail against"],["tear into","idioms:3:tear into"],["pander to","idioms:3:pander to"],["harp on","idioms:3:harp on"],["border on","idioms:3:border on"],["frown on","idioms:3:frown on"],["verge on","idioms:3:verge on"],["reckon with","idioms:3:reckon with"],["guard against","idioms:3:guard against"]],"qs":[{"q":"What do critics say about Epps?","o":["He keeps returning to the same few subjects","He flatters his readers","He supports only one party","He rarely writes about money"],"a":0,"why":"he tends to harp on the same few subjects とある。"},{"q":"What does the story about the budget speech show?","o":["Epps once worked for the government","Ministers refuse to read his column","Governments take his columns seriously","His editors changed his drafts"],"a":2,"why":"governments have learned to reckon with him とあり、大臣がコラムに答えるため演説を遅らせた。"}]},
{"id":"i-0010","page":"idioms","group":"その他 ③ 動詞＋前置詞","kind":"祖母の日記","text":"Last winter, while clearing my late grandmother's attic, I happened to stumble upon a box of her diaries. I began to leaf through the first one, expecting shopping lists. Instead, I found the story of a young engineer. She arrived in Canada in 1951, at the age of twenty-two. In the early pages, she seems to pine for her village in Hungary, and to yearn for her mother's cooking. The first winter was hard. Dishonest landlords would prey on new immigrants, and she lost two months' rent to one of them. More than once she admits that she might lapse into despair. But she did not succumb to homesickness for long. She found work in a radio factory. In the evenings, she liked to tinker with broken sets that the company had thrown away. She began to toy with the idea of opening her own repair shop. It seemed impossible for a woman without money. Then she learned to tap into a network of other Hungarian families, who lent her small sums. The shop opened in 1956 and stayed in business for thirty years. I never knew any of this. To me, she was simply the old woman who fixed my toys and never threw anything away.","ja":"去年の冬、亡くなった祖母の屋根裏部屋を片付けていて、私は彼女の日記の入った箱を偶然見つけた。買い物のメモだろうと思いながら、最初の1冊をぱらぱらめくり始めた。ところが、そこにあったのは若い技術者の物語だった。彼女は1951年、22歳でカナダに着いた。初めのほうのページでは、ハンガリーの村を恋しがり、母親の料理を切望しているようだ。最初の冬は厳しかった。不誠実な家主たちが新しい移民を食い物にしており、彼女はその一人に2か月分の家賃を取られた。絶望に陥るかもしれないと、彼女は一度ならず認めている。しかし、長くホームシックに屈することはなかった。彼女はラジオ工場で仕事を見つけた。夜には、会社が捨てた壊れたラジオをいじくるのが好きだった。自分の修理店を開くという考えを漠然と抱き始めた。金のない女性には不可能に思えた。やがて彼女は、ほかのハンガリー人家族のつながりを活用することを覚え、彼らが少額ずつ貸してくれた。店は1956年に開き、30年間営業を続けた。私はこのことを何も知らなかった。私にとって彼女は、おもちゃを直してくれて、何も捨てなかったおばあさんにすぎなかった。","words":[["stumble upon","idioms:3:stumble upon"],["leaf through","idioms:3:leaf through"],["pine for","idioms:3:pine for"],["yearn for","idioms:3:yearn for"],["prey on","idioms:3:prey on"],["lapse into","idioms:3:lapse into"],["succumb to","idioms:3:succumb to"],["tinker with","idioms:3:tinker with"],["toy with","idioms:3:toy with"],["tap into","idioms:3:tap into"]],"qs":[{"q":"What difficulty did the grandmother face in her first winter?","o":["She could not find any work","A dishonest landlord took two months' rent from her","She was sent back to Hungary","Her radio factory closed"],"a":1,"why":"Dishonest landlords would prey on new immigrants, and she lost two months' rent to one of them とある。"},{"q":"How did she get the money to open her shop?","o":["She sold her diaries","The radio company gave her a loan","She won a prize for engineering","Other Hungarian families lent her small sums"],"a":3,"why":"a network of other Hungarian families, who lent her small sums とある。"}]}
];
